  <graph edgedefault="undirected">
    <node id="Basal Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal Cell Skin Cancer is a common form of skin cancer that can recur or metastasize, requiring proper diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell skin cancer, or basal cell carcinoma (BCC), is the most common type of skin cancer, occurring predominantly in areas exposed to the sun and diagnosed in around 3 million cases annually in the United States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin">
      <data key="d0">Skin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin is the largest organ of the human body, serving as a barrier that protects against bacteria, chemicals, and helps regulate body temperature.'&lt;SEP&gt;Skin is the largest organ of the human body, serving protective functions and regulating temperature, forming the outermost barrier against disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="UV Radiation">
      <data key="d0">UV Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">UV radiation from the sun or tanning beds is a significant risk factor for developing basal cell skin cancer, potentially damaging the DNA in skin cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age">
      <data key="d0">Age</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age is a leading risk factor for developing prostate cancer, with increased risk as one gets older.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is a risk factor for basal cell skin cancer, with increased risk as sun exposure builds up over time, although it's becoming more common among younger individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, especially in men over 65, as incidence rates increase with age.&lt;SEP&gt;Age is a significant risk factor for prostate cancer, with increased incidence after the age of 65.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Suppression">
      <data key="d0">Immune Suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune suppression can increase the likelihood of developing basal cell skin cancer, especially in patients with weakened immune systems due to organ transplants or autoimmune diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal Cell Carcinoma is the most common skin cancer type and is generally less aggressive than squamous cell carcinoma, but more prevalent.&lt;SEP&gt;Basal cell carcinoma (BCC) is the most frequent skin cancer type, characterized by abnormal growth of basal cells in the epidermis, which rarely metastasizes and can often be treated effectively if caught early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="United States">
      <data key="d0">United States</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The United States is a country where approximately 3 million cases of basal cell skin cancer are diagnosed annually, making it a significant public health concern.&lt;SEP&gt;The United States is the country where an HPV vaccine protecting against several high-risk strains is available and recommended.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epidermis">
      <data key="d0">Epidermis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The epidermis is the outer layer of skin where basal cell skin cancer primarily occurs, consisting of basal cells, squamous cells, and melanocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cells">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cells are flat cells on the surface of the skin and other organs, and when cancerous, can lead to squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cells are a type of epithelial cell found in various tissues, including the cervix, which can become cancerous in cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cells are flat cells located in the upper part of the epidermis, and though they are not directly related to basal cell cancer, they represent one of the key types of skin cells involved in skin health and cancer. </data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Basal Cells">
      <data key="d0">Basal Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Basal cells are located in the lower part of the epidermis and are responsible for generating new skin cells that replace old or damaged ones; they are the primary cells affected in basal cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanocytes">
      <data key="d0">Melanocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanocytes are cells in the epidermis that produce melanin, responsible for skin pigmentation; they can also be involved in other skin cancers such as melanoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Lesions">
      <data key="d0">Skin Lesions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin lesions related to basal cell skin cancer can appear in various forms, such as flat pale areas, shiny bumps, or open sores that do not heal, signaling the need for medical attention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tanning Beds">
      <data key="d0">Tanning Beds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tanning beds are artificial sources of UV radiation that can increase the risk of developing basal cell skin cancer when used regularly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History">
      <data key="d0">Family History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A history of skin cancer in family members increases the likelihood of developing basal cell skin cancer, emphasizing the genetic component of cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Family History refers to the health background of blood relatives, which is important for understanding hereditary risks associated with cancers and other diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history encompasses the health conditions and diseases experienced by blood relatives, which can indicate potential genetic risk factors for conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history focuses on the health issues of blood relatives, which may indicate genetic predispositions to certain conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves gathering health information about relatives to assess risks for inherited diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves the health conditions and diseases that relatives have experienced, which can be relevant in assessing cancer risk, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history involves the health histories of family members, which can increase the likelihood of certain cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history is crucial in assessing genetic risks related to liver cancer, as some predispositions can be hereditary.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history is the health background of family members that can influence an individual's risk for conditions like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to health information about diseases and conditions within a person's family, influencing individual risk for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to health issues in a patient's relatives that can impact the patient's risk and approach to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the genetic predisposition that may increase the risk of developing thyroid cancer, often tied to inherited gene mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health background of a person's relatives, which can influence cancer risk and hereditary syndromes.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health background of relatives and can provide important insights into genetic risks for diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health background of relatives, which may provide insights into cancer risks and hereditary factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the health information passed down through generations, indicating possible hereditary risks for conditions like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the medical background of family members, which provides crucial information regarding genetic risk for diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family history refers to the record of health issues among family members, which can provide important insights for risk assessment in breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Full Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Full Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Full skin exams are regular check-ups recommended for individuals who have been treated for basal cell skin cancer to monitor for recurrence or new skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-66dd071b6c2be50e36cee1a1c1bd3b33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Glands">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The adrenal glands are essential components in the body's endocrine system, responsible for hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;The adrenal glands are small, crucial components of the endocrine system, essential for producing hormones that regulate metabolism and immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;The adrenal glands are small, triangle-shaped endocrine glands located atop each kidney, responsible for producing various hormones that regulate bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal Tumors are abnormal growths located on the adrenal glands, which produce essential hormones and can affect various bodily functions and health conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal Tumors can be benign or malignant and form in one or both adrenal glands, affecting hormone production and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenocortical Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adrenocortical Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenocortical Carcinoma is a rare type of cancer that begins in the adrenal cortex and can influence treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma is a specific type of adrenal cancer that develops in the adrenal cortex, which can be either functioning or nonfunctioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenocortical carcinoma is a type of adrenal gland cancer that begins in the adrenal cortex, which can be functioning or nonfunctioning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cushing Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cushing Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cushing Syndrome is a condition caused by excess cortisol production, often linked to adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 3: Types of Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 3: Types of Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 3 discusses various types of treatments available for patients diagnosed with cancer, focusing on personalized approaches based on individual circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapter 4: Treatment by Risk and Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Chapter 4: Treatment by Risk and Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter 4 provides information about treatment options tailored to the patient's risk factors and the likelihood of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI Scan (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) uses strong magnets and radio waves to produce detailed images of the body's interior without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI Scan (Magnetic Resonance Imaging) uses strong magnets to produce detailed images of the body's internal structures without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI Scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues, particularly useful in cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI Scan utilizes radio waves and strong magnets to create detailed images of the inside of the body without using x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan employs strong magnets and radio waves to capture detailed images of soft tissues, used to assess conditions like leukemic meningitis.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique used to visualize internal structures of the body, particularly the brain, often used in diagnosing conditions like lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses magnetic fields to create detailed images of organs and tissues, useful for detecting leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to generate images of organs and structures inside the body, often enhanced with contrast for clarity.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to produce images of the interior of the body without radiation exposure.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a type of imaging test that uses powerful magnets and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues in the body without using radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of the body's internal structures without using x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI Scan is a non-invasive imaging technique used to visualize internal structures of the body, including the prostate's size.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI Scans are imaging tests used to identify and diagnose adrenal tumors, particularly when other tests are inconclusive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormones">
      <data key="d0">Hormones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormones are chemical messengers produced by glands in the body, influencing various physiological processes and potentially implicated in adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormones are chemical substances produced by endocrine glands like the adrenal glands, influencing various bodily functions and processes.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormones are natural chemicals produced in the body that can influence the growth of hormone-sensitive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormones are regulatory substances produced by the ovaries that influence various bodily functions, including menstrual cycles and reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine System">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Endocrine System is a network of glands that produce hormones regulating metabolism, growth, and mood among other functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Cortex">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Cortex</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Adrenal Cortex is the outer part of the adrenal gland responsible for producing key hormones such as cortisol and aldosterone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Medulla">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Medulla</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Adrenal Medulla is the inner part of the adrenal gland that produces hormones responsible for the body's fight or flight response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8eae4d6dadf6a6fbd339efa4dec2172</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adrenal Gland Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Adrenal Gland Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adrenal gland cancer is a rare cancer type affecting the adrenal glands, occurring in about 1 to 2 people out of every 1 million.&lt;SEP&gt;Adrenal gland cancer is a rare type of cancer affecting the adrenal glands, affecting approximately 1 to 2 people out of every 1 million.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroblastoma">
      <data key="d0">Neuroblastoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroblastoma is a rare cancerous adrenal tumor that predominantly affects children age 5 and under, often found in infants.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroblastoma is a rare, cancerous adrenal tumor predominantly affecting infants and children under 5 years of age, often spreading to bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pheochromocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Pheochromocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pheochromocytoma is a type of tumor originating in the adrenal medulla, which can cause symptoms such as high blood pressure and sweating.&lt;SEP&gt;Pheochromocytoma originates in the adrenal medulla and is characterized by symptoms like high blood pressure and rapid heartbeat, mostly benign but can also be malignant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenoma is a common type of colon polyp that may become invasive colon cancer over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma is a non-cancerous growth that can develop into invasive rectal cancer over time. It is the most common type of polyp found in the colon and rectum.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma, or adrenocortical adenoma, is the most common type of adrenal gland tumor, typically noncancerous.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenoma, or adrenocortical adenoma, is the most prevalent type of noncancerous adrenal tumor that may be functioning or nonfunctioning.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomas are a type of polyp that, while non-cancerous, hold potential for developing into invasive colon cancer over time.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenomas are common types of polyps that can become cancerous if not detected and removed early.&lt;SEP&gt;An adenoma is a benign tumor that can develop in the colon or rectum and has the potential to become invasive rectal cancer over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Beckwith-Wiedemann Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a genetic condition associated with a higher risk of developing adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome is a genetic disorder that can increase the risk of developing adrenal tumors among other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carney Complex">
      <data key="d0">Carney Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carney complex is a genetic syndrome associated with various tumors, including adrenal tumors, due to an endocrine regulation malfunction.&lt;SEP&gt;Carney complex is a genetic syndrome that predisposes individuals to adrenal tumors and other neoplasias.&lt;SEP&gt;Carney complex is a rare genetic disorder that associates with various tumors, including thyroid tumors, due to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis (FAP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FAP is a hereditary condition linked to colorectal cancer and may increase the risk of adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;FAP is a hereditary syndrome that significantly increases the risk of developing various cancers, including thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;FAP is a rare inherited condition that leads to the formation of numerous polyps in the colon and rectum, increasing cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial Adenomatous Polyposis is a genetic disorder characterized by the formation of thousands of polyps in the colon, which can lead to colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Familial adenomatous polyposis is a genetic syndrome that primarily influences colorectal cancer risk but is linked to an increased risk of adrenal tumors as well.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Li-Fraumeni Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Li-Fraumeni Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary cancer predisposition syndrome associated with various cancers, including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary condition that predisposes individuals to a variety of cancers, including the potential for adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Li-Fraumeni syndrome is a hereditary disorder that predisposes individuals to several types of cancer, including pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lynch Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Lynch Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lynch Syndrome is a genetic condition that increases the risk of colorectal and other cancers due to mutations in mismatch repair genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of developing several types of cancer, particularly colorectal and endometrial cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch Syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of developing colon and other cancers, associated with mutations in MMR genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a genetic condition that increases the risk of developing certain cancers, including colorectal cancer, due to inherited mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary cancer syndrome that increases the risk of multiple types of cancers, including some adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of colon, uterine, and ovarian cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of various cancers, potentially including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is a hereditary condition that significantly increases the risk of colorectal and other cancers, including pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lynch syndrome is an inherited condition that increases the risk of various cancers, including bladder cancer, due to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 1 (MEN 1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MEN 1 is a genetic disorder characterized by tumors in endocrine glands, which may include adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1 is a genetic condition that results in tumors in various endocrine glands, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia Type 2 (MEN 2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MEN 2 is a hereditary syndrome associated with hormone-producing tumors, sometimes affecting the adrenal glands.&lt;SEP&gt;Multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2 is a group of inherited endocrine disorders associated with an increased risk of tumors, including adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)">
      <data key="d0">Neurofibromatosis Type 1 (NF1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NF1 is a genetic disorder that can increase the risk of developing various tumors, including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Neurofibromatosis type 1 is a genetic disorder that causes tumor growth in the nervous system and is associated with a higher risk for adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)">
      <data key="d0">Von Hippel-Lindau Syndrome (VHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">VHL is a hereditary condition linked to increased risk of tumors, including adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Von Hippel-Lindau syndrome is a hereditary condition that predisposes individuals to various tumors, including those that can occur in the adrenal glands.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7102a43dfb62e649d725bc95ba292688</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® Adrenal Tumors, 2022 is a set of updated guidelines providing information on managing adrenal tumors for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="family history">
      <data key="d0">family history</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family history encompasses health issues and diseases that might run in families, providing crucial information for understanding cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT scan">
      <data key="d0">CT scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that uses x-rays and computer technology to create detailed three-dimensional images of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A CT scan is a medical imaging test that uses x-rays to create detailed pictures of the inside of the body, often using contrast material for clarity in diagnosing conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scan is a medical imaging technique used to visualize internal structures of the body, particularly in detecting colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI scan">
      <data key="d0">MRI scan</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI scan employs radio waves and powerful magnets to capture images of the body's internal structures, often used in oncology.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI scan is a medical imaging technique that employs radio waves and magnets to visualize structures inside the body, often enhanced with contrast material for improved image quality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET scan">
      <data key="d0">PET scan</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan is an imaging test that uses radioactive tracers to identify cancer cells in the body by detecting how they absorb sugar, with bright spots indicating cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells in the body, allowing for more accurate imaging of tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="blood tests">
      <data key="d0">blood tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood tests are diagnostic procedures performed to analyze the patient's blood for abnormalities that can help in the diagnosis and treatment of conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are diagnostic procedures that analyze the patient's blood to detect abnormalities relevant for diagnosing various medical conditions, including cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood tests are laboratory analyses conducted to assess a patient’s health, including checking for potential diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="medical binder">
      <data key="d0">medical binder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical binder is an organizational tool for keeping medical records, test results, and health-related documents in one place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="imaging tests">
      <data key="d0">imaging tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests are medical procedures that visualize the inside of the body, crucial for diagnosing and monitoring conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Primary Care Physician is a medical professional who oversees overall patient health and coordinates care among specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician (PCP) acts as a coordinator of care and keeps informed of changes in the patient's treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a healthcare provider responsible for overseeing patient's overall health and addressing non-emergency medical needs.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician plays a crucial role in managing patient health and coordinating care among specialists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Records">
      <data key="d0">Medical Records</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical records are comprehensive documents containing a patient's health history, test results, and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical records are detailed documentation of a patient’s health history, treatment plans, and test results crucial for ongoing care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms are physical or mental indicators of a disease or condition, crucial for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms are physical or mental signs indicative of a disease or condition that a patient reports to their healthcare provider.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms are physical signs that may indicate health issues, including frequent urination or difficulty related to prostate problems.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of gliomas can include headaches, seizures, nausea, and cognitive changes; these occur due to increased pressure on the brain or tissue invasion.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of primary CNS lymphoma can include weakness, coordination problems, changes in personality, memory issues, and various neurological disruptions.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms refer to the physical or mental signs that indicate the presence of a disease, like those experienced in Primary CNS Lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, confusion, and personality changes can be associated with brain tumors including gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Health Care Provider is a trained professional who delivers medical services and oversees patient treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A Health Care Provider refers to professionals who deliver medical services, helping patients understand their test results and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a medical professional responsible for advising and guiding patients through their diagnosis, treatment options, and care plans for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional who delivers medical services and support, particularly important for patients dealing with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional who offers medical services and advice to patients regarding their health conditions and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider is a professional who offers medical services and guidance, including treatment options for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A health care provider, such as a hospital or clinic, delivers medical services and support, especially important for patients dealing with head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Care Providers are professionals who assist in diagnosing, treating, and maintaining health, particularly in relation to breast health screenings and tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests Results">
      <data key="d0">Tests Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test results are the outcomes of laboratory tests conducted on a patient to determine health status or diagnose conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Results">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging results are reports and visuals derived from imaging tests like CT scans, MRI, and PET scans that help in diagnosing health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging results refer to findings from medical imaging procedures used to diagnose and monitor health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Members">
      <data key="d0">Family Members</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Family members are relatives whose health history can inform a patient’s health risks and the likelihood of conditions like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family members refer to relatives who may provide health history information relevant to a patient's medical background.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical History">
      <data key="d0">Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical history is a comprehensive record of a patient's past health issues and treatments, essential for determining the best care.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical history is a comprehensive record of a patient's past health issues and treatments, essential for tailoring cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a comprehensive account of an individual's past health issues, treatments, and current medications, crucial for informing healthcare decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a comprehensive record of a patient's past health issues and treatments, essential for informing the current medical care strategy.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a comprehensive record of a patient's past health issues, treatments, and risk factors relevant to their current health.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a comprehensive record of an individual's past health issues and treatments, important for guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a comprehensive record that includes all health issues and treatments a patient has experienced, important for understanding their current condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history is a record that includes all past health issues, treatments, and treatments a patient has received.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history refers to a comprehensive record of a patient’s past health issues and treatments that assist in determining future medical care and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical history refers to a detailed record of a patient's health issues and treatments over their lifetime, which aids in deciding treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Physical Exam includes various evaluations performed by a healthcare provider to assess a person's overall health and identify any medical concerns related to AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A Physical Exam is a routine check-up performed by healthcare providers to assess a patient's general health and identify potential issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam evaluates a patient’s symptoms and overall health and is vital for diagnosing throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam includes assessments by a healthcare provider to evaluate overall health and check for signs of liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a clinical assessment conducted by health care providers to evaluate a patient's overall health and identify any abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a comprehensive assessment performed by healthcare providers to check various body systems and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;A physical exam is a routine medical procedure where a healthcare provider assesses the patient's overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical exam is a clinical assessment conducted by a healthcare provider to evaluate a patient's general health and detect any potential medical issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Questions">
      <data key="d0">Questions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Questions refer to inquiries that patients prepare to ask during medical appointments to understand their health better.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating diseases and injuries by using medical imaging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting images from diagnostic tests, crucial for cancer detection and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in interpreting medical images and diagnoses based on those images.&lt;SEP&gt;A Radiologist is a medical doctor with expertise in interpreting complex imaging results to assist in diagnosis and treatment planning for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging tests and diagnosing conditions based on imaging results.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging tests to aid in the diagnosis and treatment of conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging tests, essential in diagnosing and planning treatment for conditions like prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specialized in interpreting imaging tests, providing critical information to aid in patient diagnosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating diseases and injuries using medical imaging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor who interprets imaging results and conveys them to other healthcare professionals, such as oncologists.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing and treating diseases and injuries using medical imaging techniques.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical expert who specializes in interpreting medical images like MRIs and CT scans, playing a vital role in diagnosing conditions such as primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specialized in interpreting imaging exams like CT scans, providing crucial information for diagnosing diseases like CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional specialized in interpreting imaging tests to provide accurate reports regarding patients' health conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional who interprets imaging tests such as CT or MRI scans to diagnose conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a medical professional who specializes in interpreting medical images, crucial for diagnosing conditions such as prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist is a physician specialized in interpreting medical images which can aid in diagnosing thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiologist specializes in interpreting imaging results to assist in diagnosing conditions like gliomas and planning further treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa14ecd19e2d721cb99ea9590f0ede3a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG-PET Scan is a medical imaging test utilizing radioactive sugar to trace cancer cell locations, particularly beneficial in detecting adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SSTR-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">SSTR-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SSTR-PET Scan is a specialized imaging method that identifies cancerous cells based on somatostatin receptors, aiding in tumor diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal squamous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that arises from squamous cells, which can metastasize and requires careful treatment management.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can metastasize and affect lymph nodes, with several treatment options available.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer is the second most prevalent type of skin cancer, primarily impacting fair-skinned individuals and characterized as highly treatable.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can develop in areas of the skin and is often linked to prior skin damage such as wounds, burns, or scars.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can develop in various skin areas, often linked to old wounds, burns, or scars.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer characterized by the uncontrolled growth of squamous cells, often diagnosed through a skin biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell skin cancer is a type of skin cancer that may require various treatments including surgery and radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can spread to healthy tissue, forming tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Cells are abnormal cells that grow uncontrollably, potentially forming tumors, including types like squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer Cells refer to aberrant cells that divide uncontrollably, leading to cancer development, identified through biopsy analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade and destroy surrounding tissue, often leading to tumor formation.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade nearby tissues; in prostate cancer, these cells specifically arise from the prostate gland.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade surrounding tissues, leading to malignancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow and divide uncontrollably, leading to the formation of tumors and the potential spread of cancer to other body parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow uncontrollably and can be detected through various imaging tests, such as PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow uncontrollably and can spread to other parts of the body, necessitating various treatment options for removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that proliferate uncontrollably, leading to the formation of tumors and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are cells that divide uncontrollably and can invade neighboring tissues, leading to the development of tumors and disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biochemical Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biochemical Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biochemical Tests measure hormone levels in body fluids, aiding in the diagnosis and treatment monitoring of adrenal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanoma">
      <data key="d0">Melanoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanoma is a less common but potentially more aggressive form of skin cancer that arises from melanocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET (Positron Emission Tomography) scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancerous activity in the body by showing metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan (Positron Emission Tomography) involves the use of a radioactive tracer to detect areas of high metabolic activity in the body, often associated with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan (Positron Emission Tomography) uses radioactive tracers to visualize metabolic activity in tissues, particularly for cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan is a type of imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to visualize metabolic processes in the body, commonly used to evaluate cancer spread or treatment response.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET Scan uses a radioactive drug to visualize cancer cells in the body by detecting their metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is a type of imaging test that uses a radioactive drug (tracer) to highlight areas of cancerous activity in the body, indicating the presence of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that helps reveal how tissues and organs are functioning, particularly focusing on detecting cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to highlight cancerous cells in the body, commonly used in cancer detection and treatment monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan uses a radioactive tracer to visualize cancer cells and observe their metabolic activity, indicating growth rates.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET scan utilizes a radioactive tracer to observe metabolic activity in tissues, identifying cancer cell locations and growth rates.&lt;SEP&gt;A Positron Emission Tomography (PET) scan is an imaging test that helps identify cancerous tissues by showing metabolic activity, used alongside CT scans for comprehensive cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;A positron emission tomography (PET) scan is an imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to detect cancer cells in the body by identifying areas using sugars.&lt;SEP&gt;PET (Positron Emission Tomography) scan is a type of imaging test that uses a radioactive drug to detect cancer cells based on their metabolic activity.&lt;SEP&gt;PET Scan is a type of imaging test that helps identify cancerous cells in the body, particularly useful for diagnosing tumors such as adrenal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scan is a type of imaging test that helps show how your cells are using sugar and is effective in identifying cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PET scan is a type of imaging test that uses a radioactive tracer to show how tissues and organs function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine Tumors are a type of tumor that arises from neuroendocrine cells, which can produce hormones and have different characteristics compared to typical cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Neuroendocrine tumors are a rarer form of pancreatic cancer, which differ in their origin and treatment from more common forms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CSCC">
      <data key="d0">CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CSCC refers to cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, a common type of skin cancer that can be classified based on risk levels such as low-risk and high-risk.&lt;SEP&gt;CSCC refers to cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma, which is a common form of skin cancer that varies in risk levels, including low-risk and high-risk categories.&lt;SEP&gt;CSCC, or Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma, refers specifically to squamous cell cancer occurring in the skin, often requiring distinct treatment from other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors for Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors for Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Factors for Skin Cancer include various conditions and behaviors that increase the likelihood of developing skin cancer, particularly squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Wounds">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Wounds are persistent injuries that do not heal over time, which can lead to the development of skin cancers such as CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melanin">
      <data key="d0">Melanin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Melanin is a pigment produced by melanocytes in the skin, responsible for its color, and plays a role in protecting from UV radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1379433bc205cdec8af2edc77613acb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="squamous cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">squamous cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that can develop in skin damaged by various factors, including environmental toxins and chronic wounds.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a subtype of lung cancer that originates in the squamous cells lining the airways of the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="actinic keratosis">
      <data key="d0">actinic keratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratosis is a precursor to squamous cell carcinoma, characterized by rough, scaly patches on the skin caused by prolonged sun exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xeroderma pigmentosum">
      <data key="d0">Xeroderma pigmentosum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) is a genetic disorder that impairs the body's ability to repair DNA damage caused by sunlight, increasing the risk of skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa">
      <data key="d0">Recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a rare inherited skin disorder that causes skin fragility and increases the risk of squamous cell carcinoma due to trauma-induced blisters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="immunosuppressants">
      <data key="d0">immunosuppressants</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunosuppressants are drugs used to prevent organ rejection that weaken the immune system, increasing susceptibility to infections and skin cancers like squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biopsy">
      <data key="d0">biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy is a medical procedure that involves removing a sample of tissue for testing, crucial for confirming cancer presence.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical procedure that involves taking a sample of tissue for examination to diagnose diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical procedure used to confirm the presence of skin cancer by removing samples of suspicious lesions for microscopic examination.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical procedure where a small sample of tissue is extracted for analysis, critical in diagnosing cancers like cutaneous B-cell lymphoma and bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A biopsy is a medical test involving the extraction of a sample of tissue for examination to determine the presence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="environmental toxins">
      <data key="d0">environmental toxins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Environmental toxins include pollutants such as tobacco smoke and exhaust from vehicles, which contribute to skin damage and an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chronic wounds">
      <data key="d0">chronic wounds</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic wounds such as ulcers and burns can lead to the formation of squamous cell carcinoma, known as Marjolin’s ulcer, which is difficult to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="genetic syndromes">
      <data key="d0">genetic syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain genetic syndromes, such as Xeroderma pigmentosum, increase the risk of developing squamous cell carcinoma due to impaired DNA repair mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="weakened immune system">
      <data key="d0">weakened immune system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A weakened immune system can lead to increased risks of various infections and diseases, including squamous cell carcinoma, especially after organ transplants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the lymphatic system and can contribute to a weakened immune system, heightening the risk of developing squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chronic lymphocytic leukemia">
      <data key="d0">chronic lymphocytic leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic lymphocytic leukemia is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, which can weaken the immune system and increase cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV/AIDS">
      <data key="d0">HIV/AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV/AIDS can severely weaken the immune system, making individuals more susceptible to squamous cell carcinoma and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="skin biopsy">
      <data key="d0">skin biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin biopsy is a medical procedure used to confirm the presence of skin cancer by removing samples of suspicious lesions for microscopic examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3821a6febf2d21784059ac62a6e659c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Punch Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Punch Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin punch biopsy is a medical procedure used to obtain a sample of skin and connective tissue for genetic testing related to leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A skin punch biopsy is a procedure where a small section of skin is removed to analyze genetic material, often used for evaluating leukemia predisposition.&lt;SEP&gt;Skin punch biopsy is a medical procedure where a small piece of skin is removed for testing, typically to diagnose lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excision Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excision Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Excision biopsy involves the complete removal of a lesion and is performed to obtain a definitive diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Cutaneous Squamous Cell Carcinoma (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CSCC is a common type of skin cancer that primarily affects lighter-skinned individuals, often caused by sun exposure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratosis">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic Keratosis is a common skin condition characterized by rough, scaly patches caused by prolonged sun exposure, which can potentially progress to skin cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Actinic keratosis is a skin condition characterized by rough patches, which may develop into squamous cell carcinoma if untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Binder">
      <data key="d0">Medical Binder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical binder is a system for organizing medical information and records, aiding cancer patients in managing their treatment process and relevant documents.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is a tool for organizing medical records, including test results and treatment history for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is a tool used to organize patient health information, such as test results and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical binder is an organizational tool used by patients to keep track of important medical records, test results, and treatment information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History">
      <data key="d0">Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A health history refers to a record of a patient's past and present health conditions, essential for determining treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Health History refers to a comprehensive account of an individual's past medical history, including diseases and conditions in family members.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history is a comprehensive record of an individual's past health issues and treatments, crucial for determining appropriate medical care.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history is a comprehensive record of an individual's past health issues, treatments, and surgeries.&lt;SEP&gt;Health history refers to the medical information and background of a patient that helps healthcare providers make informed decisions about testing and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Needle biopsy involves using a needle to extract tissue from a specific area for testing, often used in lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Nodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nodal disease refers to the condition where cancer has spread to the lymph nodes, indicating advanced stages of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6eafb110f88c7af38808f77f59ed155d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marjolin’s Ulcer">
      <data key="d0">Marjolin’s Ulcer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Marjolin’s ulcer is a more aggressive form of squamous cell skin cancer that arises in scars or chronic wounds, making it harder to treat.&lt;SEP&gt;Marjolin’s ulcer is a more aggressive form of squamous cell skin cancer that occurs in chronic wounds, making it harder to treat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The health care team comprises various medical professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive care for patients diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care team consists of medical professionals involved in diagnosing, treating, and supporting patients with oscillating medical needs, including those affected by cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care team for cancer management includes various specialists who collaborate in the diagnosis, treatment, and care of cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The health care team is a collective of medical professionals involved in a patient's care, including doctors, nurses, and support staff.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologist">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dermatologists are medical doctors who specialize in diagnosing and treating skin conditions, including skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgeons are dermatologists with specialized training in Mohs surgery, which is used to treat skin cancer while conserving healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical oncologist specializes in the surgical treatment of cancer, including the removal of tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical oncologists are specialists in cancer surgery and are trained to remove cancerous tumors and affected tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A medical oncologist is a doctor who specializes in treating cancer with chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and other medications.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a doctor who specializes in treating cancer with systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is a physician who treats cancer with chemotherapy and manages overall cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is an expert in treating cancer using chemotherapy and other medicinal approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist is an expert in treating cancer with medications, including chemotherapy and other targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in treating cancer using chemotherapy and other systemic treatments, playing a key role in the cancer care team.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in treating cancer with chemotherapy and assists in managing patients with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist specializes in treating cancer with medications, including chemotherapy and immunotherapy for lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer using systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer with systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and immunotherapy and often coordinates with other specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer with systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical oncologist treats cancer with systemic therapies such as chemotherapy and targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Medical oncologists are specialized doctors who administer chemotherapy and other drug therapies to treat cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist analyzes cells and tissues from biopsies to provide diagnoses and staging for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a doctor who examines tissues, cells, and organs to diagnose diseases including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues, cells, and organs, crucial for interpreting biopsy results.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who examines tissue samples to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing diseases by examining tissue samples and biopsy results.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing diseases by examining tissue samples, including those from ovarian cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues and cells under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical doctor who specializes in diagnosing diseases by examining tissues and cellular samples.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert specializing in diagnosing diseases by examining tissue, blood, and other samples from patients, including those with leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who examines cells using a microscope to confirm cancer diagnoses and assess tumor characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical expert who examines tissues and cells to diagnose diseases, including various forms of lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines biopsy samples to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines cancer cells compared to normal cells to determine the grade of cancer and its potential spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical professional who examines removed tissue to determine the presence and extent of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical specialist responsible for examining tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a medical specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to determine the presence and type of cancer, aiding in the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist is a specialized medical doctor who examines tissues and samples to diagnose diseases, particularly cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists analyze samples from biopsies or surgeries to diagnose diseases, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologists analyze tissue samples from biopsies to determine cancer stages and assist in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Plastic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Plastic surgeons perform reconstructive surgeries post-cancer removal to restore appearance and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck surgeons treat cancerous and noncancerous conditions in the head and neck regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A radiation oncologist administers radiation therapy as part of a treatment plan for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist designs and oversees radiation therapy regimens for cancer patients, including those with thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist develops and administers radiation therapy plans for treating cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer using radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical doctor specialized in treating cancer with radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a medical professional specializing in treating cancer with radiation therapy and plays a role in the treatment of primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist is a physician who specializes in treating cancer with radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist specializes in treating cancer with radiation therapy, often used in lung cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiation oncologist uses radiation therapy to treat cancer, particularly as part of a comprehensive treatment plan for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiation oncologists are trained to use radiation therapy to treat various cancers, including skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacist">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pharmacist prepares cancer-related medications and provides treatment advice to patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Pharmacists play a role in preparing and dispensing medications, including those for cancer treatment, and counseling patients on their use.&lt;SEP&gt;Pharmacists prepare and dispense cancer-related medications and may provide advice on drug interactions and side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geneticist">
      <data key="d0">Geneticist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Geneticists study inherited traits and how certain genetic disorders may correlate with cancer risk, providing counseling for families at risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology nurses provide direct care to cancer patients, managing their treatment and helping them cope with side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photodynamic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Photodynamic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Photodynamic therapy (PDT) is a treatment method using a light-sensitive drug that activates upon light exposure to kill cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Photodynamic therapy (PDT) is a treatment that utilizes a light-sensitive drug to kill cancer cells upon exposure to specific wavelengths of light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cryotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Cryotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Cryotherapy is a localized treatment that freezes and destroys abnormal skin cells, often used for precancerous and cancerous lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a technique that uses extreme cold to destroy cancerous and precancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cryotherapy is a treatment that uses extreme cold to freeze and destroy cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Topical Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Topical Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Topical therapies are treatments applied directly to the skin to combat skin conditions, including actinic keratosis and squamous cell carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Topical therapies involve applying medications directly to the skin to treat skin cancers and precancerous lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;Topical therapies involve the application of medication directly to the skin to treat conditions such as skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">5-Fluorouracil is a chemotherapy agent applied topically to treat superficial skin cancers and precancerous lesions.&lt;SEP&gt;5-Fluorouracil is a topical chemotherapy agent used to treat early stages of squamous cell skin cancer and actinic keratoses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imiquimod">
      <data key="d0">Imiquimod</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Imiquimod is a topical immune response modifier that helps combat abnormal skin growths by stimulating the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Imiquimod is a topical immune response modifier used to treat superficial squamous cell carcinomas and actinic keratoses.&lt;SEP&gt;Imiquimod is a topical treatment that stimulates the immune system to fight skin cancer and is often used for superficial cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologists">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Dermatologists are physicians specializing in skin conditions, responsible for diagnosing and managing skin cancers including squamous cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs surgeons are dermatologists with specialized training in Mohs micrographic surgery, a technique used to treat skin cancer while preserving healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical oncologists are doctors who perform surgeries to remove tumors and affected tissues in cancer patients, including those with skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Medical Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical oncologists specialize in the treatment of cancer through medications such as chemotherapy and targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologists">
      <data key="d0">Pathologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologists analyze biopsy samples to diagnose cancer and determine its characteristics and stage, playing a critical role in treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plastic Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Plastic Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Plastic surgeons perform reconstructive surgeries after tumor removals related to skin cancer to restore function and appearance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck surgeons treat conditions related to the deeper tissues and structures of the head and neck, including cancers that affect these areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Oncologists">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Oncologists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation oncologists specialize in using various forms of radiation therapy to treat cancer, including skin cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharmacists">
      <data key="d0">Pharmacists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Pharmacists are responsible for dispensing medications and advising on their proper use and potential side effects, particularly in the context of cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Geneticists">
      <data key="d0">Geneticists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Geneticists study the genetic components associated with cancer and provide counseling to families at risk for hereditary conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nurses">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nurses</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology nurses are specialized nurses who provide direct care to cancer patients, manage therapies, and support patients through their treatment journey.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide direct care to cancer patients and assist with management of treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide direct care to cancer patients, managing symptoms and supporting treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncology nurses provide direct patient care and support, helping manage cancer treatments and addressing side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Superficial Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Superficial Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Superficial treatments are therapeutic procedures that target only the outermost layer of skin, often used for early stages of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8ac9bf8f198bc5ad54fd1a7b843e810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-fluorouracil">
      <data key="d0">5-fluorouracil</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">5-fluorouracil (5-FU) is a chemotherapy agent applied topically for treating skin cancers like actinic keratosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcipotriene">
      <data key="d0">Calcipotriene</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcipotriene, sold under brands like Sorilux and Dovonex, is a topical vitamin D analog used to enhance the immune response against skin conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tirbanibulin">
      <data key="d0">Tirbanibulin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tirbanibulin, branded as Klisyri, is a topical microtubule inhibitor used for treating actinic keratosis by eliminating fast-growing cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage">
      <data key="d0">Curettage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage is a surgical method involving scraping away abnormal skin growths using a spoon-shaped tool.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and Electrodesiccation (C&amp;E)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">C&amp;E is a procedure combining scraping of visible tumors with cauterization to kill any remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohs Surgery is a specialized surgical procedure used to treat high-risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer by removing the cancerous tissue layer by layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs Surgery is a specific surgical technique used for high-risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, ensuring complete removal of cancerous tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique that removes cancerous skin along with surrounding normal tissue, ensuring complete removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique used to remove high-risk squamous cell carcinoma and ensure complete cancer removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a precise surgical technique used to remove skin cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer, in layers to ensure complete removal while preserving as much healthy tissue as possible.&lt;SEP&gt;Mohs surgery is a specialized surgical technique used for high-risk squamous cell carcinoma to achieve complete excision with minimal impact on healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Standard Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard Surgical Excision is a recommended treatment for high-risk local Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC), involving the removal of a wider margin of normal tissue around the lesion.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision involves removing a tumor along with surrounding normal tissue, performed under local anesthesia.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision involves removing the cancerous tumor along with a margin of healthy tissue for local CSCC treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard surgical excision is a conventional method for removing cancerous tissue, ensuring clear margins to reduce the risk of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph Node Dissection is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes to check for the presence of cancer cells, particularly in the context of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph Node Dissection is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes to test for the spread of cancer during cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a more extensive surgery used to remove lymph nodes that may contain cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node dissection is a surgical procedure that removes lymph nodes to determine if cancer spread has occurred and is part of staging cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A sentinel lymph node biopsy (SNLB) is a surgical procedure that helps determine if cancer has spread to lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;A sentinel lymph node biopsy helps in determining the spread of cancer by identifying the first lymph nodes cancer may have reached.&lt;SEP&gt;A sentinel lymph node biopsy identifies and removes the first node likely to be affected by cancer spread, often conducted during tumor surgeries.&lt;SEP&gt;A sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure to identify and assess potential spread of cancer to nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy (SLNB or SNB) is a surgical procedure to remove sentinel lymph nodes to check for the presence of cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy is a diagnostic procedure used to identify the first lymph node(s) that cancer cells may spread to, involving the injection of a dye or radioactive substance.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel Lymph Node Biopsy is a procedure to identify lymph nodes that may contain cancer by injecting a dye or radioactive substance.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy involves the removal of specific lymph node tissue samples to check for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Sentinel lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure to remove and examine sentinel nodes for cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Skin Cancer (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer that arises from squamous cells and may develop from actinic keratosis if left untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Skin Cancer is a type of skin cancer that can vary in severity and risk levels and is categorized as local, regional, or metastatic based on its spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medical Office">
      <data key="d0">Medical Office</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Medical Office is a facility where various minor surgical procedures, including Curettage and Electrodesiccation, can be performed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Local Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local anesthesia is a technique used to numb a specific area of the body, allowing patients to undergo minor surgical procedures without pain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Margin">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Margin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical margin refers to the normal tissue around a tumor that is removed during surgery to ensure no cancer cells remain.&lt;SEP&gt;The surgical margin is the normal tissue surrounding a cancerous lesion, which is critical in determining whether additional treatment is required after surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;The surgical margin refers to the healthy tissue surrounding a tumor that is removed during excision to ensure all cancerous cells are eradicated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dermatologic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Dermatologic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Dermatologic Surgeon specializes in surgical procedures related to skin conditions, including Mohs Surgery and other treatments for skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d994e430bb9890c905d7914fad9c118</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">General Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General Anesthesia is a medically induced state of unconsciousness used during surgeries to prevent pain and awareness.&lt;SEP&gt;General anesthesia is a medically induced state of unconsciousness used during procedures like direct laryngoscopy to ensure patient comfort and immobility.&lt;SEP&gt;General anesthesia is a state of controlled unconsciousness used during surgery to prevent pain and awareness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External beam radiation therapy directs radiation at the tumor from a machine for treatment of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Brachytherapy involves placing a radiation source close to or in direct contact with the tumor for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation therapy where a radiation source is placed inside or near the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation therapy where a source of radiation is placed inside or very close to the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of internal radiation treatment used for cervical cancer, delivered directly to the tumor site.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy where radioactive material is placed inside the body to target cancer cells directly, delivering a high dose of radiation to a tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a form of radiation treatment where a radioactive source is placed inside or next to the tumor, allowing for targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a localized radiation therapy that involves placing radioactive sources directly in or near a tumor to deliver effective radiation treatment for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a type of internal radiation treatment where a source of radiation is placed inside or close to the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is a type of radiation treatment that places radioactive material directly inside or near the tumor.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is an internal radiation therapy technique that delivers a high dose of radiation directly to the tumor while minimizing exposure to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Brachytherapy is an internal radiation therapy technique where radioactive material is placed inside the body, allowing high doses of radiation to be directed at the tumor while preserving surrounding healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cisplatin">
      <data key="d0">Cisplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug given intravenously, used in combination with 5-FU for treating anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug known for its efficacy in fighting cancer but is associated with several side effects, including kidney damage and hearing loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug that contains platinum and is used for treating squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug that is frequently employed as a primary treatment option for metastatic cervical cancer, often in combination with other agents.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a chemotherapy drug used to treat various cancers but is associated with side effects such as kidney damage, tinnitus, and hearing loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a commonly used platinum-based chemotherapy drug that disrupts the growth of cancer cells, particularly effective in the treatment of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat bladder cancer, but it may cause kidney damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug frequently used in the management of cervical cancer, inhibiting cancer cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug often included in treatment regimens for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used as a standard treatment for various cancers, including metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used for treating various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug used to treat various types of cancer, including squamous cell skin cancer, by targeting rapidly dividing cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Cisplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy medication used to treat several cancers by interfering with DNA replication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carboplatin">
      <data key="d0">Carboplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carboplatin is a chemotherapy agent used to treat various cancers, including ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a chemotherapy drug recommended for treating metastatic anal cancer, often used in combination with paclitaxel.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is a platinum-based chemotherapy drug that is commonly administered alongside paclitaxel to treat ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is an alternative chemotherapy drug used when cisplatin is too harsh for the patient.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is an alternative platinum-based chemotherapy drug used when patients cannot tolerate cisplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another platinum chemotherapy agent used to treat similar cancers as Cisplatin and has a similar mechanism of action.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another platinum-based chemotherapy drug that targets fast-growing cancer cells, similar to Cisplatin.&lt;SEP&gt;Carboplatin is another platinum-based chemotherapy drug used for treating squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that play a critical role in the body's immune system and are involved in filtering substances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Grafts">
      <data key="d0">Skin Grafts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin grafts are patches of skin surgically removed from one area of the body to be transplanted to another area needing repair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Intention Healing">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Intention Healing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary intention healing is a wound healing process where the wound is left open to heal naturally without stitches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohs Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Mohs Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mohs procedure is a surgical technique used to treat skin cancer where layers of cancer-containing skin are progressively removed and examined.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrons">
      <data key="d0">Electrons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrons are subatomic particles used in external beam radiation therapy to treat superficial tumors effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Photons">
      <data key="d0">Photons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Photons are high-energy particles used in external beam radiation therapy to target and treat deeper, more invasive tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9cc65c1c26b9e24bfb2c8c966f734a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Therapy may involve chemotherapy or immunotherapy and is recommended for patients with high-risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer or when surgery is not an option.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic Therapy refers to treatments that target cancer throughout the body, typically involving medications like chemotherapy or immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy encompasses treatments like chemotherapy or immunotherapy that distribute medication throughout the body to target cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves administering chemotherapy or immunotherapy that targets cancer cells throughout the body, particularly for metastatic anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves administering treatments that travel through the bloodstream to reach cancer cells throughout the body, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves medical treatment methods that work throughout the body, especially important for managing metastatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves the use of medications to treat cancer that has spread throughout the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves treating cancer with medication, including chemotherapy, which can be used when surgery is not viable or as an adjunct to radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves treatment administered through the bloodstream to target cancer cells throughout the body, including chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves treatments that affect both cancer and normal cells, typically administered intravenously or orally, with side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy involves treatments that enter the bloodstream and affect cells throughout the body, including chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is a broad category of treatment methods that may include chemotherapy and targeted therapies, typically used in ALL treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy is a treatment approach involving medications that target the entire body, often used for metastatic cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy refers to treatments that address cancer throughout the body, including chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy refers to treatments that affect the entire body, such as chemotherapy, designed to target cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy refers to treatments that affect the entire body, usually delivered intravenously, like chemotherapy and immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy refers to treatments that are distributed throughout the body, often used to target cancerous cells in AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy refers to treatments that can affect both cancerous and normal cells. These therapies can be given intravenously or orally and include several categories.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic therapy refers to treatments that work throughout the body to treat cancer, which can include chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted Therapy is a form of treatment that uses drugs or substances to identify and attack specific cancer cells without harming normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapies attack specific receptors on cancer cells, including EGFR inhibitors used for treating squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy drugs attack specific parts of cancer cells to slow their growth and spread, offering a more focused approach to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy employs drugs to specifically target cancer cell mutations, a modern approach to treat various stages of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy involves drugs that specifically attack cancer cells while minimizing damage to normal cells, offering a personalized treatment approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy involves medicines aimed at specific types of receptors on cancer cells, enhancing the precision of cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a cancer treatment that uses drugs or other substances to precisely identify and attack cancer cells, typically with fewer side effects compared to traditional treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a form of cancer treatment that specifically targets genetic or molecular features of cancer cells to inhibit their growth or survival.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a treatment approach focusing on specific cancer cell markers to deliver drugs directly to the cancerous cells, minimizing harm to healthy cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy is a type of cancer treatment that targets specific pathways or abnormalities in cancer cells, often used when other treatments are not effective.&lt;SEP&gt;Targeted therapy utilizes drugs that specifically target cancer cell mutations associated with bladder cancer, representing a tailored approach to treatment for patients with genetic predispositions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cetuximab">
      <data key="d0">Cetuximab</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cetuximab (Erbitux) is an EGFR inhibitor drug specifically designed to target certain types of cancer, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cetuximab (Erbitux) is an EGFR inhibitor used for treating squamous cell skin cancer and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cemiplimab">
      <data key="d0">Cemiplimab</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cemiplimab (Libtayo) is a PD-1 inhibitor used in immunotherapy for squamous cell skin cancer and other cancers, enhancing the immune response against tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Cemiplimab (Libtayo) is a PD-1 inhibitor used in immunotherapy for squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cemiplimab (Libtayo) is an immunotherapy agent used in treating various cancers, particularly those with specific genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is a PD-1 inhibitor, which is another drug used in immunotherapy to treat various cancers, including squamous cell skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is also a PD-1 inhibitor employed in immunotherapy for various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is an immune checkpoint inhibitor that helps the immune system attack cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is an immunotherapy agent that helps the immune system recognize and destroy cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab, also known by its brand name Keytruda, is an immunotherapy drug used to treat cancers with microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H) or deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDA">
      <data key="d0">FDA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The FDA (Food and Drug Administration) is a federal agency responsible for the regulation and approval of drugs and new treatment methodologies in the United States.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA (U.S. Food and Drug Administration) is the agency responsible for regulating and approving drugs and treatments in the United States.&lt;SEP&gt;The FDA, or Food and Drug Administration, is a government agency that approves drugs and treatments based on trial results.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is responsible for approving medications and treatments after clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is responsible for approving new drugs and treatment methods, ensuring their safety and efficacy for public use.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is responsible for regulating and approving new drugs and treatments after thorough evaluation from clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trial">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Clinical Trial is a research study designed to evaluate the effectiveness and safety of new treatments or drugs, often using placebos as control.&lt;SEP&gt;A Clinical Trial is a research study that tests new treatments or therapies for cancer and involves patient participation to evaluate their effectiveness and safety.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study aimed at evaluating a medical, surgical, or behavioral intervention, including new treatments for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study conducted to test new treatments or therapies for efficacy and safety in patients with conditions like primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study conducted with patients to evaluate a medical, surgical, or behavioral intervention, often for new cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that explores new treatments and is encouraged for patients with anal cancer where available.&lt;SEP&gt;A clinical trial is a research study that tests new treatments or procedures to improve cancer care and patient outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical Trials may offer experimental treatment options for Squamous Cell Skin Cancer patients, including new chemotherapy or immunotherapy approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical Trials offer experimental treatment options for patients with Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, including new therapies such as immunotherapy or targeted therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments and therapies for conditions such as AML to determine their effectiveness and safety.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are systematic studies to assess new medical treatments or drugs in human subjects to evaluate their safety and efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials provide opportunities for patients to receive new treatments, including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, or immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects are adverse effects experienced by patients due to treatments like chemotherapy and radiation, varying in severity and type among individuals.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are adverse reactions that may occur as a result of cancer treatments, such as fatigue, nausea, and hair loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects are the unintended health impacts resulting from cancer treatment, such as fatigue, nausea, and hair loss.&lt;SEP&gt;Side effects encompass adverse effects experienced by patients undergoing systemic therapy, differing based on the specific treatments administered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EGFR">
      <data key="d0">EGFR</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">EGFR (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor) is a protein that influences cell growth and division; inhibiting it can prevent cancer cell proliferation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1 inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PD-1 inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 inhibitors are a class of immunotherapy drugs that block the PD-1 protein, enhancing the immune system's ability to fight cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6fd03791661f76f6c1a246b2074b2a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Cancer Centers">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Cancer Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Cancer Centers are research facilities in the United States that provide cancer treatment and clinical trials for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Cancer Centers are specified cancer treatment centers in the United States that offer information and access to clinical trials for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Cancer Centers provide resources and guidelines for cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Cancer Centers provide access to cancer treatment and clinical trials in the United States.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) cancer centers are a network of leading cancer treatment institutions that provide high-quality care and conduct clinical trials for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) consists of leading cancer centers that offer treatment and support for those participating in clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a U.S. government agency that provides information about cancer treatment options, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a leading federal agency for cancer research and funding, including clinical trial access.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a part of the National Institutes of Health (NIH) that conducts and supports cancer research and provides information about cancer treatments, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a prominent organization in the U.S. focused on cancer research and treatment, including managing information about clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute is a government agency focused on cancer research and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) provides resources and information regarding clinical trials through its website clinicaltrials.gov.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) provides resources for finding clinical trials and medical information to the public.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Information Service">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Information Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCI’s Cancer Information Service (CIS) offers assistance and information to individuals regarding cancer, including clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Information Service provides support and information to individuals seeking guidance on cancer treatment and clinical trial options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Actinic Keratoses">
      <data key="d0">Actinic Keratoses</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Actinic keratoses are pre-cancerous lesions that can develop into skin cancer if untreated, often treated with various therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Placebo">
      <data key="d0">Placebo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A placebo is an inactive treatment often used in clinical trials to compare against active drugs, helping to assess the true effects of the treatment being tested.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Standard Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard Treatment includes established therapies that are commonly accepted practices in the medical community for specific diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard treatment refers to established therapies that are commonly used as benchmarks in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard treatment refers to the established and widely accepted therapies for managing various types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trial Enrollment">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trial Enrollment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical trial enrollment involves patients participating in clinical trials to test new treatments under research conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk Assessment is the evaluation process that determines the risk level of Squamous Cell Skin Cancer based on tumor characteristics and growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk Assessment is the process of evaluating the characteristics of Squamous Cell Skin Cancer to determine the appropriate level of treatment required.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk assessment in oncology refers to evaluating tumor features and patient factors to determine the likelihood of cancer recurrence and severity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Curettage and Electrodesiccation">
      <data key="d0">Curettage and Electrodesiccation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Curettage and electrodesiccation (C&amp;E) is a recommended treatment option for low-risk CSCC, particularly for early-stage cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Curettage and electrodesiccation are surgical techniques used to treat squamous cell skin cancer by scraping away the cancerous tissue and using electrical current to destroy remaining cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Standard Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard cancer care involves routine medical practices provided to patients, including diagnosis, treatment planning, and ongoing support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factor">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A risk factor is any condition or behavior that increases the likelihood of developing a disease, such as certain cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Risk factors are characteristics or behaviors that increase the likelihood of developing cancer, such as location and size of the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60426652ddf8265bf75caba550026d38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDEMA">
      <data key="d0">PDEMA</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">PDEMA refers to another surgical approach for skin cancer treatment, often preferred for very high-risk cases of CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;PDEMA stands for another surgical approach (Potentially Deep Excision with Margin Assessment) that is utilized for very high-risk cases of CSCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-based chemotherapy is a class of antineoplastic agents commonly used in the treatment of various cancers, including cervical cancer, to inhibit tumor growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a systemic therapy option used in combination with radiation therapy for treating certain cases of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is the primary systemic treatment for cervical cancer and is often part of chemoradiation strategies for more advanced stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is utilized in systemic therapy for patients with CSCC, particularly when there is a risk of recurrence after initial treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy refers to a class of chemotherapy drugs, such as cisplatin, that are often used for treating various cancers, including metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapy with Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapy with Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy enhances the immune response against tumors and may be preferred for treating certain eligible patients with CSCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy using immune checkpoint inhibitors is a treatment option for patients with high-risk Squamous Cell Skin Cancer who are eligible for this approach.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapy with immune checkpoint inhibitors is a preferred treatment for eligible patients, enhancing the immune response against cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Grade">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor grade describes how abnormal the cancer cells appear under a microscope and is a key factor in predicting cancer behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade indicates how abnormal cancer cells appear under a microscope and is associated with the aggressiveness of the cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade is an evaluation of how quickly cancer is expected to grow and spread based on how abnormal the tumor cells appear compared to normal cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor grade refers to a classification that indicates the degree of abnormality of tumor cells and their growth rate, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Connective Tissue in Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Connective Tissue in Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The presence of fibrous or connective tissue in a tumor is a sign of high-risk cancer and can influence treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weakened Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Weakened Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A weakened immune system increases the risk of developing CSCC and the likelihood of cancer recurrence after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Excision">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical excision is a primary treatment for CSCC, involving removal of the cancerous tissue and a margin of surrounding healthy tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a9d0006f1c78fe4fa7bb74b756033104</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-Based Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-Based Chemotherapy can be used alone or in combination with radiation therapy for patients with Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, particularly in high-risk cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-Based Chemotherapy is a specific type of systemic chemotherapy that is often used as part of treatment regimens for various cancers, including skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-Based Chemotherapy is a systemic therapy option recommended for treating Squamous Cell Skin Cancer, particularly in combination with radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-based chemotherapy is a type of systemic treatment recommended for use with radiation when treating cancer after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA) Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A fine-needle aspiration biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure using a thin needle to obtain tissue samples from a tumor for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration Biopsy is a procedure used to obtain samples from suspicious lymph nodes to diagnose regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A core needle biopsy is a procedure used to obtain a tissue sample from a tumor for diagnosis and staging of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A core needle biopsy is a type of biopsy that removes a small cylinder of tissue using a hollow needle for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Core Needle Biopsy is another type of biopsy used to analyze lymph nodes for possible cancer spread in regional Squamous Cell Skin Cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure used to obtain tissue samples from a tumor for diagnostic evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50c5e5d6c7a62da56b73abb9a3384301&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monitoring and Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d0">Monitoring and Follow-Up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring and follow-up care encompass regular skin examinations and assessments after treatment to detect any recurrence of skin cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Monitoring and follow-up care involve regular skin exams after treatment to detect any recurrence of squamous cell skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stereotactic Body Radiotherapy is a specialized form of radiation treatment that aims high doses of radiation at specific areas to treat small tumors more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic Cancer occurs when cancer cells spread from their original site to other parts of the body, complicating treatment options and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer indicates that cancer has spread to distant parts of the body, presenting more challenges for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is cancer that has spread from the original site to other parts of the body, requiring more extensive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer is defined as cancer that has spread from the primary site to other parts of the body, such as the lungs or lymph nodes, and is often referred to as advanced disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer occurs when cancer cells spread from the original site to other parts of the body, complicating treatment and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the initial site to other parts of the body, such as the liver or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic cancer refers to cancer that has spread to other parts of the body beyond the original site, often resulting in stage 4 cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extracapsular Extension">
      <data key="d0">Extracapsular Extension</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extracapsular Extension refers to the spread of cancer outside of the lymph node capsule, indicating a more aggressive disease that may affect treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64895edc4d35c98b293c555e32022a37</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer caused by the BCR::ABL1 gene and characterized by an increase in white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, resulting from the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene and characterized by excessive myeloid progenitor cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of certain white blood cells due to a genetic mutation involving the Philadelphia chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a specific genetic abnormality found in CML patients, identified as a result of the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome that is created by the translocation of genetic material between chromosome 9 and chromosome 22, leading to the BCR::ABL1 gene fusion associated with CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Cells are the components of blood, including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, all of which play vital roles in the body's function and health.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood cells consist of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets which play crucial roles in oxygen transport, immune response, and blood clotting, respectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Skin Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin cancer, including types like squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC), involves the unchecked growth of skin cells, which require regular check-ups and monitoring after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Experts">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Experts are the professionals that make recommendations regarding cancer care guidelines, including monitoring schedules for skin cancer and chronic diseases like CML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Exams">
      <data key="d0">Skin Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Skin exams are regular checks performed by a doctor to detect any new skin cancer developments and are vital for patients who have previously been diagnosed with skin cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Local CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Local cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) is a type of skin cancer requiring specific monitoring and treatment protocols depending on the risk level of the patient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regional CSCC">
      <data key="d0">Regional CSCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regional cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma (CSCC) refers to skin cancer that may have spread to nearby lymph nodes, requiring more frequent medical examinations and possibly imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scans">
      <data key="d0">CT Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CT scans are imaging tests that may be used to monitor patients with regional CSCC for signs of cancer spread to other areas of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans are imaging tests that take detailed pictures of the inside of the body, often used to determine if cancer has spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Blood Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood stem cells are immature cells found in bone marrow that can develop into any type of blood cell, playing a crucial role in the blood cell production process.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood stem cells are immature cells in bone marrow that can develop into various blood cells including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune System">
      <data key="d0">Immune System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The immune system is the body’s defense mechanism against infections and diseases, relying on white blood cells to protect the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Granulocytes">
      <data key="d0">Granulocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Granulocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the body's response to infection; they include neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils.&lt;SEP&gt;Granulocytes are a type of white blood cell that includes neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils, crucial for the body's immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Granulocytes are a type of white blood cell that plays a key role in the immune response; they are often elevated in cases of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) Guidelines for Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide trusted and comprehensive recommendations for patients to help make informed decisions about cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b66e38931f28ed6ac9b231faaf202fb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML">
      <data key="d0">CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CML (Chronic Myeloid Leukemia) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of myeloid progenitor cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BCR::ABL1">
      <data key="d0">BCR::ABL1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BCR::ABL1 is a fused gene responsible for the development of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML), created by a translocation between chromosome 9 and chromosome 22.&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1 is an abnormal fused gene that results from the translocation of chromosome 9 and chromosome 22, leading to the development of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;BCR::ABL1 is an abnormal gene associated with CML, resulting from the translocation of genetic material between chromosomes 9 and 22, causing uncontrolled blood cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC (Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer) is the most common type of lung cancer, characterized by uncontrolled growth of cells in the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer (NSCLC) is a type of lung cancer that includes various subtypes, characterized by how cancerous cells form and grow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Lung Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung carcinomas are a category of cancers that arise from the cells lining the airways in the lungs, including types such as NSCLC and small cell lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Myeloid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloid progenitor cells are a type of blood cell that are precursors to myeloid cells and are increased in patients with chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloid progenitor cells are early cells in blood cell development that can differentiate into various types of blood cells, including myeloblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">Myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature myeloid cells that can be found in elevated numbers in chronic myeloid leukemia (CML) and are referred to as blasts in this context.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that accumulate in the bone marrow and blood in cases of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that are typically seen in higher quantities in certain types of leukemia, including CML.&lt;SEP&gt;Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that can develop into granulocytes and are significantly involved in the pathology of AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast phase CML (BP-CML) is an advanced stage of Chronic Myeloid Leukemia characterized by significant increases in immature cells, impacting treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML Treatment">
      <data key="d0">CML Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CML treatment aims to inhibit the BCR::ABL1 protein to control blood cell proliferation, often requiring specialized medical care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for CML">
      <data key="d0">Testing for CML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for CML involves a variety of procedures, including blood and bone marrow tests, to diagnose the illness and inform treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">CML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A CML specialist is a medical professional with expertise in diagnosing and treating Chronic Myeloid Leukemia, crucial for optimal patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCP (Primary Care Physician)">
      <data key="d0">PCP (Primary Care Physician)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Primary Care Physician (PCP) is a healthcare provider responsible for coordinating patient care and managing general health concerns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Tests">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow Tests involve analyzing bone marrow samples to diagnose conditions such as leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are diagnostic procedures used to identify blood disorders, including leukemia, by analyzing samples taken from the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are medical procedures used to diagnose and assess treatment for ALL by examining the presence of lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are performed to examine the marrow for signs of blood cancers such as CML, providing crucial information for diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are procedures used to diagnose and assess cancers like AML by analyzing samples from the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are procedures used to diagnose conditions like leukemia by examining bone marrow samples.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow tests are used to diagnose conditions affecting blood cells by extracting a sample from the bone marrow, though they are not commonly required for CLL diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef6586b896c47b361bf4f6aabb4c35d5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airways of the Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Airways of the Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Airways of the Lungs refers to the network of structures involved in the respiration process, facilitating the movement of air in and out of the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;The airways of the lungs are crucial for respiratory function, allowing air to travel to and from the lungs; they can be affected by lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lungs">
      <data key="d0">Lungs</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lungs are vital organs in the respiratory system responsible for gas exchange, taking in oxygen and expelling carbon dioxide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bronchi">
      <data key="d0">bronchi</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchi are the primary air passages that branch from the trachea into the lungs, serving as conduits for air.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bronchioli">
      <data key="d0">bronchioli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchioli are smaller air passages that branch from the bronchi and lead to the alveoli where gas exchange occurs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="alveoli">
      <data key="d0">alveoli</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alveoli are tiny air sacs in the lungs where oxygen is transferred to the blood and carbon dioxide is removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a form of cervical cancer that arises from glandular cells in the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC that often forms from cells lining the alveoli and producing mucus, representing the most common type of lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that can develop from adenomas in the rectum, leading to rectal cancer if not detected early.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that forms in mucus-secreting glands and is often associated with rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in adenomas of the rectum and colon; it is the most common form of rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is a type of cancer that originates in mucus-secreting cells, often found in the lower esophagus.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most common type of colon cancer, originating from adenomas, which are non-cancerous growths in the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma is the most prevalent form of colon cancer, developing from adenomas in the colon lining.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinoma refers to cancer that starts in an adenoma, often leading to serious health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Large Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large Cell Carcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC characterized by large, atypical cells that can grow rapidly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous Cell Carcinoma (SCC) is the most frequent type of throat cancer that originates in the thin, flat cells of the mucus membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a subtype of NSCLC that forms from flat cells lining the bronchi.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma (SCC) is the most prevalent type of mouth cancer, arising from the flat cells in the mouth's mucous membranes.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a common type of cervical cancer originating from the squamous cells lining the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of cancer arising from thin, flat cells in the esophagus, primarily affecting the upper and middle parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of cancer that arises from squamous cells, which can occur in various tissues including the nasopharynx and cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is a type of cancer that begins in squamous cells, commonly associated with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of anal cancer, originating in squamous cells, which are located in the skin and other body areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of nasopharyngeal cancer, forming from the surface lining cells of the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients®">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients®</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines are patient-oriented guidelines providing information on the diagnosis and treatment of cancers including rectal cancer and melanoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® are expert recommendations for the treatment of various cancers, including NSCLC, aimed at educating patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines for Patients® is a trusted source for cancer care recommendations, created by experts to guide patients and providers.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) publishes guidelines and resources for patients, especially those concerning cancer treatment and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d0">Patient Guides for Cancer App</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Patient Guides for Cancer App provides accessible information regarding cancer treatment and management for patients diagnosed with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment Options include a variety of therapies such as surgery, radiation, and chemoradiation, tailored to the patient's needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Airways">
      <data key="d0">Airways</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Airways refer to the passageways in the respiratory system through which air flows in and out of the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat">
      <data key="d0">Throat</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Throat is the passage that connects the mouth and nasal cavity to the esophagus and windpipe, playing a crucial role in respiration and digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The throat, which is part of the neck, serves as a passageway for air and food and includes important structures that can be affected by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Windpipe">
      <data key="d0">Windpipe</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Windpipe, or trachea, is the main airway that connects the throat to the bronchi in the lungs, allowing air to pass through during breathing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org/patientguidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org/patientguidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org provides valuable guidelines and resources for patients dealing with cancer, including treatment options and supportive care information.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org/patientguidelines is a resource provided by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network that offers patient guidelines related to cancer, including treatment and management information.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org/patientguidelines is an online resource providing guidelines and information for patients regarding cancer treatments and care.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org/patientguidelines offers a wealth of resources and guidelines for patients dealing with cancer, including insights specific to pancreatic cancer and leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NSCLC Types">
      <data key="d0">NSCLC Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NSCLC Types refer to the various subtypes of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer, each characterized by different cell types and behaviors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stages">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stages</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Stages classify the extent of cancer spread within the body, specifically how far lung cancer has progressed at diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer stages classify the extent to which cancer has developed in the body and are crucial for determining appropriate treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 NSCLC refers to the early stage of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer where the cancer is localized and has not spread beyond the lung tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 NSCLC denotes a locally advanced stage of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer, where it has begun to spread to nearby lymph nodes or structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 NSCLC indicates a more advanced state of Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer with significant local spread to lymph nodes and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 NSCLC is characterized by metastatic lung cancer, indicating that cancer has spread to distant areas of the body at diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery for NSCLC">
      <data key="d0">Surgery for NSCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery for NSCLC is a treatment option involving the removal of lung cancerous tissues and possibly some surrounding healthy tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoradiation">
      <data key="d0">Chemoradiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy and radiation therapy to enhance treatment efficacy for lung cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy for enhanced effectiveness in treating bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy for treating advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation involves administering EBRT and chemotherapy simultaneously and is recommended for treating locally advanced cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment combining chemotherapy and radiation therapy to manage locally advanced cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment modality that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy, commonly used for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment strategy that involves administering external beam radiation therapy (EBRT) and chemotherapy concurrently for effective management of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation is a treatment that combines chemotherapy with radiation therapy to enhance the effects of treatment on cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation refers to the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy as a standard treatment for non-metastatic anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemoradiation refers to the combined use of chemotherapy and radiation therapy, typically used to treat locally advanced cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supportive Care">
      <data key="d0">Supportive Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive Care includes therapies and interventions aimed at improving the quality of life for lung cancer patients, addressing symptoms and side effects of treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care aims to alleviate symptoms caused by cancer or its treatment, ultimately improving patients' quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care aims to improve the quality of life for cancer patients by alleviating symptoms and providing psychological support throughout treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses a variety of services designed to improve the quality of life for patients with serious illnesses, including pain management and emotional support.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses the practices that help manage symptoms and improve the quality of life for patients undergoing treatment for primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care encompasses various therapies aimed at alleviating symptoms and improving the quality of life for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care focuses on relieving symptoms of cancer or side effects from treatments to improve the patient's quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care in the context of cancer involves the management of symptoms and challenges faced by patients to improve their quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care is a crucial component of cancer treatment focused on alleviating symptoms and improving the quality of life for patients undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care is aimed at relieving symptoms and improving quality of life for cancer patients at any stage of their disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Supportive care refers to the supportive measures taken during cancer treatment to alleviate symptoms, manage side effects, and improve quality of life for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd06bc83e13e7562ce65d6eee1b6024f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN.org">
      <data key="d0">NCCN.org</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN.org is a website provided by the National Comprehensive Cancer Network that offers patient guidelines and resources related to cancer treatment and care.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing information and guidelines for cancer patients, particularly in relation to treatment options and fertility preservation.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website providing patient guidelines for cancer, especially Hodgkin lymphoma information for both adults and children.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is a website that provides patient guidelines for cancer and information on lung cancer screening.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization focused on providing information and resources for cancer patients regarding treatment options and guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org is an organization that provides resources and guidelines for cancer patients, particularly in terms of distress management and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN.org provides information and guidelines for cancer patients, focusing on treatment and management strategies for various cancers including Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a resource that provides cancer patients with access to treatment guidelines and patient education.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers resources related to cancer care and screening options for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app serves as a mobile tool for patients to access cancer information and guidance regarding treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pulmonologist">
      <data key="d0">Pulmonologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pulmonologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating lung diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;A pulmonologist specializes in diagnosing and treating respiratory system diseases, including lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic radiologist is a doctor who specializes in imaging and interpreting images of the chest.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic radiologist is a specialist who uses imaging techniques to diagnose diseases of the chest, including lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thoracic Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Thoracic Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A thoracic surgeon is a physician who specializes in surgical procedures of the chest, including the lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;A thoracic surgeon specializes in surgical procedures on the chest, including operations for lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Nodule">
      <data key="d0">Lung Nodule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A lung nodule is a small mass of tissue in the lung, often discovered through imaging, and most are benign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer screening is a procedure aimed at detecting lung cancer in individuals at high risk before symptoms appear.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco smoke is identified as the biggest risk factor for developing lung cancer, containing over 50 carcinogenic compounds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the cells of the lungs, often presenting as carcinoma. It is life-threatening and can develop in people with or without risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Lung cancer often refers to malignancies originating in lung tissues, typically categorized into non-small cell and small cell lung cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinomas are a type of cancer that begins in the cells that make up the skin or the tissues lining organs. Most lung cancers are classified as carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC) is the most common type of lung cancer, accounting for approximately 85% of cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Lung Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Lung Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell lung cancer (SCLC) is a less common but more aggressive type of lung cancer that is characterized by small, oval-shaped cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radon">
      <data key="d0">Radon</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radon is a naturally occurring radioactive gas that is a known risk factor for lung cancer, particularly in homes with poor ventilation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asbestos">
      <data key="d0">Asbestos</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Asbestos is a group of minerals known to increase the risk of lung cancer and other respiratory diseases when its fibers are inhaled.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Air Pollution">
      <data key="d0">Air Pollution</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Air pollution from fine particulates and gases, such as nitrogen oxides and sulfur dioxide, has been linked to an increased risk of lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7843955573cdc294b34e74019195fafb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lung cancer">
      <data key="d0">lung cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the lungs and is classified into various types, including small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="large cell carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">large cell carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Large cell carcinoma is a form of non-small cell lung cancer that grows in large, undifferentiated cells and can develop anywhere in the lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="small cell lung cancer (SCLC)">
      <data key="d0">small cell lung cancer (SCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small cell lung cancer is a fast-growing type of lung cancer that arises from neuroendocrine cells and is known for its aggressive nature.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="early detection">
      <data key="d0">early detection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Early detection of lung cancer refers to screening processes aimed at identifying lung cancer at an early stage, improving treatment success.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="health care provider">
      <data key="d0">health care provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A health care provider is a professional responsible for the assessment, diagnosis, and treatment of patients, including cancer screening and risk assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="risk factors">
      <data key="d0">risk factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors are elements that increase the probability of developing lung cancer, including genetic predispositions and environmental exposures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)">
      <data key="d0">non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-small cell lung cancer is the most common type of lung cancer that encompasses various subtypes including adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma, and large cell carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="adenocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">adenocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinoma is a subtype of non-small cell lung cancer that begins in the mucus-secreting glands of the lungs and is often associated with non-smokers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="adenosquamous carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">adenosquamous carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a less common subtype of non-small cell lung cancer that contains both glandular and squamous cell features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sarcomatoid carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">sarcomatoid carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid carcinoma is a rare and aggressive subtype of non-small cell lung cancer characterized by a more complex cell structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="risk assessment">
      <data key="d0">risk assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk assessment involves evaluating an individual's likelihood of developing lung cancer based on their personal and family medical history, lifestyle, and exposure to risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="symptoms of SCLC">
      <data key="d0">symptoms of SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of small cell lung cancer may include persistent cough, chest pain, shortness of breath, and unexplained weight loss, which can affect early diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="treatment options for SCLC">
      <data key="d0">treatment options for SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment options for small cell lung cancer can include chemotherapy, radiation therapy, and immunotherapy, aimed at managing the disease effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-08475506372bb4ce9ec1bd326caf0fe9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SCLC">
      <data key="d0">SCLC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small Cell Lung Cancer (SCLC) is a type of lung cancer primarily associated with smoking and characterized by rapid growth and poor early detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interventional Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Interventional Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An interventional radiologist is a physician who uses imaging techniques to guide minimally invasive surgical procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;An interventional radiologist performs minimally invasive procedures guided by imaging to treat conditions related to lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophageal cancer arises from the abnormal growth of cells in the esophagus, part of the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer arises in the esophagus and can occur near the junction with the stomach, related to stomach cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Esophageal cancer refers to various types of cancer that originate in the esophagus, primarily adenocarcinomas and squamous cell carcinomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digestive System">
      <data key="d0">Digestive System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The digestive system comprises organs including the liver, stomach, and intestines, which play a vital role in digestion and metabolism, and are affected by liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system encompasses organs that aid in the ingestion, digestion, and absorption of food, including the esophagus, stomach, and intestines.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is a complex network of organs that work together to process food, absorb nutrients, and remove waste from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The digestive system is responsible for the intake, breakdown, absorption of nutrients from food, and waste elimination, consisting of various organs including the stomach and intestines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer App</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app is a digital resource that provides patients with guidelines for understanding and managing various types of cancer, including lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers convenient access to patient guidelines related to cancer care and treatment management through a mobile platform.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer app offers patients accessible guidelines and support for cancer care management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking">
      <data key="d0">Smoking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking is a behavior that can adversely affect health and treatment outcomes, particularly in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a health risk factor that is correlated with various diseases, including an increased risk of bladder cancer and other cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a lifestyle factor recognized as a significant risk contributor to the development of pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is a primary risk factor for developing small cell lung cancer (SCLC) and is associated with significant negative health impacts, including lung diseases and cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is highlighted as a risk factor for cancer, and quitting is strongly advised for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is identified as a lifestyle factor that may negatively affect the outcomes of cervical cancer treatment, making cessation beneficial for patient recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is identified as a risk factor for developing various cancers, including anal cancer, due to its harmful effects on the immune system and overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is identified as the greatest risk factor for developing bladder cancer, due to the harmful substances that can cause cellular mutations leading to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is recognized as a risk factor linked to various forms of cancer, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Smoking is the act of inhaling tobacco, which has detrimental effects on health and significantly raises the risk of developing various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondhand Smoke">
      <data key="d0">Secondhand Smoke</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondhand smoke is the smoke inhaled from burning tobacco products and exhaled by smokers, contributing to increased lung cancer risk among non-smokers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer-Causing Chemicals">
      <data key="d0">Cancer-Causing Chemicals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer-causing chemicals, also known as carcinogens, are substances like radon and asbestos that increase the risk of developing cancers, including lung cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Exposure to cancer-causing chemicals, such as pesticides, is a risk factor linked to the development of pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ages Over 70">
      <data key="d0">Ages Over 70</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Being over 70 years old is identified as a risk factor for developing small cell lung cancer (SCLC), with older individuals exhibiting higher susceptibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Cancer Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Lung Cancer Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung cancer symptoms may vary based on the tumor's location and can include breathing difficulties, nausea, and symptoms associated with paraneoplastic syndromes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Lung Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung tumors are abnormal growths in the lung tissue and are characteristic of many types of lung cancer, including SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer treatment team consists of healthcare professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive care and support to cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer treatment team consists of various healthcare professionals collaborating to provide comprehensive care for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer treatment team typically consists of various specialists involved in diagnosing and treating cancer, including SCLC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ecc6c4bcd3cab65fa05ab5f3c55e528d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrett Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Barrett Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrett esophagus is a condition where the normal squamous cells of the lower esophagus are replaced by abnormal cells due to chronic reflux, increasing the risk of adenocarcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagogastric Junction">
      <data key="d0">Esophagogastric Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagogastric junction is the area where the esophagus meets the stomach, critical in esophageal cancer discussions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="North America">
      <data key="d0">North America</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">North America is one of the regions where esophageal adenocarcinoma is particularly prevalent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Europe">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Eastern Europe is a region where squamous cell carcinoma is more commonly found compared to Western regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment of pancreatic cancer can involve surgery, drugs, and clinical trials based on the individual's condition and cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment encompasses the medical interventions used to cure or manage diseases, tailored to individual patient needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment encompasses various medical interventions aimed at managing or eradicating cancer, influenced by staging and individual patient factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment for esophageal cancer involves various approaches depending on the type and stage of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f43f96815f23658d5b24fe95ed4bd5ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagus">
      <data key="d0">Esophagus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophagus is a muscular tube that connects the throat (pharynx) with the stomach and plays a crucial role in transporting food.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is a muscular tube that connects the throat (pharynx) with the stomach, playing a crucial role in the digestive system.&lt;SEP&gt;The esophagus is the muscular tube that carries food and liquid from the mouth to the stomach, and its health is crucial in the context of throat and head cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophageal Wall Layers">
      <data key="d0">Esophageal Wall Layers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The esophageal wall consists of four main layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and tunica adventitia, each serving distinct functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer staging describes the process of determining the extent of cancer in a patient, which aids in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging determines how deeply cancer has invaded the bladder and how far it has spread, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is a system used to determine the extent of cancer, which helps in planning treatment and predicting outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of classifying the extent of cancerous tumors in the body, which is crucial for determining appropriate treatments and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer in a person's body, which is crucial for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer in the body, based on size, location, and whether it has spread to lymph nodes or other organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging is the process of determining the extent of the cancer in the body, which influences treatment decisions and prognoses.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer staging refers to the classification of cancer based on the extent of tumor spread, which aids in determining prognosis and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance status indicates a patient's functional ability, which is significant when deciding on treatment options for liver cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's functioning and ability to carry out daily activities, important for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's general well-being and ability to carry out daily activities, important in determining treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a person's general fitness and ability to perform daily self-care tasks, important for treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure used to evaluate an individual's general well-being and capability to undergo further medical treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is an assessment of a patient's ability to perform ordinary day-to-day activities, which helps the medical team gauge treatment options and eligibility for trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status refers to a measure used to evaluate a patient's general health and ability to perform everyday activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic risk testing evaluates hereditary cancer risks, particularly for individuals with a family history of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic risk testing involves analyzing an individual's DNA to identify predispositions to certain types of cancer, including esophageal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Reflux">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Reflux</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic reflux refers to the prolonged backflow of stomach contents into the esophagus, which can contribute to conditions like Barrett esophagus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb36cedd3f66584d65304e58b668eeab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Test Results">
      <data key="d0">Test Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Test results are outcomes of various diagnostic procedures that inform decisions about cancer staging and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results include information from blood tests, imaging studies, and biopsies used to diagnose and determine the treatment for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results include various diagnostic evaluations that inform treatment decisions for breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results include various medical evaluations such as blood tests, imaging studies, and biopsies used to determine treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Test results refer to the outcomes of medical tests that inform treatment decisions and help in staging cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates from lymphocytes in the lymphatic system, characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin Lymphoma is a type of cancer that typically starts in lymph nodes and is characterized by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hodgkin lymphoma (HL) is a type of lymphoma distinguished by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells, treated differently than non-Hodgkin lymphomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Foundation">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Foundation supports patients affected by cancer by funding and distributing NCCN Guidelines for Patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MyChart">
      <data key="d0">MyChart</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">MyChart is a health record system that helps patients track their appointments and communicate with their healthcare team.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an electronic health record application that allows patients to manage their health care information, appointments, and test results online.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that allows individuals to access medical records, test results, and communicate with their healthcare providers seamlessly.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that allows users to track appointments and communicate with their care teams.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online patient portal that facilitates access to test results, communication with healthcare teams, and management of medical records.&lt;SEP&gt;MyChart is an online portal that allows patients to access medical records, test results, and communicate with their healthcare team.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit alliance of cancer centers that provides guidelines and support for cancer care and treatment recommendations.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) is a nonprofit alliance of cancer centers that provides guidelines and support for cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and information for cancer treatment and support.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines and information related to cancer care, including resources on AML.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network is an alliance of top cancer centers in the United States focused on improving cancer care and treatment standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Studies">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Studies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging Studies include various techniques like X-rays, CT scans, or MRIs used to visualize internal body structures for cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies are tests that create visual representations of the inside of the body, which aid in diagnosing cancer and planning treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies refer to diagnostic tests that create visual representations of the interior of a body for clinical analysis.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies refer to various techniques such as CT scans or MRIs used to visualize internal structures for medical evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies such as CT scans are utilized to visualize the stomach and determine the extent of any cancer present.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support System">
      <data key="d0">Support System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A support system comprises friends and family who assist patients emotionally and logistically during diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insurance Forms">
      <data key="d0">Insurance Forms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Insurance forms are documents required by insurance companies to claim medical expenses and reimbursements.&lt;SEP&gt;Insurance forms are documents required for health insurance claims and managing patient coverage related to medical procedures and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reed-Sternberg Cells">
      <data key="d0">Reed-Sternberg Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Reed-Sternberg cells are a specific type of oversized lymphocyte characteristic of Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Reed-Sternberg cells are large, abnormal lymphocytes that are a hallmark of Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults">
      <data key="d0">Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hodgkin Lymphoma in Adults focuses on the particular challenges and treatment options for adult patients with this type of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Patient Portals">
      <data key="d0">Online Patient Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online patient portals are digital platforms that enable patients to access their medical information and communicate with healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Online patient portals provide patients access to their medical information, facilitating communication with healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Online patient portals, such as MyChart, provide patients with digital access to their health records and communication with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines are evidence-based recommendations designed to improve the quality of cancer care provided to patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines for cancer treatment and management, including anal cancer specifics.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) provides guidelines for cancer treatments and patient education resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Providers">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthcare providers are professionals responsible for diagnosing, treating, and managing patient health.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthcare providers encompass a wide range of professionals involved in the diagnosis, treatment, and ongoing management of bladder cancer, forming a multidisciplinary team to support patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer care encompasses a variety of medical services and treatments provided to patients diagnosed with cancer, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care encompasses the medical and emotional support needed for patients diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care encompasses the medical, psychological, and supportive services provided to patients diagnosed with cancer, focusing on improving their health outcomes and quality of life.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer care refers to the comprehensive approach to diagnosing, treating, and managing cancer, including support for patients and their families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Contributors">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Contributors</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Contributors are professionals involved in the development and updating of the NCCN guidelines for cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64f36a4a5be80c8fa51631db16e52b85</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic System">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic System</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymphatic system is a crucial part of the immune system, including lymph nodes, lymph vessels, and organs that help the body fight infections.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network crucial for the immune system, transporting lymph and housing lymphocytes that fight infections and diseases, including Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of tissues and organs that help rid the body of toxins and waste, playing a crucial role in the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of vessels and nodes that transports lymphatic fluid, crucial in immune response and can be involved in the spread of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of vessels and organs that play a crucial role in the immune system, aiding in the transportation of lymph and lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is a network of vessels that can transport cancer cells from the liver to other areas of the body, playing a role in metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is an essential part of the immune system, comprising a network of vessels and organs that help protect the body from infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;The lymphatic system is crucial in fighting infections and consists of lymph nodes, vessels, tonsils, thymus, and spleen, creating a framework for the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphocytes are a subtype of white blood cells that are essential for adaptive immunity, including B cells and T cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a subtype of white blood cells that play a critical role in the immune response, aiding in the attack against infections and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell crucial for the immune response and comprise B cells and T cells, which are involved in treating ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell crucial for the immune response, playing significant roles in identifying and combating infections and cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell essential for the immune response and include B cells and T cells, which are specifically affected in cases of ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell essential for the immune system, which can become dysfunctional in various diseases, including Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell essential to the immune response, playing a key role in identifying and combating cancers such as Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, which are significantly impacted in the context of acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell vital for the immune response, particularly against infections and diseases like Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are disease-fighting white blood cells crucial to the immune system that are prevalent in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL).&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are disease-fighting white blood cells of the immune system, playing a critical role in fighting infections and in the pathology of cancers like ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphocytes are white blood cells integral to the immune response, crucial for fighting infection and preventing disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphatic Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymphatic Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphatic vessels are ducts responsible for transporting lymph throughout the body, which is crucial for the immune system's function.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphatic vessels are ducts that transport lymph throughout the body, similar to how blood vessels carry blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spleen">
      <data key="d0">Spleen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The spleen is the largest organ in the lymphatic system that produces lymphocytes and filters blood, thus playing a critical role in managing infections and cancers like Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The spleen is the largest organ of the lymphatic system, vital for producing lymphocytes and filtering blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone Marrow is the soft, spongy tissue found inside bones, crucial for the production of blood cells, including lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone Marrow is the spongy tissue located inside bones responsible for producing blood cells and maintaining the supply of different blood components throughout life.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is a spongy tissue found in the center of bones, responsible for producing blood cells including red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is a vital tissue in the body responsible for producing blood cells; its function can be compromised during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft tissue inside bones where new blood cells, including lymphocytes, are produced.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the soft tissue within bones that produces blood cells, including the lymphocytes involved in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the sponge-like tissue found in the center of most bones, where blood cell formation occurs.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue found in the center of bones where blood stem cells produce all types of blood cells, playing a key role in the formation of cells affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced, and is essential in diagnosing blood cancers such as CLL through biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced, including lymphocytes involved in ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells are produced, playing a crucial role in diagnosing and treating leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue inside bones where blood cells, including lymphocytes, are produced.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue where blood cells, including lymphocytes, are produced, contributing to the body’s immune defense against cancers such as Hodgkin lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the spongy tissue within bones where blood stem cells produce blood cells, including those affected by ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow is the tissue in the center of most bones where blood stem cells are produced and blood cell formation occurs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thymus">
      <data key="d0">Thymus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The thymus is a small organ located in front of the windpipe and plays a role in the development of T-cell lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ where lymphocytes mature into T cells, one of the two primary types of lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;The thymus is a small organ where lymphocytes mature into T-cells, essential for adaptive immunity that can affect the progression of Hodgkin lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tonsils">
      <data key="d0">Tonsils</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The tonsils are lymphoid tissues located in the throat that play a role in immune function and can be sites of cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Tonsils are collections of lymphatic tissue that trap pathogens entering through the mouth and throat, playing a role in immune response.&lt;SEP&gt;Tonsils are masses of lymphatic tissue that help trap germs entering through the mouth or nose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma (CHL)">
      <data key="d0">Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma (CHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma (CHL) is the most common type of Hodgkin lymphoma, marked by Reed-Sternberg cells and requiring similar care across subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Classic Hodgkin Lymphoma, the most common form, is marked by the presence of Reed-Sternberg cells and requires specific treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL)">
      <data key="d0">Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma (NLPHL) is a rare form of Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by popcorn-shaped cells that grow slowly but may become aggressive.&lt;SEP&gt;Nodular Lymphocyte-Predominant Hodgkin Lymphoma is a rarer form characterized by popcorn-shaped cells, and it has a different treatment approach than CHL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)">
      <data key="d0">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL) is a fast-growing type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that can arise from transformations of NLPHL.&lt;SEP&gt;Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma is an aggressive form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that can develop from transformations of NLPHL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d67bee20d5e5400994f8c8f03052dd06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Duct Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bile Duct Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile duct cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the bile ducts, which are responsible for transporting bile from the liver to the small intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a common type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by the slow growth of B lymphocytes in the lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus Classification">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus Classification</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Consensus Classification (ICC) is an organization that groups and classifies cancers like follicular lymphoma based on various criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World Health Organization">
      <data key="d0">World Health Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The World Health Organization (WHO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations responsible for international public health and recognizes cancer classifications.&lt;SEP&gt;The World Health Organization (WHO) provides guidelines for the classification and treatment of various health conditions, including follicular lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Serous Membrane">
      <data key="d0">Serous Membrane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The serous membrane, also known as the serosa or visceral peritoneum, serves as a protective layer covering internal organs and lining body cavities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Duct">
      <data key="d0">Bile Duct</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The bile duct is a passage that conveys bile from the liver to the small intestine, composed of multiple layers including membranes and connective tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a form of cancer that develops from lymphocytes, affecting the lymphatic system and immune function.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a group of blood cancers that includes different types of lymphomas, such as follicular lymphoma, characterized by abnormal lymphocyte growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Hodgkin lymphoma (NHL) is a type of blood cancer that affects lymphocytes and is characterized by diverse forms and reactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germinal Centers">
      <data key="d0">Germinal Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germinal centers are structures within lymph nodes where B cells proliferate and differentiate, often in response to infection or inflammation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centrocytes">
      <data key="d0">Centrocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Centrocytes are a type of B cell found in the light zones of germinal centers, characterized by their smaller size and lower rate of division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centroblasts">
      <data key="d0">Centroblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Centroblasts are larger B cells in the dark zones of germinal centers, known for their rapid division and role in the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Extranodal Disease">
      <data key="d0">Extranodal Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extranodal disease is a condition where cancer occurs outside the lymph nodes, presenting diagnostic and therapeutic challenges.&lt;SEP&gt;Extranodal disease refers to lymphoma occurring outside of lymph nodes, such as in the bone marrow or other tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b00f93aa3d24d0275d4290db3fb2cca3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="follicular lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">follicular lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular lymphoma (FL) is a type of slow-growing non-Hodgkin lymphoma that typically forms in the lymph nodes, characterized by clusters of abnormal B lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Consensus classification">
      <data key="d0">International Consensus classification</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Consensus classification (ICC) is a system that categorizes grades of follicular lymphoma for treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="diffuse large B-cell lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">diffuse large B-cell lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL) is a more aggressive type of lymphoma that follicular lymphoma can potentially transform into.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-829d6b7840e9f2c8d97d22d2ce2c43c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymphatic system">
