    <node id="Health Care Providers">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care providers (HCPs) are professionals who offer medical services related to breast health, including doctors and specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are crucial for diagnosing and treating prostate cancer, offering screenings and medical advice.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are medical professionals involved in diagnosing and treating patients, including those with prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Health care providers are professionals who oversee the diagnosis, treatment, and management of patients dealing with cancer, including mouth cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Physician (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Physician (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A PCP is often the first healthcare provider that patients consult regarding symptoms related to anal cancer or general health concerns.&lt;SEP&gt;A PCP is typically the first medical professional people consult for symptoms, including those related to anal cancer, who often provides referrals for specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary Care Physician (PCP) is a healthcare partner who helps coordinate care and stays informed of changes in the patient's health.&lt;SEP&gt;The Primary Care Physician is a key member of the healthcare team, responsible for overseeing overall patient care and coordinating treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician (PCP) acts as a central figure in the patient's healthcare team, coordinating care and informing specialists of changes.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a general medical doctor responsible for coordinating the patient's overall care and updating medical information.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician is a healthcare provider who manages general health issues and maintains communication with the patient regarding their cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary care physician plays a key role in coordinating care and managing the health of patients at risk for or diagnosed with liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Groups">
      <data key="d0">Support Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Support groups are community resources that provide emotional and psychological support for cancer patients and their families.&lt;SEP&gt;Support groups offer emotional and social assistance to patients and families dealing with cancer, facilitating connections and shared experiences among members.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portals">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portals</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient portals are online platforms that provide patients with access to their health records and test results.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are online systems that allow patients to access their medical records, test results, and facilitate communication with their healthcare providers.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are secure online platforms that allow patients to access their medical information, including test results and treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General Health Tests">
      <data key="d0">General Health Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General health tests are assessments performed to understand a patient's overall health and monitor conditions related to various cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests are various medical examinations that help provide an overview of a patient’s health and can indicate the presence of liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests assess overall health and can provide critical information relevant to the diagnosis of throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests include various examinations and evaluations that help healthcare providers determine patient well-being and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;General health tests involve various baseline tests used to assess a patient's health status and guide further diagnostic decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d47a420aa2acde1175ad95bf6dc2e664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Exam">
      <data key="d0">Dental Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A dental exam is a procedure where a dentist checks the health of the teeth and gums, especially important for those with head and neck cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutrition assessment involves consulting with a dietitian to ensure optimal nutrition support before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutrition Assessment involves evaluating a patient's dietary needs to ensure adequate nutrition before, during, and after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility Preservation encompasses techniques aimed at safeguarding reproductive health during cancer treatment, ensuring options for conception in the future.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation refers to medical strategies to maintain reproductive options for patients undergoing treatments that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility Preservation refers to methods that allow individuals to maintain the possibility of having children after cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation encompasses measures taken to protect one's ability to reproduce before undergoing cancer treatments that could impact fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation encompasses strategies to maintain the ability to conceive before cancer treatments that may affect reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation includes methods like sperm banking and egg freezing to safeguard fertility before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation involves medical options available to preserve the ability to have children in the future, particularly before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation is the process of taking steps to save or protect fertility, particularly important for cancer patients before they undergo treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to medical techniques to help individuals preserve their ability to conceive, which may be a concern during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation refers to techniques that help individuals retain the ability to conceive in the future, especially before undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Scale">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG scale is a tool used to assess a patient's performance status, crucial in determining treatment plans for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Height Measurement">
      <data key="d0">Height Measurement</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Height measurement is a procedure to determine an individual's height, often performed during medical checkups to assess growth and health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Measurement">
      <data key="d0">Weight Measurement</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Weight measurement is a common assessment to monitor an individual's body mass, typically part of routine health evaluations and cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Exam">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A head and neck exam is a specialized physical evaluation to assess the health of these areas, particularly important for patients with cancer in these regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Distress">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional distress encompasses a range of negative feelings related to cancer diagnoses, affecting patients' mental health and well-being.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Tests">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine tests are medical evaluations to measure hormone levels in the body, crucial for monitoring side effects of cancer treatments that affect endocrine glands.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine tests measure hormone levels and are performed to check the function of glands that may be affected by cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Test">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A hearing test assesses an individual's hearing ability and is essential for identifying hearing loss, especially in cancer patients affected by tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;A hearing test assesses hearing loss and its severity, particularly important for patients with tumors in the head and neck.&lt;SEP&gt;A hearing test evaluates potential hearing loss in patients, which can be caused by tumors in the head and neck or treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Speech and Swallowing Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Speech and Swallowing Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A speech and swallowing assessment evaluates a patient's ability to communicate and swallow, often necessary for those undergoing head and neck cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Worker">
      <data key="d0">Social Worker</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A social worker is a professional who assists cancer patients and their families in dealing with the emotional and practical aspects of cancer care.&lt;SEP&gt;A social worker provides support services to help patients cope with the emotional and practical challenges of dealing with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bdde77ad1be3ee9308db3c7703674b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A doctor provides guidance and health advice related to cancer treatment and pregnancy planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A medical professional who provides guidance and treatment options to patients, especially in the context of cancer care and survivorship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Material">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Material</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Material is a substance used to enhance imaging results in scans like MRIs and CTs, improving the clarity of the images.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material enhances the quality of imaging in medical procedures by improving the visibility of internal organs and structures in medical images.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the clarity of pictures of the body's internal structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is a substance used in medical imaging to enhance the clarity of internal body images during tests such as CT scans and MRIs.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is used in imaging tests to enhance the visibility of internal structures, improving the quality of diagnostic images.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast material is used in medical imaging to enhance and improve the quality of images of internal organs and structures. It aids in making diagnostic images clearer, particularly during tests such as CT and MRI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG-PET/CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG-PET/CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An FDG-PET/CT combines a PET scan and a CT scan to provide detailed images and functional information about tissues, particularly useful in cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;An FDG-PET/CT combines a PET scan with a CT scan, utilizing a radiotracer to help identify cancerous growths and track their development.&lt;SEP&gt;An FDG-PET/CT scan combines a PET scan with a CT scan using a radiotracer to detect cancerous tissues, especially when other imaging results are not definitive.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a combined imaging procedure that utilizes fluorodeoxyglucose radiotracer to detect cancerous cells when other imaging results are inconclusive.&lt;SEP&gt;FDG-PET/CT is a type of imaging that helps identify sites of leukemia outside the blood and bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound uses high-energy sound waves to create internal body images and is commonly used for cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a common imaging technique used to evaluate thyroid nodules and detect potential cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique that employs high-frequency sound waves to produce images of the inside of the body, especially for detecting small tumors and abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to produce images of the inside of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a medical imaging technique that uses high-energy sound waves to visualize the inside of the body without using x-rays.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a medical imaging technique that utilizes sound waves to create images of the internal body structures and is useful for cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses sound waves to produce images of internal body structures, commonly used in medical diagnostics.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging test that uses sound waves to create pictures of the inside of the body, especially useful for examining small areas of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is an imaging test that uses sound waves to take pictures of the internal structures of the body, commonly used to investigate ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is commonly the first imaging test performed to examine suspected ovarian cancer and evaluates the size and shape of the ovaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is one of the first imaging tests conducted to investigate suspected ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-Ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-Ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures an image of the entire mouth, commonly used by dentists for treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray gives a comprehensive view of the mouth's structure, helping in dental treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Provider (HCP)">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Provider (HCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A health care provider is responsible for delivering medical care to patients, including interpreting imaging test results.&lt;SEP&gt;Health Care Provider (HCP) refers to medical professionals involved in the diagnosis and treatment of NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm">
      <data key="d0">Sperm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm is the male reproductive cell that can be affected by cancer treatments, impacting fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraception">
      <data key="d0">Contraception</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contraception methods, such as condoms, are recommended during and after cancer treatment to prevent unintended pregnancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Portal">
      <data key="d0">Patient Portal</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient portals are online platforms that allow patients to access their health information, including test results and medical records, facilitating better care management.&lt;SEP&gt;Patient portals are online platforms that allow patients to access their health records and communicate with healthcare providers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT">
      <data key="d0">CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CT (computed tomography) scans are imaging tests that use x-rays to produce detailed pictures of the inside of the body, often used in cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scans may be recommended if ultrasound shows suspicious lymph nodes associated with thyroid nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed tomography (CT) is an imaging method that allows doctors to see cross-sectional pictures of the body, particularly useful in cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed tomography (CT) is an imaging technique that provides detailed images of the body's internal structures and can be combined with PET for enhanced imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET">
      <data key="d0">PET</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A positron emission tomography (PET) scan is a medical imaging technique that allows for the observation of metabolic processes in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET (positron emission tomography) scans use radioactive tracers to visualize metabolic processes in the body, aiding in cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Positron Emission Tomography (PET) is an imaging test that uses radioactive materials to visualize metabolic processes in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panoramic Dental X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Panoramic Dental X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A panoramic dental x-ray captures an image of the entire mouth, including teeth, jaw, and surrounding structures, and is commonly used in dental practices.&lt;SEP&gt;A panoramic dental x-ray is a type of imaging that captures a single, comprehensive view of a patient’s entire mouth and jaw, useful for planning dental procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Panoramic dental x-rays are used to capture a complete view of the teeth and jaws, often employed in dental treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking and Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Smoking and Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking and vaping are habits that significantly increase the risk of developing various types of cancer and can hinder treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-705ff2bd04b2b58b98187f60f23a659e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicotine">
      <data key="d0">Nicotine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nicotine is a chemical compound found in tobacco that makes quitting smoking difficult and is linked to addiction.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that creates the addictive urge to smoke and makes quitting difficult, especially when faced with stress like a cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that encourages addictive behavior, complicating cessation efforts for smokers and vapers, particularly when facing health issues like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that is addictive and can have significant impacts on cancer treatment and recovery.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is a chemical found in tobacco that is addictive and can negatively impact cancer treatment outcomes, making smoking cessation vital.&lt;SEP&gt;Nicotine is the addictive chemical found in tobacco products, which can significantly impact health, particularly increasing the risk of various cancers and complicating treatment outcomes in cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancers of the Head and Neck">
      <data key="d0">Cancers of the Head and Neck</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers refer to a group of cancers that affect the mouth, throat, and surrounding structures, often linked to smoking and alcohol use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR Mutation">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H/dMMR mutation refers to a defect in the mismatch repair system that leads to microsatellite instability, which can be observed in certain types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI-H/dMMR mutation signifies a genetic condition where microsatellite instability prevents DNA errors from being corrected, often linked to certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is a programmed death-ligand that can also interfere with the immune system's response to cancer cells when expressed on their surface.&lt;SEP&gt;PD-L1 is an immune protein that cancer cells may exploit to evade the immune system, crucial in cancer treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Treatment refers to the various methods employed to combat cancer, which may include surgery, radiation therapy, chemotherapy, and hormone therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses a variety of methods including surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation aimed at eliminating or controlling cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various approaches to managing and treating cancer, including surgery, chemotherapy, and radiotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical interventions aimed at eradicating cancer, including chemotherapy, surgery, and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various medical procedures and therapies aimed at eliminating cancer and managing its symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment encompasses various therapies and interventions aimed at combating cancer, including chemotherapy, radiation, and supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment includes various methods like surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation, aimed at eliminating cancer or managing symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to the methods used to manage and combat cancer, including surgery, radiation, and chemotherapy, tailored to individual patient needs.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to the various medical interventions used to manage and alleviate cancer, tailored based on tumor location and type.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to various medical approaches to treating cancer, which can impact fertility and may require planning for pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer treatment refers to various medical practices and interventions aimed at combating cancer, including diagnosis, treatment types, and monitoring health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutation testing analyzes cancer cells for specific mutations to identify targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutation testing analyzes tumor DNA for specific mutations to inform targeted treatment strategies, involving methods like next-generation sequencing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENT Doctor">
      <data key="d0">ENT Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An Ear, Nose, and Throat (ENT) doctor specializes in diagnosing and treating conditions affecting the head and neck area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dentist">
      <data key="d0">Dentist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A dentist is a healthcare professional who specializes in oral health and is vital for managing the oral care of cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A dentist is a healthcare professional who specializes in oral health, responsible for performing dental checkups and cleanings, especially for patients with head and neck cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A dentist is a healthcare professional who specializes in the diagnosis, prevention, and treatment of oral health issues, including dental surgeries and treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Oral Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral surgeons are dental specialists who perform surgical procedures on the mouth, jaws, and related structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaping refers to the inhalation of vapor produced by electronic cigarettes, which still poses health risks similar to smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastasis describes the process by which cancer cells spread from the primary tumor to form new tumors in other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis describes the process through which cancer cells spread from the primary tumor site to distant organs or tissues in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer cells spread from the primary site to other parts of the body, leading to new tumor formations.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer spreads from its original location to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process by which cancer spreads from the original site to other parts of the body, such as lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process through which cancer cells detach from the original tumor and spread to form secondary tumors in distant organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the process through which cancer cells spread from the original tumor site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis is the spread of cancer cells from the original tumor site to other parts of the body, which may require targeted diagnostic approaches to confirm the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the process by which cancer cells spread from the primary tumor to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from the primary site to distant organs, often involving the liver, lung, and bone.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastasis refers to the spread of cancer to distant parts of the body requiring confirmation through biopsy of the affected areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Histology">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor histology refers to the cellular characteristics of a tumor determined through biopsy analysis, which aids in treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor histology refers to the examination of tumor tissues under a microscope to aid in the selection of appropriate cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor histology refers to the microscopic examination and classification of tumor cells to determine cancer type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing analyzes a sample from a biopsy to identify specific DNA mutations, protein levels, or other molecular features relevant to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes genes or proteins to identify mutations assisting in personalized cancer treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes specific mutations within cancer cells to guide targeted treatment options for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing analyzes tumor features to guide treatment options, identifying mutations that may affect cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing includes various tests to identify biological markers in the body that can indicate the presence or progression of diseases such as cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing biological markers to identify specific characteristics of cancer, aiding in treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing biological markers to personalize cancer treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing cancer characteristics to guide treatment decisions and determine eligibility for clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor characteristics to guide treatment decisions and identify eligible clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumor tissues or blood samples to identify specific mutations or genes in cancer cells to guide treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves analyzing tumors for specific genetic mutations and protein levels to inform treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves evaluating genes, proteins, or mutations in breast cancer cells that may guide treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing is a laboratory method used to identify specific substances in the body that may indicate the presence of cancer, aiding in diagnosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing looks for changes in genes, proteins, and other markers in glioma tumors, improving diagnosis and treatment options based on detected mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular testing identifies genetic mutations within tumors, influencing therapy options for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Support">
      <data key="d0">Online Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Online support refers to digital resources and communities available for individuals seeking assistance with quitting smoking or managing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smokefree.gov">
      <data key="d0">Smokefree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Smokefree.gov is a website aimed at providing resources and support for individuals looking to quit smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy of Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy of Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy of metastasis is a procedure to confirm the presence of cancer in metastatic tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC">
      <data key="d0">CDC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) is a national public health institute in the United States that provides information and support on health issues, including tobacco use and cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Plans">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment plans are structured strategies devised by healthcare teams to manage and treat cancer effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d609bd1a55d409470c55ad55e2d125d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AJCC">
      <data key="d0">AJCC</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">AJCC (American Joint Committee on Cancer) has established a staging system to determine cancer extent and assist in treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dental Cleaning">
      <data key="d0">Dental Cleaning</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Dental cleaning is a preventive procedure that helps maintain oral health and involves the removal of plaque and tartar from teeth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility">
      <data key="d0">Fertility</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility refers to the natural capability of individuals to conceive children and can be impacted by various medical factors, including cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor sample is collected during a biopsy for testing purposes to identify mutations and guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA Mutations">
      <data key="d0">DNA Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DNA mutations refer to changes in the genetic material of cells that may influence cancer behavior and treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alcohol Use">
      <data key="d0">Alcohol Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alcohol use is also a significant risk factor associated with head and neck cancer and may influence survival rates.&lt;SEP&gt;Alcohol use refers to the consumption of alcoholic beverages and its potential impact on health, especially during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Staging Process">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Staging Process</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The cancer staging process involves determining the extent and severity of cancer in a patient, which informs treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-28e4e845dfd1390f7f94cfb11bee1526</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that originates in the stem cells of bone marrow and involves the production of abnormal myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of blood cancer that originates in the stem cells of bone marrow and is characterized by the accumulation of immature white blood cells, known as myeloid blasts, leading to serious health issues if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the accumulation of immature white blood cells called myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid growth of abnormal white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 cancer refers to advanced cancer that has spread to other parts of the body, indicating a severe progression of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumors are abnormal masses of tissue that can be benign or malignant, with characteristics affecting their resectability and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumors are abnormal masses of tissue that can form in the stomach and other organs, often leading to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM Staging is a classification system used to describe the extent of cancer spread in the body, particularly for squamous cell carcinoma in this context.&lt;SEP&gt;TNM staging is a classification system used to describe the extent of cancer based on tumor size, node involvement, and the presence of metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tis">
      <data key="d0">Tis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tis refers to carcinoma in situ, a stage where cancerous cells are found only in the squamous cell layer without invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T1">
      <data key="d0">T1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T1 signifies a tumor size of 2 cm or less with a depth of invasion of 5 mm or less, representing an early stage of cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T2">
      <data key="d0">T2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T2 indicates a tumor size that is 2 cm or less with a depth of invasion greater than 5 mm, or between 2 cm and 4 cm with a thickness of less than that.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T3">
      <data key="d0">T3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T3 represents a tumor larger than 4 cm or between 2 cm and 4 cm with greater depth of invasion, indicating a more advanced stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T4">
      <data key="d0">T4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T4 indicates a tumor that is significantly larger, extending into nearby structures, marking a critical stage of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9882c04dd7676c6433074e5e393c72e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML">
      <data key="d0">AML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer characterized by the rapid proliferation of abnormal white blood cells in the bone marrow, leading to serious health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by an overproduction of malignant white blood cells that can disrupt normal blood function.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the presence of myeloid blasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, often requiring genetic testing for appropriate treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that begins in the blood stem cells and affects the development of blood cells in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood">
      <data key="d0">Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood is a bodily fluid that carries oxygen and nutrients, plays a crucial role in the immune system, and comprises plasma and various types of blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a connective tissue essential for transporting oxygen, nutrients, and immune responses, containing red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital connective tissue composed of plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets that plays a crucial role in oxygen transport, immune response, and nutrient distribution.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood is a vital tissue comprising plasma, red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets, playing essential roles in the body's functions and immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chapters">
      <data key="d0">Chapters</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapters refer to sections in the book that provide detailed information on various aspects of AML, including testing, types of treatment, and resources for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blasts">
      <data key="d0">Blasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blasts are immature white blood cells that are committed to becoming a type of blood cell, significant in diagnosing conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts refer to immature blood cells that can indicate the presence of leukemia when found in elevated numbers in blood tests.&lt;SEP&gt;Blasts refer to the very immature cells in the blood, which can indicate the severity and stage of leukemias such as CML and ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets">
      <data key="d0">Platelets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs or thrombocytes) are blood components that play a crucial role in blood clotting and controlling bleeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Platelets (PLTs) are components of blood that help in clotting, preventing bleeding, and indicating blood health in various tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red Blood Cells (RBCs or erythrocytes) are responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body and removing carbon dioxide.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White Blood Cells (WBCs or leukocytes) are a crucial part of the immune system, helping the body fight infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monocytes">
      <data key="d0">Monocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Monocytes are a type of white blood cell that can differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells which are vital for the immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem Cells, specifically hematopoietic stem cells, are early cells in the bone marrow that can develop into various blood cells through a process called differentiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cells are primitive cells in the bone marrow that have the potential to develop into various types of blood cells, including red and white blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APL">
      <data key="d0">APL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL) is a subtype of AML characterized by a specific genetic fusion of the PML and RARA genes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BPDCN">
      <data key="d0">BPDCN</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN) is an aggressive subtype of leukemia that can occur alongside or in relation to AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for AML">
      <data key="d0">Testing for AML</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for AML encompasses various diagnostic procedures including blood tests, bone marrow biopsies, and genetic testing essential for accurate diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bd7ea43a74059d6781866faec437f19a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Blood Count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">Complete Blood Count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a comprehensive blood test that measures red and white blood cells and platelets to assess overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a test used to evaluate the number and type of cells in the blood, especially relevant during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A Complete Blood Count is a blood test that measures the number of blood cells, aiding in the detection of health problems such as colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A complete blood count (CBC) measures the levels of different blood cells to assess overall health and detect diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a common blood test used to evaluate overall health and detect various disorders, including anemia and infections.&lt;SEP&gt;Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a key blood test that measures levels of red blood cells, white blood cells, and platelets to assess overall health.&lt;SEP&gt;The Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a common blood test that measures several components of blood, including red and white blood cells, to assess a patient's health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures various substances in the blood, giving insight into organ health and functioning.&lt;SEP&gt;A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) tests blood substances to provide insights about kidney and liver function, among other health aspects.&lt;SEP&gt;Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) is a blood test that evaluates how well organs like kidneys and liver are working and checks for electrolyte imbalances.&lt;SEP&gt;The CMP is a panel of blood tests that provides information about the body's metabolism, including kidney and liver function.&lt;SEP&gt;The Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) consists of a group of blood tests that provide important information about metabolism, including blood sugar and electrolyte levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bone Marrow Aspirate and Biopsy involves extracting bone marrow to diagnose AML and evaluate genetic markers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are medical procedures used to diagnose AML and assess the bone marrow's condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate and biopsy are procedures that involve extracting a sample of bone marrow to study it for the presence of leukemia cells or other abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Medical History">
      <data key="d0">AML Medical History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Medical History refers to the compilation of health issues and treatments pertaining to patients diagnosed with Acute Myeloid Leukemia, which informs their treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clotting Tests">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clotting Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood clotting tests are crucial assessments that evaluate how well blood clots in patients, especially relevant for those undergoing treatment for AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood clotting tests are medical evaluations that assess the blood's ability to form clots, important for preventing excessive bleeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) Typing is a test that determines compatibility for organ or stem cell transplants and assesses immune compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain CT">
      <data key="d0">Brain CT</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A Brain CT is a diagnostic imaging procedure used to detect potential complications such as central nervous system bleeds in patients with suspected AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain MRI">
      <data key="d0">Brain MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A Brain MRI is an imaging technique utilized for detailed visualization of the brain, especially if there are suspicions of leukemic meningitis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture (LP)">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture (LP)</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A Lumbar Puncture (LP) involves sampling cerebrospinal fluid to check for leukemic cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Creatinine is a waste product measured in blood tests, used to assess kidney functionality, especially in patients with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Creatinine is a waste product measured to assess kidney function based on its level in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)">
      <data key="d0">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is measured to evaluate kidney function, crucial for AML patients undergoing treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood Urea Nitrogen is a measure of kidney efficiency, with elevated levels indicating potential kidney dysfunction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrolytes">
      <data key="d0">Electrolytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrolytes are ions in the body necessary for various functions including nutrition transport and waste removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrolytes are ions that are vital for various bodily functions and are monitored in patients with AML for health assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Electrolytes are vital ions in the blood that help regulate various physiological processes, including muscle and nerve function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LDH is an enzyme released by damaged tissues, including tumor cells, and is measured to assess tissue health.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme measured in blood tests; elevated levels can indicate tumor activity or tissue damage.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that can indicate cell damage and is often released by dying cells, including tumor cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that can indicate cell damage and is particularly relevant in identifying potential cancer activity.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme that is measured to assess tissue breakdown, including in patients with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 is a crucial nutrient required for the production of red and white blood cells, with deficiency potentially leading to anemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 is a water-soluble vitamin that plays a vital role in the formation of red blood cells and maintaining the health of the nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Vitamin B12 is essential for healthy blood cell production and is often monitored in patients with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Folic Acid is a vitamin that contributes to the production of red and white blood cells, making it important for AML treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Folic Acid is a vitamin that helps in the formation of blood cells and is monitored to ensure sufficient levels for health.&lt;SEP&gt;Folic Acid, also known as folate, is a B vitamin that is essential for DNA synthesis and repairing, making it crucial for normal red and white blood cell formation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-69e63736c94b8b08a565c3d8679d3b57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron">
      <data key="d0">Iron</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron is a critical mineral necessary for various bodily functions, including the production of hemoglobin, which carries oxygen in the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is a crucial element for hemoglobin production; its levels are monitored to prevent deficiency or overload.&lt;SEP&gt;Iron is an essential mineral vital for producing hemoglobin; its levels must be managed to avoid deficiencies or overload.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HLA Typing">
      <data key="d0">HLA Typing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA Typing is a test performed to match donor and recipient tissues before hematopoietic cell transplants.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA Typing is a test used to assess compatibility for bone marrow transplants and involves analyzing human leukocyte antigens.&lt;SEP&gt;HLA typing is a blood test that identifies a person's human leukocyte antigen type, crucial for matching organ and bone marrow donors to recipients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Score">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG score is a scale used to assess a patient's performance status and overall health in oncology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">KPS is a standard way of measuring the ability of cancer patients to perform ordinary tasks.&lt;SEP&gt;Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a scale used to measure a cancer patient's general health and ability to perform daily activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a scale used to measure patients' functional status and overall health in oncology.&lt;SEP&gt;Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is a system used to evaluate the functional status of cancer patients and inform treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flow Cytometry">
      <data key="d0">Flow Cytometry</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow Cytometry is a laboratory technique that detects and analyzes cell characteristics, crucial for identifying different types of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique that analyzes the physical and chemical characteristics of cells or particles.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze the physical and chemical characteristics of particles in a fluid as it passes through a laser, crucial in blood cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Flow cytometry is a technology used to analyze the characteristics of cells, particularly for identifying blood cancers through markers on their surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotyping">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotyping</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotyping is a process that uses antibodies to detect specific antigens on white blood cells, crucial for diagnosing blood cancers like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a process that uses antibodies to identify specific cell markers, essential in classifying and treating blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunophenotyping is a technique used to identify and study various immune cells in the body, especially in cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver">
      <data key="d0">Liver</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The liver is a vital organ located on the right side of the body, playing crucial roles in digestion, blood filtration, and substance production.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ responsible for filtering blood, producing lymph, and metabolizing nutrients, and is the site where primary liver cancer originates.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ responsible for various metabolic processes, including detoxification and production of biochemicals necessary for digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver is a vital organ that processes nutrients from the bloodstream and produces bile, which aids in digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver plays a critical role in detoxifying blood and processing chemicals that can be affected in cancer patients, which is monitored through liver function tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bile Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Bile Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bile ducts are channels that transport bile from the liver to the gallbladder and small intestine, playing a crucial role in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous (IV) Iron Supplement">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous (IV) Iron Supplement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An IV iron supplement is a medical treatment administered to patients to correct iron deficiency, particularly when oral supplements are not effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulation Panel">
      <data key="d0">Coagulation Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Coagulation Panel tests how well blood clots, which is important for assessing bleeding disorders, especially in leukemia cases.&lt;SEP&gt;The coagulation panel is a series of blood tests used to evaluate how well and how quickly the blood clots.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Panel">
      <data key="d0">Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (DIC) Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The DIC panel is a specific test to determine whether a patient is experiencing disseminated intravascular coagulation, a serious bleeding condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by an overproduction of abnormal white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia is a type of cancer that originates in blood-forming tissues, leading to the production of abnormal blood cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d73843c009e523101e1464a2b28bc144&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Treatment">
      <data key="d0">AML Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML treatment often involves targeting specific biomarkers identified through tests like immunophenotyping and genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Testing in the context of AML helps identify specific mutations and alterations in leukemia cells, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic cancer risk testing is a medical test that examines blood or saliva for inherited gene mutations that may increase the risk of developing cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing assesses an individual’s risk of developing certain conditions and can provide insights into hereditary breast cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing assesses inherited mutations that may contribute to cancer development, providing important information for treatment and prevention strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing can detect genes linked to hereditary conditions that increase the risk of pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing can help identify specific mutations associated with ALL, influencing treatment plans and outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing detects specific genes linked to hereditary conditions that can increase the risk of pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing for gliomas checks for inherited mutations or abnormalities in brain cells that may lead to tumor development.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing for leukemia involves analyzing cells for mutations and changes in genes and chromosomes to guide treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing identifies specific mutations related to prostate cancer and other cancers, helping assess individual risk based on family history and hereditary factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing DNA to identify inherited mutations that may increase cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing DNA to identify inherited mutations that may increase the risk of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing involves analyzing genetic material to identify mutations that may contribute to the development of gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a medical test done to identify changes in genes associated with hereditary conditions, including ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a medical test that determines changes in chromosomes, genes, or proteins, often used to assess hereditary cancer risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a medical test that identifies mutations in genes which may affect cancer risk, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic testing is a method used to identify specific mutations in tumor cells, which can provide information about the tumor type and help guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6268b6efa690f6e094e443dca6b4cb52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Cells">
      <data key="d0">AML Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML cells are leukemia cells that can exhibit specific genetic changes, which are essential for diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karyotype">
      <data key="d0">Karyotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A karyotype is a laboratory procedure that creates a visual profile of an individual's chromosomes, used to identify genetic abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;A karyotype is a test used to visualize chromosomes to identify genetic defects, crucial in assessing cancer-related conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A karyotype is a visual representation of chromosomes from a sample, showing abnormalities in the number or structure of chromosomes.&lt;SEP&gt;A karyotype is a visual representation of chromosomes from a sample, used to detect abnormalities in the number or structure of chromosomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Karyotype is a method used to visualize chromosomes and identify abnormalities, which can help in the diagnosis and treatment of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NGS is a modern technique used to analyze DNA sequences to identify mutations that may affect gene function.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a cutting-edge technique used to analyze DNA sequences and identify mutations in leukemia cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing (NGS) is a modern DNA sequencing technology that helps identify mutations by analyzing a person's genetic makeup.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-Generation Sequencing is a modern technique to analyze DNA sequences in detail, identifying mutations affecting gene functions.&lt;SEP&gt;Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput method that allows for the rapid sequencing of DNA to detect genetic mutations in tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peripheral Blood">
      <data key="d0">Peripheral Blood</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peripheral Blood refers to the circulating blood in the body, from which cells can be analyzed using techniques like flow cytometry to detect blood cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspirate">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspirate</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow aspirate is a procedure that involves extracting bone marrow for testing, essential in diagnosing conditions like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a medical procedure to collect bone marrow samples for testing, commonly used in diagnosing blood cancers like AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure that extracts liquid and cells from the bone marrow using a hollow needle.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure to extract bone marrow sample for testing, often used in the context of diagnosing various blood disorders including leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow aspirate is a procedure where a sample of bone marrow is extracted to test for the presence of cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antibodies">
      <data key="d0">Antibodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antibodies are proteins produced by the immune system used in immunophenotyping to identify specific markers on the surface of cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarkers are biological molecules found in blood or tissues that indicate a condition, especially in relation to specific types of blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are biological molecules found in blood, other body fluids, or tissues, that indicate the presence or risk of cancer, aiding in treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are specific indicators found in tissue samples that help determine the nature of specific gliomas, guiding treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarkers are substances tested in tumor samples to provide details about the glioma, indicating conditions or abnormalities, often related to genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genomic Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genomic Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genomic testing assesses alterations in the DNA of cancer cells, aiding in personalized medicine approaches for treating leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Genomic testing examines the genetic material of cells to identify mutations that may affect disease progression and treatment responses in AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutations refer to changes in the DNA sequence that can lead to cancer progression and are key points of analysis in leukemia diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Donors">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Donors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic cell donors are biological family members who might be tested as potential donors for patients with blood disorders such as leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic cell donors are individuals whose stem cells can be used for transplantation in leukemia patients, potentially saving lives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FISH">
      <data key="d0">FISH</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH (Fluorescence in situ hybridization) is a testing method using dyes to attach to DNA in cells for diagnostic purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Fluorescence in situ Hybridization (FISH) is a molecular biology technique used to detect and localize specific DNA sequences on chromosomes for genetic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytogenetics">
      <data key="d0">Cytogenetics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytogenetics is the study of chromosomes and their role in disease, crucial for diagnosing conditions such as AML through karyotyping.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Profiling">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Profiling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor profiling involves analyzing the genetic makeup of cancer cells to understand their characteristics and inform treatment choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor profiling involves analyzing the genetic makeup of tumors, including AML cells, to guide targeted treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor profiling is a process that identifies mutations in cancer cells to tailor personalized treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Changes">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic changes refer to alterations in DNA that can be linked to the development and progression of leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes refer to alterations in the DNA of leukemia cells that can affect their behavior and response to treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic changes refer to mutations or alterations in genes that can affect the characteristics of leukemic cells and influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b63f0a9aec8d42c8426ded05a9bf92b4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)">
      <data key="d0">Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR)</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">PCR is a laboratory method used to amplify DNA or RNA sequences, allowing for the detection of abnormal cells in samples.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR is a molecular technique used to amplify DNA or RNA sequences, useful for detecting genetic anomalies associated with leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;PCR is a technique that amplifies DNA or RNA samples, enabling detection of genetic abnormalities even in very low amounts.&lt;SEP&gt;Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a technique used to amplify small segments of DNA, making it easier to analyze genetic material from tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG)</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">An ECG is a test that measures the electrical activity of the heart, providing insights into its rhythm and function.&lt;SEP&gt;An ECG test measures electrical activity in the heart, important for identifying potential treatment-related cardiac issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Echocardiogram">
      <data key="d0">Echocardiogram</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">An echocardiogram is a procedure that uses sound waves to create images of the heart, helping assess heart function and structure.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram is an ultrasound test that visualizes heart motion and structure, useful in assessing heart function in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram is an ultrasound test that visualizes the heart's structure and function, often used to monitor cardiac health.&lt;SEP&gt;An echocardiogram is an ultrasound test used to evaluate the heart's structure and function, providing images of the heart's chambers and valves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Normal Human Chromosomes">
      <data key="d0">Normal Human Chromosomes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Normal human cells contain 23 pairs of chromosomes, totaling 46, which are crucial for genetic makeup.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Sample">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow sample is collected to analyze chromosomes for conditions such as leukemia, aiding in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Sample">
      <data key="d0">Blood Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood sample is taken from a patient to perform various tests, including those that evaluate PSA levels.&lt;SEP&gt;A blood sample is used for karyotyping and genetic testing to detect chromosomal abnormalities in leukemic patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Time PCR">
      <data key="d0">Real-Time PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">Real-Time PCR is a variant of PCR that monitors the amplification of DNA during the PCR process in real time, aiding in diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracers">
      <data key="d0">Tracers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tracers are radioactive substances injected into the body during PET scans to help visualize metabolic activity of cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Tests">
      <data key="d0">Heart Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart tests are procedures to evaluate cardiovascular health, often necessary for monitoring patients undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4724f6acaaa7931560b29614efc2927f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Myeloid Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Myeloid Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the rapid proliferation of immature white blood cells called myeloblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute myeloid leukemia (AML) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by an increased number of immature white blood cells, often resulting from prior cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lumbar puncture (LP) is a medical procedure used to collect spinal fluid for diagnostic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture is a medical procedure used to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing, particularly for cancer cells in cases of CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;A lumbar puncture, also known as a spinal tap, is a procedure used to collect spinal fluid for testing, important for diagnosing conditions like leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;Lumbar puncture, also known as spinal tap, is a procedure to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing, crucial in diagnosing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment for AML">
      <data key="d0">Treatment for AML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment for Acute Myeloid Leukemia involves multiple phases and approaches including chemotherapy, targeted therapy, clinical trials, and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematologist is a medical expert specializing in blood diseases, including blood cancers like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="QTc">
      <data key="d0">QTc</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">QTc refers to the time interval between the start of the Q wave and the end of the T wave in the heart's electrical cycle, important for cardiac health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart Rhythms">
      <data key="d0">Heart Rhythms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Heart rhythms refer to the patterns of electrical activity in the heart, which can be affected by prolonged QTc and other conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ejection Fraction">
      <data key="d0">Ejection Fraction</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ejection fraction is a measurement of the percentage of blood leaving the heart each time it contracts, indicating heart efficiency and function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow biopsy involves extracting a small piece of bone and marrow tissue for disease diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;A bone marrow biopsy is a procedure that involves taking a sample from the bone marrow to test for cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow biopsy is a medical procedure that involves removing a small piece of bone and bone marrow for diagnostic testing, particularly for blood cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone marrow biopsy is a procedure in which a small sample of bone marrow is collected to diagnose conditions affecting blood cells, including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis of AML">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis of AML</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The diagnosis of Acute Myeloid Leukemia involves identifying a certain percentage of abnormal myeloblasts in the blood or bone marrow through various tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic Tests analyze DNA to identify genetic predispositions to certain health conditions, including various types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests analyze DNA to identify inherited mutations that may increase the risk of developing certain cancers, including pancreatic cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are performed to identify mutations or abnormalities in cells that may indicate specific subtypes of AML and guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests are used to identify specific mutations or abnormalities in genes that can inform treatment decisions for patients with AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic tests assess an individual’s DNA for mutations or changes that may influence their risk for cancer or guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biomarker Tests">
      <data key="d0">Biomarker Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker tests help in assessing responses to treatment and identifying specific features of the leukemia that can impact prognosis and therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker tests identify specific unique features in CLL cells that help guide treatment decisions and predict disease behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic cell transplant is a procedure where a patient's blood-forming stem cells are replaced, used in certain cases of AML treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97836a515d346931beb424ae40a89877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antimetabolites">
      <data key="d0">Antimetabolites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antimetabolites are a class of chemotherapy agents that prevent the building blocks of DNA from being utilized, thereby hindering DNA synthesis in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cladribine (Mavenclad)">
      <data key="d0">Cladribine (Mavenclad)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cladribine is an antimetabolite drug used in the treatment of certain leukemias, effectively targeting cancer cells by blocking DNA synthesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clofarabine (Clolar)">
      <data key="d0">Clofarabine (Clolar)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Clofarabine is an antimetabolite drug used for treating pediatric leukemia, inhibiting the synthesis of DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytarabine (Ara-C)">
      <data key="d0">Cytarabine (Ara-C)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytarabine (Ara-C) is an anthracycline used in AML treatment, administered in various doses depending on patient factors and treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine (Ara-C) is an important chemotherapy drug used to treat AML, often administered in various dosing regimens.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is a chemotherapy medication used frequently in treating AML, effective in reducing high white blood cell counts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fludarabine">
      <data key="d0">Fludarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Fludarabine is an antimetabolite chemotherapy drug affected for leukemias, affecting DNA synthesis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Methotrexate">
      <data key="d0">Methotrexate</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat various cancers, including primary CNS lymphoma, known for its effectiveness but also its potential toxicity.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug that interrupts DNA synthesis, used in various cancer treatments including leukemias.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug that is effective against CNS lymphoma, blocking the body's use of folate which cancer cells need to grow.&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is a chemotherapy drug used in a combination regimen known as dose-dense methotrexate, vinblastine, doxorubicin, and cisplatin (ddMVAC).&lt;SEP&gt;Methotrexate is the main chemotherapy drug used to treat primary CNS lymphoma, damaging rapidly dividing cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Daunorubicin">
      <data key="d0">Daunorubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Daunorubicin is a chemotherapy drug often used in combination with other treatments for Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;Daunorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug known for causing cell death by damaging DNA.&lt;SEP&gt;Daunorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug that is a key component in the treatment of AML, known for its effectiveness but potential heart toxicity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Idarubicin (Idamycin PFS)">
      <data key="d0">Idarubicin (Idamycin PFS)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Idarubicin is an anthracycline antibiotic that serves as an effective treatment for AML, causing DNA damage and cell death.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitoxantrone (Novantrone)">
      <data key="d0">Mitoxantrone (Novantrone)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitoxantrone is an anthracycline compound used in chemotherapy, particularly for various types of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO)">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin (GO)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is a targeted therapy linked to chemotherapy, designed to treat AML by attaching to cancer cell surface proteins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Azacitidine (Vidaza)">
      <data key="d0">Azacitidine (Vidaza)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Azacitidine (Vidaza) is a hypomethylating agent used in the treatment of AML, aimed at inhibiting the proliferation of leukemic cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent used in the treatment of leukemia that blocks methyl groups from binding to DNA.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent used to treat AML by blocking abnormal DNA methylation.&lt;SEP&gt;Azacitidine is a hypomethylating agent used to treat AML by reactivating silenced genes in leukemic cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decitabine (Dacogen)">
      <data key="d0">Decitabine (Dacogen)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Decitabine is another hypomethylating agent that aids in the treatment of AML through the modulation of DNA methylation.&lt;SEP&gt;Decitabine is another hypomethylating agent that helps reactivate silenced genes in leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Binding Factor (CBF)">
      <data key="d0">Core Binding Factor (CBF)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Binding Factor relates to genetic abnormalities in AML that impact blood cell development and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3">
      <data key="d0">FLT3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3 is a gene that encodes a protein involved in cell signaling; mutations in this gene are associated with Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).&lt;SEP&gt;FLT3 is a gene that, when mutated, plays a significant role in the development and treatment response of AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and may spread to other parts of the body, often requiring complex treatment including surgery and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Cancer refers to cancer that originates in the ovaries, involving complex treatment strategies and varying types.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a malignancy that begins in the ovaries and requires treatment approaches such as chemotherapy and maintenance therapies to improve outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can be influenced by genetic factors such as mutations in the BRCA genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and can cause various bodily changes and symptoms that may go unnoticed until advanced stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and is often associated with genetic mutations, such as those in the BRCA genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries and may involve various mutations and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the ovaries, and treatments often include chemotherapy, maintenance therapy, and targeted therapies such as PARP inhibitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the ovaries and is often asymptomatic until advanced stages. Symptoms include abdominal pain, bloating, and changes in bowel habits.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and can be hereditary, commonly associated with mutations in BRCA1 and BRCA2 genes.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the ovaries and is categorized into stages that indicate the extent of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer refers to cancers that begin in the ovaries and can manifest in various forms, primarily affecting the epithelium layer surrounding ovaries.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian cancer refers to cancers that originate in the ovaries, known for their impact on female reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard of Care">
      <data key="d0">Standard of Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard of Care refers to the best-known methods and treatments established through clinical trials for specific diseases, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;Standard of care refers to the best-known treatments for a specific disease based on clinical trials and expert consensus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multi-Agent Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Multi-Agent Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multi-Agent Chemotherapy involves combining multiple chemotherapy drugs to improve treatment efficacy against cancers like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrathecal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intrathecal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrathecal Chemotherapy is a method of delivering chemotherapy directly into the spinal fluid to treat cancers affecting the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrathecal chemotherapy involves administering chemotherapy directly into the spinal fluid to treat leukemia more effectively.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrathecal chemotherapy involves infusing chemotherapy drugs directly into the cerebrospinal fluid, often used for treating primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrathecal chemotherapy is a procedure where chemotherapy is delivered directly into the cerebrospinal fluid to target cancer cells that may be present in that area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents (HMAs)">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents (HMAs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents are drugs that prevent the binding of methyl groups to DNA, which can help reactivate silenced genes in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cytarabine">
      <data key="d0">Cytarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Cytarabine is a chemotherapy medication commonly used to treat AML, administered through various methods including intrathecal delivery.&lt;SEP&gt;Cytarabine is another chemotherapeutic agent that is commonly used in the treatment of various types of leukemia including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Idarubicin">
      <data key="d0">Idarubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Idarubicin is an anthracycline used in treating AML, with significant impact on cell death and DNA disruption.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab ozogamicin">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab ozogamicin is a targeted therapy designed to deliver chemotherapy directly to cancer cells by targeting specific proteins like CD33.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53afdc6713818c4177b64a712ecce11b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Serous Carcinoma (HGSC)">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Serous Carcinoma (HGSC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade serous carcinoma is the most common type of ovarian cancer, characterized by aggressive growth and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-grade Endometrioid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade endometrioid carcinoma is a less common but serious variant of ovarian cancer, known for its poor prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tube">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tube</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The fallopian tube is a part of the female reproductive system that connects the ovaries to the uterus and is involved in the transport of the egg.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uterus">
      <data key="d0">Uterus</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The uterus is a female reproductive organ that may need to be removed during radical cystectomy if bladder cancer has spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is a female reproductive organ where a fertilized egg implants and grows during pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is a hollow organ in the female reproductive system where a fetus develops during pregnancy.&lt;SEP&gt;The uterus is a major female reproductive organ where fetal development occurs during pregnancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologic oncologist is a medical expert specialized in treating female reproductive cancers, including ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist is a medical professional specializing in the treatment of cancers related to the female reproductive system, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A gynecologic oncologist is a medical specialist trained in diagnosing and treating cancers of the female reproductive system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Peritoneal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Primary Peritoneal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary peritoneal cancer forms in the tissue lining the abdominal wall and is often considered in conjunction with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovaries">
      <data key="d0">Ovaries</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The ovaries are a pair of female reproductive organs that produce eggs and hormones, playing a crucial role in the menstrual cycle and reproduction.&lt;SEP&gt;The ovaries are reproductive organs in females that may be affected during a radical cystectomy depending on cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;The ovaries are the female reproductive organs responsible for producing estrogen and progesterone, vital hormones influencing various bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fallopian Tubes">
      <data key="d0">Fallopian Tubes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The fallopian tubes are structures that connect the ovaries to the uterus, facilitating the movement of eggs and fertilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The cervix is the lower part of the uterus connecting it to the vagina, where cervical cancer originates.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina, and it is where cervical cancer primarily develops.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that connects to the vagina, playing a crucial role in female reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;The cervix is the lower part of the uterus that opens into the vagina, playing a crucial role in the menstrual cycle and childbirth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vagina">
      <data key="d0">Vagina</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The vagina is the muscular passage leading from the external genitals to the cervix of the uterus, part of the female reproductive tract.&lt;SEP&gt;The vagina is the muscular passage leading from the external genitals to the uterus, involved in childbirth and sexual reproduction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Common Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Common Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Common symptoms of ovarian cancer include bloating, heartburn, pelvic pain, and other related discomforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor tissue is the material removed during a biopsy to analyze and diagnose ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor tissue refers to the sample of cancerous cells taken for diagnosis and treatment planning for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor tissue samples are used for various cancer tests, including biomarker testing and histological analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cancer Care Team consists of various healthcare professionals, such as oncologists and nurses, working together to provide comprehensive cancer treatment and support.&lt;SEP&gt;The Cancer Care Team provides support and counseling for individuals dealing with a cancer diagnosis, including managing emotional and physical distress.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of healthcare professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive treatment and support for patients with bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of medical professionals who collaborate to provide comprehensive treatment and guidance for patients diagnosed with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team consists of various medical professionals, including oncologists and support staff, involved in the treatment of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The cancer care team is a group of specialists who work together to provide comprehensive treatment and support for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4da30dee72e228147daa825e4f5be72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominal Exam">
      <data key="d0">Abdominal Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An abdominal exam is a clinical procedure where healthcare providers check the abdomen for abnormalities, including signs of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;An abdominal exam is a physical examination that checks for abnormalities in the abdomen, helping to identify changes indicative of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exam">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A pelvic exam is a clinical procedure used to assess women's reproductive organs and can help identify ovarian cancer and other gynecological issues.&lt;SEP&gt;A pelvic exam is a procedure to examine a woman's reproductive organs and can help identify issues such as ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transvaginal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transvaginal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A transvaginal ultrasound is a specific type of ultrasound that provides clearer images of the ovaries by inserting a probe into the vagina.&lt;SEP&gt;Transvaginal ultrasound is a diagnostic tool that employs sound waves to produce images of the female reproductive organs, useful for assessing the cervix and nearby areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Transvaginal ultrasound is a procedure that uses sound waves to create images of the internal reproductive organs by inserting a probe into the vagina.&lt;SEP&gt;Transvaginal ultrasound is a specific type of ultrasound that provides closer examination of the ovaries, often used when ovarian cancer is suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Agent">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A contrast agent is a substance used in medical imaging to enhance the clarity of images during procedures like CT scans and MRIs.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast agent is a substance used in imaging tests like MRI to enhance the visibility of blood vessels and tissues in photographs.&lt;SEP&gt;Contrast agents enhance the quality of imaging tests like CT scans and MRIs, providing clearer visibility of potential tumors or irregularities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms associated with ovarian cancer can include bloating, painful intercourse, changes in appetite, and urinary urgency.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of ovarian cancer include bloating, pelvic pain, and difficulty eating, often leading to nutritional deficiencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e5406c28b41e499fad4ae44f2c44fdd7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Laparoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Laparoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic laparoscopy is a minimally invasive surgical procedure used to view and assess the extent of cancer in the abdomen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, increases the risk of breast and other cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, increases the risk of several cancers, including breast and prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene that, when mutated, significantly increases the risk of developing ovarian and breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA1 is a gene whose mutations are linked to higher risks of breast, ovarian, and other cancers, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA2">
      <data key="d0">BRCA2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA2 is a gene associated with increased risk for breast, ovarian, and prostate cancers when mutated.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is a gene similar to BRCA1, with mutations associated with increased risks of breast, ovarian, and additional cancers, including prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is another gene associated with hereditary cancer risk that plays a role similar to BRCA1 in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA2 is another gene where mutations can heighten the risk for several cancers, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselor">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic counselor is a healthcare professional specializing in genetics who helps patients understand gene-related diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a professional trained to provide information and support regarding genetic testing and inherited conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained expert in genetic diseases who assists individuals in understanding genetic tests and implications for cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained expert who assists individuals in understanding genetic diseases and the implications of genetic testing.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor is a trained professional who helps individuals understand genetic testing results and their implications for health and family.&lt;SEP&gt;A genetic counselor offers support and information regarding genetic testing and implications for diseases like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sedative">
      <data key="d0">Sedative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sedatives are medications prescribed to help patients relax, particularly for those who might feel nervous in enclosed spaces during medical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracer">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Radiotracer is a substance injected into the body that releases small amounts of radiation for imaging purposes, helping to highlight areas of concern in bone scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiotracer is a small amount of radioactive material injected into the body to help visualize internal structures during imaging tests like PET scans.&lt;SEP&gt;A radiotracer, such as fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG), is a substance used in PET scans to visualize cancer activity in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiotracer is a radioactive substance used in PET scans to help visualize organs and tissues, especially in cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Chest X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A chest X-ray is an imaging test used to visualize the lungs and check for any spread of cancer or other diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Core Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A core biopsy is a procedure that removes a larger tissue sample using a hollow needle to yield more information for cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Core biopsy (CB) is a procedure that removes larger samples of tissue for examination, usually performed under imaging guidance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paracentesis">
      <data key="d0">Paracentesis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paracentesis involves inserting a long thin needle to remove fluid from the abdomen, which can be tested for cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Symptoms">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Symptoms</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer symptoms refer to the physical signs indicating the presence of cancer, including bloating and pain in the abdomen typically associated with ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae40f53e9d867c255e1e6901f5cfb058</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA Genes">
      <data key="d0">BRCA Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA genes are critical in understanding hereditary ovarian cancer, particularly related to inherited mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA genes are crucial for DNA repair mechanisms, and mutations in these genes are significant markers in hereditary cancer risk, particularly ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;BRCA genes refer to a group of genes that, when mutated, significantly increase the risk of developing ovarian cancer and other hereditary cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Registered Dietitian">
      <data key="d0">Registered Dietitian</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A registered dietitian is a health professional specializing in nutrition who can guide patients on healthy dietary habits for improved treatment outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;A registered dietitian is a professional who provides advice on diet and nutrition, crucial for managing health and treatment outcomes in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A registered dietitian provides nutritional advice and guidance, particularly important in cancer care to support patient health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fb70c1c798caae12ac2d64e61bcbad4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125 Test">
      <data key="d0">CA-125 Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The CA-125 test is a blood test used to measure the level of CA-125 protein, which can indicate ovarian cancer and other conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;The CA-125 test measures levels of a protein in the blood that may indicate the presence of ovarian cancer or other health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gastrointestinal Tract">
      <data key="d0">Gastrointestinal Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The gastrointestinal tract consists of the organs food passes through and is essential for digestion and nutrient absorption.&lt;SEP&gt;The gastrointestinal tract includes organs through which food passes and is significant for diagnosing and managing cancer-related symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutrition encompasses the dietary practices and food choices that can influence health and recovery for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Markers">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Markers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor markers are substances found in blood or tissues that can be indicators of cancer presence or progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances found in blood or tissues that can indicate the presence of cancer, helping in diagnosis and treatment monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor markers are substances such as proteins tested to indicate the presence, growth, or spread of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Changes">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal changes associated with ovarian cancer can affect symptoms and overall health, impacting treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96238b4b361e393ff452a09d1ce26154</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing surgery is an option for early-stage ovarian cancer to preserve the ability to become pregnant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laparotomy">
      <data key="d0">Laparotomy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Laparotomy is a surgical method commonly used in ovarian cancer treatment, involving a large incision in the abdomen for tumor access.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IVF">
      <data key="d0">IVF</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">In vitro fertilization (IVF) is an assisted reproductive approach that may be utilized after surgery to help women conceive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microsatellite Instability (MSI)">
      <data key="d0">Microsatellite Instability (MSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI is a condition associated with certain cancers, indicating defects in the DNA mismatch repair system that can influence treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI is a condition characterized by the accumulation of mutations within microsatellites, affecting the tumor's ability to repair DNA and guiding treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI is a condition of genetic hypermutability that is assessed in tumor testing for cancer treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;MSI is characterized by the accumulation of DNA mutations reflecting defects in cellular repair mechanisms, influencing cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Microsatellite instability (MSI) is a condition where there is a defect in the DNA mismatch repair system, leading to an accumulation of mutations in microsatellites, which can drive certain cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB is a measure of the number of mutations within the tumor, important for guiding treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;TMB quantifies the total number of mutations present in a tumor's DNA, which can indicate the tumor's response to immunotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden is an evaluation used to assess the genetic alterations present in a tumor, offering insights into treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair (MMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair (MMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">MMR refers to a group of proteins involved in repairing errors in DNA; its deficiency is linked to various cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Expression">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 expression is a measure of the presence of the HER2 protein in tumors, which can affect prognosis and treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 expression is a status marker that can influence treatment decisions in cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRAF V600E Mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRAF V600E Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">The BRAF V600E mutation is a specific genetic alteration that can promote cancer growth, relevant in certain ovarian cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα) Expression">
      <data key="d0">Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα) Expression</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">FRα expression is often elevated in ovarian cancer and can be targeted by specific therapies to improve treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RET Mutations">
      <data key="d0">RET Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">RET mutations are genetic changes that can influence cancer development and are assessed in various cancers, including ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NTRK Gene Fusion">
      <data key="d0">NTRK Gene Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">NTRK gene fusion refers to a genetic alteration that can drive cancer cell growth and is a target for certain therapies in ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Initial Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Initial Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Initial surgery is often the first operative intervention in ovarian cancer, where diagnostics, biopsies, and possibly tumor removal occur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Before Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Before Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy administered before surgery is a treatment strategy aimed at reducing tumor size to facilitate surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A hysterectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the uterus and may include the cervix, affecting a woman's ability to bear children.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure involved in the removal of the uterus, often performed on patients with advanced ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hysterectomy refers to the surgical removal of the uterus, including the cervix, and is commonly performed to treat cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (BSO)">
      <data key="d0">Bilateral Salpingo-Oophorectomy (BSO)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">BSO is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of both ovaries and fallopian tubes, often performed in ovarian cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Debulking Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Debulking Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Debulking surgery aims to remove as much of the tumor as possible in cases where cancer has spread significantly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscopic Metastases">
      <data key="d0">Microscopic Metastases</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Microscopic metastases are tiny cancerous cells that may spread beyond the ovaries and are detected through surgical staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Microscopic metastases refer to cancer cells that have spread outside the ovaries or pelvis, detectable only with a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="In Vitro Fertilization (IVF)">
      <data key="d0">In Vitro Fertilization (IVF)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">IVF is a fertility treatment that involves fertilizing an egg outside the body and is sometimes used after ovarian cancer surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ac570b4029abb181150e0d0975aefb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS)">
      <data key="d0">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery (ICS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Interval Cytoreductive Surgery is performed after chemotherapy to remove as much cancer as possible and is crucial in managing advanced ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy (HIPEC)">
      <data key="d0">Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy (HIPEC)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperthermic Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy is a treatment during surgery that involves circulating heated chemotherapy solutions in the abdominal cavity to target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">AJCC is responsible for the development of staging systems, including for anal cancer, helping to determine treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The AJCC is a professional organization that develops staging systems, including the TNM system for cancer, used to classify the extent of cancer spread and severity.&lt;SEP&gt;The AJCC is a professional organization that develops staging systems, including the TNM system for cancer, which is crucial for classifying the extent of cancer spread and severity.&lt;SEP&gt;The AJCC is an organization that developed a staging system for various cancers, including ovarian cancer, to classify the disease's severity.&lt;SEP&gt;The AJCC provides a standardized system for cancer staging and helps guide treatment decisions based on various criteria related to tumor size and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)">
      <data key="d0">International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">FIGO is an organization that created a staging system for gynecological cancers, frequently used to describe the extent of ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The FIGO system is used to stage cervical cancer, categorizing it into four main stages with sub-stages to guide treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Omentum">
      <data key="d0">Omentum</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The omentum is a fatty layer of tissue in the abdomen that is removed during cancer surgery if metastases are suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peritoneal Washing">
      <data key="d0">Peritoneal Washing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Peritoneal washing is a procedure where fluid is circulated in the abdominal cavity to collect samples for testing cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paclitaxel">
      <data key="d0">Paclitaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paclitaxel is a chemotherapeutic agent commonly used in combination regimens for treating various types of cancer, including cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapeutic agent used alongside cisplatin in treatment regimens for metastatic cervical cancer and other malignancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug frequently used in treatment regimens for metastatic anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug often combined with carboplatin to treat ovarian cancer, used every three weeks.&lt;SEP&gt;Paclitaxel is a chemotherapy drug used in treating ovarian cancer, often in combination with carboplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab (Avastin) is a monoclonal antibody used in cancer treatment, particularly as part of a combination regimen with chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab is a monoclonal antibody used in cancer treatment that inhibits blood vessel growth, often employed in advanced ovarian cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, also known as Avastin, is a drug that may be added to chemotherapy for recurrent ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, commonly known as Avastin, is a monoclonal antibody used to stop the growth of new blood vessels that feed tumors, particularly in the treatment of cancers such as ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, commonly known by its brand name Avastin, is a medication that inhibits the growth of new blood vessels feeding tumors, commonly used in combination with chemotherapy for ovarian cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Thinners">
      <data key="d0">Blood Thinners</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood thinners are medications given to patients after surgery to prevent blood clots, especially after treatments for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)">
      <data key="d0">Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deep Vein Thrombosis is a condition in which blood clots form in deep veins, a risk associated with cancer and surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Menopause">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Menopause</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical menopause is caused by the removal of both ovaries during surgery, leading to sudden hormonal changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical menopause occurs when both ovaries are removed during a hysterectomy, leading to an abrupt decline in estrogen levels and associated symptoms related to menopause.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical menopause occurs when both ovaries are removed during surgery, leading to a sudden drop in estrogen and associated menopause symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf2d987135a5936876bc819136e3a5b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGO Staging System">
      <data key="d0">FIGO Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The FIGO Staging System is used to classify ovarian cancer into different stages (I-IV), providing detailed information about the cancer's progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bevacizumab (Avastin)">
      <data key="d0">Bevacizumab (Avastin)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bevacizumab is a drug that can be administered alongside chemotherapy for treating recurrent platinum-resistant ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bevacizumab, also known as Avastin, is a drug that may be added to chemotherapy regimens to inhibit the growth of new blood vessels supplying tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Spill">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Spill</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical spill refers to the rupture of the ovarian capsule during surgery, which can affect the staging and prognosis of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage I">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage I</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I of ovarian cancer indicates that the cancer is present in one or both ovaries without any spread to nearby tissues or organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage II">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage II</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II indicates that ovarian cancer has spread beyond the ovaries to nearby organs such as the uterus or fallopian tubes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage III">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III indicates advanced ovarian cancer that has spread to lymph nodes and the lining of the abdomen and possibly other areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Cancer Stage IV">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Cancer Stage IV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV represents the most advanced stage of ovarian cancer, indicating spread to distant organs such as the liver, spleen, or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ascites">
      <data key="d0">Ascites</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ascites refers to the accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity, which can occur in advanced stages of ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peripheral Neuropathy">
      <data key="d0">Peripheral Neuropathy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peripheral neuropathy is a common side effect of certain chemotherapy drugs, leading to pain and numbness, particularly in the hands and feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neoadjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Neoadjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neoadjuvant therapy refers to chemotherapy given before surgery to shrink tumors and improve surgical outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Neoadjuvant therapy refers to treatment, often chemotherapy, given before primary surgery to shrink tumors in breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant chemotherapy is administered after surgery to eliminate remaining cancer cells and reduce recurrence risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Taxanes">
      <data key="d0">Taxanes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Taxanes are a class of chemotherapy drugs, such as paclitaxel and docetaxel, used in treatment regimens for ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b69d38fafe014cece3427bd0459ae42b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olaparib">
      <data key="d0">Olaparib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Olaparib is a PARP (poly ADP-ribose polymerase) inhibitor utilized as a maintenance therapy for ovarian cancer patients, especially suitable for those with BRCA mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib is a PARP inhibitor intended for the treatment of breast cancer in patients with BRCA mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib is a PARP inhibitor used as a maintenance therapy option for patients with BRCA-mutated cancers, particularly after initial chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib is a PARP inhibitor used in maintenance therapy for certain types of cancer, particularly after initial treatment for ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Olaparib is a PARP inhibitor used in maintenance therapy for certain types of cancer, particularly after initial treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Niraparib">
      <data key="d0">Niraparib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Niraparib is a PARP inhibitor used in maintenance therapy for certain cancers, serving as a key treatment option for patients who cannot take Olaparib.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib is a PARP inhibitor utilized in maintenance therapy for certain cancers, particularly in cases where Olaparib cannot be used.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib is a maintenance therapy drug that can be administered for up to 3 years for patients with specific types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Niraparib is a maintenance therapy drug that can be used for up to 3 years for specific cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rucaparib">
      <data key="d0">Rucaparib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rucaparib is another PARP inhibitor provided as a maintenance therapy for HRD-positive ovarian cancers, particularly effective for cases involving BRCA mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib is another PARP inhibitor that can be administered for up to 2 years as maintenance therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib is another PARP inhibitor that may be utilized for maintenance therapy for ovarian cancer, lasting up to 2 years.&lt;SEP&gt;Rucaparib is another PARP inhibitor that serves as a maintenance therapy option for HRD-positive cancers, particularly those with BRCA mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maintenance Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Maintenance Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintenance therapy involves ongoing treatments to prevent cancer recurrence after initial successful treatment, typically involving intravesical procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy refers to ongoing treatment after initial therapy aimed at prolonging remission and minimizing cancer recurrence risk, especially in ovarian cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Maintenance therapy refers to the continued treatment following initial successful cancer therapy aimed at prolonging remission and reducing recurrence risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravenous Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravenous Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravenous chemotherapy refers to the delivery method of chemotherapy drugs directly into the bloodstream through a vein, commonly used in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intraperitoneal Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intraperitoneal chemotherapy is a method where chemotherapy is administered directly into the abdominal cavity to deliver therapeutic agents closer to cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Port">
      <data key="d0">Port</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A port is a small medical device placed under the skin for easier access to veins, commonly used for administering chemotherapy in patients over extended periods.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is an implanted device that allows for easy access to the bloodstream for administering treatments such as chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A port is an implanted device used for delivering chemotherapy and other treatments, typically placed in the chest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catheter">
      <data key="d0">Catheter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A catheter is a flexible tube used to drain urine from the bladder by being inserted through the stoma into the pouch.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a thin tube inserted into a vein to facilitate the delivery of chemotherapy and other treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a thin tube placed in a large vein to deliver therapies such as chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;A catheter is a thin tube used to deliver medications or fluids directly into the body, particularly used in intraperitoneal chemotherapy procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125">
      <data key="d0">CA-125</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CA-125 is a tumor marker often tested in women with ovarian cancer to monitor disease progression and response to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Myelodysplastic Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Myelodysplastic Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myelodysplastic syndrome (MDS) is a type of cancer characterized by ineffective blood cell production leading to abnormal cells in the bone marrow and blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8b44f0b4da13a804cd1dfb917ac7e981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Ovarian Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 ovarian cancer indicates cancer that has spread to the pelvis but not further, making patients eligible for certain maintenance therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 ovarian cancer refers to cancer that has spread to the pelvis but not beyond and may be eligible for maintenance therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CA-125 Level">
      <data key="d0">CA-125 Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CA-125 is a tumor marker used to monitor treatment response and potential cancer recurrence in patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-gynx (Elahere)">
      <data key="d0">Mirvetuximab Soravtansine-gynx (Elahere)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx (Elahere) is a targeted therapy recommended for certain recurrent ovarian cancer treatments, particularly for tumors with the FRα biomarker.&lt;SEP&gt;Mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx (Elahere) is a targeted treatment for certain recurrent ovarian cancers with identified biomarkers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-resistant Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-resistant Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-resistant cancer does not improve or worsens during platinum-based chemotherapy and requires different treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-resistant cancer is defined as cancer that does not respond to platinum-based chemotherapy or returns shortly after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platinum-sensitive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Platinum-sensitive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum-sensitive cancer is characterized as tumors that respond well to platinum-based chemotherapy and allow for different treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Platinum-sensitive cancer is defined as cancer that enters complete remission after platinum-based chemotherapy and returns more than 6 months later.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Secondary Cytoreductive Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary cytoreductive surgery aims to remove all visible cancer before initiating further recurrence treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Secondary cytoreductive surgery is performed to remove visible cancer before starting recurrence treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Throat Cancer is a type of cancer located in the throat or oropharynx, often linked to HPV exposure and requiring specific diagnostic measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer can form in parts of the throat including the oropharynx, which involves the back of the mouth and neck.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer encompasses cancers that develop in the throat, specifically in the oropharynx, and is often assessed and treated based on its location.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer forms in different throat parts, including the oropharynx, and requires specific management strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Throat cancer includes various malignancies affecting the oropharynx and requires specific testing for diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance testing involves regular physical examinations and imaging tests to monitor patients after cancer treatment for signs of recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Surveillance testing refers to follow-up examinations conducted after cancer treatment to detect any signs of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recurrence">
      <data key="d0">Recurrence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Recurrence is the return of cancer after a cancer-free period, necessitating different treatment strategies based on its location.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence pertains to anal cancer returning after initial treatment, a significant consideration in patient care and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return of cancer after treatment, which is a key concern in managing rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Recurrence refers to the return or relapse of cancer after initial treatment, with various symptoms indicating its onset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1 and BRCA2 Mutations">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1 and BRCA2 Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA1 and BRCA2 are genes whose mutations are crucial for assessing treatment options for ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD) Status">
      <data key="d0">Homologous Recombination Deficiency (HRD) Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HRD status is a biomarker used to guide treatment options in patients with recurrent ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα)">
      <data key="d0">Folate Receptor Alpha (FRα)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FRα is a protein that can act as a biomarker for treatment eligibility for ovarian cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-platinum Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Non-platinum Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-platinum chemotherapy may be the first-line treatment recommended for platinum-resistant ovarian cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c622c677deca24391324ecd5f16ce4a3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharynx">
      <data key="d0">Oropharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The oropharynx is a part of the throat located behind the mouth, involved in both digestive and respiratory systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat located behind the mouth, involved in both the digestive and respiratory systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat that includes structures such as the soft palate, tonsils, and the base of the tongue, and is commonly associated with throat cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The oropharynx is a part of the throat that includes the soft palate, tonsils, and base of the tongue, and is the focus of the book.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharynx">
      <data key="d0">Pharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharynx is a muscular tube in the neck facilitating air passage and food intake, subdivided into nasopharynx, oropharynx, and hypopharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The pharynx is a muscular tube in the neck that connects the nasal cavity to the esophagus, and it has three parts: nasopharynx, oropharynx, and hypopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Volume Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">High-Volume Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-Volume Cancer Centers are institutions recommended for treating complex cases of head and neck cancer due to their specialized experience.&lt;SEP&gt;High-volume cancer centers are specialized hospitals or facilities that have extensive experience in treating specific cancer types, providing optimal care for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer is a resource designed to provide patients with information on cancer treatment options and guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guidelines for Cancer provides comprehensive information and support for cancer patients regarding their treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guidelines provide important information and recommendations on cancer treatment, ensuring best practices are followed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Cancers">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Cancers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and neck cancers refer to a group of malignancies occurring in the head and neck region, including throat cancer and other cancers affecting associated structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Larynx">
      <data key="d0">Larynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The larynx, also known as the voice box, is a vital organ involved in breathing, producing sound, and protecting the trachea against food aspiration.&lt;SEP&gt;The larynx, also known as the voice box, is an anatomical structure in the neck involved in breathing and sound production, and can be affected by throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Soft Palate">
      <data key="d0">Soft Palate</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The soft palate is a flexible part of the roof of the mouth located at the back, involved in speech and swallowing, and may be affected by throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for throat cancer involves a range of procedures used to diagnose the presence of cancer in the throat, significantly impacting treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a84075aaac61b0ad85e5f811fc4f24b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">testing</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV Testing is a diagnostic procedure recommended for individuals diagnosed with oropharyngeal cancer to gather information about their cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV testing is a procedure used to detect the presence of human papillomavirus, a risk factor for cervical and anal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV testing is a procedure used to determine the presence of human papillomavirus, which is linked to certain types of cancers, including throat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Planning">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment planning for throat cancer is based on test results and involves a strategy devised by the healthcare team.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment planning is the process of developing a comprehensive strategy for cancer treatment, based on diagnostic testing and patient needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tobacco Use">
      <data key="d0">Tobacco Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tobacco use is a known risk factor for developing head and neck cancers and can impact treatment effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Support">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional Support is the encouragement and comfort offered by friends and family, vital for individuals undergoing diagnosis and treatment for illnesses.&lt;SEP&gt;Emotional support refers to the assistance provided by friends and family during the treatment process of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-38185832d6395b9952def9a72ecc339d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B (HBV) is a viral liver disease that can be influenced by cancer treatments, potentially causing additional health complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis C">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis C</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis C (HCV) is another viral liver disease, similar to HBV, and can affect individuals undergoing cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is involved in cancer research and provides a performance status scale to rate a person's fitness in relation to cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) is involved in cancer research, particularly in assessing the performance status of patients using scales like ECOG score.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) provides a performance status scale extensively used in cancer research and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group provides a performance status scale to evaluate a patient's general fitness and ability to undergo treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesia">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anesthesia is a medical intervention used to prevent pain during procedures, essential for surgeries such as cystoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia is a medical treatment that prevents sensation during surgical procedures, including biopsies, to ensure patient comfort.&lt;SEP&gt;Anesthesia is used during surgical procedures to prevent pain, requiring assessment prior to administration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3c0f6d26f2f58ca13201c1a90b0a180&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mind and Body Therapists">
      <data key="d0">Mind and Body Therapists</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Mind and Body Therapists are specialists who provide psychological and lifestyle support to cancer patients, helping them cope with the emotional stresses associated with their diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress">
      <data key="d0">Distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress is a term that describes a range of negative emotional experiences, including fear, sadness, and anxiety that cancer patients may face.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Loss">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hearing Loss refers to the partial or total inability to hear and can be assessed through various tests, important for cancer patients with head and neck tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Head and Neck Tumors">
      <data key="d0">Head and Neck Tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Head and Neck Tumors refer to growths located in the head or neck region that can impact various bodily functions, including hearing and swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)">
      <data key="d0">Pulmonary Function Tests (PFTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pulmonary Function Tests assess the performance of the lungs, crucial for patients undergoing treatment that might affect respiratory function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Disease">
      <data key="d0">Liver Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Disease encompasses illnesses affecting the liver, including Hepatitis B and C, which can complicate cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver disease encompasses various conditions affecting the liver, which can lead to liver cancer over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutrition Expert">
      <data key="d0">Nutrition Expert</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Nutrition Expert, such as a nutritionist or dietician, provides dietary guidance to cancer patients to promote health and recovery during treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A nutrition expert provides dietary guidance and consultations to patients, particularly crucial before and during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A nutrition expert, such as a dietitian, advises cancer patients on diet and nutrition to support their treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weight Loss">
      <data key="d0">Weight Loss</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Weight Loss refers to unintentional loss of body mass that can occur in cancer patients during treatment and needs to be monitored.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking or Vaping">
      <data key="d0">Smoking or Vaping</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Smoking or Vaping is the inhalation of smoke or vapor from tobacco or cannabis products, which can increase cancer risk and impact treatment efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33608457001e818e2b300adae9c6c0d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV">
      <data key="d0">HPV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV (Human Papillomavirus) is a common sexually transmitted virus strongly linked to an increased risk of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV (human papillomavirus) is a common sexually transmitted infection that is linked to the development of cervical cancer and other types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV, or human papillomavirus, is a virus with more than 100 strains, some of which are linked to various types of cancer including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is a group of viruses associated with various cancers, particularly cervical and throat cancers, and is often tested during cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a virus that can lead to abnormal tissue growth and certain cancers, including those related to the head and neck.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16 HPV Testing">
      <data key="d0">p16 HPV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">p16 HPV testing involves testing biopsy samples for the presence of the p16 protein, which acts as a surrogate marker for HPV infection in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contraceptive Methods">
      <data key="d0">Contraceptive Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contraceptive methods include various approaches to prevent pregnancy, such as barrier methods and IUDs.&lt;SEP&gt;Contraceptive methods include various birth control options like condoms, IUDs, and diaphragms used to prevent pregnancy during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormonal Birth Control">
      <data key="d0">Hormonal Birth Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormonal Birth Control is a method of preventing pregnancy that may involve pills, patches, or injections that alter hormone levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormonal birth control consists of methods that use hormones to prevent pregnancy, which may be recommended or avoided during cancer treatment depending on individual situations.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormonal birth control involves various methods that prevent pregnancy, which may or may not be recommended during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)">
      <data key="d0">Intrauterine Devices (IUDs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrauterine Devices (IUDs) are small, T-shaped devices placed inside the uterus to prevent pregnancy, often used during medical treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrauterine devices (IUDs) are a form of long-term, reversible contraception that can be considered for patients undergoing treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Barrier Methods">
      <data key="d0">Barrier Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Barrier Methods include contraceptive techniques that physically block sperm from reaching the egg, such as condoms and diaphragms.&lt;SEP&gt;Barrier methods are contraceptive techniques that physically block sperm from reaching the egg, including condoms and diaphragms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testosterone">
      <data key="d0">Testosterone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testosterone is a hormone that might be relevant in endocrine therapy for those assigned male at birth.&lt;SEP&gt;Testosterone is a hormone that plays a crucial role in the development of male reproductive tissues and the promotion of secondary sexual characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Testosterone is a hormone whose levels are often reduced in patients undergoing androgen deprivation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Testosterone is a male hormone that is targeted in hormone therapies, particularly in the treatment of prostate cancer to control growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Papillomavirus (HPV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Papillomavirus (HPV)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV is a common sexually transmitted infection that is a significant risk factor for developing cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV is a sexually transmitted virus linked to various cancers, including anal cancer, and can cause long-term cell changes leading to malignancy.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV) is associated with various cancers and can lead to tissue growth abnormalities and cancer, particularly in the cervix and throat.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiology">
      <data key="d0">Radiology</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiology is a branch of medicine that utilizes imaging techniques to diagnose and treat diseases, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Patient Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A patient care team is a multidisciplinary team of healthcare professionals who coordinate and manage care for an individual patient.&lt;SEP&gt;The Patient Care Team consists of healthcare professionals including doctors, nurses, and specialists who collaborate to provide comprehensive treatment to patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The patient care team consists of healthcare providers who support and guide patients through diagnosis, treatment, and health management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5fa7c7369d88ae71c842152510867223</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open MRI">
      <data key="d0">Open MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An Open MRI is a type of magnetic resonance imaging that features a larger opening, reducing feelings of claustrophobia for patients.&lt;SEP&gt;An Open MRI is a type of magnetic resonance imaging that has an open design, making it more comfortable for patients with claustrophobia.&lt;SEP&gt;Open MRI refers to a type of magnetic resonance imaging that has an open design to alleviate claustrophobia by allowing an open end on both sides.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Closed MRI">
      <data key="d0">Closed MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Closed MRI is a conventional type of magnetic resonance imaging where the patient is placed inside a tube-like structure.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed MRI is a more common type of MRI where the patient is enclosed within a magnetic tube, which may cause discomfort for those with claustrophobia.&lt;SEP&gt;Closed MRIs are more common and involve a tunnel-like structure, which may induce anxiety in patients with claustrophobia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scoping Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Scoping Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scoping procedures involve using tools to examine internal cavities, often utilized for biopsies or diagnostics regarding tumors in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscope">
      <data key="d0">Endoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An endoscope is a medical instrument used for visual examinations inside the body, particularly in areas such as the digestive tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Laryngoscopy is a diagnostic procedure examining the larynx and surrounding areas, often for detecting tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)">
      <data key="d0">Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI is an imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to generate images of organs and tissues, especially useful in soft tissue evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is a medical imaging technique used to visualize the internal structures of the body in detail using powerful magnets and radio waves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Claustrophobia">
      <data key="d0">Claustrophobia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Claustrophobia is an anxiety disorder characterized by an extreme fear of confined spaces, which may affect patients' comfort during MRIs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Team">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The healthcare team comprises medical professionals who support patients in the diagnosis and treatment of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The healthcare team comprises medical professionals, including doctors and nurses, responsible for patient care and support during medical tests and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The healthcare team refers to a group of medical professionals involved in the diagnosis, treatment, and care of patients with breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Biopsy (FNB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Fine-Needle Biopsy is a procedure that uses a thin needle to extract a small sample of tissue for examination, often guided by imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Gel">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Gel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound gel is a conductive substance used during ultrasound procedures to facilitate the transmission of sound waves between the transducer and the skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tracer">
      <data key="d0">Tracer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tracer is a radioactive substance injected during scans like PET to help visualize areas of concern, especially in cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammation">
      <data key="d0">Inflammation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammation is a biological response of body tissues to harmful stimuli, which can mimic or obscure cancerous growths in imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50531346164ea7c08dd087a868b3cc2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Fiberoptic Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fiberoptic laryngoscopy is a minimal invasive medical procedure used to examine the throat through a flexible scope and numbing medicine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Direct Laryngoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Direct Laryngoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Direct laryngoscopy is a comprehensive examination conducted under general anesthesia to visualize deeper throat structures and acquire biopsy samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Esophagoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Esophagoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Esophagoscopy is a procedure to examine the esophagus for abnormalities, performed under anesthesia with the aid of a guiding device.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronchoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Bronchoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bronchoscopy involves examining the airway through inserted devices to detect cancer or perform treatment, requiring anesthesia for patient comfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Panendoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Panendoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Panendoscopy allows thorough examination of the mouth, larynx, esophagus, trachea, and bronchi utilizing different endoscopes under anesthesia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)">
      <data key="d0">Programmed Death-Ligand 1 (PD-L1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 is an immune checkpoint protein that can inhibit anti-tumor immune responses if present on cancer cell surfaces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Testing by p16">
      <data key="d0">HPV Testing by p16</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV testing by p16 is a recommended assessment for throat cancer diagnosis, indicating presence of the HPV virus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Numbing Medicine">
      <data key="d0">Numbing Medicine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Numbing medicine is a local anesthetic used to minimize discomfort during flexible scope procedures such as fiberoptic laryngoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laryngoscope">
      <data key="d0">Laryngoscope</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A laryngoscope is a medical instrument used in direct laryngoscopy to visualize the throat and collect tissue samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tissue Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tissue sample is a specimen taken during a biopsy to be analyzed for cancer or other diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration (FNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-Needle Aspiration is a diagnostic procedure used to extract samples from nodules in the thyroid for examination, which cannot definitively diagnose certain carcinomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a minimally invasive procedure to extract tissue from lymph nodes for cancer testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a procedure to sample tissue from thyroid nodules for cancer testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-Needle Aspiration is a technique used to extract cells from a lump or mass using a thin needle, often performed to ascertain the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Fine-needle aspiration is a biopsy technique where a thin needle is used to extract cells from a tumor or lymph node for examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Core Needle Biopsy (CNB)">
      <data key="d0">Core Needle Biopsy (CNB)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Core Needle Biopsy is a procedure that removes a larger tissue sample from a suspicious area, allowing for a more comprehensive evaluation of potential cancer presence.&lt;SEP&gt;Core needle biopsy is a method of obtaining tissue samples from a tumor using a larger needle to provide more material for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ad38c9dc069a8ff8df58c42f00eba23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound (US)">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound (US)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique that uses sound waves to visualize internal organs, employed in screening for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Ultrasound is a medical imaging technique used to visualize structures within the body and can guide needle biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0739b33959299ea67072f390ce38bffd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oropharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Oropharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oropharyngeal Cancer refers to cancer occurring in the throat, typically associated with HPV and requiring specialized diagnostic testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden (TMB) is a measure related to the number of mutations present in a tumor's DNA, which can impact treatment choices with immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d0">Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating Tumor DNA (ctDNA) testing is a method to detect cancer cells or DNA fragments in the blood, aiding in monitoring and treatment responses.&lt;SEP&gt;ctDNA testing is an emerging method for analyzing DNA from tumor cells found in the blood, used in cancer detection and monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PDL1">
      <data key="d0">PDL1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PDL1 is a protein that is expressed on cancer cells and is measured using a combined positive score (CPS) to guide treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CPS">
      <data key="d0">CPS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CPS (Combined Positive Score) is a metric used to assess PDL1 expression in tumors, helping to determine eligibility for certain cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitor therapy is a form of cancer treatment that blocks proteins that prevent the immune system from attacking cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Testing">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Testing encompasses various diagnostic tests used to analyze the characteristics of a tumor, influencing treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor testing examines tissue samples from tumors to understand their molecular components and potential aggressiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor testing refers to the analysis of tumor material to identify genetic mutations that could inform treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Mutational Burden-High (TMB-H) indicates a high number of mutations in a tumor's DNA, which can affect responses to immunotherapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Testing">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy Testing is a medical procedure that involves taking a small sample of tissue from a tumor to assess its characteristics and guide treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f8639a06098656be20a04fe129660616</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by an overproduction of immature lymphocytes known as lymphoblasts, leading to difficulties in blood function.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer commonly diagnosed in children, originating from lymphocytes, which are a type of white blood cell.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer that starts in lymphocytes, affecting blood cell production and the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts that primarily affects children and young adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-ALL refers to B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, a subtype of ALL that affects B-lymphocyte progenitor cells.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of ALL that specifically affects B-cell lymphocytes and is more common than its counterpart, T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-ALL, or B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is characterized by the proliferation of immature B-cell lymphoblasts and is a more common form of ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a common form of ALL, requiring specific treatment strategies as highlighted in the book.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (B-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that begins in B lymphocytes, leading to the overproduction of immature lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) is another subtype of ALL relating to the B-cell lymphoblasts, treated in a unique way compared to T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (B-ALL) is another subtype of ALL relating to the B-cell lymphoblasts, which is treated differently compared to T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-ALL, or T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, is a subtype of ALL involving T-cell lymphocytes, treated differently from B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-ALL, or T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia, occurs when T-cell lymphoblasts overpopulate, possibly causing thymus enlargement and respiratory issues.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is a less common type of ALL that is treated distinctly from B-ALL as discussed in the chapters.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is another subtype of ALL that starts in T lymphocytes, contributing to similar symptoms as B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (T-ALL) is another subtype of ALL, affecting T-lymphocyte progenitor cells and is less common than B-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that originates from T-cell lymphocytes and can lead to symptoms such as an enlarged thymus and breathing difficulties.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) is a subtype of ALL that originates from T-cell lymphocytes, potentially causing various symptoms, including enlarged thymus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Stage">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical stage refers to the assessment of the extent of cancer before treatment, based on diagnostic tests and physical exams.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage refers to the categorization of cancer based on examinations and tests conducted before any treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage refers to the rating given to cancer before any treatment, based on initial examinations and imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic stage is determined by examining tissue removed during surgery, providing a more specific cancer assessment after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage refers to the assessment of cancer spread following surgical removal of the tumor and is based on microscopic examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage refers to the cancer stage determined by examining tissue removed during surgery and provides detailed information about the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM System">
      <data key="d0">TNM System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a classification tool used to stage most bone cancers by assessing tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a standardized method for staging cancer based on Tumor size (T), Node involvement (N), and Metastasis (M).&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a standardized method used to describe the extent of cancer spread, which includes Tumor size, Node involvement, and Metastasis status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16-positive">
      <data key="d0">p16-positive</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16-positive indicates throat cancers linked to the human papillomavirus (HPV), which may influence treatment options and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="p16-negative">
      <data key="d0">p16-negative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">p16-negative refers to cancers not associated with HPV, also affecting treatment strategies and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging Numbers">
      <data key="d0">Staging Numbers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Staging numbers (0 to 4) indicate the severity of cancer, with higher numbers representing more advanced stages of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resectable Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Resectable Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Resectable tumors are those that can be fully removed through surgery, often leading to better survival outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Resectable tumors can be completely removed through surgery, whereas unresectable tumors cannot.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unresectable Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Unresectable Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unresectable tumors are those that cannot be removed surgically due to their involvement with nearby vital structures.&lt;SEP&gt;Unresectable tumors are those that cannot be safely removed due to their involvement with nearby structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional Disease">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional disease indicates that cancer has spread to adjacent lymph nodes or tissues but not to distant sites.&lt;SEP&gt;Locoregional disease refers to cancer that has spread to nearby lymph nodes or tissues but not to distant parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Locoregional disease refers to tumors that have spread to local tissues, organs, or lymph nodes within the affected area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d8e20e6004dd43ae0f68185383b594d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer characterized by the overproduction of immature lymphocytes called lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing blood cancer that originates in the immune system's lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a fast-growing cancer that starts in lymphoid cells, most commonly affecting children and requiring specific treatment strategies based on age and disease type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblasts are immature B cells associated with B-ALL, which can crowd out normal blood cells in the bone marrow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphoblasts are immature T cells linked to T-ALL, which can lead to complications such as an enlarged thymus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)">
      <data key="d0">National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN is an organization that provides guidelines and recommendations for cancer treatment, including colorectal cancer screening.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN provides guidelines and resources for patients and healthcare providers regarding cancer treatment options and supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN provides guidelines for patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia, offering trusted recommendations for cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric ALL">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric ALL refers to cases of acute lymphoblastic leukemia found in infants, children, and young adults aged 39 or younger.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AYAs">
      <data key="d0">AYAs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs, or adolescents and young adults, defines individuals aged 15 to 39 at the time of initial cancer diagnosis, treated at pediatric or adult centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burkitt Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Burkitt Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burkitt Lymphoma is a rare subtype of B-cell ALL, treated differently than typical B-ALL despite being similar in some respects.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a rare subtype of mature B-cell ALL, sharing similarities with B-ALL but requiring distinct treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;Burkitt lymphoma is a type of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that is closely related to a rare subtype of B-cell ALL known as mature B-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progenitor cells are early blood-forming cells found in the bone marrow that differentiate into various blood cell types, including lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoid Progenitor Cells">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoid Progenitor Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoid progenitor cells are immature blood cells that can proliferate in the blast phase of chronic myeloid leukemia (BP-CML), influencing treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymphoid progenitor cells are specific progenitors that develop into lymphoblasts, ultimately maturing into lymphocytes involved in ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopoietic Stem Cells">
      <data key="d0">Hematopoietic Stem Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hematopoietic stem cells are early cells in the bone marrow that give rise to all types of blood cells including red and white blood cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hematopoietic stem cells are stem cells located in bone marrow that give rise to all blood cell types, including those affected in ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81c87ec3c2682da4d531da53f6e5e18c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ALL">
      <data key="d0">ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer that affects lymphoid progenitor cells, leading to the overproduction of immature white blood cells called lymphoblasts.&lt;SEP&gt;Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow, requiring various testing and treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b11b0c66441242d2f687a0c55adee585&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Rectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the rectum and can be treated if detected early; adenocarcinoma is the most common type.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissues of the rectum, often arising from polyps, and has specific diagnostic and treatment protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the rectum, often requiring various imaging tests for staging and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the rectum, with distinct stages that guide treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the rectum, with staging used to determine the extent of the disease and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Rectal cancer refers to cancer that arises from the rectum, often starting from polyps, particularly adenomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer encompasses cancers that originate in either the colon or rectum, often sharing similarities in treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal cancer refers to cancers that start in the colon or rectum, often beginning in glandular cells, and is distinct from anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapies">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapies refer to treatments that target cancerous cells throughout the body, often including chemotherapy and medications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5bc83f5f4f3e29b7f0a6f4787c3ad83</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon and remove polyps to prevent the development of colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a diagnostic procedure used to inspect the colon for abnormalities, including polyps and tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure recommended for surveillance testing after colorectal surgery, especially for cancer detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the colon and rectum, important for cancer screening and polyp removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the inner lining of the colon and rectum, recommended every 5 years after an initial procedure.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the colon and rectum, preventing potential cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a procedure for examining the entire colon and rectum, important for surveillance after the excision of rectal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a procedure for examining the inside of the colon and rectum, recommended as part of the surveillance regimen following treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Colonoscopy is a procedure used to examine the colon for polyps or cancer, involving camera insertion through the rectum.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Genes">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch repair genes are genes that correct DNA errors; their mutations are significant indicators in colorectal cancer diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps are abnormal growths in the colon that can be benign or precursors to colon cancer if not removed during procedures like colonoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Polyps are non-cancerous growths found in the lining of the colon and rectum, with the potential to develop into cancer over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperplastic Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Hyperplastic Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperplastic polyps are a type of polyp that rarely turns into cancer and is generally considered benign.&lt;SEP&gt;Hyperplastic polyps are non-cancerous growths in the colon that rarely become cancerous, yet are important for surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory polyps are another kind of polyp that typically does not lead to cancer, often arising due to inflammation.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory polyps are benign growths resulting from inflammation in the colon and are not generally associated with cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d0">Polypectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps from the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps and prevent the progression to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the colon, often performed during a colonoscopy.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the rectum, which can help in preventing the development of rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a minor surgical procedure used to remove polyps from the rectum, which can help prevent the development of rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is a surgical procedure to remove polyps from the colon or rectum, performed during a colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide critical information and recommendations for screening and managing colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoembryonic Antigen (CEA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CEA is a protein that can be present in higher levels in individuals with colorectal cancer, and is used for monitoring purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CCP (Complete Blood Count)">
      <data key="d0">CCP (Complete Blood Count)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A complete blood count measures different components of the blood, providing essential information about health and disease status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging tests">
      <data key="d0">Imaging tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging tests, such as MRI or CT scans, are used to visualize areas inside the body to aid in diagnosing and staging cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor analysis involves examining tumor samples to detect genetic mutations, which are critical for planning treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncologist is a medical doctor specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer, who performs physical exams as part of patient surveillance.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating cancer, performing physical exams and overseeing follow-up care.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical doctor who specializes in treating cancer and is involved in planning and managing cancer treatment strategies for patients with conditions such as CLL.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a medical professional who specializes in diagnosing and treating cancer, working closely with a care team.&lt;SEP&gt;An oncologist is a physician specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of cancer, playing a critical role in patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm banking is a process of freezing and storing semen for future use, particularly for patients concerned about fertility due to cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d0">Egg Freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg freezing is a fertility preservation method that involves removing and storing unfertilized eggs for future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Tissue Banking">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Tissue Banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian tissue banking involves freezing ovarian tissue for future implantation, helping preserve fertility for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian Transposition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian Transposition is a surgical procedure aiming to relocate the ovaries outside the radiation field to protect them during treatments like EBRT.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Transposition is a surgical procedure to move the ovaries out of the radiation field to protect them during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical method to relocate the ovaries out of the radiation field during cancer treatment to preserve fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure that moves the ovaries out of the radiation field to preserve fertility during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is a surgical procedure that moves the ovaries out of the radiation field to protect them during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast">
      <data key="d0">Contrast</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast is a substance used in imaging tests to enhance the clarity of medical images, often injected into veins or consumed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroid">
      <data key="d0">Steroid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are medications given to patients to reduce inflammation and allergic reactions, often used before imaging tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antihistamine Medication">
      <data key="d0">Antihistamine Medication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Antihistamines, such as Benadryl, are used to prevent allergic reactions during medical procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging Protocol">
      <data key="d0">Staging Protocol</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The staging protocol is a set of methods used to determine the extent and severity of cancer, essential for treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic MRI">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic MRI is a specific imaging procedure used to visualize the rectum and assess the extent of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound (EUS)">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound (EUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endorectal ultrasound is an imaging method that utilizes sound waves to visualize the rectum and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan provides detailed images of the body's internal structures and assists in determining the extent of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;CT scan is a diagnostic imaging technique that produces detailed images of the inside of the body, particularly for cancer assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Preservation Options">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Preservation Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility preservation options are medical procedures available to individuals to protect their reproductive ability before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility preservation options like sperm banking and egg freezing are methods available to patients concerned about their ability to conceive post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oophoropexy">
      <data key="d0">Oophoropexy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oophoropexy is a surgical procedure that moves ovaries out of the radiation field to protect them from damage during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Oophoropexy is a surgical procedure to relocate ovaries out of the range of radiation, helping preserve fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopic Submucosal Dissection (ESD)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">ESD is a minimally invasive procedure for removing small rectal cancers that haven't grown beyond the muscle layer of the rectal wall.&lt;SEP&gt;ESD is a recommended minimally invasive procedure for removing small rectal cancers that haven't grown beyond the muscle layer of the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transanal Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Transanal Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transanal local excision is a surgical option for removing T1 tumors located at the lower rectum, performed through the anus.&lt;SEP&gt;Transanal local excision is a surgical technique used to remove T1 tumors located at the end of the rectum through the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Sigmoidoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Sigmoidoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Flexible sigmoidoscopy is a diagnostic procedure used for surveillance after treatments for rectal cancer, typically performed every 6 months.&lt;SEP&gt;Flexible sigmoidoscopy is a procedure that allows for the visualization of the rectum and lower colon, used for monitoring after treatment of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endorectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Endorectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endorectal ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique used to assess rectal tumors and monitor for cancer recurrence after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Endorectal ultrasound is a diagnostic method used in conjunction with MRI to monitor for recurrence of rectal cancer after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Polyps are abnormal growths on the lining of the rectum that can develop into cancer, and their removal is crucial for cancer prevention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2c9770fd98dbbe3c7947601fb3e654c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Transabdominal Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transabdominal surgery is a recommended treatment for rectal tumors that have invaded the muscle layer, involving the removal of the tumor and surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Anterior Resection">
      <data key="d0">Low Anterior Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Anterior Resection (LAR) is a type of transabdominal surgery for tumors in the mid to upper rectum, allowing for potential bowel reconnection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) is a surgical procedure for lower rectal tumors, involving removal of the anus and rectum, resulting in a permanent colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) is a surgical procedure for lower rectal tumors, involving removal of the anus and rectum, which results in a permanent colostomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxaliplatin">
      <data key="d0">Oxaliplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Oxaliplatin is a chemotherapy drug that may cause neuropathy as a side effect, often used in treatment regimens for rectal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxaliplatin is a chemotherapy drug used as part of combination therapy for certain cancers, associated with specific side effects.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxaliplatin is a chemotherapy drug used to treat certain cancers, often included in treatment regimens for rectal cancer, and can cause side effects like neuropathy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irinotecan">
      <data key="d0">Irinotecan</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Irinotecan is a chemotherapy agent associated with side effects such as abdominal cramping, nausea, and diarrhea, used in regimens for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Irinotecan is a chemotherapy agent commonly used in cancer treatment regimens, associated with side effects such as abdominal cramping and hair loss.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Local Excision">
      <data key="d0">Local Excision</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Local excision is a surgical procedure to remove a tumor and small surrounding tissue, recommended for early-stage, low-risk perianal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Local excision is a surgical procedure to remove a tumor and some surrounding tissue from the colon or rectum, potentially followed by more treatment if needed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal Anastomosis is a surgical procedure to reconnect the colon to the remaining rectum after tumor removal, aiming for normal bowel function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coloanal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d0">Coloanal Anastomosis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Coloanal Anastomosis is a surgical technique used when the colon cannot be connected to the rectum; instead, it connects directly to the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Staging">
      <data key="d0">Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Staging is the process of determining the extent of cancer spread, crucial for treatment planning and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Staging refers to determining the extent of cancer in the body, which influences treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colostomy">
      <data key="d0">Colostomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A colostomy is a procedure that creates an opening in the abdomen to allow stool to exit when reconnection of the colon is unsafe.&lt;SEP&gt;A colostomy is a surgical opening created for stool elimination, formed when part of the colon is removed or bypassed.&lt;SEP&gt;A colostomy is a surgical procedure connecting the end of the colon to the outside of the abdomen, used when the anus is removed, creating a stoma for stool to exit.&lt;SEP&gt;A colostomy is a surgical procedure that connects the colon to an opening in the abdomen to allow stool to exit the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A colostomy is a surgical procedure that may be necessary for patients with anal cancer, creating an opening for stool to exit the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Colostomy is a surgical procedure creating an opening (stoma) in the abdomen to allow stool to exit the body when normal bowel function cannot be preserved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney cancer refers to various cancers that start in the tissues of the kidneys, with renal cell carcinoma (RCC) being the most prevalent form.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of kidney cancer, originating in the cells of the renal tubules.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal cell carcinoma (RCC) is the most common type of kidney cancer, which begins in the cells that line the renal tubules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial carcinoma is a type of cancer that starts in the cells lining the renal pelvis and ureter, often confused with kidney tumors but treated differently.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Wilms Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Wilms tumor, also known as nephroblastoma, typically occurs in young children and involves cancer cells found in the kidney.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Renal Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal sarcoma starts in the blood vessels or connective tissue of the kidney and is less common than RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidneys">
      <data key="d0">Kidneys</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidneys are a pair of organs located behind other organs in the abdomen, responsible for filtering blood, removing waste, and producing hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are vital organs responsible for filtering blood, creating urine, and regulating various bodily functions including electrolytes and blood pressure.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are vital organs responsible for filtering waste from the blood and producing urine, which is a component of the urinary tract.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys can show bright spots on PET scans, which are typically indicative of normal or abnormal conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Capsule">
      <data key="d0">Renal Capsule</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal capsule is a thin layer of tissue covering each kidney, essential for protecting the organ.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary System">
      <data key="d0">Urinary System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary system comprises a group of organs, including the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra, that remove waste from the body in the form of urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Artery">
      <data key="d0">Renal Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal artery carries blood that contains waste products into the kidneys for filtration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d0">Renal Tubules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal tubules are tiny filtering tubes within the kidneys that remove waste and other substances from blood to form urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Renal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The renal vein transports clean, filtered blood away from the kidneys back to the heart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureter">
      <data key="d0">Ureter</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ureter is a long tube that carries urine from each kidney to the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder">
      <data key="d0">Bladder</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The bladder is a hollow organ in the pelvic region that stores urine until it is excreted from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is a muscular sac that stores urine before it is excreted from the body, essential for the urinary system's functionality.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ in the human body that may be removed during pelvic exenteration surgery if involved with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder is an organ that stores urine until it is expelled from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The bladder may also display bright spots on PET scans, suggesting either normal functionality or potential medical issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urethra">
      <data key="d0">Urethra</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urethra is a duct that allows urine to exit the body from the bladder, playing a crucial role in urinary function.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is a duct that allows urine to pass from the bladder and semen to exit the body, surrounded by the prostate in males.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the duct through which urine is discharged from the bladder, integral to the urinary tract's function.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube that carries urine from the bladder and semen from the reproductive system out of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The urethra is the tube through which urine is expelled from the bladder to the outside of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Kidney Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced kidney cancer refers to the stage where cancer has grown into and beyond Gerota’s fascia, indicating a severe progression of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC) is the most prevalent subtype of renal cell carcinoma (RCC), identified by its clear appearance under microscopic examination.&lt;SEP&gt;Clear cell renal cell carcinoma (ccRCC) is the most common subtype of RCC, characterized by pale or clear cancer cells observed under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-clear cell RCC (nccRCC) is a subgroup of renal cell carcinoma that includes several subtypes with different characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clear Cell RCC is the most prevalent subtype of RCC, accounting for approximately 70% of cases, characterized by pale or clear cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Clear Cell RCC encompasses various subtypes of renal cell carcinoma that do not exhibit clear cell characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Non-Clear Cell RCC includes several subtypes such as Papillary RCC and Chromophobe RCC, representing about 30% of RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC (PRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC (PRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC is the most common subtype of non-clear cell RCC, identified by long, thin, finger-like growths.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromophobe RCC">
      <data key="d0">Chromophobe RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromophobe RCC cells are larger than clear cells and are characterized by pale staining under a microscope.&lt;SEP&gt;Chromophobe RCC is a rare subtype of renal cell carcinoma that features pale cells, which are larger than those found in clear cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d0">Translocation RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation RCC can occur at any age, particularly in children, characterized by unique gene fusions leading to cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Translocation RCC is a rare form of kidney cancer characterized by unique genetic fusions and gene rearrangements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Collecting Duct RCC (cdRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Collecting Duct RCC (cdRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Collecting Duct RCC is a rare subtype of RCC that originates in the collecting ducts of the kidneys and is known for being aggressive.&lt;SEP&gt;Collecting duct RCC originates in the collecting ducts or tubules and is classified as a rare type of non-clear cell RCC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)">
      <data key="d0">Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal Medullary Carcinoma can occur in young individuals, particularly of African descent, and represents a rare form of RCC.&lt;SEP&gt;Renal Medullary Carcinoma is a rare variant of RCC commonly diagnosed in young adults of African descent and characterized by specific genetic alterations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid RCC (sRCC)">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid RCC (sRCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid RCC describes any type of RCC that exhibits sarcomatoid features, typically associated with a poor prognosis and aggressive behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Sarcomatoid RCC refers to any RCC that shows sarcomatoid features, which are associated with aggressive growth and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney">
      <data key="d0">Kidney</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The kidneys are vital organs responsible for filtering blood and producing urine, playing a crucial role in the body's waste management system.&lt;SEP&gt;The kidneys are vital organs responsible for filtering blood and producing urine, playing a significant role in kidney cancer pathology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Histology">
      <data key="d0">Histology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Histology is the study of the anatomy of cells and tissues under a microscope, used to make treatment decisions for cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Histology is the study of the microscopic structure of tissues, particularly in the context of diagnosing diseases such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary RCC">
      <data key="d0">Papillary RCC</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary RCC is a subtype of non-clear cell RCC characterized by elongated, finger-like growths under microscopy and found in about 10% of RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Plan</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A treatment plan for anal cancer is a strategic approach to determine the best forms of therapy based on the cancer stage and characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan is a comprehensive strategy designed by healthcare teams tailored to the individual needs of a cancer patient.&lt;SEP&gt;A treatment plan refers to the personalized approach to cancer management, tailored to the specific subtype and characteristics of the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic Tests are medical procedures used to confirm the presence of cancer, assess its stage, and guide treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests are procedures used to confirm the presence of prostate cancer when initial tests, such as PSA or digital rectal exams, suggest a problem.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnostic tests encompass various examinations including imaging, blood tests, and biopsies utilized to confirm the presence of kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dc281a303d0d4c5300c0c1c45b739ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoblasts">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoblasts are immature lymphocytes produced in excess in ALL, which can crowd out other blood cells, leading to functional impairments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">B Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that produce antibodies and can be affected in cases of ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">T Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell responsible for fighting infections and can also be impacted by ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation Support">
      <data key="d0">Transportation Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transportation Support refers to the assistance provided by friends, family, or peers to help individuals reach medical appointments, crucial during treatment for health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peering Support">
      <data key="d0">Peering Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Peering Support involves the assistance from peers who share similar experiences, offering compassion and understanding during difficult times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Telehealth Visits">
      <data key="d0">Telehealth Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Telehealth Visits allow patients to consult healthcare providers remotely, which can be helpful during diagnosis and treatment phases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Blood Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Cancer refers to malignancies that affect the production and function of blood cells, with Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia being one of the major types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a99a89bec88823cedf4c1f0cd1a78e81</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">B-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that originates in B-cell lymphocytes and is more prevalent than T-cell ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell ALL">
      <data key="d0">T-cell ALL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell ALL is a subtype of Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia that begins in T-cell lymphocytes and can cause symptoms related to thymus enlargement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">B-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (B-LBL) occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts primarily grow in lymph nodes instead of the bone marrow, leading to lymphoma rather than leukemia.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (B-LBL) occurs when B-cell lymphoblasts primarily grow in lymph nodes rather than bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;B-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma is a disease where B-cell lymphoblasts predominantly grow in lymph nodes rather than bone marrow, linked to B-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">T-cell Lymphoblastic Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (T-LBL) is a condition where T-cell lymphoblasts grow mainly in the thymus rather than the bone marrow.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma (T-LBL) results when T-cell lymphoblasts predominantly grow in the thymus rather than in the bone marrow, presenting a different clinical picture from T-ALL.&lt;SEP&gt;T-cell lymphoblastic lymphoma occurs when T-cell lymphoblasts primarily expand in the thymus instead of the bone marrow and is associated with T-ALL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)">
      <data key="d0">Adolescent and Young Adult (AYA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AYAs are individuals aged 15 to 39 years at the time of initial cancer diagnosis, who may receive treatment in either pediatric or adult oncology settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82c7205fc2067a4d00e43fd22276db22</data>
    </node>
    <node id="myeloblasts">
      <data key="d0">myeloblasts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Myeloblasts are immature white blood cells that develop from blood stem cells and are involved in the pathology of Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL)">
      <data key="d0">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Acute Promyelocytic Leukemia (APL) is a rare subtype of AML characterized by the presence of the abnormal fusion gene PML::RARA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)">
      <data key="d0">Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPDCN is a rare type of leukemia that may be suspected in patients with unusual skin lesions or blood abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;Blastic Plasmacytoid Dendritic Cell Neoplasm (BPDCN) is an aggressive subtype of AML that can be found in blood, bone marrow, lymph nodes, and/or skin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Underarm">
      <data key="d0">Underarm</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The underarm area is part of the human body that contains lymph nodes and is often examined for signs of health issues including lymphadenopathy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Groin">
      <data key="d0">Groin</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The groin is another area of the body where lymph nodes are located and can be checked for enlargement, which may indicate health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Blood Cells (RBCs)">
      <data key="d0">Red Blood Cells (RBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Red blood cells (RBCs) are responsible for carrying oxygen throughout the body and are vital for maintaining healthy bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cells (WBCs)">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cells (WBCs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">White blood cells (WBCs) are essential components of the immune system that help the body fight infections and diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Platelets (PLTs)">
      <data key="d0">Platelets (PLTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platelets (PLTs) are small cell fragments in the blood that play a crucial role in blood clotting and wound healing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2dc31077c053f79ff82959575d335956</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Specialist">
      <data key="d0">AML Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An AML specialist is a medical professional with expertise in Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) and provides guidance on treatment and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gene mutations are changes in the DNA sequence of a gene that may play a role in the development and aggressiveness of AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Testing">
      <data key="d0">AML Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">AML Testing refers to various diagnostic procedures conducted to diagnose Acute Myeloid Leukemia, including blood tests, bone marrow tests, and imaging studies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Biomarkers">
      <data key="d0">AML Biomarkers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML biomarkers are specific indicators in the blood or bone marrow that help in diagnosing and monitoring Acute Myeloid Leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Questions to Ask">
      <data key="d0">Questions to Ask</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Questions to Ask is a section in the NCCN Guidelines that provides suggested inquiries patients should make to understand their care better.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for AML">
      <data key="d0">Tests for AML</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests for AML include various procedures like blood tests, bone marrow biopsies, and imaging tests that help diagnose and understand the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Options">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment Options encompass various medical procedures and therapies available to patients based on their cancer risk group and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options for bladder cancer vary and include various therapies, which should be tailored based on stage and patient health.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options for gliomas include surgery, radiation therapy, and chemotherapy; these may be combined for better efficacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options for prostate cancer include various therapies tailored to the patient's specific condition and cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options include various therapies available for thyroid cancer, such as surgery, radioactive iodine therapy, targeted therapies, and observation.&lt;SEP&gt;Treatment options refer to the various therapies available for managing and treating Acute Myeloid Leukemia, including chemotherapy and targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exam Results">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exam Results</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical exam results provide essential insights into a patient’s overall health status and may reveal important indicators relevant to AML care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ae28e3852f4a9f229250fc19eb39036b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="White Blood Cell Differential">
      <data key="d0">White Blood Cell Differential</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The White Blood Cell Differential is a test that counts different types of white blood cells to assess blood health and detect abnormalities such as leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hemoglobin">
      <data key="d0">Hemoglobin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hemoglobin (Hgb) is a protein in red blood cells responsible for transporting oxygen throughout the body, and its levels indicate the need for medical interventions like blood transfusions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Absolute Neutrophil Count">
      <data key="d0">Absolute Neutrophil Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC) measures the amount of neutrophils in the blood, which are critical in fighting infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Metabolic Panel">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Metabolic Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) evaluates various substances in the blood, providing insight into kidney and liver function.&lt;SEP&gt;A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures various important substances in the blood, indicating potential health problems.&lt;SEP&gt;A Comprehensive Metabolic Panel (CMP) measures vital chemicals and proteins in the blood, helping to detect potential health problems, particularly related to the kidneys and liver.&lt;SEP&gt;A comprehensive metabolic panel is a blood screening test that evaluates overall health and can indicate issues with organs such as the kidneys and liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Urea Nitrogen">
      <data key="d0">Blood Urea Nitrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) is used to evaluate kidney health and indicates how well the kidneys are filtering waste.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactate Dehydrogenase">
      <data key="d0">Lactate Dehydrogenase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lactate Dehydrogenase (LDH) is an enzyme released by damaged cells, often measured to detect tissue damage or tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver function tests (LFTs) assess how well the liver is working through the measurement of specific chemicals in the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;Liver function tests assess the liver enzymes and indicators of liver health, critical for understanding the extent of cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid">
      <data key="d0">Vitamin B12 and Folic Acid</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitamin B12 and folic acid are nutrients necessary for new protein and blood cell formation, monitored to assess nutritional health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA) typing determines tissue compatibility for blood or organ transplants and plays a role in immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Scale">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) scale is used to assess a patient's performance status and fitness level.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnofsky Performance Status">
      <data key="d0">Karnofsky Performance Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) is another scale used to evaluate a patient's overall health and ability to perform daily activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differential">
      <data key="d0">Differential</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differential is a lab test that counts different types of white blood cells, helping to assess overall blood health and detect abnormalities such as leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC)">
      <data key="d0">Absolute Neutrophil Count (ANC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ANC specifically measures the number of neutrophils, a type of white blood cell essential for fighting infections.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Creatinine Level">
      <data key="d0">Creatinine Level</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The creatinine level in blood indicates kidney function, with higher levels suggesting impaired kidney health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Tests (LFTs)">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Tests (LFTs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Function Tests evaluate chemicals produced by the liver to assess its health and detect possible diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Coagulation Factors">
      <data key="d0">Coagulation Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Coagulation Factors are proteins necessary for blood clotting, often analyzed through a coagulation panel to assess bleeding disorders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)">
      <data key="d0">Human Leukocyte Antigen (HLA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HLA proteins are unique markers on cells that play an essential role in immune response and organ transplant compatibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Performance Status (PS)">
      <data key="d0">Performance Status (PS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Performance Status indicates a patient's overall health and functioning, impacting treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Performance status is a measure of a patient's general fitness and ability to perform everyday tasks, which influences treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9fa573748870dc50a98a935843997c68&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia)">
      <data key="d0">AML (Acute Myeloid Leukemia)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AML is a type of cancer that starts in the bone marrow and affects blood cells, often requiring various tests for diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PS Scale">
      <data key="d0">PS Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PS Scale, or Performance Status Scale, is used in oncology to evaluate a patient's general health and functional capability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Aspiration">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Aspiration</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow aspiration is a procedure where a sample of bone marrow is taken using a needle to diagnose conditions like leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry (IHC)">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry (IHC)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry is a lab technique used to visualize the presence of specific proteins in tissue sections, playing a role in diagnosing different types of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA; abnormalities in chromosomes can indicate the presence of leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes">
      <data key="d0">Leukemia Predisposition Syndromes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leukemia predisposition syndromes are inherited conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, emphasizing the importance of family medical history in treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Leukemia predisposition syndromes refer to hereditary conditions that increase the risk of developing leukemia, potentially passed from parents to children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutation testing searches for specific genetic abnormalities in leukemia cells to guide targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsied Cells">
      <data key="d0">Biopsied Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsied cells are samples taken from patients suspected of having cancer, which are analyzed for diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Cells">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthy cells are normal cells in the body that function properly, in contrast to cancerous cells that become dysfunctional.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0786666c65b5bf00705439d5cb60d5a0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">RUNX1::RUNX1T Leukemia is a hereditary cancer syndrome linked to leukemia predisposition, which can be passed down from parent to child.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (FISH)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization (FISH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FISH is a laboratory technique used to detect and localize the presence of specific DNA sequences, which aids in identifying genetic abnormalities in CLL patients.&lt;SEP&gt;FISH is a testing method that uses fluorescent probes to detect specific genetic abnormalities in leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family History of Leukemia">
      <data key="d0">Family History of Leukemia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A family history of leukemia indicates genetic factors in a family that may influence the likelihood of developing leukemia in future generations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Testing">
      <data key="d0">Blood Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood testing is performed to identify genetic changes associated with leukemia, providing critical information for diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AML Mutation Testing">
      <data key="d0">AML Mutation Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">AML mutation testing is a diagnostic process to identify specific mutations within leukemia cells, guiding treatment choices for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Scan of the Brain">
      <data key="d0">CT Scan of the Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A CT scan of the brain is a diagnostic imaging procedure used to detect bleeding and other complications associated with leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI of the Brain">
      <data key="d0">MRI of the Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI of the brain employs magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images, helping to evaluate leukemia-related effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db4e8318949a3fda5683c53985c6af05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocardiogram (ECG)">
      <data key="d0">Electrocardiogram (ECG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An electrocardiogram (ECG) is a test that measures the electrical activity of the heart and can indicate various heart conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Machine">
      <data key="d0">MRI Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An MRI machine is a large medical device used to perform MRI scans, generating detailed inside body images using magnetic fields and radio waves.&lt;SEP&gt;An MRI machine is a medical device that uses strong magnets to create detailed images of the inside of the body, aiding in the diagnosis of various conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeafa486bfd320cdaa7811ad75777912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trace Element">
      <data key="d0">Trace Element</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A trace element is a substance used in PET scans as a tracer to identify metabolic activity in tissues, particularly cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorodeoxyglucose (FDG) is a radiotracer commonly used in PET scans that helps visualize metabolic activity in cancerous tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cardiologist">
      <data key="d0">Cardiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A cardiologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing and treating heart-related conditions, often involved in monitoring patients undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Phases">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Phases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment phases refer to the structured stages involved in managing cancer, including chemotherapy and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A multidisciplinary care team includes various specialists who collaboratively manage and treat cancer patients, ensuring comprehensive care throughout treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A multidisciplinary care team refers to a group of healthcare professionals from various specialties collaborating to provide comprehensive care for patients with AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29c9b8b5fd2cd967c48522af79a9598f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Team (MDT)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is composed of various healthcare professionals who collaborate in planning and implementing a patient's cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;An MDT includes healthcare professionals from different specialties that work together to create a treatment plan for cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is a group of professionals from various fields who collaborate in planning and implementing treatment for AML.&lt;SEP&gt;The Multidisciplinary Team (MDT) is a group of professionals from various specialties collaborating on planning and implementing treatment strategies for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematologist or Hematologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematologist or Hematologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematologist or hematologic oncologist specializes in diagnosing and treating blood diseases and blood cancers, including AML.&lt;SEP&gt;A hematologist or hematologic oncologist specializes in diagnosing and treating blood diseases including various cancers like AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologist or Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">Pathologist or Hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathologist or hematopathologist analyzes biopsy samples to provide essential diagnoses, staging, and information about the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathologist or hematopathologist analyzes tissues from biopsies to provide diagnoses and staging for cancers including AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8333423c91919d7adde114b8286cca7e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Philadelphia Chromosome">
      <data key="d0">Philadelphia Chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">genetic_abnormality</data>
      <data key="d2">The Philadelphia chromosome is a shortened chromosome 22 associated with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) and contains the BCR::ABL1 gene.&lt;SEP&gt;The Philadelphia chromosome is an abnormal chromosome 22 that indicates the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene and is a hallmark of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO)">
      <data key="d0">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin (GO) is a targeted therapy linked to chemotherapy that targets CD33 proteins found on leukemic blasts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d0">Guidelines for Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">document</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide expert recommendations for cancer care based on current research and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hypomethylating Agents">
      <data key="d0">Hypomethylating Agents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hypomethylating agents (HMAs) are drugs that block methyl groups from binding to DNA, allowing silenced genes to be reactivated in leukemia cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CD33">
      <data key="d0">CD33</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">CD33 is a cell surface protein commonly found on leukemic blasts, making it a target for specific cancer therapies like Gemtuzumab Ozogamicin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FLT3-ITD">
      <data key="d0">FLT3-ITD</data>
      <data key="d1">mutation</data>
      <data key="d2">FLT3-ITD is a specific mutation in the FLT3 gene commonly encountered in AML patients, influencing treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Danorubicin and Cytarabine">
      <data key="d0">Danorubicin and Cytarabine</data>
      <data key="d1">combination</data>
      <data key="d2">Daunorubicin and Cytarabine are chemotherapy agents commonly used together to treat certain forms of leukemia, such as AML.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immature Blood Cells">
      <data key="d0">Immature Blood Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">cell_type</data>
      <data key="d2">Immature blood cells, such as myeloblasts and lymphoblasts, can be indicators of leukemia when present in high numbers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06f823f3a91fcbc32c62fac3c6347715</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blast Phase CML (BP-CML)">
      <data key="d0">Blast Phase CML (BP-CML)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blast Phase CML is an advanced stage of chronic myeloid leukemia characterized by an increase in immature progenitor cells, which affects the treatment approach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patients with CML">
      <data key="d0">Patients with CML</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Patients with Chronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) require specialized care and treatment based on the presence of the BCR::ABL1 gene and the Philadelphia chromosome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome 9">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome 9</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome 9 is one of the two chromosomes involved in the translocation that creates the BCR::ABL1 gene, playing a critical role in the development of chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chromosome 22">
      <data key="d0">Chromosome 22</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosome 22 is the other chromosome involved in the translocation that creates the Philadelphia chromosome, a key genetic marker for chronic myeloid leukemia (CML).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7606b44d6a30ad0519bc4c10785de79a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pediatric Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Pediatric Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pediatric cancer care encompasses treatment and support for all pediatric patients diagnosed with cancers, including ALL, often guided by experts in children's cancer centers.&lt;SEP&gt;Pediatric cancer care refers to the specialized treatment and management of cancer in infants, children, and adolescents, offering guidance and support based on expert recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic and Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic and Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic and biomarker testing involves analyzing a patient's genetic material to identify mutations that can inform cancer treatment decisions and potential therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53e3a9418b1d7916e3c8717a397bbb58</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids and Multiagent Chemotherapy">
