    </node>
    <node id="Germline Testing">
      <data key="d0">Germline Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline testing identifies inherited genetic mutations that may increase cancer risk, helping in personalized medicine approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Germline testing involves analyzing inherited mutations in genes that are present in every cell of the body, which can uncover risks for various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ATM">
      <data key="d0">ATM</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">ATM is a gene that plays a role in DNA repair; mutations can elevate cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;ATM is a gene that, when mutated, can contribute to an elevated risk for certain types of cancer, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CHEK2">
      <data key="d0">CHEK2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">CHEK2 is a gene that may increase the risk of various cancers, including prostate cancer, when mutated.&lt;SEP&gt;CHEK2 is a gene that plays a role in cancer risk, particularly in relation to breast and prostate cancers when mutated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MLH1">
      <data key="d0">MLH1</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">MLH1 is a gene associated with DNA repair, and its mutations can increase the risk for colorectal and other cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH2">
      <data key="d0">MSH2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">MSH2 is a gene associated with the DNA mismatch repair process; mutations can lead to increased cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;MSH2 is a gene whose mutations lead to heightened risks of colorectal and uterine cancers, as well as prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSH6">
      <data key="d0">MSH6</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">MSH6 is a gene involved in DNA repair; mutations in MSH6 are linked to a greater risk of certain types of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;MSH6 is a gene linked to Lynch syndrome and can raise the risk for various cancers when mutated, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PALB2">
      <data key="d0">PALB2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">PALB2 is a gene related to breast cancer risk and is also implicated in increased prostate cancer risk when mutated.&lt;SEP&gt;PALB2 is a gene that helps repair damaged DNA; mutations can increase susceptibility to breast and pancreatic cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PMS2">
      <data key="d0">PMS2</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">PMS2 is a gene linked to DNA mismatch repair and its mutations can escalate the likelihood of several types of cancer, including prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Molecular Biomarker Testing">
      <data key="d0">Molecular Biomarker Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Molecular biomarker testing involves evaluating tumor samples to assess cancer risk and treatment options based on genetic markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic mutations are changes in DNA that can increase the risk of developing certain diseases, including various types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-789ec6c06f8c999ac21c89b085b3fdb7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group is a system that categorizes prostate cancer based on Gleason scores, helping to estimate the aggressiveness of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Patterns">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Patterns</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason Patterns indicate the arrangement of cancer cells in a biopsy sample, which are used to determine the Gleason score and Grade Group.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transrectal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Transrectal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transrectal Ultrasound is a medical imaging technique used to measure the size of the prostate, aiding in PSA density calculation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Group">
      <data key="d0">Risk Group</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Risk Group categorizes patients based on their likelihood of disease progression, influencing treatment decisions and prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognosis">
      <data key="d0">Prognosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognosis is a medical term that predicts the likely course and outcome of a disease, guiding patient treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Stage">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor Stage refers to the classification of cancer based on its size and extent of spread, which informs treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Results">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy Results provide critical information about cancer presence and aggressiveness, used for determining Gleason scores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Score">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Score</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason Score is a grading system used to evaluate the aggressiveness of prostate cancer based on cellular patterns found in biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason Score is a grading system that helps determine the aggressiveness of prostate cancer, with scores ranging from 6 to 10.&lt;SEP&gt;The Gleason score is a grading system used to evaluate the aggressiveness of prostate cancer cells, impacting treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSA Density">
      <data key="d0">PSA Density</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PSA Density is a calculation that adjusts PSA levels based on prostate size, improving cancer risk assessment accuracy.&lt;SEP&gt;PSA density is a measurement that may indicate the presence of prostate cancer, based on the amount of PSA in the bloodstream compared to the volume of the prostate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d61a5e7031ea811cc4dcc55a0a0e8fa</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group System">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grade Group System is a classification tool used in prostate cancer diagnosis to assess the aggressiveness of cancer based on the Gleason score.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)">
      <data key="d0">Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BPH is a common condition in men that involves the enlargement of the prostate, which can lead to urinary difficulties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of prostate cancer may include blood in urine, frequent urination, and pain during urination, but many cases are asymptomatic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Score 6">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Score 6</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason Score 6 is associated with Grade Group 1, indicating a low level of risk for prostate cancer aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gleason Score 9 or 10">
      <data key="d0">Gleason Score 9 or 10</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gleason Score 9 or 10 is associated with Grade Group 5, reflecting the highest level of risk for prostate cancer aggressiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 1">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 1 indicates a low risk of cancer progression based on a Gleason score of 6.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 2">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 2 indicates an intermediate risk associated with higher Gleason scores but not as severe as Grade Group 3.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 3">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 3</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 3 indicates a more serious level of risk compared to Grade Group 2, as it involves a higher Gleason score pattern.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 4">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 4</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 4 predicts high risk for cancer progression based on Gleason scoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade Group 5">
      <data key="d0">Grade Group 5</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade Group 5 signifies a very high risk for prostate cancer progression, corresponding to the highest Gleason scores.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Key Points of Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Key Points of Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Key points are essential pieces of information regarding the nature, risks, and symptoms of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c7418fb993bb3daa95d8563909da1de</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Early-Stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide information and recommendations for individuals diagnosed with early-stage prostate cancer, offering guidance on treatment options and management.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Guidelines provide comprehensive information for patients regarding the management and treatment of early-stage prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Abnormalities">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Abnormalities</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic abnormalities refer to mutations or changes in genes that may or may not be inherited and can affect cancer susceptibility or characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diet and Lifestyle">
      <data key="d0">Diet and Lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diet and lifestyle factors, including nutrition and physical activity, can impact the risk and progression of prostate cancer, particularly regarding fat intake and obesity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Access">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Access</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care access refers to the ability of individuals to obtain necessary health services, which can impact early detection and treatment of prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exercise">
      <data key="d0">Exercise</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exercise is a physical activity that can improve health outcomes and has been associated with a lower risk of advanced prostate cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-Stage Metastatic Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-Stage Metastatic Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-stage metastatic prostate cancer is characterized by spread beyond the prostate gland to distant sites in the body, complicating treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced-stage Prostate Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Advanced-stage Prostate Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced-stage prostate cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the prostate gland to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Genetic Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Genetic Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited genetic mutations are specific changes in genes that can increase the risk of developing prostate cancer among individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-697db1653f51e966e79b92eefeb01ec6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptom Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Symptom Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptom Assessment involves evaluating a patient's symptoms to understand the impact on their quality of life and to inform healthcare decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quality of Life">
      <data key="d0">Quality of Life</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Quality of Life encompasses a person's overall well-being and satisfaction, which can be affected by health conditions and their treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of life is a measure of a patient's overall well-being and is a significant consideration in supportive care.&lt;SEP&gt;Quality of life refers to a person’s overall enjoyment of life and well-being, crucial in guiding cancer treatment and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67e9c4dc95841db7d0af39e1267ae71b</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PSMA-PET Scan">
      <data key="d0">PSMA-PET Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PSMA-PET scan is a specialized imaging technique used to detect and monitor prostate cancer by targeting the prostate-specific membrane antigen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)">
      <data key="d0">Transrectal Ultrasound (TRUS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Transrectal ultrasound is a method used to guide prostate biopsies by providing images of the prostate through sound waves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate Cancer Cells">
      <data key="d0">Prostate Cancer Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate cancer cells are abnormal cells that develop in the prostate and are characterized by elevated levels of prostate-specific membrane antigen (PSMA).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA)">
      <data key="d0">Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prostate-Specific Membrane Antigen (PSMA) is a protein that is highly expressed on the surface of prostate cancer cells and is targeted for diagnostic imaging and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiological Tracers">
      <data key="d0">Radiological Tracers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiological tracers are compounds used in imaging tests, specifically designed to attach to PSMA on prostate cancer cells for visualization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Bone Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone metastasis is the spread of prostate cancer to the bones, often detected through specific imaging techniques like bone scans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Probe">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Probe</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound probe is a device used in transrectal ultrasound to visualize the prostate and guide biopsy procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hollow Needle">
      <data key="d0">Hollow Needle</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A hollow needle is a medical instrument used in biopsies to extract tissue samples from the prostate for cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI-TRUS Fusion">
      <data key="d0">MRI-TRUS Fusion</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI-TRUS fusion is a method that combines MRI images with transrectal ultrasound to improve the accuracy of prostate biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-975e540ddee1810d43caf8df4adcf205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Test">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A genetic test analyzes DNA to identify harmful mutations that may increase cancer risk or affect treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Risk">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer risk refers to the likelihood of developing cancer based on genetic, environmental, and lifestyle factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liquid Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Liquid Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A liquid biopsy is a method of detecting cancer cells in the blood through a blood sample.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counseling involves discussing genetic testing options and interpreting results with patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A mutation is a change in a gene's DNA sequence that can lead to a malfunction in gene function, potentially causing disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Gene Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Gene Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary gene testing assesses inherited risk factors for cancer and is done using a sample of blood, urine, or saliva.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Node Metastasis System">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Node Metastasis System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM system is a standardized method used to describe the extent of cancer spread, including tumor size and involvement of lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-965e5a6bad03f2129a98be4373f99176</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor, Node, Metastasis (TNM) System">
      <data key="d0">Tumor, Node, Metastasis (TNM) System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TNM is a system used for staging anal cancer, assessing tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a classification system created by cancer experts to stage tumors based on their size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;The TNM system is a standardized method for describing the extent of cancer that is important for staging and treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Castration">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Castration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical Castration is a procedure that involves removing the testicles to drastically lower testosterone levels, used in prostate cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical castration is a procedure that physically lowers testosterone levels and can be an effective treatment for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical castration, or orchiectomy, is a surgical procedure to remove one or both testicles, reducing testosterone levels in prostate cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT)">
      <data key="d0">Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ADT is a treatment strategy to lower testosterone levels, which can help slow the growth of prostate tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Staging">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor staging is a method used to categorize different tumors based on their size and spread, allowing for standardized comparisons and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Experts">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer experts are medical professionals focused on the study, diagnosis, treatment, and research of cancer and oncological diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging Scans">
      <data key="d0">Imaging Scans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging scans are diagnostic tests used to visualize tumors and assess their size and spread in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Somatic Mutation">
      <data key="d0">Somatic Mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Somatic mutation refers to a change in the DNA sequence that occurs after conception, affecting only the cells derived from the mutated cell and not passed to offspring.&lt;SEP&gt;Somatic mutations are changes in DNA that occur after conception, often found only in tumor cells and not in inherited DNA.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone-Lowering Medication">
      <data key="d0">Hormone-Lowering Medication</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone-lowering medications are used in prostate cancer treatment to reduce testosterone levels, crucial for the growth of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3958b5f7df8a8fc2661705e37dc1e06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Androgen Deprivation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Androgen Deprivation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Androgen Deprivation Therapy (ADT) is a treatment aimed at lowering testosterone levels in cancer patients, especially for prostate cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) is a treatment aimed at lowering testosterone levels in patients, particularly for prostate cancer therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Agonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH Agonists such as goserelin and leuprolide are medications that lower testosterone by acting on the pituitary gland to reduce hormone production.&lt;SEP&gt;LHRH agonists, such as goserelin, leuprolide, and triptorelin, are drugs that help to lower testosterone levels by affecting hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH Antagonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH Antagonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH Antagonists, including degarelix and relugolix, directly block LHRH receptors, preventing testosterone production from testicles.&lt;SEP&gt;LHRH antagonists, like degarelix and relugolix, are medications that block the action of LHRH to decrease testosterone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast Cancer is a specific type of cancer that develops in breast tissue and is often diagnosed through imaging such as mammograms and MRIs.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Cancer is a type of cancer originating in breast tissue, which can become invasive and spread to other body parts.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a disease that begins in the cells of the breast and can vary widely in its type and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a disease that can affect individuals, characterized by the growth of malignant cells in the breast tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a disease where malignant cells form in the tissues of the breast, categorized by tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the cells of the breast and can be invasive, spreading to surrounding tissues or other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that can develop from the epithelial cells in the terminal duct lobular units of the breast, and it can affect anyone regardless of gender.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast cancer is a type of cancer that develops from breast tissue and involves various treatment decisions and staging for prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive Breast Cancer is a form of breast cancer that has spread into surrounding healthy tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer is a subtype of breast cancer that has spread beyond the ducts or lobules into surrounding tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer is a type of cancer that has progressed beyond the ducts or lobules into surrounding tissue, and can spread through lymph or blood to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer refers to cancer that has spread from its original location in the milk ducts or lobules to nearby breast tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue or lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Invasive breast cancer refers to cancer that has spread from the milk ducts or lobules into surrounding breast tissue or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Primary Tumor is the initial tumor from which cancer cells originate before spreading to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary tumor is the initial mass formed by cancer cells, which can subsequently spread to other areas of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary tumor is the initial mass of cancer cells that forms in the breast, which can eventually invade surrounding tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;The primary tumor is the original tumor site where cancer begins before possibly spreading to other locations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terminal Duct Lobular Units">
      <data key="d0">Terminal Duct Lobular Units</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Terminal duct lobular units (TDLUs) are structures in the breast where most breast carcinomas originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary Lymph Nodes are lymph nodes located in the armpit area and are often examined to check for cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located in the armpit area and are commonly examined to check for the spread of breast cancer during staging.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that can be affected by the spread of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that drain lymph fluid from the breast and can be affected by breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are small glands located in the armpit that play a critical role in the immune system and are involved in cancer staging and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization that provides guidelines and resources for cancer patients, assisting them in navigating treatment and care options.&lt;SEP&gt;NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer provides resources and information for cancer patients, including guidelines for nasopharyngeal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The NCCN Patient Guides for Cancer is an organization providing resources and information for cancer patients, including guidelines for treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terminal Duct Lobular Units (TDLUs)">
      <data key="d0">Terminal Duct Lobular Units (TDLUs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Terminal Duct Lobular Units are structures in the breast where cancerous growths commonly originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-53c0ac935ec10ce8a0839db4ad053ad1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer (MBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) indicates that breast cancer has spread beyond the breast to other parts of the body, such as bones and lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer is breast cancer that has progressed beyond the initial site to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic breast cancer refers to breast cancer that has spread beyond the breast and lymph nodes, potentially affecting other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast">
      <data key="d0">Breast</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The breast is a glandular organ located on the chest that produces milk and can be affected by various conditions, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The breast is a glandular organ that consists of milk ducts and lobules and is essential in the context of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The breast is an anatomical structure consisting of milk ducts, lobules, and connective tissues, essential for lactation and implicated in breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The breast is an organ composed of milk ducts and lobules, connected to the nipple and surrounded by connective tissue; it plays a significant role in the context of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Nodes (ALNs)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary Lymph Nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit, playing a crucial role in the spread of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are located in the armpit and are often involved in the spread and staging of breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Axillary lymph nodes are lymph nodes located near the armpit that are critical in the spread of breast cancer through lymphatic drainage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Tests">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment tests are various medical procedures and screenings used to assess the type and stage of cancer, guiding treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Support">
      <data key="d0">Patient Support</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient support includes emotional and logistical assistance provided by friends, family, or peers during the cancer treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Testing">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility testing is an important consideration for patients undergoing treatment for breast cancer, as it may affect reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Cancer Risk Testing">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Cancer Risk Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic cancer risk testing evaluates hereditary risk factors that may influence cancer development and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Health History">
      <data key="d0">Family Health History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Family health history details the medical background of one's relatives, which can influence cancer diagnosis and treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history entails the health records of family members, used to identify genetic risks for conditions like cancer and inform treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history pertains to the health records of relatives that may assist in understanding genetic risks for conditions like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the health conditions and diseases that relatives may have had, which can inform risk assessment for bladder cancer and other health issues.&lt;SEP&gt;Family health history refers to the medical history of family members, which can indicate risks for inherited diseases, including certain types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone Receptor Status">
      <data key="d0">Hormone Receptor Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone receptor status pertains to whether cancer cells respond to hormones, influencing treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status refers to the presence of hormone receptors in breast cancer cells, which influences treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Hormone receptor status refers to the presence of hormone receptors on cancer cells, which can affect growth and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress Screening">
      <data key="d0">Distress Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening assesses the emotional and psychological impact of cancer on patients, facilitating support and resources.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is a process used to assess the emotional and psychological state of cancer patients to identify levels of stress and distress.&lt;SEP&gt;Distress screening is a process used to assess the emotional and psychological state of cancer patients, identifying levels of stress and distress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nipple-Areola Complex (NAC)">
      <data key="d0">Nipple-Areola Complex (NAC)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nipple-Areola Complex is the structure consisting of the nipple and surrounding areola, significant in breast anatomy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Circulation">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Circulation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph circulation refers to the movement of lymph fluid through the body, which is essential for immune response and cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a06038ec84f20e873b9e275a23aea8d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic breast cancer (MBC) refers to breast cancer that has spread beyond the breast and nearby lymph nodes to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for MBC">
      <data key="d0">Tests for MBC</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests for MBC refer to various medical assessments conducted to diagnose metastatic breast cancer and determine its characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal cancer is a common type of breast cancer that originates in the ductal tissues of the breast and can become invasive if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobular Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Lobular Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobular cancer is another type of breast cancer that arises from the lobules, which are the milk-producing glands, and may not always form a detectable lump.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastases">
      <data key="d0">Metastases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastases are secondary tumors that develop in parts of the body far from the original cancer site, indicating the spread of cancer cells through blood or lymph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Blood Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood vessels are the conduits through which cancer cells can travel to spread metastatic disease due to their connection to the circulatory system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Vessels">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Vessels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph vessels are part of the lymphatic system that can also be pathways for cancer cells to spread from the original tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostics">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostics</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostics in cancer care involve various tests and imaging studies to identify the presence and extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c37f547042fc10baabdbfaadf010bce5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS) is a noninvasive type of breast cancer found in the cells lining the breast ducts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Mammogram is a specific type of breast imaging test used to detect breast cancer early through X-ray technology.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is a diagnostic imaging test used to examine breast tissue and screen for breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A mammogram is a radiological examination of the breast, used for screening and diagnosing breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Diagnostic Mammogram uses x-rays to examine specific areas of the breast and is utilized when there are symptoms or abnormalities found in a screening mammogram.&lt;SEP&gt;A diagnostic mammogram is a specialized imaging test for individuals with symptoms of breast cancer or abnormal findings from screening tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobules">
      <data key="d0">Lobules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lobules are glandular structures in the breast that produce milk and can be implicated in breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are glandular structures in the breast where milk is produced, and they can also be affected by breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are structures in the breast that produce milk, essential for breastfeeding.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobules are the milk-producing glands within the breast that play a vital role in lactation and may be involved in breast cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ducts">
      <data key="d0">Ducts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ducts are channels in the breast that transport milk from the lobules to the nipple and can be a site where breast cancer develops.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts are the thin tubes in the breast that carry milk from lobules to the nipple and can be the sites where breast cancer originates.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts are tubes that transport milk from lobules to the nipple in the breast anatomy.&lt;SEP&gt;Ducts are tubular structures in the breast that transport breast milk from the lobules to the nipple, and can also be sites of cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7390896141393e0b4d1c15df77b5c0a7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer – Ductal Carcinoma In Situ">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer – Ductal Carcinoma In Situ</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide comprehensive information and recommendations for patients dealing with ductal carcinoma in situ breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer – Invasive">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer – Invasive</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients include information on invasive breast cancer, helping patients understand treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer – Metastatic">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Breast Cancer – Metastatic</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients focus on metastatic breast cancer, offering guidelines for treatment and management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard 2D Digital Mammogram">
      <data key="d0">Standard 2D Digital Mammogram</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A standard 2D digital mammogram is a traditional imaging technique that uses low-dose x-rays to produce images of the breast.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3D Mammogram (Tomosynthesis)">
      <data key="d0">3D Mammogram (Tomosynthesis)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 3D mammogram, or tomosynthesis, is an advanced imaging technique offering a detailed view of breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A Breast Specialist is an expert focusing on breast health and diseases, playing a crucial role in breast cancer diagnosis and treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast specialist is a healthcare professional trained in diagnosing and treating breast health issues, including breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A breast specialist is a medical professional who focuses on diagnosing and treating breast-related health issues, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Diagnostic Radiologist">
      <data key="d0">Breast Diagnostic Radiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast diagnostic radiologist is responsible for interpreting mammograms and other breast imaging tests, playing a crucial role in diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Breast Diagnostic Radiologists interpret results from imaging tests like mammograms and perform necessary biopsies to diagnose conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gynecologist">
      <data key="d0">Gynecologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A gynecologist is a doctor specializing in the female reproductive system, providing care related to breast and reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;Gynecologists specialize in female reproductive health, diagnosing and treating diseases related to female organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lactation Consultant">
      <data key="d0">Lactation Consultant</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A lactation consultant provides support and guidance on breastfeeding, aiding mothers in establishing effective feeding practices.&lt;SEP&gt;Lactation Consultants specialize in breastfeeding and support new mothers in their breastfeeding journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mammograms">
      <data key="d0">Mammograms</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mammograms are images produced through x-ray technology used to screen for breast cancer and other abnormalities in breast tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DCIS">
      <data key="d0">DCIS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS) is a non-invasive type of breast cancer that starts in the milk ducts and can become invasive if not treated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCP">
      <data key="d0">PCP</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Primary Care Physician (PCP) is the first point of contact in the healthcare system, responsible for general health care and referrals to specialists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Breast Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast tissue comprises ducts, lobules, and supportive connective tissues that can develop into cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nipple-Areola Complex">
      <data key="d0">Nipple-Areola Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The nipple-areola complex refers to the structure of the nipple and the surrounding pigmented area, essential for breastfeeding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dc8d8e5ed4f165f3e56305a7644ba05</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound Technologist">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound Technologist</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ultrasound Technologists operate machines that visualize internal structures, including breast tissue, to assist in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Provider">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced Practice Providers, including nurse practitioners and physician assistants, offer comprehensive care in prevention and diagnosis related to various diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obstetrician">
      <data key="d0">Obstetrician</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Obstetricians are specialists in pregnancy and childbirth, providing care to expectant mothers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Ductal Carcinoma (IDC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive Ductal Carcinoma is a type of breast cancer that begins in the milk ducts and spreads to surrounding breast tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer that often presents without a lump, characterized by symptoms such as swelling, redness, and warmth in the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory Breast Cancer (IBC) is a rare and aggressive type of breast cancer characterized by inflammation, often with no palpable lump.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory Breast Cancer is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer that blocks lymph vessels and causes skin changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer screening involves various tests aimed at detecting cancer at an early stage when treatment is more effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Provider (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Provider (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary Care Providers give a wide range of care, including prevention, treatment, and health screenings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a93aa507dd7d98a02e8ba92d39e3649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgeon is a medical professional specializing in performing operations, particularly in this context related to the diagnosis and treatment of IBC.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a medical professional who performs operations and was involved in the diagnosis and treatment of the inflammatory breast cancer described.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is a medical specialist who performs operations to treat diseases, injuries, or deformities, including cancer surgeries.&lt;SEP&gt;A surgeon is responsible for performing operations to remove cancerous tissues from the thyroid gland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Testing Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing procedures refer to varied medical tests including blood tests, imaging, and biopsies that help diagnose and stage breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Milk">
      <data key="d0">Breast Milk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast milk is a nutrient-rich liquid produced by the mammary glands in females, essential for infant nutrition and health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nurse">
      <data key="d0">Nurse</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse provides medical care and support to patients, including education on breast cancer symptoms and treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Nurses provide patient care and support, playing a vital role in monitoring and assisting patients undergoing treatment for primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Tests">
      <data key="d0">Health Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health tests are necessary to diagnose gliomas and determine the most appropriate treatment plan, including imaging and lab testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Health tests include various medical evaluations, including imaging and blood tests necessary for diagnosing conditions like breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 Status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 Status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 status indicates the overexpression of the HER2 protein in breast cancer cells, which is critical for targeted therapy decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Concerns">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Concerns</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility concerns address the potential impacts of cancer treatment on a patient's ability to conceive and have children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient&quot;\">
      <data key="d0">Patient"\</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The patient is an individual undergoing evaluation, diagnosis, and treatment for inflammatory breast cancer and is typically involved in care planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5846f51c2ca4fae54459ce9c50e3e820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A fertility specialist helps individuals or couples understand and preserve their fertility options, especially before cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a healthcare professional who helps individuals understand fertility preservation options, especially important before cancer treatments that may affect reproductive health.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a healthcare provider who helps individuals understand their reproductive options and preserves fertility, especially before cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical expert who assists individuals in understanding their reproductive options, particularly relevant for those undergoing cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical professional who assists individuals with options for fertility preservation and managing reproductive health, especially in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;A fertility specialist is a medical professional who helps individuals or couples understand and preserve fertility options, especially before cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Exam">
      <data key="d0">Breast Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A breast exam is a medical procedure where a healthcare provider palpates the breast and surrounding areas to check for lumps or abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer diagnosis refers to the identification of cancer in a patient, necessitating various tests and evaluations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Risks">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility risks are potential complications that arise due to cancer treatments like radiation and chemotherapy, affecting the ability to conceive.&lt;SEP&gt;Fertility risks encompass the potential threats to an individual's ability to conceive, especially relating to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sperm Damage">
      <data key="d0">Sperm Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm damage refers to harm that can occur to sperm due to cancer treatments, potentially affecting fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy Test">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pregnancy test is a medical test that determines if an individual is pregnant, often done before starting treatments that could harm a fetus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Test">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pathology test examines tissues to diagnose diseases, including different types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone X-ray">
      <data key="d0">Bone X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Bone X-ray uses low-dose radiation to produce images of bones and is effective in identifying tumors and other bone issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast MRI">
      <data key="d0">Breast MRI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Breast MRI is a specialized MRI used in addition to mammograms to provide detailed views of the breast's interior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Sodium Fluoride PET-CT is a specific variation of a PET-CT scan potentially used in lieu of a bone scan to identify bone conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A sodium fluoride PET-CT is a specific type of PET scan used to replace a bone scan for better imaging of bone-related issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation refers to the emission of energy as electromagnetic waves or as moving subatomic particles, which is an important aspect in imaging techniques and medical diagnostics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Issues">
      <data key="d0">Health Issues</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Health Issues broadly refer to various conditions that may affect an individual's physical or mental condition, leading to the need for diagnostic imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Previous Injuries">
      <data key="d0">Previous Injuries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Previous Injuries refer to past physical injuries which may cause lasting damage that can be assessed using diagnostic imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compression Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Compression Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compression Techniques in diagnostic imaging refer to methods used to minimize movement and enhance image quality, particularly in mammography.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contrast Use">
      <data key="d0">Contrast Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Contrast Use refers to the application of substances that enhance the quality of images obtained through various imaging techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Breast Cancer Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Breast Cancer Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast Cancer Treatment encompasses various medical interventions, potentially incorporating findings from imaging tests to guide therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a2778e34af702d49d5e70867726e14b2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET-CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET-CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET-CT scan combines positron emission tomography (PET) and computed tomography (CT) to provide detailed images for cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FDG PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG PET-CT uses F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose as a radiotracer, helping in detecting cancer in lymph nodes and distant sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FES PET-CT">
      <data key="d0">FES PET-CT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">FES PET-CT is used specifically for identifying estrogen receptor-positive cancers using a radioactive form of estrogen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Node Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Node Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An axillary lymph node biopsy samples lymph nodes in the armpit to check for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain">
      <data key="d0">Brain</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The brain is an organ that can sometimes appear as bright spots on PET scans, indicating normal functioning or possible issues.&lt;SEP&gt;The brain is the central organ affected by primary CNS lymphoma, which can lead to various neurological issues including memory problems and coordination difficulties.&lt;SEP&gt;The brain is the central organ where gliomas develop, affecting its normal functions based on the tumor's location.&lt;SEP&gt;The brain is the organ where gliomas develop, consisting of neural cells and glial cells, which may be impacted by glioma tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;The brain is the primary organ affected by primary CNS lymphoma, and its involvement leads to various neurological and cognitive symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Heart">
      <data key="d0">Heart</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The heart is another organ that may show bright spots on PET scans, representing normal activity or potential health concerns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Cancer Center is a specialized facility focused on diagnosing and treating cancer using various advanced medical procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;A Cancer Center provides specialized treatment and care for individuals diagnosed with cancer, including diagnostic imaging and various treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;A cancer center is a specialized facility that offers diagnostic testing, treatment, and care for patients with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sodium Fluoride">
      <data key="d0">Sodium Fluoride</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sodium fluoride is a radiotracer used in PET scans, particularly for imaging the bones instead of using a traditional bone scan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FDG">
      <data key="d0">FDG</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">FDG (F-18 fluorodeoxyglucose) is a type of radiotracer used in PET scanning, particularly effective in detecting cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen is a hormone that plays a vital role in women's health, influencing the condition of the vaginal lining and overall reproductive system.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen is a hormone whose radioactive form (FES) is used in certain PET scans to identify hormone receptor-positive cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Estrogen is a key hormone in female reproductive health that affects various bodily functions, including the maintenance of vaginal health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Report">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Report</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy report is a document generated by a pathologist that provides analysis and results from tissue samples taken to check for cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fine-Needle Aspiration">
      <data key="d0">Fine-Needle Aspiration</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fine-needle aspiration (FNA) is a biopsy technique that uses a thin needle to extract tissue or fluid samples for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Vacuum-Assisted Core Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Vacuum-assisted core biopsy (VACB) is a method that uses suction to collect tissue samples, providing larger amounts for analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Incisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Incisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An incisional biopsy involves the surgical removal of a portion of tissue rather than the entire lump, used for larger masses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Excisional Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Excisional Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An excisional biopsy is a more invasive procedure where part or all of a lymph node is surgically removed for testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Excisional biopsy involves removing an entire abnormal area for further examination and may be necessary if other types are not feasible.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Skin Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Skin Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A skin biopsy involves sampling an area of inflamed skin, often done to examine changes or concerns related to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Axillary Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Axillary Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Axillary lymph nodes drain lymph fluid from the breast region and are often biopsied to assess for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sentinel Lymph Node">
      <data key="d0">Sentinel Lymph Node</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The sentinel lymph node is the first lymph node that cancer cells are likely to spread to from a primary tumor, often assessed during surgical procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;The sentinel lymph node is the first node where cancer is likely to spread from a primary tumor and is often biopsied during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f9fe20fe42489996573eb1b436128b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mastectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of one or both breasts, commonly performed for breast cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of all or part of the breast and is often performed on patients with breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mastectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of all or part of the breast to treat breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumpectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lumpectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Lumpectomy is a surgery that removes a tumor and some surrounding tissue, often performed when treating breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The estrogen receptor (ER) is a protein that, when stimulated by estrogen, signals cancer cells to survive and proliferate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The progesterone receptor (PR) is a protein involved in the growth signaling of breast cancer cells when bound to progesterone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Endocrine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Endocrine therapy is a treatment designed to block hormones that fuel certain types of breast cancer, often utilized in hormone-sensitive cases.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy is a treatment that blocks hormone receptor signaling in hormone receptor-positive breast cancer to inhibit cancer growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Endocrine therapy reduces the levels of hormones that can contribute to cancer growth, especially in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2">
      <data key="d0">HER2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 is a gene that can influence breast cancer treatment outcomes and is part of biomarker testing.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 is a protein that can promote the growth of cancer cells; in breast cancer, its overproduction is associated with more aggressive disease.&lt;SEP&gt;HER2 is a protein that, when overexpressed, leads to increased growth and division of breast cancer cells, impacting treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunohistochemistry">
      <data key="d0">Immunohistochemistry</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a laboratory process that uses chemical markers to identify hormone receptors in cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory breast cancer is a rare and aggressive form of breast cancer characterized by the obstruction of lymph vessels in the skin of the breast.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is a type of breast cancer that is characterized by the rapid onset of skin changes and can require specific treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory breast cancer is an aggressive form of breast cancer that requires specific treatment approaches, such as surgery and systemic therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Open Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An open biopsy involves a surgical procedure to remove a larger tissue sample for diagnosis when a tumor is accessible.&lt;SEP&gt;Open biopsy is a surgical procedure that involves removing a portion of tissue to examine under a microscope for cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-562e1870b5e20f8648a2e56d960a1148</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA">
      <data key="d0">BRCA</data>
      <data key="d1">gene</data>
      <data key="d2">BRCA refers to a gene that helps prevent tumor growth and repair damaged cells. Mutations in BRCA1 or BRCA2 can increase the risk of several cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TMB-H">
      <data key="d0">TMB-H</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB-H (tumor mutational burden-high) is a classification used in oncology to describe tumors with a high number of mutations, affecting treatment strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;TMB-H stands for tumor mutational burden-high, indicating a higher number of mutations per million base pairs of tumor DNA, which can predict the response to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-1">
      <data key="d0">PD-1</data>
      <data key="d1">protein</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-1 is a programmed death protein that, when expressed on cancer cells, may suppress the immune response against the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IHC">
      <data key="d0">IHC</data>
      <data key="d1">testing_method</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunohistochemistry (IHC) is a staining process used to study cancer or immune cells by adding a chemical marker reviewed under a microscope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Next-Generation Sequencing">
      <data key="d0">Next-Generation Sequencing</data>
      <data key="d1">testing_method</data>
      <data key="d2">Next-generation sequencing (NGS) is a high-throughput method that determines the sequence of DNA in tumor cells to identify gene changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PCR">
      <data key="d0">PCR</data>
      <data key="d1">testing_method</data>
      <data key="d2">Polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a sensitive laboratory process that amplifies DNA, allowing for the detection of abnormal cells among normal ones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genes">
      <data key="d0">Genes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genes are segments of DNA that are key to the biological functions of organisms, influencing everything from physical traits to disease susceptibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proteins">
      <data key="d0">Proteins</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Proteins are large molecules composed of amino acids that perform various functions in the body, including structural, transport, and regulatory roles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immune checkpoint inhibitors are a form of cancer treatment that help the immune system recognize and attack cancer cells by blocking proteins that suppress immune responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor DNA">
      <data key="d0">Tumor DNA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor DNA refers to genetic material obtained from cancer cells that can provide insights into the mutations and characteristics of a tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthcare Provider">
      <data key="d0">Healthcare Provider</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A healthcare provider is a professional or institution that offers medical services, including assessments and recommendations for screenings and treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Healthcare providers are professionals who deliver medical care and guidance to patients, playing a crucial role in cancer diagnosis and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-650982a589c2d527bc13923ccd0569ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG">
      <data key="d0">ECOG</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ECOG refers to the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group performance status scale, which assesses a person's general level of fitness and ability to perform daily tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee on Cancer">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee on Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) created a staging system to determine the extent and staging of cancers, including nasopharyngeal carcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) is responsible for creating a staging system used to evaluate the extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="siblings">
      <data key="d0">siblings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Siblings are family members who share at least one biological or legal parent, and they may provide support in understanding family health history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="half-siblings">
      <data key="d0">half-siblings</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Half-siblings are individuals who share one biological parent, either mother or father, and contribute to family health history assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="aunts and uncles">
      <data key="d0">aunts and uncles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aunts and uncles are relatives that can offer insights into family medical history and hereditary diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nieces and nephews">
      <data key="d0">nieces and nephews</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nieces and nephews are the children of one's siblings and can play a role in the family's health narrative.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="grandparents">
      <data key="d0">grandparents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grandparents are the parents of one's parents and often have significant health history that can impact family health knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="grandchildren">
      <data key="d0">grandchildren</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grandchildren are the offspring of one's children and can be an important aspect of family demographics and health tracking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="distress screening">
      <data key="d0">distress screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress screening is a method used by healthcare teams to assess the psychological impact of a cancer diagnosis on patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer care team">
      <data key="d0">cancer care team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A cancer care team consists of healthcare professionals who provide treatment and support to cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mental distress">
      <data key="d0">mental distress</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental distress is the psychological pain and emotional discomfort a patient may experience during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ECOG PS Scale">
      <data key="d0">ECOG PS Scale</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The ECOG Performance Status Scale is a tool used to measure how a patient's health condition affects their daily living abilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="diagnostic tests">
      <data key="d0">diagnostic tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic tests are procedures to determine the presence of diseases, plan treatment, and monitor health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surgery">
      <data key="d0">surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery is a medical procedure involving the removal or treatment of disease, often relevant in cancer diagnostics or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="estrogen receptor status">
      <data key="d0">estrogen receptor status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Estrogen receptor status involves testing whether cancer cells have receptors for estrogen, impacting treatment options for breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="progesterone receptor status">
      <data key="d0">progesterone receptor status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progesterone receptor status is assessed to determine how cancer responds to hormonal therapies in breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2 status">
      <data key="d0">HER2 status</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2 status indicates the presence of the HER2 protein in breast cancer cells, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="breast cancer staging">
      <data key="d0">breast cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Breast cancer staging involves evaluating the extent of cancer's spread in the body at diagnosis to inform treatment choices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="invasive ductal carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">invasive ductal carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive ductal carcinoma is a common type of breast cancer that begins in the milk ducts and spreads to surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="advanced disease">
      <data key="d0">advanced disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced disease refers to cancer that has progressed to stage 3 or 4, indicating a more severe progression and treatment challenge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc469adbdd7a41816d59f249439bc726</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standard-of-Care Approaches">
      <data key="d0">Standard-of-Care Approaches</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standard-of-care approaches refer to the established treatment protocols recommended for managing breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Estrogen Receptor (ER)">
      <data key="d0">Estrogen Receptor (ER)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The estrogen receptor is a protein within cells that, when activated by estrogen, can promote cell growth, playing a significant role in breast cancer biology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progesterone Receptor (PR)">
      <data key="d0">Progesterone Receptor (PR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The progesterone receptor is involved in breast tissue growth and differentiation; its status influences prognosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Micrometastasis">
      <data key="d0">Micrometastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Micrometastasis indicates the presence of a small number of cancer cells that have spread to nearby lymph nodes but are not detectable through imaging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Size (T)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Size (T)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor size (T) refers to the dimension of the primary tumor in breast cancer and is a critical component in staging, indicating how large the cancer is.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor size is one of the key metrics in cancer staging that describes how large the primary cancerous tumor is.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Status (N)">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Status (N)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node status (N) determines whether breast cancer has spread to nearby lymph nodes, which is a crucial factor in staging and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Lymph node status evaluates whether cancer has spread to regional lymph nodes and how many are involved.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Metastasis (M)">
      <data key="d0">Distant Metastasis (M)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant metastasis (M) describes whether breast cancer has spread to distant organs, which significantly impacts treatment decisions and prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Distant metastasis assesses whether cancer has spread to distant organs like the lungs or liver, an important aspect of staging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prognostic Stage">
      <data key="d0">Prognostic Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Prognostic stage combines the TNM classification with tumor grade and biomarker status to offer a more comprehensive understanding of breast cancer progression.&lt;SEP&gt;Prognostic stage incorporates various assessments including TNM scoring and is crucial for predicting cancer outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Stage (c)">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Stage (c)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical stage (c) is determined through tests and evaluations conducted before cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical stage (c) is the initial rating of breast cancer before any treatment based on examinations and tests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathologic Stage (p)">
      <data key="d0">Pathologic Stage (p)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pathologic stage (p) is defined by examining tissue samples after surgery to provide a more accurate cancer assessment.&lt;SEP&gt;Pathologic stage (p) is determined after surgical intervention, providing details based on tissue samples about the extent of cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drug Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Drug Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Drug therapy encompasses medicinal treatments used for managing breast cancer, including hormone treatments and chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0d388da75afb5ea2d01c97e55cb2b872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IBM Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">IBM Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The IBM Cancer Center is a facility that supports cancer research and treatment, focusing on innovative therapies and patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Grades">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Grades</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor grades describe how abnormal the tumor cells look under a microscope and help in determining the behavior of the cancer, such as its growth rate and spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma In Situ (DCIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DCIS is a non-invasive type of breast cancer that is contained within the ducts and has not spread to surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infraclavicular Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Infraclavicular Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Infraclavicular nodes are lymph nodes located beneath the clavicle, which can be affected by breast cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supraclavicular Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Supraclavicular Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Supraclavicular nodes are lymph nodes located above the clavicle, relevant in the staging and treatment of breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ductal Carcinoma in Situ (DCIS)">
      <data key="d0">Ductal Carcinoma in Situ (DCIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DCIS is a non-invasive breast cancer type that is confined to the ducts and has not spread to surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 breast cancer is characterized by non-invasive cancer, such as DCIS, where the cancer has not spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV Breast Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV Breast Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV breast cancer is metastatic, indicating that cancer has spread to distant organs or tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Pharmacists">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Pharmacists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology pharmacists specialize in medication management for cancer treatments, ensuring safety and efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychologists and Psychiatrists">
      <data key="d0">Psychologists and Psychiatrists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental health professionals who assist cancer patients in managing anxiety and depression related to their diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Mental health professionals who support patients' emotional well-being by managing stress and anxiety associated with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;These mental health professionals assist in managing the emotional impacts of a cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone-strengthening Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Bone-strengthening Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone-strengthening therapy is crucial for cancer patients at risk of bone loss due to certain treatments, such as hormonal therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb41559e96b71d2bb4ea207c806e8d5f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Workers">
      <data key="d0">Social Workers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Social Workers assist patients in accessing mental health and financial resources within treatment centers.&lt;SEP&gt;Social workers are trained professionals who assist individuals in coping with everyday problems, including mental and emotional issues, often working in settings like cancer centers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Social Workers">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Social Workers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Clinical social workers are specialized social workers who diagnose and treat mental, behavioral, and emotional issues, particularly within clinical settings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Research Team">
      <data key="d0">Research Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Research teams are groups of professionals who gather data and conduct studies to advance medical treatments and patient care, particularly in clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The research team is a group of experts responsible for planning and managing clinical trials and ensuring participant safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Wellbeing">
      <data key="d0">Physical Wellbeing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical wellbeing refers to the state of health and fitness of an individual, which is a critical aspect of cancer treatment and care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mental Wellbeing">
      <data key="d0">Mental Wellbeing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mental wellbeing encompasses emotional health and psychological state, which can be affected by cancer diagnoses and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Wellbeing">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Wellbeing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional wellbeing involves the ability to cope with stress, maintain relationships, and meet daily challenges, which is essential for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Therapy Examples">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Therapy Examples</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy examples include various drug treatments such as chemotherapy agents and targeted therapies utilized in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e90bcd28496f1f39a21973656e3e496f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified Radical Mastectomy">
      <data key="d0">Modified Radical Mastectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical mastectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the entire breast along with some lymph nodes and possibly part of the chest wall muscle to treat breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2-targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">HER2-targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2-targeted therapy is a specialized drug therapy for HER2-positive breast cancer that targets the HER2 protein to inhibit cancer cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDK4/6 Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">CDK4/6 Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_drug</data>
      <data key="d2">CDK4/6 inhibitors are a class of drugs that help control hormone receptor-positive breast cancer by inhibiting proteins that promote cell growth and division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">mTOR Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_drug</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR inhibitors are drugs used to restore the efficacy of endocrine therapy in breast cancer when the mTOR protein is overactive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chest Wall Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Chest Wall Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Chest wall radiation is a form of radiation therapy specifically targeting the chest area following breast surgery to eliminate residual cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Infraclavicular Region Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Infraclavicular Region Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Infraclavicular region radiation therapy targets the area below the collarbone to treat lymph nodes affected by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Supraclavicular Area Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Supraclavicular Area Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Supraclavicular area radiation therapy focuses on the treatment of lymph nodes above the collarbone in breast cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node radiation therapy, also known as regional nodal irradiation (RNI), involves irradiating lymph nodes at risk to prevent cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adjuvant Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Adjuvant Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Adjuvant therapy involves treatments given after surgery to reduce the risk of cancer recurrence, typically chemotherapy or radiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Doxorubicin">
      <data key="d0">Doxorubicin</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Doxorubicin is a chemotherapy medication employed in the treatment protocols for various cancers, including bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Doxorubicin is an anthracycline chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat various cancers, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Docetaxel">
      <data key="d0">Docetaxel</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Docetaxel is a chemotherapy drug often used in combinations for treating various types of cancer, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Docetaxel is a taxane chemotherapy drug used in breast cancer treatment to inhibit cancer cell growth and division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gemcitabine">
      <data key="d0">Gemcitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Gemcitabine is an antimetabolite chemotherapy drug used to treat several types of cancers, including breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trastuzumab">
      <data key="d0">Trastuzumab</data>
      <data key="d1">medical_drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Trastuzumab is a monoclonal antibody used to treat HER2-positive breast cancer by targeting the HER2 protein on cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Antioxidants">
      <data key="d0">Antioxidants</data>
      <data key="d1">subcategory</data>
      <data key="d2">Antioxidants are substances that may prevent or delay some types of cell damage, often discussed in the context of dietary supplements during cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Drug Interactions">
      <data key="d0">Drug Interactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Drug interactions occur when the effectiveness of a medication is altered by other drugs or substances, which is critical in cancer therapy management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9040f5949ae0ff36ce88faa1f4b67ead</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ibrance">
      <data key="d0">Ibrance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibrance is a medication used in cancer treatment, particularly for hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kisqali">
      <data key="d0">Kisqali</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kisqali is a medication utilized in oncology to treat hormone receptor-positive breast cancer alongside other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">mTOR inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR inhibitors are a class of drugs that target the mTOR protein to help manage cancer growth, often used in conjunction with endocrine therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Everolimus">
      <data key="d0">Everolimus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Everolimus is an mTOR inhibitor prescribed to treat certain types of breast cancer, specifically in combination with other medications like exemestane.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PARP inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">PARP inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PARP inhibitors are drugs that block the PARP protein to promote cancer cell death, often used for patients with specific genetic mutations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Talazoparib">
      <data key="d0">Talazoparib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Talazoparib is another PARP inhibitor used in treating breast cancer for patients who meet specific genetic criteria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BRCA1/BRCA2 mutation">
      <data key="d0">BRCA1/BRCA2 mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations are genetic alterations that increase the risk of breast and ovarian cancer and determine eligibility for certain treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HER2- breast cancer">
      <data key="d0">HER2- breast cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HER2- breast cancer is a classification of breast cancer that does not overexpress the HER2 protein, affecting treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic therapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic therapy encompasses various treatments that work throughout the body, including chemotherapy, immunotherapy, and endocrine therapy for cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpelisib">
      <data key="d0">Alpelisib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alpelisib is a PIK3CA inhibitor used for treating breast cancer characterized by mutations in the PIK3CA gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hormone therapy">
      <data key="d0">Hormone therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hormone therapy is a treatment approach that modifies hormonal activity to control hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bilateral oophorectomy">
      <data key="d0">Bilateral oophorectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bilateral oophorectomy involves the surgical removal of both ovaries to halt hormone production that fuels certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian ablation">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian ablation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian ablation is a treatment that permanently stops the ovaries from producing hormones, aiding in cancer management.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ovarian suppression">
      <data key="d0">Ovarian suppression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ovarian suppression uses medications to temporarily halt hormone production, utilized in certain cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LHRH agonists">
      <data key="d0">LHRH agonists</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">LHRH agonists are drugs that inhibit gonadotropin production, helping manage hormone levels in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goserelin">
      <data key="d0">Goserelin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Goserelin is a medication that acts as an LHRH agonist, suppressing ovarian hormone production in breast cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Goserelin is an LHRH agonist used in breast cancer treatment, having the effect of reducing hormone levels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leuprolide">
      <data key="d0">Leuprolide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Leuprolide is another LHRH agonist used for lowering hormone levels in hormone-sensitive breast cancer management.&lt;SEP&gt;Leuprolide is another LHRH agonist used for managing hormone-sensitive cancers by lowering hormone production.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aromatase inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Aromatase inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aromatase inhibitors are medications that block the enzyme aromatase, thus reducing estrogen levels and slowing tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anastrozole">
      <data key="d0">Anastrozole</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anastrozole is a non-steroidal aromatase inhibitor employed in treating hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Letrozole">
      <data key="d0">Letrozole</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Letrozole is another aromatase inhibitor used to lower estrogen levels in hormone-sensitive breast cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exemestane">
      <data key="d0">Exemestane</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Exemestane is a steroidal aromatase inhibitor that is utilized to treat hormone-sensitive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Exemestane is a steroidal aromatase inhibitor used to reduce estrogen levels in postmenopausal women with hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tamoxifen">
      <data key="d0">Tamoxifen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) commonly used to treat estrogen receptor-positive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Tamoxifen is a selective estrogen receptor modulator (SERM) used in the treatment of hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fulvestrant">
      <data key="d0">Fulvestrant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fulvestrant is a drug that acts as an estrogen receptor degrader, used in the treatment of hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Fulvestrant is a selective estrogen receptor degrader (SERD) used for treating hormone receptor-positive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elacestrant">
      <data key="d0">Elacestrant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Elacestrant is a SERD that targets estrogen receptors in hormone-sensitive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Megace ES">
      <data key="d0">Megace ES</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Megace ES is a medication used in hormone therapy for certain cancer treatments, including hormone-sensitive breast cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Premenopause">
      <data key="d0">Premenopause</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Premenopause is a stage in a woman's life marked by hormonal changes that can lead to various symptoms, including potential menstrual irregularities.&lt;SEP&gt;Premenopause refers to the phase before menstruation ceases, where hormonal activity significantly impacts breast cancer treatment considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ribociclib">
      <data key="d0">Ribociclib</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ribociclib is a medication used in cancer therapy, particularly for hormone receptor-positive breast cancer, often combined with other drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mTOR">
      <data key="d0">mTOR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">mTOR is a cell protein that is crucial for cell growth and division, and its overactivity can lead to cancer progression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PIK3CA Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">PIK3CA Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">PIK3CA inhibitors target mutations in the PIK3CA gene, which is frequently mutated in breast cancers, to inhibit cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aromatase Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Aromatase Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aromatase inhibitors are a class of drugs designed to lower estrogen levels in the body, thus reducing the growth of hormone-sensitive tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, requiring multidisciplinary medical care for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, often affecting individuals with weakened immune systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45947107c74d8a06e350be57a94a408&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary CNS Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary CNS Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of aggressive cancer that originates in the central nervous system, particularly affecting lymphocytes in the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer originating in the central nervous system, often requiring the use of specialized treatments such as radiation and chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the brain and spinal cord, specifically the central nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that affects the central nervous system, requiring treatment through chemotherapy and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, requiring specialized treatment options including radiation and targeted therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS Lymphoma refers to a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, which can affect the brain and spinal cord.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer primarily affecting the brain and can also involve the spinal cord, often resulting in neurological symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare and aggressive cancer that primarily affects the brain and can spread to the spinal cord, cerebrospinal fluid, and eyes, often causing neurological symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a rare cancer that affects the central nervous system, necessitating specialized medical treatment and care.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of brain cancer that is treated with various therapies, including chemotherapy and stem cell rescue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System (CNS)">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System (CNS)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Central Nervous System (CNS) is composed of the brain and spinal cord, playing a crucial role in processing sensory information and controlling bodily responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV">
      <data key="d0">HIV</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) infection is another risk factor associated with anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is an immunodeficiency virus that significantly increases the risk of developing Primary CNS Lymphoma by compromising the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a virus that is often associated with nasopharyngeal cancer and is tested for during cancer staging.&lt;SEP&gt;The Epstein-Barr Virus is a common virus linked to several illnesses and is one of the risk factors for developing Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organ Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Organ Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organ transplants require immunosuppressive medications, which heighten the risk of infections and diseases, including Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age-related Risk">
      <data key="d0">Age-related Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain age groups, particularly older adults in their 60s or 70s, exhibit a higher incidence of Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amenorrhea">
      <data key="d0">Amenorrhea</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Amenorrhea refers to the absence of menstrual periods, which can occur during premenopause and as a result of certain treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A brain tumor is an abnormal growth of cells within the brain, which can be benign or malignant, impacting normal brain functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)">
      <data key="d0">Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF)</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) is a clear liquid that surrounds and protects the brain and spinal cord, playing a crucial role in the central nervous system's function.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerebrospinal Fluid (CSF) is a liquid surrounding the brain and spinal cord used for diagnosing conditions such as primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerebrospinal fluid is a clear fluid surrounding the brain and spinal cord, involved in the administration of intrathecal chemotherapy for certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Abnormality">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Abnormality</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic abnormalities refer to mutations or changes in the DNA that can lead to uncontrolled cell growth, contributing to the development of cancers like Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Risk Factors">
      <data key="d0">Risk Factors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk factors are attributes or exposures that increase the likelihood of developing a disease, including immunodeficiency and viral infections related to Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunodeficiency Disorder">
      <data key="d0">Immunodeficiency Disorder</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunodeficiency disorders weaken the immune system's ability to fight off infections, increasing the risk of diseases such as Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Disease">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoimmune diseases occur when the immune system mistakenly attacks healthy cells, which can heighten the risk of developing conditions such as Primary CNS Lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dd58424a14d9942181ba4ca3c1ec81c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cerebrospinal Fluid">
      <data key="d0">Cerebrospinal Fluid</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Cerebrospinal fluid is a clear liquid surrounding the brain and spinal cord, and can also be affected by primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cerebrospinal fluid surrounds the brain and spinal cord and can be involved in the spread of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Transplant">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Transplant</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem cell transplant can be a treatment approach for primary CNS lymphoma, particularly for those who have had relapses or aggressive disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell transplant is considered in aggressive cases of primary CNS lymphoma to potentially restore healthy bone marrow after high-dose treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Memory Problems">
      <data key="d0">Memory Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Memory problems are one of the cognitive difficulties experienced by patients with primary CNS lymphoma due to its effect on brain function.&lt;SEP&gt;Memory problems are one of the common neurological symptoms associated with primary CNS lymphoma, affecting cognitive functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weakness">
      <data key="d0">Weakness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Weakness in limbs can be a symptom of primary CNS lymphoma due to its impact on brain function and coordination.&lt;SEP&gt;Weakness, particularly in the arms and legs, is a physical symptom that may occur as a result of primary CNS lymphoma affecting motor functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Incontinence">
      <data key="d0">Incontinence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Incontinence refers to a loss of bladder control which can be a symptom of primary CNS lymphoma due to disruption of central nervous system functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vision Problems">
      <data key="d0">Vision Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vision problems can arise as a side effect of radiation therapy targeting the eyes and affect the patient's visual acuity.&lt;SEP&gt;Vision problems such as blurry or double vision can occur in patients with primary CNS lymphoma, affecting their overall quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Depression">
      <data key="d0">Depression</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Depression can be a psychological symptom in individuals diagnosed with primary CNS lymphoma, influencing their mental health and treatment outlook.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4966a628b12ecec4187796598764cf23</data>
    </node>
    <node id="primary CNS lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">primary CNS lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, characterized by the development of lymphoma in the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary CNS lymphoma is a type of cancer that occurs in the central nervous system, where cancerous cells form in the lymph tissues of the brain or spinal cord.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A stereotactic biopsy is a minimally invasive procedure used to obtain tissue samples from a tumor located in hard-to-reach areas of the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;A stereotactic biopsy is a specialized procedure used for diagnosing brain tumors, involving precise tissue sampling using imaging guidance.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a diagnostic procedure performed by a neurosurgeon to obtain a tissue sample from the brain using a thin, hollow needle.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a medical procedure used to obtain tissue samples from the brain to diagnose primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic biopsy is a precise method used to take a tissue sample from a tumor located in difficult-to-reach areas of the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Nervous System">
      <data key="d0">Central Nervous System</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The central nervous system (CNS) includes the brain and spinal cord, where gliomas can occur, disrupting normal functions.&lt;SEP&gt;The central nervous system consists of the brain and spinal cord, and is the primary area affected by primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Tissue Sample">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Tissue Sample</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor tissue sample is a small piece of tissue removed from a tumor for testing and diagnosis of conditions like primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurological Exam">
      <data key="d0">Neurological Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurological exam involves testing a patient's cognitive abilities, reflexes, and sensory perceptions to assess brain function and identify issues such as gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurological exam is an assessment designed to evaluate the function of the brain and nervous system, often a preliminary step before more advanced diagnostic procedures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gadolinium Contrast Agent">
      <data key="d0">Gadolinium Contrast Agent</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">Gadolinium is a contrast agent used in MRI scans to enhance the visibility of internal structures, aiding in the diagnosis of primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0d4d6b091e033fd01aa721422b68ef5</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stephanie E. Weiss">
      <data key="d0">Stephanie E. Weiss</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Stephanie E. Weiss is a medical doctor specializing in oncology, specifically in the treatment and understanding of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Exam">
      <data key="d0">Eye Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An eye exam is a diagnostic procedure recommended for patients with nasopharyngeal cancer to assess any potential effects on vision.&lt;SEP&gt;An eye exam is a procedure performed to check for signs of primary CNS lymphoma in the eyes, particularly the retina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-052101b592e186cce702983d2acc67e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET)">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PET is an imaging test that detects cancerous cells based on their glucose metabolism, indicating the presence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;PET is an imaging test that utilizes radioactive materials to visualize metabolic processes within the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Positron Emission Tomography (PET) is an imaging technique used to detect cancer cells in the body, including in cases of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Computed Tomography (CT)">
      <data key="d0">Computed Tomography (CT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">CT is an imaging technique that uses X-rays to create detailed pictures of the internal structures of the body, often used in cancer diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Computed Tomography (CT) scan is a diagnostic imaging method that provides detailed images of the body's internal structures, utilized to assess the spread of lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoma is a type of cancer that originates in the lymphatic system, which includes lymph nodes and lymphoid tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Laboratory">
      <data key="d0">Laboratory</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A laboratory is a facility that conducts scientific tests and analyses, such as examining tissue samples for cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancerous Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Cancerous Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancerous tissue refers to abnormal tissue growth that occurs when cells divide uncontrollably, often leading to tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vitreoretinal Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Vitreoretinal Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vitreoretinal lymphoma refers to a specific type of lymphoma that affects the vitreous body and retina of the eye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Slit Lamp">
      <data key="d0">Slit Lamp</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A slit lamp is a specialized microscope used by eye doctors to examine the front and back parts of the eye, especially during an eye exam.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lumbar Puncture Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Lumbar Puncture Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The lumbar puncture procedure, also known as a spinal tap, is a method used to collect cerebrospinal fluid for testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PET/CT Scan">
      <data key="d0">PET/CT Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET/CT scan combines the functional imaging of a PET scan with the structural imaging of a CT scan, useful for detecting cancer spread.&lt;SEP&gt;A PET/CT scan is a diagnostic imaging procedure that helps in identifying the presence of cancer in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;PET/CT scan is a combined imaging technique that provides detailed images of the body for detecting cancer, including lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fba176235a4410ba8723447134c92579&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steroids">
      <data key="d0">Steroids</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment option</data>
      <data key="d2">Steroids are medication options that can help reduce inflammation and may assist in managing symptoms related to cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CNS Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d0">CNS Guidelines for Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines for Patients is a resource that provides essential information and support regarding cancer care, including emotional support for distress.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ultrasound of the Testicles">
      <data key="d0">Ultrasound of the Testicles</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">An ultrasound of the testicles is a medical imaging technique used to examine the testicles for abnormalities, particularly in the context of cancer evaluation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">The HIV blood test is crucial for patients with primary CNS lymphoma, as it helps determine the appropriate specialized care required.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Testing">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">test</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B testing is necessary for patients undergoing immunotherapy treatment to evaluate potential risks associated with past infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Individual Treatment Choices">
      <data key="d0">Individual Treatment Choices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Individual treatment choices refer to the options discussed by patients and care teams based on diagnosis and personal preferences in treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distress During Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">Distress During Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distress during cancer care identifies the emotional challenges faced by patients and caregivers, including anxiety and depression related to cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Corticosteroids">
      <data key="d0">Corticosteroids</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Corticosteroids are anti-inflammatory drugs used in cancer treatment to help reduce swelling and inflammation, thereby alleviating symptoms in patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Corticosteroids are anti-inflammatory medications that may be included in the treatment regimen for patients with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Corticosteroids are drugs used to reduce inflammation and swelling in lymphoma patients and can rapidly alleviate signs and symptoms of the disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stem Cell Rescue">
      <data key="d0">Stem Cell Rescue</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Stem Cell Rescue is a treatment option for restoring blood cell production after chemotherapy, often used in cases of severe side effects or when cancer treatment is aggressive.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure involving the harvesting, freezing, and returning of stem cells to a patient after high-dose chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue is a medical procedure that involves using stem cells to restore bone marrow function after intensive cancer treatments like chemotherapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue, or hematopoietic stem cell transplant, involves harvesting stem cells before high-dose chemotherapy to restore the bone marrow after treatment for primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Stem cell rescue, or hematopoietic stem cell transplant, involves using harvested stem cells to recover the bone marrow after intensive chemotherapy for primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d0f15711706bc171fed17a7fab91b4b6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for protecting public health by ensuring the safety and efficacy of drugs, including those resulting from clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clinical trial">
      <data key="d0">clinical trial</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A clinical trial is a research study designed to evaluate new treatments or drugs in human participants, which is essential for determining their safety and effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that assess new treatments or medications and their effectiveness during various stages.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test how well new medical approaches work against diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Clinical trials are research studies that test new treatments or procedures in patients to determine their efficacy and safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Cancer Institute (NCI)">
      <data key="d0">National Cancer Institute (NCI)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The NCI is the U.S. government’s principal agency for cancer research and training.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is a part of the U.S. federal government that conducts and supports cancer research and provides information about cancer treatment and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;The National Cancer Institute (NCI) is part of the U.S. National Institutes of Health and provides information about cancer research and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER Information Service">
      <data key="d0">CANCER Information Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The CANCER Information Service is a program by the NCI that offers help in finding cancer-related information and resources for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Informed Consent">
      <data key="d0">Informed Consent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Informed consent is a process by which patients are educated about the details and risks of a clinical trial before participation.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is a process by which patients are fully briefed about the risks and benefits of participating in a clinical trial before they agree to take part.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is a process in which patients are informed about the potential risks and benefits of participating in clinical trials before agreeing to participate.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is the process through which patients are educated about the clinical trial they are considering, ensuring they understand the risks, benefits, and expectations before participating.&lt;SEP&gt;Informed consent is the process where participants are fully informed about the trial's purpose, risks, and benefits before agreeing to take part.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clinical Trials Eligibility Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Clinical Trials Eligibility Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eligibility criteria are the set of guidelines that determine who can participate in a clinical trial, based on factors such as age, cancer type, and treatment history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Patients">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Patients</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer patients are individuals diagnosed with cancer, who may explore various treatment options, including standard therapies and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-725b48dc82aad42ba3dc14f4b5207551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Whole-Brain Radiation Therapy (WBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">WBRT is a radiation therapy approach used to treat the entire brain for primary CNS lymphoma, aimed at targeting tumors effectively while preserving healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;WBRT is the most commonly used radiation technique for treating primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy treatment refers to medical treatments involving drugs aimed at killing cancer cells, commonly associated with various side effects and protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Failure">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Failure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney failure is a severe health risk that can occur as a side effect from high doses of chemotherapy drugs like methotrexate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eye Surgeon">
      <data key="d0">Eye Surgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An eye surgeon is a medical professional specialized in surgeries related to the eyes, capable of treating complications from treatments like intraocular chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nausea and Vomiting">
      <data key="d0">Nausea and Vomiting</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nausea and vomiting are common side effects experienced during cancer treatment that can be managed with appropriate medical intervention.&lt;SEP&gt;Nausea and vomiting are common side effects of chemotherapy treatment, potentially controlled with medications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hospital">
      <data key="d0">Hospital</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Hospitals are healthcare institutions providing treatment and care to patients, particularly crucial during chemotherapy for monitoring and supportive care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Cycles">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Cycles</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy cycles refer to the scheduled periods of treatment and recovery, differing in duration with each treatment plan, commonly lasting 14, 21, or 28 days.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fbedfa2d88a7b3852f4c2bd0bb64af0</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rituximab">
      <data key="d0">Rituximab</data>
      <data key="d1">medication</data>
      <data key="d2">Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody used in immunotherapy for treating primary CNS lymphoma by targeting specific proteins on cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is a monoclonal antibody used in immunotherapy specifically for treating primary CNS lymphoma by targeting the CD20 protein on cancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is a targeted therapy used in conjunction with chemotherapy for certain types of lymphoma, including DLBCL, to enhance treatment effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug commonly used in conjunction with chemotherapy for treating CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Rituximab is an immunotherapy drug used to harness the immune system to fight primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Team">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment team consists of healthcare professionals collaborating to provide comprehensive care and support to cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team consists of healthcare professionals involved in managing the care and treatment of patients with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team consists of healthcare professionals who collaborate on patient care and manage treatment strategies for individuals with primary CNS lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team consists of healthcare professionals who collaborate with a patient to determine the most appropriate treatment options for bladder cancer based on individual circumstances.&lt;SEP&gt;The treatment team consists of healthcare professionals who work together to design and administer the best treatment plans for patients with bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Allergic Reactions">
      <data key="d0">Allergic Reactions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Allergic reactions are common side effects associated with various cancer treatments, including immunotherapy and targeted therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fatigue">
      <data key="d0">Fatigue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fatigue is a frequent issue among cancer patients, necessitating supportive care and strategies for alleviation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fatigue is a prevalent symptom experienced by patients undergoing treatment for primary CNS lymphoma, impacting their overall quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monoclonal Antibodies">
      <data key="d0">Monoclonal Antibodies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Monoclonal antibodies are laboratory-engineered molecules designed to bind specifically to cancer cells, playing a key role in targeted therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a65ba958750476cc543741efe815c7c4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multidisciplinary Care">
      <data key="d0">Multidisciplinary Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Multidisciplinary care involves a team of healthcare providers working together to ensure comprehensive treatment for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuro-Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuro-Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuro-oncologist is a medical doctor specializing in the diagnosis and treatment of brain and spinal cord tumors, including primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neurosurgeon">
      <data key="d0">Neurosurgeon</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neurosurgeon is a medical doctor who specializes in performing surgeries on the brain and spinal cord, including biopsies to diagnose conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a medical doctor who specializes in surgery of the brain and nervous system, conducting procedures like biopsies to diagnose conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;A neurosurgeon is a specialist who performs surgeries on the brain and spinal cord, often involved in the treatment of brain cancers like primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ophthalmologist">
      <data key="d0">Ophthalmologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An ophthalmologist is a medical doctor specializing in eye care and may be involved in treating vision problems related to lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nurse Practitioner">
      <data key="d0">Nurse Practitioner</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nurse practitioner is a licensed nurse with advanced training who can diagnose and manage patient care in collaboration with doctors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physician Assistant">
      <data key="d0">Physician Assistant</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A physician assistant is a licensed medical professional who practices medicine under the supervision of a physician and may assist in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Care Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Care Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A palliative care specialist focuses on relieving symptoms and improving the quality of life for patients with serious illnesses, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pain Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Pain Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A pain specialist focuses on managing and alleviating pain associated with cancer and other serious health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Psychologist">
      <data key="d0">Psychologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A psychologist addresses the psychological aspects of living with cancer, providing support and therapy to patients and their families.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritionist">
      <data key="d0">Nutritionist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nutritionist advises on dietary practices to support health and well-being for cancer patients during treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rehabilitation Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Rehabilitation Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A rehabilitation specialist offers therapeutic services to help cancer patients regain function and adapt to changes in health status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e8b25c2957355218780bdfd8b72df45</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemo Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemo Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemotherapy is a common treatment for various cancers, including CNS lymphoma, that uses potent drugs to kill cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon cancer is a common and treatable cancer that arises from the lining of the colon, often starting from polyps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonic Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Colonic Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colonic polyps are non-cancerous growths that can develop in the colon, with some types potentially evolving into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Induction Phase">
      <data key="d0">Induction Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The induction phase of treatment aims to eliminate as many lymphoma cells as possible and is crucial for managing primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consolidation Phase">
      <data key="d0">Consolidation Phase</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The consolidation phase follows the induction phase, focusing on destroying remaining cancer cells and preventing relapse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Dose Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">High-Dose Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-dose chemotherapy, particularly with methotrexate, is the primary treatment for primary CNS lymphoma during the induction phase.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Function">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Function</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney function is a critical consideration in determining suitable high-dose chemotherapy options for patients with lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temozolomide">
      <data key="d0">Temozolomide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Temozolomide is a chemotherapy medication that may be used as an alternative treatment option for CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenalidomide">
      <data key="d0">Lenalidomide</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenalidomide is an immunomodulatory drug considered for treatment options alongside other therapies for CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vincristine">
      <data key="d0">Vincristine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vincristine is a chemotherapy drug used as part of the treatment regimen for primary CNS lymphoma to target cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Procarbazine">
      <data key="d0">Procarbazine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Procarbazine is an oral chemotherapy agent that may be included in the treatment plan for patients with primary CNS lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon">
      <data key="d0">Colon</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The colon is a part of the digestive system and is crucial for the absorption of water and the formation of stool.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon is a part of the digestive system where polyps can grow and where cancers may develop.&lt;SEP&gt;The colon, or large intestine, is the part of the digestive system responsible for processing waste and absorbing water from indigestible food.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rectum">
      <data key="d0">Rectum</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The rectum is part of the digestive system that may also be involved in surgical procedures for cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, holding stool until it is expelled from the body.&lt;SEP&gt;The rectum is the final section of the large intestine, where waste is stored before being excreted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cecum">
      <data key="d0">Cecum</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The cecum is the initial part of the colon, connected to the appendix, and plays a role in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Appendix">
      <data key="d0">Appendix</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The appendix is a small, tube-like structure attached to the cecum, though it has a lesser role in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mismatch Repair Deficiency (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Mismatch Repair Deficiency (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mismatch Repair Deficiency is a biomarker feature of some colon cancers that is significant for testing and treatment planning.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer screening is a preventive measure that includes tests and procedures to detect early signs of colon cancer and polyps.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CTDNA">
      <data key="d0">CTDNA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) is a genetic material released from cancer cells into the bloodstream, used for monitoring treatment response and detecting relapse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery for Non-Metastatic Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Surgery for Non-Metastatic Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery is a primary treatment for non-metastatic colon cancer, which may involve colectomy and aims to remove cancerous tissue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic Colonoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A diagnostic colonoscopy is a specific type of colonoscopy performed to thoroughly examine the colon for possible cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA)">
      <data key="d0">circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">ctDNA refers to testing that looks for small pieces of DNA released by tumor cells into the blood and is associated with early-stage colon cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="colorectal cancer">
      <data key="d0">colorectal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the colon or rectum and emphasizes the importance of early detection and various treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sperm banking">
      <data key="d0">sperm banking</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sperm banking is the process of storing semen for future use through cryopreservation, often discussed in relation to cancer treatment and its effects on fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="egg freezing">
      <data key="d0">egg freezing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Egg freezing, also known as oocyte cryopreservation, involves removing and freezing unfertilized eggs for potential future use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Self-Care">
      <data key="d0">Self-Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Self-care refers to the actions taken to maintain or enhance one's mental and physical health, particularly emphasized during health challenges such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Screening">
      <data key="d0">Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening is the process of testing for diseases like colorectal cancer, involving family history awareness and medical diagnostics to catch issues early.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colectomy">
      <data key="d0">Colectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Colectomy is a surgery that removes part of the colon, often necessary when cancer is found.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of part or all of the colon, often performed to treat colon cancer or other colon-related diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;Colectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part or all of the colon, often performed as a treatment for colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis">
      <data key="d0">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis is another hereditary condition that leads to numerous polyps in the colon, which can become cancerous over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Pedunculated Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pedunculated polyps are mushroom-shaped growths that protrude from the inner lining of the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d0">Sessile Polyps</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sessile polyps are flatter growths on the colon wall that do not have a stalk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A malignant polyp is one in which cancer has just started to grow, requiring careful monitoring or treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="American Joint Committee">
      <data key="d0">American Joint Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The American Joint Committee provides guidelines and staging criteria for various cancers, including colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colon Polyp">
      <data key="d0">Colon Polyp</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Colon polyps are growths on the inner lining of the colon wall, and can be either benign or cancerous, necessitating medical monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond its original site, often requiring more aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bowel Blockage">
      <data key="d0">Bowel Blockage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bowel blockage occurs when a tumor grows large enough to obstruct the flow of stool, necessitating surgical intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stent">
      <data key="d0">Stent</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A stent is a mesh metal tube used to keep the colon open, allowing gas and stool to pass in cases of blockage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anastomotic Leak">
      <data key="d0">Anastomotic Leak</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An anastomotic leak is a possible complication after colectomy where the connection between two healthy ends of the colon leaks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resection">
      <data key="d0">Resection</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Resection refers to the surgical removal of a piece of tissue or organ, often performed for tumor treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical procedure like a resection aims to remove tumors, alleviating symptoms and allowing for further pathological evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Surgical procedures encompass operations aimed at diagnosing or treating medical conditions, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 colon cancer is characterized by abnormal cells on the innermost layer of the colon wall, which may develop into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 colon cancer is characterized by the presence of abnormal cells on the innermost layer of the colon wall, which may develop into cancer if not addressed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 colon cancer involves cancer that has grown into the second or third layer of the colon wall without affecting nearby lymph nodes or distant areas.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 colon cancer is identified when the cancerous growth has penetrated the second or third layer of the colon wall but has not spread to nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2 Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 colon cancer indicates that the cancer has extended into or beyond the fourth layer of the colon wall and is still localized, not affecting nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 colon cancer indicates that the cancer has grown into or beyond the fourth layer of the colon wall but has not spread to lymph nodes or outside the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 colon cancer is when the cancer has expanded from the colon to nearby lymph nodes or includes small tumor deposits around the colon.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 colon cancer is when the cancer has spread from the colon to nearby lymph nodes or includes tumor deposits around the colon.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Colon Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Colon Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 colon cancer denotes cancer that has metastasized to distant organs, such as the liver and lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 colon cancer signifies that the cancer has metastasized to distant organs, commonly the liver and lungs, indicating advanced disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)">
      <data key="d0">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda)</data>
      <data key="d1">medical</data>
      <data key="d2">Pembrolizumab (Keytruda) is another immune checkpoint inhibitor used to improve immune system activity against metastatic anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is an immunotherapy drug that helps enhance the immune system's ability to detect and destroy cancer cells, especially for dMMR/MSI-H cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Pembrolizumab is an immunotherapy drug used to enhance the immune system's ability to target and destroy cancer cells, specifically in dMMR/MSI-H cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nivolumab (Opdivo)">
      <data key="d0">Nivolumab (Opdivo)</data>
      <data key="d1">medical</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab (Opdivo) is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used to treat metastatic anal cancer by enhancing immune response against cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Nivolumab is a checkpoint inhibitor used in immunotherapy for treating certain types of cancer, including cases of advanced colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Nivolumab is an immunotherapy drug that functions as a checkpoint inhibitor, potentially used for treating certain types of cancer, including colon cancer in conjunction with other therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Associated Gene Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Associated Gene Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain genetic mutations can increase the risk of developing colon cancer and affect treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)">
      <data key="d0">5-Fluorouracil (5-FU)</data>
      <data key="d1">medical</data>
      <data key="d2">5-Fluorouracil is a chemotherapy drug commonly used to treat various cancers, including colon cancer, often in combination with leucovorin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leucovorin">
      <data key="d0">Leucovorin</data>
      <data key="d1">medical</data>
      <data key="d2">Leucovorin is a medication used alongside chemotherapy to enhance the efficacy of treatments, particularly with 5-FU in colon cancer therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Leucovorin is used in combination with 5-FU to enhance its effectiveness in treating anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1c1687a8a20b9c451956cf8bba241cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal cancer (NPC) is a type of cancer that originates in the nasopharynx, often related to Epstein-Barr virus and characterized by specific staging methods for treatment decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal cancer is a rare type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, often arising in people from certain regions like Asia and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The treatment regimen refers to the planned duration and methods used in therapeutic approaches for conditions like cancer, typically lasting 3 to 6 months.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surveillance Follow-Up Testing">
      <data key="d0">Surveillance Follow-Up Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surveillance follow-up testing is conducted to monitor for recurrence of cancer after treatment, which can include various tests to detect early signs of cancer growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colonoscopy for Stage 1">
      <data key="d0">Colonoscopy for Stage 1</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A colonoscopy is a procedure recommended one year after surgery for stage 1 cancers to check for any signs of cancer recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma (NPC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a type of cancer that affects the nasopharynx, which connects the back of the nose to the back of the mouth.&lt;SEP&gt;Nasopharyngeal carcinoma is a specific type of cancer affecting the nasopharynx, often with symptoms that resemble less severe conditions, complicating diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Exams">
      <data key="d0">Physical Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical exams are conducted by medical professionals to assess health status and detect signs of cancer or recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Physical exams are part of the recommended surveillance testing for stages 2 and 3 colon cancer patients to check for any abnormalities post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9383db20cd696688798a193862cb5cc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr Virus (EBV) testing may be performed since it is associated with an increased risk of developing NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Comprehensive Cancer Center">
      <data key="d0">Comprehensive Cancer Center</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A comprehensive cancer center specializes in evaluating and treating various types of cancer, including NPC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasopharynx">
      <data key="d0">Nasopharynx</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, connecting the nasal cavity to the pharynx.&lt;SEP&gt;The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose, where nasopharyngeal carcinoma can develop.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Counseling">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Counseling</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility counseling provides guidance for patients concerned about potential impacts of cancer treatment on their reproductive health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Support Person">
      <data key="d0">Support Person</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A support person is a friend, family member, or peer providing assistance and emotional support to a patient during their treatment journey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-711bee9440e11d7c737bc4a49b880910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Epstein-Barr virus">
      <data key="d0">Epstein-Barr virus</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) is a common virus that can cause infectious mononucleosis and is linked to EBV-related cancers, particularly nasopharyngeal cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Epstein-Barr virus is a virus linked to various illnesses, including some types of cancer such as nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nasopharyngeal cancer">
      <data key="d0">nasopharyngeal cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nasopharyngeal cancer is a type of cancer that is often associated with the Epstein-Barr virus and its staging guides treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Throat Cancer">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Throat Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Throat Cancer provides information and resources for patients regarding throat cancer treatment options and considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tumor mutational burden">
      <data key="d0">tumor mutational burden</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tumor mutational burden indicates the count of mutations in a tumor DNA sample, with high levels often treated with immunotherapy drugs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="imaging studies">
      <data key="d0">imaging studies</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging studies are tests that provide images of the inside of the body to help assess cancers and determine treatment plans.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies include various techniques to visualize the inside of the body, aiding in the detection of diseases like cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging studies utilize various technologies to create visual representations of the interior of a body for diagnosing health conditions like cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tumor">
      <data key="d0">tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A tumor is an abnormal mass of tissue that may or may not be cancerous, and its analysis is vital in cancer diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymph node">
      <data key="d0">lymph node</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph nodes are small structures that are part of the lymphatic system and play a key role in the body's immune response; they can be affected by cancer through metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biopsy of metastasis">
      <data key="d0">biopsy of metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A biopsy of metastasis is a medical procedure to collect a sample from metastatic cancer sites to confirm the presence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer">
      <data key="d0">cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrolled growth of abnormal cells in the body, and patients often discuss various aspects of treatment and survivorship.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer is a disease characterized by uncontrolled cell growth, which can spread to other parts of the body, leading to various health complications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44a870810cef881997b741c32ec368fe</data>
    </node>
    <node id="biomarker testing">
      <data key="d0">biomarker testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biomarker testing involves analyzing biological markers to determine eligibility for treatments like targeted therapy or immunotherapy in cancer patients.&lt;SEP&gt;Biomarker testing involves examining genes or proteins in the tumor to identify mutations that influence treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DNA">
      <data key="d0">DNA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) is the molecule that carries genetic instructions in all living organisms, playing a crucial role in cancer genetics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chromosome">
      <data key="d0">chromosome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chromosomes are structures within cells that contain DNA and carry genetic information; humans typically have 23 pairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mutation">
      <data key="d0">mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence, which may lead to cancer if it occurs in genes that regulate cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea7b3f83164304ee4a562046eb0f5033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TNM Staging System">
      <data key="d0">TNM Staging System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The TNM Staging System is a method to categorize the extent of cancer based on tumor size, lymph node involvement, and metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moderately Advanced Disease">
      <data key="d0">Moderately Advanced Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Moderately advanced disease describes cancer that has spread but still may be treatable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Very Advanced Disease">
      <data key="d0">Very Advanced Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Very advanced disease is typically associated with metastasis and significantly worsened prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f05c8ea6f7c7b4ba1fb0ff1c1733980</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma is a type of cancer that begins in cells of various tissues, in this context specifically referencing nasopharyngeal carcinoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical Cancer is a malignant condition affecting the cervix, often requiring treatments such as lymph node dissection and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical Cancer is a type of cancer that affects the cervix, and it may require treatments including lymph node dissection and radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a malignant condition that begins in the cervix and is typically diagnosed through biopsies of cervical tissue to identify cancerous changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor that forms in the cervix, often as a result of long-term infection with HPV and manifests in several forms, including squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor that occurs in the cells of the cervix and is often monitored through regular follow-ups and screenings.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a malignant tumor that occurs in the cells of the cervix, and its management includes physical exams, imaging, and biomarker testing.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer originating in the cervix, with most cases caused by the human papillomavirus (HPV).&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that affects the cervix, often linked to HPV and requiring screening for early detection.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the cervix, diagnosed through methods like cervical biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that can spread to other organs like the liver, lungs, and bones, and requires multiple treatment options if metastatic.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the cells of the cervix, often diagnosed through various biopsies and medical examinations.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the cells of the cervix, often requiring a combination of treatments like chemotherapy and radiation for management.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the cells of the cervix, with various treatment options dependent on the cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the cervix, with specific staging used to determine treatment pathways and outcomes.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer is a type of cancer that primarily affects the cervix and can metastasize to other organs such as the liver, lungs, and bones, leading to complex treatment considerations.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical cancer originates in the cervix and can be diagnosed through various procedures including biopsies and is characterized by abnormal cell growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nasal Cavity">
      <data key="d0">Nasal Cavity</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The nasal cavity is the space behind the nose where air passes before reaching the lungs and can be affected by tumors from the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parapharyngeal Space">
      <data key="d0">Parapharyngeal Space</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The parapharyngeal space is an anatomical area adjacent to the pharynx that can be invaded by tumors originating from the nasopharynx.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dysplasia refers to abnormal cell growth that may precede cancer, notable in cervical changes leading to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamo-Columnar Junction">
      <data key="d0">Squamo-Columnar Junction</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The squamo-columnar junction is an anatomical site in the cervix where squamous and columnar epithelial cells meet and is significant in cervical cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical dysplasia encompasses precancerous changes in the cervix that can lead to cervical cancer if untreated.&lt;SEP&gt;Cervical dysplasia refers to abnormal cellular changes on the cervix, which can be classified as low-grade (LSIL) or high-grade (HSIL) lesions, the latter being a potential precursor to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia (CIN)">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia (CIN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) is a classification of precancerous changes in the cervix, graded based on severity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Smear">
      <data key="d0">Pap Smear</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap smear is a screening procedure used to detect abnormal cells in the cervix, significantly reducing cervical cancer rates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carotid Artery">
      <data key="d0">Carotid Artery</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The carotid artery is a major blood vessel that supplies blood to the brain, neck, and face, located in proximity to the parapharyngeal space.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jugular Vein">
      <data key="d0">Jugular Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The jugular vein carries deoxygenated blood from the head to the heart and is located near the parapharyngeal region, relevant in head and neck cancer assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d249262ff9e7e657de99a30199159cdf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine carcinoma of the cervix (NECC) is a rare and aggressive form of cervical cancer that poses a unique treatment challenge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A cervical biopsy involves removing small samples of tissue from the cervix to diagnose cervical cancer, which is examined by a pathologist.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy involves removing small tissue samples from the cervix to diagnose cervical cancer, typically examined by a pathologist.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy involves taking a sample of cervical tissue to test for dysplasia or cervical cancer, serving as a primary diagnostic tool.&lt;SEP&gt;A cervical biopsy is a medical procedure used to remove small samples of cervical tissue for diagnostic testing, crucial in confirming the presence of cervical cancer or dysplasia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN II">
      <data key="d0">CIN II</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN II (cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade 2) is a classification of moderate dysplasia that reflects abnormal cell growth and is associated with an increased risk of cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CIN III">
      <data key="d0">CIN III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CIN III (cervical intraepithelial neoplasia grade 3) refers to severe dysplasia, indicating a high risk for developing into cervical cancer if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LSIL">
      <data key="d0">LSIL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (LSIL) is a classification for mild cervical dysplasia characterized by abnormal cell changes, often resolving without intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HSIL">
      <data key="d0">HSIL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion (HSIL) is a type of cervical dysplasia indicating moderate to severe cellular changes, considered a potential precursor to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)">
      <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system and can weaken the body's ability to fight off infections, increasing the risk of developing cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that attacks the immune system, making individuals more susceptible to various cancers, including anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;HIV is a virus that targets the immune system, potentially increasing the risk of different cancers including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AIDS">
      <data key="d0">AIDS</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AIDS (Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome) is the advanced stage of HIV infection, leading to severe immune system compromise and higher susceptibility to various cancers, including cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal Cancer is a rare type of cancer that develops in the tissues of the anus and is often treated with chemotherapy and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal Cancer is an uncommon cancer that occurs in the anal canal or the perianal region, primarily starting from squamous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a malignant condition that affects the anal canal, with potential for metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a rare type of cancer that occurs in the tissues of the anus and can require various treatment options including surgery, chemotherapy, and radiation.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the tissues of the anus, with various stages requiring different treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissues of the anus, often associated with HPV infection. Risk factors include compromised immune systems and certain lifestyle choices.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is a type of cancer that forms in the tissues of the anus, often linked to HPV infection and characterized by abnormal cell growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer is another type of cancer that can be caused by persistent HPV infection, similar to cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer refers to cancer that starts in the anal canal or perianal region and can vary in its stages and treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal cancer starts in the anal canal or perianal region, most commonly manifesting as squamous cell carcinoma often linked with HPV infection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vulvar Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Vulvar Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vulvar cancer is a type of cancer that affects the vulva, which can be caused by HPV infection, similar to cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal cancer is another malignancy associated with HPV infection and involves the vaginal tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Vaccine">
      <data key="d0">HPV Vaccine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The HPV vaccine protects against several strains of HPV that are responsible for causing various cancers, including cervical cancer, and is recommended for young individuals to prevent infection.&lt;SEP&gt;The HPV vaccine protects against the highest-risk HPV strains and is most effective when administered before any exposure to the virus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4d036ee5fd77783b9e7ddef3a8477ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cone Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Cone Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A cone biopsy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of a cone-shaped section of the cervix for both diagnostic and treatment purposes in early-stage cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A cone biopsy is a surgical procedure where a cone-shaped section of cervical tissue is removed for evaluation or treatment of cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A cone biopsy is a surgical procedure wherein a cone-shaped piece of cervical tissue is excised for diagnostic or therapeutic purposes, particularly in early-stage cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a procedure that can be both a test and a treatment for cervical cancer, involving the removal of a cone-shaped section of the cervix for analysis or treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure used to remove a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix for diagnosing and treating early-stage cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure used to remove a cone-shaped piece of tissue from the cervix for diagnosing cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health History and Physical Exam">
      <data key="d0">Health History and Physical Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Health history and physical examinations are preliminary steps in planning treatment for cervical cancer, focusing on patient health and symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;This step involves reviewing a patient's past medical history and performing a physical exam to help plan suitable treatment options for cervical cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan">
      <data key="d0">Positron Emission Tomography (PET) Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">A PET scan uses radiotracers to visualize metabolic activity in tissues, helpful in cancer diagnostics by highlighting cancerous areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Test">
      <data key="d0">Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Test</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">An HIV test is conducted to check for the Human Immunodeficiency Virus, which may affect treatment considerations for cervical cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma (NECC) is a rare type of cervical cancer that may require specific imaging tests including MRI of the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma is a specific and rare type of cervical cancer that may require advanced imaging like MRI to check for metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiotracers">
      <data key="d0">Radiotracers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiotracers are small amounts of radioactive materials used in PET scans to emit energy that can be detected by imaging machines.&lt;SEP&gt;Radiotracers are small amounts of radioactive substances injected into patients for imaging purposes, especially used in PET scans to highlight areas of increased metabolic activity, such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oocyte Preservation">
      <data key="d0">Oocyte Preservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oocyte preservation is a fertility preservation technique that involves freezing unfertilized eggs for future use, particularly relevant for patients facing treatments that may affect their fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Oocyte preservation is a method of freezing and storing unfertilized eggs for future use, typically discussed with patients facing fertility challenges due to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrogacy">
      <data key="d0">Surrogacy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrogacy is an arrangement in which a surrogate carries a pregnancy for another individual or couple, often utilizing preserved eggs or embryos.&lt;SEP&gt;Surrogacy is an arrangement where a surrogate carries a pregnancy for another person or couple, often utilizing frozen embryos or eggs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy is a medical procedure that allows a doctor to examine the interior of the bladder using a thin tube with a camera, often used to check for abnormalities in urinary tract symptoms.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the urethra and bladder for signs of cancer, during which tissue samples may be taken for biopsy.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that allows doctors to view the bladder and urethra to detect abnormalities or cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure that involves inserting a viewing device through the urethra to look for cancer and other abnormalities in the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Cystoscopy is a procedure to examine the urethra and bladder for signs of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proctoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Proctoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Proctoscopy is a procedure that enables doctors to visually inspect the rectum and lower colon using a specialized instrument, typically used in assessing symptoms related to bowel health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d0">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">EBRT is a form of radiation therapy that delivers targeted radiation from outside the body to treat thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a form of radiotherapy that uses high-energy beams from outside the body to target and kill cancer cells, typically administered in multiple sessions.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a radiation treatment technique that uses high-energy beams to destroy cancerous cells in the thyroid area.&lt;SEP&gt;EBRT is a type of radiation therapy that uses high-energy beams to target and kill cancer cells, commonly applied in a series of sessions.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a form of cancer treatment where high-energy radiation is directed towards the tumor and nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a method of delivering planned doses of radiation to a patient's tumor from outside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a treatment method that uses high-energy rays to target and kill cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a type of radiation therapy delivered from outside the body directly to the tumor site.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a type of radiation treatment for more aggressive thyroid cancers that cannot be surgically removed.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy is a common cancer treatment that uses focused radiation beams to precisely target and kill cancer cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;External Beam Radiation Therapy is a method of delivering radiation to the cancerous area from outside the body.&lt;SEP&gt;External beam radiation therapy is a treatment that delivers targeted radiation to the area affected by cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-Sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-Sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment refers to various medical interventions aimed at preserving the ability to conceive children after cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Common Side Effects">
      <data key="d0">Common Side Effects</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Common side effects refer to adverse reactions that patients may experience during cancer treatment, impacting their quality of life and overall well-being.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Navigation Services">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Navigation Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer Navigation Services provide assistance and support for patients to help them manage their cancer journey, including treatment options and emotional support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-db3a86fc5d439d9bfb3845e468a2a551</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgery to remove the cervix and surrounding tissues, preserving the uterus for potential future pregnancies.&lt;SEP&gt;Trachelectomy is a fertility-sparing surgical procedure that removes the cervix and some other surrounding tissues while preserving the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Atrophy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal atrophy is a condition characterized by thinning, drying, and inflammation of the vaginal lining that can occur post-menopause or after certain surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e9a7fe3b15f3289198c7114b44890dea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atrophy">
      <data key="d0">Atrophy</data>
      <data key="d1">condition</data>
      <data key="d2">Atrophy is a condition characterized by the thinning, dryness, and inflammation of the vaginal lining, often due to insufficient estrogen levels.&lt;SEP&gt;Atrophy is a condition in which the lining of the vagina becomes thin, dry, and inflamed due to lack of sufficient estrogen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)">
      <data key="d0">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IMRT is a specialized radiation therapy technique that delivers precise radiation doses to tumors while sparing surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is a type of advanced radiation therapy that uses computers and imaging to target tumors more precisely while sparing normal tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;IMRT is an advanced radiation technique specifically designed to target cancer cells while minimizing damage to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced form of EBRT that delivers radiation in varying strengths to minimize exposure to surrounding healthy tissue.&lt;SEP&gt;Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced technique of EBRT that allows precise targeting of tumor tissues to minimize damage to surrounding healthy organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)">
      <data key="d0">Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">SBRT is a specialized type of radiation therapy used for treating limited metastatic lesions, allowing precise targeting of tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a specialized radiation therapy that delivers high doses to tumors with precision, often used for metastatic cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stereotactic Body Radiation Therapy (SBRT) is a specialized type of radiation therapy that delivers high doses of radiation to tumor sites with great precision, often used for metastasized cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)">
      <data key="d0">Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT) is a treatment aimed at alleviating symptoms of early menopause resulting from radiation therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Menopausal Hormone Therapy (MHT) is a treatment to alleviate the symptoms of early menopause induced by cancer therapies, helping to manage hormonal imbalances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic Nodes are lymph nodes situated in the pelvic area that are often the first site where cervical cancer might spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Para-aortic Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Para-aortic Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic Nodes are lymph nodes located in the abdomen and can be removed for testing during cervical cancer surgeries to check for cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Dose-Rate (HDR) Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">High Dose-Rate (HDR) Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">HDR Brachytherapy is a specific technique of brachytherapy that administers radiation in high doses for short periods, often used for cervical cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;HDR Brachytherapy is a type of brachytherapy that administers radiation in short bursts and is commonly used for cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Dose-Rate (LDR) Brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">Low Dose-Rate (LDR) Brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">LDR Brachytherapy delivers radiation continuously over a longer period and may be utilized in specific cases of advanced cervical cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;LDR Brachytherapy involves delivering radiation continuously over a longer period and may be applied in specific cases of advanced cervical cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surrounding Tissue">
      <data key="d0">Surrounding Tissue</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surrounding tissue refers to the healthy body tissues adjacent to a tumor, which need to be protected during radiation treatment to minimize side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Sessions">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Sessions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment sessions are the scheduled appointments during which a patient receives therapy, whether EBRT, chemotherapy, or brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d04ad65c55d0dbbee5061cf3b24030ef</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. Food and Drug Administration">
      <data key="d0">U.S. Food and Drug Administration</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a federal agency responsible for regulating food, drugs, and medical devices, including the approval process for new cancer treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is responsible for the approval of drugs and treatments after they have been tested in clinical trials, ensuring they are safe and effective for public use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eligibility Criteria">
      <data key="d0">Eligibility Criteria</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Eligibility criteria are the specific guidelines that determine who can participate in a clinical trial based on factors such as age, cancer stage, and prior treatment history.&lt;SEP&gt;Eligibility criteria are the specific requirements that determine whether a patient can participate in a clinical trial, based on factors like age and cancer stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Eligibility criteria define the parameters that determine who can enroll in a clinical trial based on specific health and treatment history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec4d44b145ca896ff1216caa90ce5dd</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage cervical cancer refers to cancers confined to the cervix, most often treatable by surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locally Advanced Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cervical cancer indicates cancer that has spread beyond the cervix but remains in the pelvic region, typically treated with chemoradiation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenosquamous Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Adenosquamous Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenosquamous carcinoma is a mixed type of cervical cancer containing both squamous and glandular components.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)">
      <data key="d0">Lymphovascular Space Invasion (LVSI)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphovascular space invasion is a risk factor indicating tumor cells are present in lymph or blood vessels, suggesting a higher likelihood of cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility-sparing Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Fertility-sparing Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility-sparing treatment refers to surgical approaches that allow patients to preserve their ability to have children after early-stage cervical cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Trachelectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Trachelectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure aimed at removing the cervix and surrounding tissue while preserving the uterus for fertility purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical trachelectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of the cervix and surrounding tissue while preserving the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Lymph Node Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph node evaluation is a surgical assessment to check for cancer spread in the lymph nodes near the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic lymph node evaluation is a surgical procedure used to assess whether cancer has spread to the lymph nodes in the pelvic region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reproductive Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Reproductive Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A reproductive endocrinologist is a specialist in fertility who can provide consultation for fertility-sparing treatment options for patients with cervical cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A reproductive endocrinologist is a specialized doctor who helps patients with fertility issues, often consulted for fertility-sparing treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95c76786e9d6dbf06ddf5d55af0cf658</data>
    </node>
    <node id="extrafascial hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">extrafascial hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Extrafascial hysterectomy is a surgical procedure recommended for certain stages of cervical cancer, primarily for removing the uterus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="brachytherapy">
      <data key="d0">brachytherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brachytherapy is a form of radiation therapy used to treat cancer and may be recommended alongside other treatments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EBRT">
      <data key="d0">EBRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT) is a method of delivering radiation to the cancer site and may be used in combination with brachytherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cervical cancer">
      <data key="d0">cervical cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cervical cancer is the type of cancer discussed in the document, with treatment options varying by stage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus and is commonly performed in the treatment of various gynecological conditions, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cone biopsy">
      <data key="d0">cone biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cone biopsy is a surgical procedure in which a cone-shaped piece of tissue is removed from the cervix for examination, often used to detect cancerous changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer stage 1A">
      <data key="d0">cancer stage 1A</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1A refers to an early stage of cervical cancer where it is confined to the cervix and has not spread beyond it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer stage 1B">
      <data key="d0">cancer stage 1B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1B signifies a more advanced form of cervical cancer that may have spread deeper into the cervical tissue but is still localized.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="platinum chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">platinum chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Platinum chemotherapy is a type of chemotherapy that uses platinum-based drugs to treat various cancers, including cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="surgical staging">
      <data key="d0">surgical staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical staging is a process that involves the removal and examination of lymph nodes to determine the extent of cancer spread during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="locally advanced cancer">
      <data key="d0">locally advanced cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locally advanced cancer refers to a stage of cancer where it has grown beyond its original site but has not spread to distant organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="follow-up visits">
      <data key="d0">follow-up visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up visits are regular check-ups that occur after cancer treatment to monitor a patient's recovery and check for signs of recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="pelvic lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">pelvic lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic lymph nodes are a group of lymph nodes located in the pelvic area that are assessed during cervical cancer surgeries for any cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="para-aortic lymph nodes">
      <data key="d0">para-aortic lymph nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Para-aortic lymph nodes are located in the abdomen near the aorta and may be involved in the assessment of cervical cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LVSI">
      <data key="d0">LVSI</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph-Vascular Space Invasion (LVSI) is a pathological finding that indicates that cancer may have spread to the lymphatic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="modified radical hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">modified radical hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the uterus, cervix, and some surrounding tissues to treat cervical cancer while preserving some pelvic structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e247ae57c50c62bded7114986471226e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)">
      <data key="d0">Small Cell Neuroendocrine Carcinoma of the Cervix (NECC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NECC is a rare and aggressive form of cervical cancer that requires specific management strategies and testing protocols.&lt;SEP&gt;NECC is a rare and aggressive form of cervical cancer that requires specific management strategies and testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Visits">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Visits</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up visits are appointments scheduled after cancer treatment to monitor for recurrence and manage ongoing health.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up visits are medical appointments scheduled after treatment to monitor the patient's health and detect any recurrence of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Follow-up visits are recommended for glioma patients to monitor treatment effectiveness and identify any recurrence of the tumor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Imaging involves various techniques like scans to visualize internal body structures and is used for monitoring cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging is a medical procedure used to visualize the internal structures of the body for diagnosis and monitoring, especially after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to medical imaging techniques used to visualize the body for the detection and monitoring of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging refers to medical techniques that provide visual representations of the interior of the body to assist in diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Imaging techniques, including CT and MRI scans, are recommended for surveillance after treatment for recurrence of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Tests">
      <data key="d0">Pap Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pap tests are screening procedures for cervical cancer that help detect abnormal or precancerous cells in the cervix.&lt;SEP&gt;Pap tests are screenings used to detect precancerous and cancerous cells in the cervix, essential for cervical cancer detection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Exploratory Surgery">
      <data key="d0">Exploratory Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Exploratory surgery is a procedure to directly look inside the body to assess the extent of cancer and inform treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Exploratory surgery is performed to directly inspect the extent of cancer and assist in guiding further treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunotherapies">
      <data key="d0">Immunotherapies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapies are treatments designed to harness the body’s immune system to fight cancer, applicable to specific tumor types like PD-L1-positive tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Immunotherapies are treatments that use the body's immune system to fight cancer, often used for specific types of tumors like PD-L1-positive tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Testing">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">PD-L1 testing measures the expression of the PD-L1 protein in tumors to determine if immunotherapy could be effective.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Years 1 to 5 Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d0">Years 1 to 5 Follow-Up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The recommended schedule for follow-up visits after cervical cancer treatment varies, focusing on monitoring for recurrence over the first five years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Exenteration</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgical procedure that involves the removal of multiple organs from the pelvis to treat cancer, especially when there is a local or regional recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Pelvic exenteration is a radical surgical procedure that removes multiple pelvic organs to treat cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostomy Bag">
      <data key="d0">Ostomy Bag</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">An ostomy bag collects waste from the body after surgical diversions have been created for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Hysterectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Hysterectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical hysterectomy is a surgical procedure that involves removing the uterus, cervix, and surrounding tissues to treat gynecological cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversions">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversions</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary diversions are surgical methods created to allow urine to exit the body after the bladder has been removed or altered during surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stoma">
      <data key="d0">Stoma</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">A stoma is a surgical opening created in the wall of the abdomen that allows urine to exit the body through an external pouch or directly.&lt;SEP&gt;A stoma is an opening created surgically on the body for the normal elimination of waste products after a diversion has been performed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer metastasis refers to the spread of cancer cells from one part of the body to another, significantly complicating treatment options and patient prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphoma (MGZL) is a type of lymphoma with overlapping features of primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma and Hodgkin lymphoma, typically treated with chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary Mediastinal B-cell Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary mediastinal B-cell lymphoma (PMBL) is a subtype of non-Hodgkin lymphoma characterized by specific cell types and treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Cervical Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic cervical cancer is an advanced stage of cervical cancer where cancer spreads beyond the cervix to other parts of the body, requiring specialized treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combination Regimen">
      <data key="d0">Combination Regimen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A combination regimen involves using multiple drugs together to enhance treatment efficacy for cancers, such as in metastatic cervical cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PD-L1 Biomarker">
      <data key="d0">PD-L1 Biomarker</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The PD-L1 biomarker is a protein that can influence the effectiveness of certain cancer therapies, including immunotherapies, in patients with specific cancer types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor">
      <data key="d0">Immune Checkpoint Inhibitor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An immune checkpoint inhibitor is a type of cancer treatment that engages the immune system to attack cancer cells, exemplified by drugs like Pembrolizumab.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Combination Therapy ">
      <data key="d0">Combination Therapy </data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Combination therapy refers to a treatment approach that uses more than one therapeutic agent to achieve better outcomes in patients with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MSI-H/dMMR">
      <data key="d0">MSI-H/dMMR</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MSI-H (microsatellite instability-high) and dMMR (deficient mismatch repair) are terms used to describe certain tumor genetic profiles that may influence treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Care">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative care focuses on providing relief from the symptoms and stress of serious illness, aiming to improve the quality of life for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Cancer Care Plans">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Cancer Care Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced cancer care plans involve proactive discussions about treatment options and end-of-life care to ensure patient wishes are honored.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ba71a5181f3814dffb21d3399441bea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid Cancer refers to malignant growths in the thyroid gland, which may necessitate treatments like RAI and surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer encompasses various types of cancer affecting the thyroid gland, including follicular carcinoma, oncocytic carcinoma, and others that may require surgical intervention and follow-up.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a common form of cancer that originates from the thyroid gland, with varying types and risks associated with it.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that develops in the thyroid gland and can lead to various complications, including recurrence and metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the thyroid gland and may require various treatment options, including surgery and medication.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid cancer refers to cancers that arise from the thyroid gland, including differentiated and anaplastic types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Gland">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Gland</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Thyroid Gland is an endocrine organ that produces hormones regulating metabolism, growth, and development.&lt;SEP&gt;The thyroid gland is a butterfly-shaped gland located in the front of the neck, responsible for producing hormones essential for various bodily functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid nodules are abnormal growths within the thyroid gland that are mostly non-cancerous and often go unnoticed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radiation Exposure">
      <data key="d0">Radiation Exposure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radiation exposure, particularly to the head and neck, is a known risk factor for developing thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Older Persons">
      <data key="d0">Older Persons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Older persons are individuals at an advanced age who may exhibit higher health risks and complications such as bone marrow involvement in certain diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Involvement">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Involvement</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow involvement refers to the occurrence of disease within the bone marrow, significantly impacting hematological health and treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Mediastinal Gray Zone Lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediastinal gray zone lymphomas are a group of lymphomas that exhibit characteristics of both Hodgkin and non-Hodgkin lymphomas, complicating treatment protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DLBCL">
      <data key="d0">DLBCL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma (DLBCL) is an aggressive form of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that can present with localized or extranodal disease.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemoimmunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Chemoimmunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chemoimmunotherapy is a combined treatment approach that uses chemotherapy along with immunotherapy agents like rituximab for enhanced efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISRT">
      <data key="d0">ISRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Involved-site radiation therapy (ISRT) is a localized radiation treatment strategy used for patients with localized cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormones">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormones, mainly thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), are critical substances produced by the thyroid gland that regulate various body functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iodine">
      <data key="d0">Iodine</data>
      <data key="d1">geochemical</data>
      <data key="d2">Iodine is an essential mineral required by the thyroid gland for the synthesis of thyroid hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parathyroid Glands">
      <data key="d0">Parathyroid Glands</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Parathyroid glands are small glands located behind the thyroid that regulate calcium levels in the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nodules are abnormal growths on the thyroid that may be producing hormones and need monitoring for cancer risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid nodules are abnormal growths within the thyroid, often benign, that can occasionally indicate underlying health issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Thyroid Nodules">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Thyroid Nodules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms associated with thyroid nodules may include a visible lump, neck pain, voice changes, and difficulty swallowing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Childhood Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Childhood Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Childhood cancer refers to malignant conditions that occur in children, with previous treatments sometimes contributing to later cancer risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cowden Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Cowden Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cowden syndrome is a hereditary condition that increases the risk of numerous cancers, including breast and thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)">
      <data key="d0">Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia (MEN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">MEN syndromes are a group of genetic conditions that lead to the development of various endocrine tumors, including those affecting the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-18b5e32980f3efa0cd012e2abab9b9fc</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormone is crucial for regulating metabolism and is tested for abnormalities in thyroid conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary carcinoma is the most common type of thyroid cancer, categorized as differentiated thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular carcinoma is another type of differentiated thyroid cancer that follows papillary carcinoma in prevalence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Carcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Carcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncocytic carcinomas are a type of differentiated thyroid cancer known for specific cellular characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Medullary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Medullary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Medullary thyroid cancer is a rare but aggressive form that can be inherited, affecting familial health history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Anaplastic Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anaplastic thyroid cancer is the most aggressive type of thyroid cancer, often found in older adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lobectomy">
      <data key="d0">Lobectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lobectomy is a surgical procedure to remove the lobe of the thyroid containing a cancerous nodule.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure involving the removal of one lobe of an organ, in this case, the thyroid gland, often used in the treatment of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of a lobe of the thyroid gland and is a treatment option for certain thyroid cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of a portion of the thyroid gland and is sometimes used in the management of thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Lobectomy is a surgical procedure where a lobe of the thyroid is removed, it is often a part of the treatment protocol for thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Total Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Total Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Total thyroidectomy involves removing the entire thyroid gland, often including lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy involves the complete surgical removal of the thyroid gland, commonly performed to treat thyroid cancer, preventing the risk of recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a complete surgical removal of the thyroid gland, often recommended for more invasive thyroid cancer types.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is a surgical procedure that involves the complete removal of the thyroid gland, often performed to treat thyroid cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Total thyroidectomy is the complete surgical removal of the thyroid gland and is a standard treatment for most thyroid cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone Replacement Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is a treatment for patients who have had their thyroid surgically removed, helping to provide necessary hormones.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid hormone replacement therapy is necessary after total thyroidectomy to maintain hormonal balance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Levothyroxine">
      <data key="d0">Levothyroxine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Levothyroxine is a commonly prescribed medication for thyroid hormone replacement therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a medication that replaces thyroid hormones when levels are insufficient due to thyroid disorders or cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Levothyroxine is a thyroid hormone replacement therapy used to maintain normal TSH levels, which may be prescribed post-surgery depending on individual cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH) is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates the production of thyroid hormones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyperthyroidism">
      <data key="d0">Hyperthyroidism</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hyperthyroidism refers to an excess of thyroid hormones, often causing weight loss, rapid heartbeat, and other symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Uptake Test">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Uptake Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Radioactive Iodine Uptake Test measures how well the thyroid gland takes up iodine, helping diagnose thyroid function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pathology Report">
      <data key="d0">Pathology Report</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pathology Report contains the findings of biopsy tests and is essential for determining the diagnosis and treatment plan.&lt;SEP&gt;A pathology report is a document that provides the results of diagnostic tests and biopsies, essential for treatment planning and patient care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differentiated Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Differentiated Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Differentiated thyroid cancer includes papillary, follicular, and oncocytic cancers, which typically grow slowly and may be treated effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgical treatments for thyroid cancer may include lobectomy and total thyroidectomy, which involve removing parts or all of the thyroid gland.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid Hormone Levels">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid Hormone Levels</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid hormone levels indicate the functioning of the thyroid gland and help determine the presence of hypo- or hyperthyroidism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8a1130b0f6bbe935ceddbafc6ce2740f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Levoxyl">
      <data key="d0">Levoxyl</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Levoxyl is a brand of levothyroxine, a medication used for thyroid hormone replacement therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Levoxyl is a brand of levothyroxine, a synthetic thyroid hormone used for thyroid hormone replacement therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Synthroid">
      <data key="d0">Synthroid</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Synthroid is another brand of levothyroxine medication used interchangeably with Levoxyl for thyroid hormone replacement.&lt;SEP&gt;Synthroid is another brand of levothyroxine medication, commonly prescribed for thyroid hormone replacement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TSH is a hormone that regulates thyroid function, and maintaining its levels is crucial for thyroid cancer therapy.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyroid-stimulating Hormone (TSH) is crucial in regulating thyroid hormone levels and is monitored closely in thyroid cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI) is a treatment method that uses radioactive iodine to target and kill thyroid cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endocrinologist">
      <data key="d0">Endocrinologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An endocrinologist is a healthcare professional specializing in hormone-related disorders, including the treatment of thyroid issues.&lt;SEP&gt;An endocrinologist is a medical doctor specializing in hormone-related conditions, including thyroid issues.&lt;SEP&gt;An endocrinologist is a physician specialized in the treatment of hormonal and gland-related disorders, which can include cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)">
      <data key="d0">Thyrotropin Alfa (Thyrogen)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyrotropin Alfa, branded as Thyrogen, is an injected medication used to increase TSH levels for certain thyroid treatments.&lt;SEP&gt;Thyrotropin Alfa, marketed as Thyrogen, is a medication used to stimulate thyroid hormone uptake in preparation for RAI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Whole-body RAI Scan">
      <data key="d0">Whole-body RAI Scan</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">A Whole-body RAI Scan is a diagnostic tool used post-treatment to detect remaining thyroid tissues or cancer cells in the body.&lt;SEP&gt;A Whole-body RAI scan is an imaging procedure used to detect remaining thyroid tissue and possible cancer spread.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine Therapy (RAI)</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive Iodine Therapy is a method used to destroy thyroid cancer cells by targeting those that absorb iodine.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine therapy (RAI) is a treatment method used after total thyroidectomy to eliminate any remaining cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine specialist administers radioactive materials for imaging or treating conditions like thyroid cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements">
      <data key="d0">Calcium and Vitamin D Supplements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Calcium and Vitamin D supplements may be recommended during thyroid cancer treatment to aid in bone health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1e017cd4fd8af1ebbcb582a93fbfe1ea</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-rays">
      <data key="d0">X-rays</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">X-rays are a form of high-energy radiation used in radiation therapy to precisely target and destroy cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gamma Rays">
      <data key="d0">Gamma Rays</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gamma rays are high-energy electromagnetic waves utilized in radiation therapy for treating various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Protons">
      <data key="d0">Protons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Protons are positively charged particles that are used in radiation therapy to target and damage cancer cells more precisely compared to traditional X-rays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd2be7a2ce722f07b19c80e6e1690a8e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)">
      <data key="d0">U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM)</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The U.S. NLM provides access to a wide range of medical data, including clinical trials and health information.&lt;SEP&gt;The U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM) is a government institution that provides access to health-related information and clinical trial data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CANCER (6237)">
      <data key="d0">CANCER (6237)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CANCER (6237) is a contact service offered by the NCI for individuals seeking information about cancer and clinical trials.&lt;SEP&gt;CANCER (6237) is a helpline provided by the NCI's Cancer Information Service for questions about cancer treatment and trials.&lt;SEP&gt;CANCER (6237) is a national resource provided by the NCI for individuals seeking information about cancer treatment and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nuclear Medicine Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Nuclear Medicine Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A nuclear medicine doctor is a physician who specializes in using radioactive materials for diagnosing and treating disease, including cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transportation Costs">
      <data key="d0">Transportation Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transportation Costs encompass the expenses related to traveling to and from treatment appointments which may not be covered by clinical trial sponsors.&lt;SEP&gt;Transportation costs refer to the expenses incurred for travel related to attending appointments for clinical trials or cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Childcare">
      <data key="d0">Childcare</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Childcare refers to the care provided to children, which may involve additional costs for caregivers during appointments related to clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)">
      <data key="d0">Papillary Thyroid Carcinoma (PTC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Papillary thyroid carcinoma (PTC) is the most common subtype of thyroid cancer, characterized by its generally slow growth and good prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Variant of Papillary Thyroid Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Variant of Papillary Thyroid Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The follicular variant of papillary thyroid cancer is a subtype of PTC that can sometimes show more aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tall-Cell/Columnar Cell Variant">
      <data key="d0">Tall-Cell/Columnar Cell Variant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This variant of papillary thyroid cancer has a higher risk of recurrence and more aggressive features compared to typical PTC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diffused Sclerosing Variant">
      <data key="d0">Diffused Sclerosing Variant</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The diffuse sclerosing variant of papillary thyroid cancer is another atypical form known to be more aggressive and can potentially metastasize.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e74c94f9f2ff33172671e8742338c6d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The thyroid is a gland in the human body that produces hormones; it is central to discussions of thyroid cancer and treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Follicular Thyroid Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follicular thyroid carcinoma is the second most common type of thyroid cancer that may require surgical intervention and follow-up treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncocytic Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Oncocytic Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncocytic carcinoma, formerly known as Hürthle cell carcinoma, is a rare type of thyroid cancer known for its invasive characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="RAI Therapy">
      <data key="d0">RAI Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">RAI therapy, or radioactive iodine therapy, is a treatment option used to kill remaining thyroid cancer cells post-surgery.&lt;SEP&gt;Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy is a treatment often used for thyroid cancer that is designed to destroy cancer cells in the thyroid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Completion Thyroidectomy">
      <data key="d0">Completion Thyroidectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Completion thyroidectomy is a secondary surgical procedure recommended after an initial surgery if concerning features are found during pathologic examination of biopsy samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NIFTP">
      <data key="d0">NIFTP</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NIFTP stands for noninvasive follicular thyroid neoplasm with papillary-like nuclear features, a low-risk tumor treated with surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8a273f96e02707e2e87fc43353ed57f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lenvatinib">
      <data key="d0">Lenvatinib</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Lenvatinib (Lenvima) is a targeted therapy recommended for treating certain aggressive forms of thyroid cancer that cannot be surgically removed.&lt;SEP&gt;Lenvatinib (Lenvima) is a targeted therapy specifically indicated for advanced thyroid cancer when surgical options are not viable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sorafenib">
      <data key="d0">Sorafenib</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Sorafenib (Nexavar) is a targeted therapy used for treating advanced thyroid cancer, particularly those with certain gene mutations.&lt;SEP&gt;Sorafenib (Nexavar) is a targeted therapy utilized in certain cases of advanced thyroid cancer, typically after other treatments fail.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cabozantinib">
      <data key="d0">Cabozantinib</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Cabozantinib (Cabometyx) is a recommended treatment for progressing thyroid cancer after the failure of initial therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Cabozantinib (Cabometyx) is recommended for patients with thyroid cancer that progresses after initial treatment options have been exhausted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-grade carcinoma refers to a type of cancer that is aggressive and characterized by poorly differentiated cells, indicating a more severe prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Follow-up care is a series of ongoing health assessments and tests conducted after cancer treatment to monitor for recurrence and manage any long-term effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neck Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Neck Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Neck ultrasound is a diagnostic imaging technique used post-thyroid surgery to monitor for potential recurrence of cancer in the neck area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Radioactive Iodine (RAI) Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radioactive iodine imaging is a diagnostic procedure used to detect thyroid cancer spread by using radioactive iodine to visualize cancerous tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone (TSH)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TSH is a hormone produced by the pituitary gland that regulates the production of thyroid hormones; its levels are vital in monitoring thyroid health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thyroglobulin (Tg)">
      <data key="d0">Thyroglobulin (Tg)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroglobulin is a protein that can serve as a tumor marker for thyroid cancer, with its levels monitored post-surgery to check for recurrence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)">
      <data key="d0">Anti-Thyroglobulin Antibodies (Tg ab)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anti-thyroglobulin antibodies can interfere with the measurement of thyroglobulin levels, affecting the monitoring of thyroid cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NED (No Evidence of Disease)">
      <data key="d0">NED (No Evidence of Disease)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">NED refers to a state in which no cancer is detected, crucial for determining if further treatment is necessary after initial therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-term Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Long-term Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-term surveillance involves ongoing monitoring strategies to ensure early detection of thyroid cancer recurrence among treated patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging)">
      <data key="d0">MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI is an imaging technique that uses magnetic fields and radio waves to create detailed images of organs and tissues, often employed in cancer monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d383e4798456aa46e381fd2b02f6f96</data>
    </node>
    <node id="targeted therapy">
      <data key="d0">targeted therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted therapy is a treatment option for cancer that focuses on specific molecular targets and may be available for patients with certain types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="immunotherapy">
      <data key="d0">immunotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunotherapy is a type of cancer treatment that helps the immune system fight cancer more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="thyroid cancer">
      <data key="d0">thyroid cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Thyroid cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the thyroid gland and may be monitored or treated depending on its progression and symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone cancer">
      <data key="d0">bone cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer includes cancers that begin in the bone or cartilage, classified as primary if they originate there or secondary if they metastasize from other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is one of the most common types of primary bone cancer, typically starting in the cells that form bone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a type of bone cancer that starts in the bone marrow and is more common in children and young adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that begins in the cartilage cells and is generally slower growing than other types of bone cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="levothyroxine">
      <data key="d0">levothyroxine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Levothyroxine is a medication that is used to manage and maintain appropriate levels of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) in patients with thyroid conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="primary bone cancer">
      <data key="d0">primary bone cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary bone cancer refers to cancers that originate within the bone itself, as opposed to those that metastasize from other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="secondary bone malignancies">
      <data key="d0">secondary bone malignancies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Secondary bone malignancies are cancers that have spread to the bone from other areas of the body, such as breast or lung cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcoma is a type of cancer that arises from connective tissues, which includes bone, cartilage, fat, muscle, and blood vessels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="soft tissue sarcomas">
      <data key="d0">soft tissue sarcomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Soft tissue sarcomas are a category of sarcomas that arise from soft tissues, as opposed to bone-associated sarcomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone tumors">
      <data key="d0">bone tumors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone tumors refer to abnormal growths within the bone, which can be benign (non-cancerous) or malignant (cancerous).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giant cell tumor of bone">
      <data key="d0">Giant cell tumor of bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Giant cell tumor of bone is an overgrowth of cells in the metaphysis of the bone, often extending into the epiphysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cordoma">
      <data key="d0">cordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a rare type of bone cancer that develops from cells of the notochord and typically occurs near the spine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-121b68d5de585c87804c9bce3cdc71eb</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer encompasses various malignancies that originate in the bone tissue, necessitating careful diagnosis and treatment planning.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer is a rare type of cancer that affects the bones, with various subtypes including osteosarcoma and chondrosarcoma, each requiring specific treatment approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignancies that arise from the bone tissues. It often requires staging using systems like TNM to evaluate its extent.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone cancer refers to malignant growths within the bone, which may disrupt natural bone formation and remodeling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chondrosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Chondrosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that arises in cartilage tissue and may present with specific genetic mutations such as IDH1.&lt;SEP&gt;Chondrosarcoma is a type of bone cancer that typically requires surgery for treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chordoma">
      <data key="d0">Chordoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chordoma is a type of bone cancer that can be treated with surgery or radiation therapy, usually affecting the spine and skull base.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ewing Sarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Ewing Sarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Ewing sarcoma is a type of bone cancer often treated with systemic therapy, commonly seen in children and young adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Ewing sarcoma is a type of cancer that commonly affects bone or soft tissue and is characterized by specific genetic mutations, particularly translocations between chromosomes 11 and 22.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giant Cell Tumor of Bone">
      <data key="d0">Giant Cell Tumor of Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Giant cell tumor of bone is generally not considered cancer, but it requires aggressive treatment to prevent bone damage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osteosarcoma">
      <data key="d0">Osteosarcoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Osteosarcoma is the most common type of bone cancer, often treated with a combination of therapies including surgery and chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Cancer Testing">
      <data key="d0">Bone Cancer Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone cancer testing encompasses various procedures used to diagnose and evaluate bone cancer, vital for determining the appropriate treatment course.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertility Impact">
      <data key="d0">Fertility Impact</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fertility impact refers to how cancer and its treatments can affect an individual's ability to conceive children, which is an important consideration for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Examination">
      <data key="d0">Physical Examination</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physical examination includes various assessments performed by healthcare providers to evaluate a patient's overall health and detect any abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Treatment Centers">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Treatment Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer treatment centers specialize in providing advanced care, support, and treatment options for individuals diagnosed with various types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba28a3ce3534c0655a0d08a953d7f038</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)">
      <data key="d0">Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Alkaline Phosphatase (ALP) is an enzyme in the blood whose levels can indicate bone disorders, liver conditions, or pregnancy-related changes.&lt;SEP&gt;Alkaline phosphatase (ALP) is an enzyme level measured in blood tests that can indicate health issues related to bones and liver.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pregnancy Risks">
      <data key="d0">Pregnancy Risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pregnancy risks during cancer treatment refer to potential consequences for those undergoing therapy, particularly regarding fertility and fetal health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="X-ray">
      <data key="d0">X-ray</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An x-ray is a type of imaging test that uses radiation to create images of the body, often used in cancer diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fbf2f6a3c150687a59f814d94f1d150</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone Marrow Test">
      <data key="d0">Bone Marrow Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone marrow tests involve taking samples from bone marrow to check for diseases such as cancer and are often used in conjunction with biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A surgical biopsy involves the removal of a tissue sample through an incision for diagnostic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germline Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Germline Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Germline mutations are genetic alterations inherited from parents that may increase the risk of developing certain cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Translocation">
      <data key="d0">Translocation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Translocation refers to the genetic process where segments of two chromosomes exchange places, which can lead to cancers like Ewing sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-30c0bb66b1a2f93e89f5342ab1077451</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nivolumab">
      <data key="d0">Nivolumab</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Nivolumab, branded as Opdivo, is an immunotherapy treatment option for tumors with high tumor mutational burden (TMB-H).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ipilimumab">
      <data key="d0">Ipilimumab</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Ipilimumab, known as Yervoy, is an immune checkpoint inhibitor used for certain cancer treatments, particularly for TMB-H tumors in specific cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IDH1 mutation">
      <data key="d0">IDH1 mutation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IDH1 mutation refers to a specific genetic alteration that can be targeted with therapies like ivosidenib, particularly in chondrosarcoma patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tumor Mutational Burden High (TMB-H)">
      <data key="d0">Tumor Mutational Burden High (TMB-H)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">TMB-H is defined by the presence of 10 or more mutations per million base pairs of tumor DNA, indicating a potential treatment pathway through specific immunotherapy drugs.&lt;SEP&gt;Tumor mutational burden-high (TMB-H) refers to tumors exhibiting a high number of mutations, which may influence treatment decisions with immunotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)">
      <data key="d0">Deficient Mismatch Repair (dMMR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deficient mismatch repair (dMMR) indicates a lack of effective DNA repair mechanisms that can lead to cancer progression, often characterized by microsatellite instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6b02315423350f3598288e189fbcb8ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diaphysis">
      <data key="d0">Diaphysis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diaphysis refers to the middle region of a long bone, important for understanding bone structure and growth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physis">
      <data key="d0">Physis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Physis is the growth plate in bones made of cartilage, critical for bone growth during childhood and adolescence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvis">
      <data key="d0">Pelvis</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The pelvis is a basin-shaped structure that supports the spinal column and houses vital organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spine">
      <data key="d0">Spine</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The spine or vertebral column consists of a series of bones called vertebrae that protect the spinal cord and support the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grades">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grades</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grades evaluate how abnormal the tumor cells appear, which helps in determining the aggressiveness of the cancer and its potential behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-74f18dd51a0091fc888512458a85b0a1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic liver cancer is a secondary cancer that arises when cancer from another part of the body spreads to the liver.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic liver cancer refers to cancer that has spread to the liver from other parts of the body, often originating from colorectal cancer or other types of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma is the most common type of primary liver cancer, which originates from the liver cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular Carcinoma is the most prevalent type of primary liver cancer, originating from liver cells, and is often aggressive.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gallbladder">
      <data key="d0">Gallbladder</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The gallbladder is a small organ located beneath the liver, responsible for storing bile, which aids in the digestion of fats.&lt;SEP&gt;The gallbladder is a small organ located under the liver that stores bile produced by the liver, playing a role in digestion.&lt;SEP&gt;The gallbladder is a small organ that stores bile produced by the liver, releasing it into the small intestine to aid digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bone">
      <data key="d0">Bone</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Bone is a connective tissue that forms the skeleton of vertebrates, often involved in the spread of metastatic cancer, particularly from liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bone is a hard tissue forming the skeletal structure of vertebrates, and is a common site for distant metastasis in cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lung">
      <data key="d0">Lung</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Lung is an organ involved in respiration that commonly receives metastasis from various cancers, including liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The lung is an organ responsible for respiration that is often a site for metastasis in various cancers, including liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inflammatory Bowel Disease">
      <data key="d0">Inflammatory Bowel Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inflammatory Bowel Disease refers to inflammatory conditions of the gastrointestinal tract, which are significant risk factors for liver cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;Inflammatory bowel disease refers to inflammatory conditions of the gastrointestinal tract, which can increase the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intrahepatic Cholangiocarcinoma is a less common type of primary liver cancer that arises in the bile ducts within the liver.&lt;SEP&gt;Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is a cancer that forms in the bile ducts within the liver, marking the second most common type of primary liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver Cancer is the general term for cancers that originate in the liver, including various types such as hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC).&lt;SEP&gt;Liver cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the liver and can be classified into primary and secondary liver cancers, with various subtypes affecting its diagnosis and treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="T, N, M and G Staging">
      <data key="d0">T, N, M and G Staging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">T, N, M, and G staging is a system used to determine the extent of cancer spread, grading tumor characteristics and lymph node involvement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Portal Vein">
      <data key="d0">Portal Vein</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Portal Vein is a major vein that carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver, influencing liver function and related health conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-84367cc8f7a94b97b4b66a1ca127c15f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatocellular Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Hepatocellular Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is a type of liver cancer that is associated with various risk factors that may increase the likelihood of developing liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is a type of liver cancer that is the primary focus of this document, which discusses its risk factors, screening, and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Hepatocellular Carcinoma (HCC) is the most common type of primary liver cancer and is a focus of the guidelines and screening recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cirrhosis">
      <data key="d0">Cirrhosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease characterized by scarring of the liver, which increases the risk of developing liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a late stage of scarring (fibrosis) of the liver caused by many forms of liver diseases and conditions, such as hepatitis and chronic alcohol abuse, and is a risk factor for liver cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Cirrhosis is a late-stage liver disease characterized by severe scarring and is identified as a significant risk factor for the development of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis B Virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis B Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis B Virus (HBV) is a virus that can cause chronic hepatitis and is a significant risk factor for liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hepatitis C Virus">
      <data key="d0">Hepatitis C Virus</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hepatitis C Virus (HCV) is another virus that can lead to chronic liver disease and increase the likelihood of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alpha-Fetoprotein Test">
      <data key="d0">Alpha-Fetoprotein Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Alpha-Fetoprotein (AFP) test is a blood test used as part of liver cancer screening to detect elevated levels that may indicate liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer Screening</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cancer screening includes regular tests like ultrasound and alpha-fetoprotein tests to detect disease early in at-risk individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Distant Metastasis">
      <data key="d0">Distant Metastasis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Distant metastasis refers to the spread of liver cancer to areas far from the primary tumor, such as the lungs or bones.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Hemochromatosis">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Hemochromatosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic hemochromatosis is an inherited condition that causes excessive iron accumulation in the liver, increasing the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD)">
      <data key="d0">Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Alcoholic Fatty Liver Disease (NAFLD) is a condition characterized by excess fat in the liver not caused by alcohol consumption, which can lead to cirrhosis and liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cancer Screening Program">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cancer Screening Program</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Liver Cancer Screening Program involves regular testing for individuals at risk of liver cancer, aiming to detect the disease as early as possible when treatment is more effective.&lt;SEP&gt;A Liver Cancer Screening Program involves routine testing for high-risk individuals, such as those with cirrhosis or chronic hepatitis B, to detect liver cancer early when treatment is more effective.&lt;SEP&gt;A liver cancer screening program is a health initiative designed to regularly test at-risk individuals for early detection of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asian Male">
      <data key="d0">Asian Male</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Asian males assigned male at birth over 40 years of age are identified as a demographic with heightened risk for developing liver cancer, especially related to hepatitis B.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Asian Female">
      <data key="d0">Asian Female</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Asian females assigned female at birth over 50 years of age are also noted as having an increased risk for liver cancer due to environmental and genetic factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="African/North American Black Persons">
      <data key="d0">African/North American Black Persons</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">African and North American Black individuals with hepatitis B are identified as at a higher risk for liver cancer, prompting special attention in screening.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Cells">
      <data key="d0">Liver Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver cells are the functional cells of the liver, which can be transformed into cancerous cells in the presence of certain risk factors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d4bc8b1b941d18ac455e5f255e1df8c1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Hepatitis B">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Hepatitis B</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Hepatitis B is a viral infection of the liver that increases the likelihood of developing liver cancer, making surveillance critical for those infected.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Hepatitis B is a viral infection that attacks the liver and can lead to chronic liver disease and increase the risk of liver cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5fdcf47f3da06d3e0213112c96eb93d4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach">
      <data key="d0">Stomach</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The stomach is a hollow organ that holds food while it's being mixed with stomach enzymes and acids, forming chyme. It plays a key role in digestion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Intestine">
      <data key="d0">Small Intestine</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The small intestine is the part of the intestine where most of the digestion and absorption of food occurs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chyme">
      <data key="d0">Chyme</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chyme is the semi-fluid mass of partly digested food that is expelled from the stomach into the small intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stomach cancer refers to malignancies that develop in the stomach, often resulting from precancerous changes in the stomach lining over many years.&lt;SEP&gt;Stomach cancer, primarily adenocarcinomas, starts in the cells lining the stomach and can spread to nearby tissues and organs, causing various symptoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mucosa">
      <data key="d0">Mucosa</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Mucosa is the inner layer of the stomach that comes into contact with food, playing a crucial role in digestion and protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adenocarcinomas">
      <data key="d0">Adenocarcinomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adenocarcinomas are a type of cancer that begins in the glandular cells lining the stomach wall and are the most common form of stomach cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Adenocarcinomas are a type of cancer that starts in glandular tissue, including that which lines the stomach.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-01c80b2d18abb91c5be598d33cf7a33a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H. pylori">
      <data key="d0">H. pylori</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">H. pylori is a type of bacteria that can infect the stomach and is associated with increasing the risk of stomach cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic stomach cancer occurs when cancer spreads beyond the stomach to other parts of the body, such as the liver or lymph nodes, often complicating treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic stomach cancer occurs when cancer spreads beyond the stomach to other parts of the body, such as the liver or lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing for Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Testing for Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing for stomach cancer involves various procedures like imaging, biopsy, and specific tests to determine the presence and extent of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Contact Information for Care Team">
      <data key="d0">Contact Information for Care Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Keeping a list of contact information for the medical care team is essential for coordinating cancer treatment and managing health care effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early-stage Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Early-stage Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Early-stage stomach cancer has not spread beyond the mucosa, often being small and localized without impacting lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Locoregional Stomach Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Locoregional Stomach Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Locoregional stomach cancer has invaded deeper layers of the stomach wall and may also involve nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Endoscopy Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Endoscopy Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Endoscopy procedures allow direct visual examination of the stomach and can be used to take biopsies for further testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77e0d4642a374a51f87e7e25b19281f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioma">
      <data key="d0">Glioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A glioma is a type of tumor that develops from glial cells in the brain and spinal cord, characterized by the potential to disrupt normal brain function and often requiring complex treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor that arises from glial cells in the brain, which can vary in grade and type based on histology and biomarkers.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma is a type of tumor that grows in the brain, originating from glial cells and is the most common malignant brain tumor in adults.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioma refers to a group of brain tumors that arise from glial cells and can vary widely in their grades and biological behavior.&lt;SEP&gt;Gliomas are a type of brain tumor that can affect various aspects of neurological function including cognitive abilities and motor skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytomas">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytomas are a common type of glioma tumors that arise from astrocytes, the star-shaped cells that support nerve cells in the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Astrocytomas are a subtype of gliomas that arise from astrocytes, characterized by the star-like appearance of the cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Astrocytomas are a type of glioma that appear star-shaped and originate from astrocytes, glial cells that maintain the brain's chemical environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendrogliomas">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendrogliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendrogliomas are a type of glioma derived from oligodendrocyte cells which protect nerve fibers in the brain.&lt;SEP&gt;Oligodendrogliomas are another type of glioma tumors that develop from oligodendrocytes, the cells that produce myelin in the nervous system.&lt;SEP&gt;Oligodendrogliomas are gliomas formed from oligodendrocytes, which protect the electrical signaling in nerve cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glioblastomas">
      <data key="d0">Glioblastomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glioblastomas are a type of high-grade glioma known for fast growth and are the most common among grade 4 gliomas.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioblastomas are the most aggressive type of glioma, characterized by rapid growth and poor prognosis, arising from both astrocytic and oligodendrocytic lineage.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioblastomas are the most aggressive type of glioma, known for rapid growth and a poor prognosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Glioblastomas are the most destructive and dangerous type of glioma, derived from precursor cells developing into astrocytes and oligodendrocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain tumor treatment typically involves surgery, radiation therapy, and chemotherapy, often in combination for effectiveness.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Gliomas are a type of tumor that originates from glial cells in the brain or spinal cord, and they are the most common malignant brain tumors in adults.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnosis">
      <data key="d0">Diagnosis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnosis of gliomas is made through various tests, including imaging and physical examinations, when symptoms arise.&lt;SEP&gt;Diagnosis refers to the process of identifying an illness, which in the case of bladder cancer, involves tests such as cystoscopy and urinalysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor Centers">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain tumor centers specialize in diagnosing and treating conditions like gliomas, offering services that include targeted treatments and clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da0bfe00946dcdff3a8a6fb3d73ce6e1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor Support">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor Support</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An organization or resource aimed at providing information and assistance to patients and caregivers dealing with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Tumor Support Resources">
      <data key="d0">Brain Tumor Support Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Resources and organizations that provide information and assistance to individuals and caregivers dealing with brain tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Brain Function Problems">
      <data key="d0">Brain Function Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Brain function problems are common symptoms associated with gliomas, leading to cognitive difficulties and physical challenges for patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Patient Caregivers">
      <data key="d0">Patient Caregivers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Patient caregivers play a critical role in supporting glioma patients by providing emotional, physical, and logistical assistance throughout the treatment process.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms of gliomas can include headaches, seizures, fatigue, nausea, memory loss, and difficulty moving or speaking, varying in severity depending on the tumor type.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa5247d6b8c17e58ab315a0d641c7ad3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Parietal Lobe">
      <data key="d0">Parietal Lobe</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The parietal lobe is a region of the brain responsible for sensory perception and language, and tumors in this area can impact these functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Occipital Lobe">
      <data key="d0">Occipital Lobe</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The occipital lobe is a region of the brain that processes visual information, and gliomas affecting this area can cause vision problems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Health Care Centers">
      <data key="d0">Health Care Centers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Health care centers provide diagnostic and treatment services for conditions like gliomas, including imaging and biopsies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49c7ff3a71e74b3f0eb59258541e739e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="MRI Imaging">
      <data key="d0">MRI Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">MRI imaging is a diagnostic tool used to create detailed images of the brain, helping guide neurosurgeons during surgeries.&lt;SEP&gt;MRI imaging is used as a navigational tool during stereotactic biopsies to accurately locate specific areas in the brain for sampling.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CT Imaging">
      <data key="d0">CT Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">technology</data>
      <data key="d2">CT imaging is a diagnostic technique that uses X-rays to produce cross-sectional images of the brain, often used to assist in biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;CT imaging serves as an alternative navigational tool for neurosurgeons during biopsies, providing detailed images of the brain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neuropathologist">
      <data key="d0">Neuropathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A neuropathologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing diseases of the nervous system, particularly by analyzing tissue samples from biopsies.&lt;SEP&gt;A neuropathologist is a specialist who examines tissue samples under a microscope to diagnose brain tumors and classify gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytoma IDH-Mutant Grade 2">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytoma IDH-Mutant Grade 2</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytoma IDH-mutant grade 2 is a specific classification of glioma characterized by its cell type, genetic mutation, and growth rate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Astrocytoma">
      <data key="d0">Astrocytoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Astrocytoma is a type of glioma that originates from astrocytes, a kind of glial cell in the brain, and can have several subtypes based on genetic and histological characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oligodendroglioma">
      <data key="d0">Oligodendroglioma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oligodendroglioma is another type of glioma that originates from oligodendrocytes, and its diagnosis often involves identifying specific molecular markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Malignant Tumor">
      <data key="d0">Malignant Tumor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Malignant tumors are cancerous growths that have the potential to invade and destroy surrounding tissues, which can be identified through biopsy analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biopsy Analysis">
      <data key="d0">Biopsy Analysis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Biopsy analysis is the process of examining collected tissue samples from patients to determine the presence and nature of tumors, including their malignancy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc3446f8ec092af24a460bda94f772f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 1 Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Grade 1 Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 1 gliomas are brain tumors that grow slowly and are typically benign, often treatable with surgery alone, especially in children.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 2 Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Grade 2 Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 2 gliomas consist of cells that look somewhat abnormal, grow slowly, but can invade normal tissue, with a chance of recurrence as higher-grade gliomas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 3 Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Grade 3 Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 3 gliomas have abnormal cells that proliferate quickly and invade nearby tissues, necessitating additional therapy after surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grade 4 Gliomas">
      <data key="d0">Grade 4 Gliomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Grade 4 gliomas, characterized by very abnormal cells, grow and spread rapidly, with glioblastomas being the most common of these tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)">
      <data key="d0">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CLL is a slow-growing type of blood cancer affecting lymphocytes that may not require treatment for years but could eventually need newer therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;CLL is a slow-growing type of blood cancer that affects lymphocytes, often requiring treatment only when symptomatic or advanced.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer that originates in the blood and bone marrow, characterized by changes and mutations leading to the increase of abnormal lymphocytes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B cells">
      <data key="d0">B cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B cells are a type of white blood cell responsible for immune responses, which can become abnormal in conditions like CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Cells">
      <data key="d0">B Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B cells are a type of white blood cell produced in bone marrow that are crucial to the immune system and can become cancerous in CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Misdiagnosis Risks">
      <data key="d0">Misdiagnosis Risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Misdiagnosis risks involve the potential for incorrect identification of cancer types, which can lead to inappropriate treatment pathways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Targeted Treatments">
      <data key="d0">Targeted Treatments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Targeted treatments are therapies that are designed to specifically address certain mutations or characteristics found in gliomas and CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc89a92e9a97781ebca0db50abc34939</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)">
      <data key="d0">Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Small Lymphocytic Lymphoma (SLL) is a cancer closely related to CLL but primarily found in lymph nodes and spleen, considered the same cancer as CLL depending on the location of cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">Hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematopathologist is a medical specialist focused on diagnosing blood-related cancers, including examining blood and bone marrow samples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Test">
      <data key="d0">Blood Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood test is a key procedure used for confirming the diagnosis of CLL, allowing for examination of blood and identification of cancerous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Relatives">
      <data key="d0">Blood Relatives</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood relatives of someone with CLL have a higher chance of developing Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) due to genetic factors that may influence cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richter Transformation">
      <data key="d0">Richter Transformation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Richter transformation is a condition related to CLL where the disease progresses and transforms into a more aggressive form of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a medical condition that is discussed in relation to lymphocyte behavior and lymphoma diagnosis.&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a rare complication of CLL where the disease escalates into a more aggressive form of cancer, such as diffuse large B-cell lymphoma (DLBCL).&lt;SEP&gt;Richter transformation is a specific medical condition that is referenced in relation to lymphocyte behavior and lymphoma diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minimal Residual Disease">
      <data key="d0">Minimal Residual Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Minimal Residual Disease refers to a state in which a patient with CLL can have mostly healthy blood cells, indicating effective treatment even in the absence of a cure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="hematopathologist">
      <data key="d0">hematopathologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A hematopathologist is a medical doctor specializing in diagnosing diseases of the blood, including various types of cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="oncologist">
      <data key="d0">oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An oncologist is a physician who specializes in the treatment of cancer and the management of patients with malignancies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CLL">
      <data key="d0">CLL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer affecting the blood and bone marrow, characterized by the accumulation of monoclonal B lymphocytes.&lt;SEP&gt;Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) is a type of cancer that affects a person's blood and bone marrow, characterized by the overproduction of lymphocytes, which are a type of white blood cell.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="lymph node biopsy">
      <data key="d0">lymph node biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A lymph node biopsy is a surgical procedure performed to remove lymph node tissue for diagnostic testing to confirm diseases such as CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bone marrow biopsy">
      <data key="d0">bone marrow biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A bone marrow biopsy is the procedure where samples of bone marrow are taken for examination to diagnose various blood disorders, including CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="complete blood count (CBC)">
      <data key="d0">complete blood count (CBC)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Complete Blood Count (CBC) is a common blood test used to evaluate overall health and detect a variety of disorders, including anemia and leukemia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="flow cytometry">
      <data key="d0">flow cytometry</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Flow cytometry is a laboratory technique used to analyze the physical and chemical characteristics of cells or particles in a fluid as they pass through a laser.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="B Cell Surface">
      <data key="d0">B Cell Surface</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">B Cell Surface refers to the external structure of B lymphocytes, which are a type of white blood cell involved in the immune response, where specific markers can signify abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microscope">
      <data key="d0">Microscope</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A microscope is an instrument used to examine small samples, such as blood, allowing hematopathologists and oncologists to observe the appearance of cells to aid in diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunophenotype">
      <data key="d0">Immunophenotype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Immunophenotype is the characteristic pattern of proteins expressed on the cell surface, which is critical for diagnosing types of leukemia, including CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monoclonal B Lymphocytes">
      <data key="d0">Monoclonal B Lymphocytes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Monoclonal B Lymphocytes are a type of white blood cell that proliferates abnormally in diseases like CLL, serving as a key indicator for diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Beta-2 Microglobulin">
      <data key="d0">Beta-2 Microglobulin</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">Beta-2 microglobulin is a small protein found on most cells, which when found in high levels in the blood may suggest progression in CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TP53 and IGHV genes">
      <data key="d0">TP53 and IGHV genes</data>
      <data key="d1">biological</data>
      <data key="d2">TP53 and IGHV genes are crucial regulatory genes that control cell health and death, with mutations in these genes affecting cancer treatment outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diagnostic CT">
      <data key="d0">Diagnostic CT</data>
      <data key="d1">medical procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Diagnostic Computed Tomography (CT) is an advanced imaging technique used to visualize internal body structures and assess conditions such as cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Differential Count">
      <data key="d0">Differential Count</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A differential count is a blood test that measures the percentage of different types of white blood cells present, assisting in the diagnosis of various conditions including CLL.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Watch and Wait">
      <data key="d0">Watch and Wait</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The watch and wait approach is a strategy in cancer treatment where immediate intervention is avoided, allowing close monitoring of the patient's condition before treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Consultation with a CLL Specialist">
      <data key="d0">Consultation with a CLL Specialist</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A consultation with a CLL specialist involves a detailed discussion about the patient's condition, treatment options, and specific questions relating to their cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-67015f3916810c4378b2ed269cc27f29</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CBCL">
      <data key="d0">CBCL</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of lymphoma originating from B cells, which can manifest as skin lesions such as nodules or papules.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of lymphoma that originates in B cells and manifests as skin nodules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Lymphomas</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary cutaneous lymphomas (PCL) are a rare subtype of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that occurs in the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary cutaneous lymphomas (PCLs) are a category of non-Hodgkin lymphoma that primarily affects the skin instead of other lymphatic sites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Marginal Zone Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Marginal Zone Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific type of primary cutaneous lymphoma that is characterized by slow-growing nodules.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary cutaneous marginal zone lymphoma (PCMZL) is a type of cutaneous B-cell lymphoma that is typically indolent and affects skin tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Follicle Center Lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Follicle Center Lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A type of primary cutaneous lymphoma that arises from follicle center B cells in the skin.&lt;SEP&gt;Primary cutaneous follicle center lymphoma (PCFCL) is another subtype of cutaneous B-cell lymphoma that arises from follicle center B cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma, Leg Type">
      <data key="d0">Primary Cutaneous Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma, Leg Type</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This form of lymphoma represents aggressive growth patterns in the skin and is noted for its appearance in the leg area.&lt;SEP&gt;This subtype of lymphoma represents an aggressive variant of primary cutaneous lymphoma typically observed in the leg area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-196a80cd0ae47fda3849b101cca91a15</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cutaneous B-cell lymphoma">
      <data key="d0">cutaneous B-cell lymphoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of cancer that affects the skin and involves B-cells, which are a part of the immune system.&lt;SEP&gt;Cutaneous B-cell lymphoma (CBCL) is a type of cancer that primarily affects the skin, arising from B-cells, which are a crucial component of the immune system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tissue tests">
      <data key="d0">tissue tests</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tissue tests involve examining samples from the body to diagnose diseases, including cancers such as cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.&lt;SEP&gt;Tissue tests involve the examination of tissue samples to identify disease conditions, specific to types of cancer including cutaneous B-cell lymphoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="support system">
      <data key="d0">support system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A support system comprises friends, family, or peers who provide emotional and practical assistance during diagnosis and treatment of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A support system refers to a network of friends, family, and peers that provide emotional, logistical, and practical help to individuals undergoing cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="primary care physician (PCP)">
      <data key="d0">primary care physician (PCP)</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care physician (PCP) is a healthcare professional who oversees a patient's overall health and coordinates their medical care.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care physician (PCP) is a medical doctor who provides comprehensive health care and coordinates patient care, especially vital for cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bladder cancer">
      <data key="d0">bladder cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder cancer is a disease characterized by the uncontrollable growth of cells in the bladder lining, leading to tumors and potential metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a form of cancer that occurs when cells in the bladder lining grow uncontrollably, leading to tumors and potential metastasis.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer affecting the bladder, with various treatment options and potential side effects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="urinary tract">
      <data key="d0">urinary tract</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary tract consists of the organs and structures that produce, store, and discharge urine, including the bladder, ureters, and urethra.&lt;SEP&gt;The urinary tract is the organ system that includes the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra, responsible for the creation, transport, and elimination of urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="urothelium">
      <data key="d0">urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urothelium is the innermost lining of the bladder and urinary tract, which is responsible for storing urine and preventing leakage.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelium is the stratified epithelium lining the bladder and parts of the urinary tract; it is where bladder cancer often originates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cancer cells">
      <data key="d0">cancer cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer cells are abnormal cells that grow and divide uncontrollably, leading to tumors and the potential invasion of neighboring tissues.&lt;SEP&gt;Cancer cells are abnormal cells that undergo uncontrolled growth and division, leading to tumors and the potential invasion of surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f9a9eb8b01f29fdf214c10bdffac3203</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer that affects the tissues of the bladder, with treatment depending on the cancer's stage and grade.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer that begins in the bladder's inner lining, often requiring advanced treatments such as intravesical therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder Cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the urothelial cells of the bladder and can vary in stages of severity and invasiveness based on its growth.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a condition characterized by the abnormal growth of cells in the bladder's inner layer, often leading to serious health implications and requiring various treatment options.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a condition that requires a multidisciplinary approach for treatment, often involving various specialists.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that affects the bladder and requires a multidisciplinary team for effective treatment and management.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that occurs in the bladder, often treated with various chemotherapy and immunotherapy approaches.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the bladder and can be associated with various risk factors, including smoking.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that originates in the urothelial cells of the bladder and can be classified into different stages based on its invasion of the muscle layer.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the bladder's inner lining. It often manifests through symptoms like blood in urine and requires various treatment approaches depending on the stage of the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the bladder, primarily affecting the urothelium and can display various symptoms such as blood in urine and urgency to urinate.&lt;SEP&gt;Bladder cancer is a type of cancer that starts in the urothelial cells of the bladder and can grow to invade other layers and spread to other parts of the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelium">
      <data key="d0">Urothelium</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The urothelium is the innermost layer of the bladder and other parts of the urinary tract, where bladder cancer can often originate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle-invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle-invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is characterized by growth through the bladder’s inner urothelial layer into its muscle layer, making it a more serious condition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic bladder cancer occurs when the cancer spreads beyond the bladder to other parts of the body such as lymph nodes or lungs.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic bladder cancer refers to the stage of the disease where cancer cells spread beyond the bladder to other parts of the body, requiring more comprehensive treatment approaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Symptoms include blood in urine, frequent urination, urgency, and painful urination, which can indicate the presence of bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms of bladder cancer may include blood in the urine, painful urination, and frequent urination, often prompting medical consultation for evaluation.&lt;SEP&gt;Symptoms such as hematuria (blood in urine), frequent urination, and painful urination can indicate the presence of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Mutations">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Mutations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer mutations refer to the genetic changes in bladder cells that lead to the development of bladder cancer, often not inherited but occurring sporadically.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Age Factor">
      <data key="d0">Age Factor</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Age is identified as a significant risk factor in developing bladder cancer, where the likelihood increases as a person ages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Layer of the Urinary Tract">
      <data key="d0">Inner Layer of the Urinary Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The inner layer of the urinary tract includes the kidneys, ureters, and urethra, and plays a critical role in the urinary system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureters">
      <data key="d0">Ureters</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The ureters are two tubes that carry urine from the kidneys to the bladder, playing a crucial role in the urinary system.&lt;SEP&gt;Ureters are tubes that transport urine from the kidneys to the bladder, forming a crucial part of the urinary system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood in Urine">
      <data key="d0">Blood in Urine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Blood in urine, or hematuria, is often the first visible symptom of bladder cancer and can vary in appearance.&lt;SEP&gt;Blood in urine, or hematuria, may occur after procedures like TURBT and is an important symptom to monitor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystoscopy and Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Cystoscopy and Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystoscopy and imaging are diagnostic methods used to visually inspect the bladder and perform scans to check for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinalysis and Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Urinalysis and Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinalysis and cytology are tests used to analyze urine samples for abnormalities, aiding in the diagnosis of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Resection and Biopsy">
      <data key="d0">Resection and Biopsy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Resection and biopsy involve surgically removing bladder tissue to analyze it for cancerous changes, playing a key role in diagnosing bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Muscle-invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non-Muscle-invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-muscle-invasive bladder cancer affects only the bladder's inner lining and has not invaded the muscle layer, making it a less serious form of bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aggressive Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Aggressive Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Aggressive cancer is described as having a tendency to grow or spread more rapidly than average, presenting significant treatment challenges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests for Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Tests for Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests for bladder cancer encompass various medical evaluations, including blood tests, imaging, and cystoscopy, that are necessary to diagnose the condition and plan appropriate treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68217ea9e5382d1756c99b5152ea98db</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Primary Care Doctor">
      <data key="d0">Primary Care Doctor</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A primary care doctor manages general health care and coordinates communication between patients and their cancer care team.&lt;SEP&gt;A primary care doctor oversees overall patient health and facilitates communication among specialists involved in cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urologic Oncologist">
      <data key="d0">Urologic Oncologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A urologic oncologist focuses on surgical interventions and therapies for cancers affecting the urinary tract, including bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;A urologic oncologist performs surgeries and therapies specific to cancers in the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anesthesiologist">
      <data key="d0">Anesthesiologist</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">An anesthesiologist administers anesthesia during surgical procedures to ensure patient comfort and safety.&lt;SEP&gt;An anesthesiologist administers anesthesia during surgical procedures to ensure patient comfort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Advanced Practice Providers">
      <data key="d0">Advanced Practice Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced Practice Providers, which include nurse practitioners and physician assistants, play an essential role in monitoring patients and delivering care.&lt;SEP&gt;Advanced practice providers include nurse practitioners and physician assistants who offer supportive care to patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nutritionists">
      <data key="d0">Nutritionists</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Nutritionists offer dietary recommendations to cancer patients tailored to their specific health needs and conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Nutritionists provide dietary advice to cancer patients based on their specific conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Genetic Counselors">
      <data key="d0">Genetic Counselors</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Genetic counselors assist patients in understanding genetic predispositions to cancers and their implications for treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Genetic counselors help patients understand how their family history might influence treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liver Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Liver Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Liver function tests assess how well the liver is working by measuring specific enzymes in the blood.&lt;SEP&gt;The liver function test assesses liver health by measuring enzymes in the blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Function Test">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Function Test</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A kidney function test checks waste products in the blood to evaluate kidney performance.&lt;SEP&gt;Kidney function tests evaluate waste products in the blood to determine if the kidneys are functioning properly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urine Cytology">
      <data key="d0">Urine Cytology</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urine cytology is a lab test that analyzes urine for the presence of cancerous or precancerous cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Urine cytology tests for cancer or precancerous cells in the urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Residents and Fellows">
      <data key="d0">Residents and Fellows</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Residents and fellows are physicians in training who may assist in care under the guidance of established doctors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dea00cf063e8831db4919330d94b1f2a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ureteroscopy">
      <data key="d0">Ureteroscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ureteroscopy is a similar procedure to cystoscopy, allowing examination of the upper parts of the urinary tract if abnormalities are suspected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Narrow-Band Imaging">
      <data key="d0">Narrow-Band Imaging</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Narrow-band imaging is a technology that enhances the visibility of tumors during cystoscopy without requiring a dye.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blue Light Cystoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Blue Light Cystoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Blue light cystoscopy is an advanced technique that uses blue light combined with a dye to better visualize tumors in the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Tract">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Tract</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The urinary tract is the bodily system that produces and expels urine, including the kidneys, ureters, bladder, and urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76a038845aaedb9ef7852018f26778b3</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urogram">
      <data key="d0">Urogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A urogram is a diagnostic imaging procedure used to visualize the urinary tract, helping to identify issues such as blockages or tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Renal Ultrasound">
      <data key="d0">Renal Ultrasound</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Renal ultrasound is a non-invasive imaging technique that provides real-time images of the kidneys, ureters, and bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyelogram">
      <data key="d0">Pyelogram</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A pyelogram is an imaging test that uses x-rays and a contrast solution to examine the kidneys and ureters for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SmokeFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">SmokeFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">SmokeFree.gov is a resource website designed to help individuals quit smoking and provide information about tobacco cessation.&lt;SEP&gt;SmokeFree.gov is a resource website designed to help individuals quit smoking and provide information on tobacco cessation.&lt;SEP&gt;SmokeFree.gov is an online resource offering support and information for individuals trying to quit smoking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BeTobaccoFree.gov">
      <data key="d0">BeTobaccoFree.gov</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">BeTobaccoFree.gov is a website providing resources and information on quitting tobacco use.&lt;SEP&gt;BeTobaccoFree.gov offers resources and support for individuals looking to quit tobacco use and encourages healthy habits.&lt;SEP&gt;BeTobaccoFree.gov offers resources and support for individuals looking to quit tobacco use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CDC.gov/tobacco">
      <data key="d0">CDC.gov/tobacco</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">CDC.gov/tobacco is a government website that provides information, resources, and support for tobacco cessation efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;CDC.gov/tobacco is a site hosted by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention with information on tobacco use and prevention strategies.&lt;SEP&gt;CDC.gov/tobacco is a site hosted by the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, providing crucial information on tobacco use, health impacts, and prevention strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Stones">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Stones</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney stones are hard deposits formed in the kidneys that can cause severe pain and complications in the urinary tract.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Blood Clot">
      <data key="d0">Blood Clot</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A blood clot is a mass formed by the coagulation of blood, which can obstruct blood flow and may be implicated in various medical conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inherited Cancer Risk">
      <data key="d0">Inherited Cancer Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inherited cancer risk refers to the genetic predisposition to develop certain types of cancers, often relatable to family history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tests">
      <data key="d0">Tests</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Tests refer to medical assessments used to diagnose conditions, often involving blood, imaging, or other methodologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 of Bladder Cancer indicates the presence of cancerous cells limited to the inner lining of the bladder, representing an early phase of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 0 of bladder cancer is characterized by the presence of cancer cells in the inner lining (urothelial layer) without invasiveness into deeper layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 Bladder Cancer signifies that the cancer has penetrated through the inner lining of the bladder into the connective tissue, but has not invaded the muscle layer yet.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 1 of bladder cancer indicates that cancer has grown into the connective tissue layer but has not invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 2">
      <data key="d0">Stage 2</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 2 Bladder Cancer indicates that the cancer has grown into the muscle layer of the bladder wall but has not spread beyond the bladder.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 2 bladder cancer has invaded the muscle layer of the bladder but has not spread further.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 Bladder Cancer suggests that the cancer has progressed through the bladder wall into surrounding fatty tissue and may have affected nearby lymph nodes.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 3 bladder cancer involves growth through the bladder wall and may affect surrounding fatty tissue and lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 Bladder Cancer represents advanced disease with possible spread to distant lymph nodes and organs, indicating a critical and possibly metastatic condition.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage 4 bladder cancer indicates advanced disease with invasion through the bladder wall and potential spread to distant lymph nodes and organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cells">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cells</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial Cells are the type of cells that line the bladder and are commonly the origin for bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Urothelial cells line the inner bladder and are the primary cells affected in bladder cancer, leading to the classification of urothelial cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carcinoma in Situ (CIS)">
      <data key="d0">Carcinoma in Situ (CIS)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Carcinoma in Situ (CIS) is a specific type of early-stage bladder cancer characterized by flat patches of cancer cells that have not invaded deeper layers yet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Low Grade Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Low Grade Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Low Grade Cancer is a classification indicating that the cancer cells are relatively slow-growing and have a better prognosis compared to high-grade cancers.&lt;SEP&gt;Low grade cancer refers to cancer cells that are slow-growing and typically have a better prognosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Grade Cancer">
      <data key="d0">High Grade Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High Grade Cancer refers to aggressive cancer cells that grow rapidly and have a higher likelihood of spreading.&lt;SEP&gt;High grade cancer refers to cancer cells that are fast-growing and often have a more aggressive behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29d1ec9b874dd38ef0f3a4a2081f548e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 0 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 0 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 0 bladder cancer indicates that abnormal cells have grown on the inside lining of the bladder without invading deeper layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 1 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 1 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 1 bladder cancer indicates that a tumor has formed and invaded the layer of connective tissue in the bladder wall but not the muscle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 3 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 3 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 3 bladder cancer indicates that the tumor has grown into the fat layer around the bladder and may affect nearby lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage 4 Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage 4 Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage 4 bladder cancer is the metastatic stage where the cancer has spread outside of the bladder to distant sites in the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="TURBT">
      <data key="d0">TURBT</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT (Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor) is a surgical procedure for bladder cancer that helps in the initial removal of tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) is a surgical procedure to examine and remove tumors from the bladder wall.&lt;SEP&gt;Transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT) is a surgical procedure to remove cancerous tumors from the bladder without invasive abdominal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical Therapy involves administering cancer medication directly into the bladder to treat bladder cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical Therapy is a method of treating bladder cancer where medication is delivered directly into the bladder to destroy cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy involves administering a liquid medication directly into the bladder to destroy cancer cells.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical therapy is a treatment often administered alongside TURBT to target bladder tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgery Options">
      <data key="d0">Surgery Options</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Surgery options for bladder cancer are varied, ranging from tumor removal to total bladder and surrounding organ removal.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treatment Options for Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Treatment Options for Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Treatment options include surgery, systemic therapy, radiation therapy, and clinical trials to manage bladder cancer effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Grade">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Grade</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer grade assesses how different cancer cells are from normal cells; high-grade cancer indicates a faster spread while low-grade cancer grows slowly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urothelial Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Urothelial Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urothelial cancer is a type of bladder cancer that primarily starts in the urothelial cells, and it may exhibit subtypes with distinct characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Glandular Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Glandular Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Glandular subtype is a form of urothelial cancer that shares features with glandular cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Squamous Cell Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Squamous Cell Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Squamous cell subtype refers to a variant of urothelial cancer that resembles squamous cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Micropapillary Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Micropapillary Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Micropapillary subtype is a rare variant of urothelial cancer characterized by small, finger-like projections of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plasmacytoid Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Plasmacytoid Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Plasmacytoid subtype of urothelial cancer features cells resembling plasma cells and may have different treatment responses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcomatoid Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Sarcomatoid Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sarcomatoid subtype is an aggressive form of urothelial cancer that can mimic sarcoma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nested Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Nested Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nested subtype is a rare variant of urothelial cancer that forms nests or clusters of cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymphoepithelioma-like Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Lymphoepithelioma-like Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymphoepithelioma-like subtype displays histological features similar to lymphoepithelioma and can complicate diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microcystic and Tubular Subtype">
      <data key="d0">Microcystic and Tubular Subtype</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">This subtype of urothelial cancer presents with microcystic and tubular features and may respond differently to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle-invasive bladder cancer is characterized by tumors that have invaded the muscle layer of the bladder wall, requiring more aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Non Muscle-Invasive Bladder Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Non muscle-invasive bladder cancer refers to early-stage cancers that have not invaded the muscle layer and typically require less aggressive treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Feedback Survey">
      <data key="d0">Feedback Survey</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The feedback survey aims to collect patient insights on the information provided about cancer treatment to improve clarity and usefulness.&lt;SEP&gt;The feedback survey is a means for patients to provide their opinions on cancer treatment information and resources provided by NCCN.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a158766f46bf752d6cdfe95c92f14547&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cystectomy is surgery to remove the bladder and is a common treatment for muscle-invasive bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Radical Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Radical Cystectomy is a surgical procedure primarily used for treating muscle-invasive bladder cancer by removing the bladder and surrounding organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Radical cystectomy is a major surgical procedure that removes the entire bladder, nearby lymph nodes, and possibly other organs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Partial Cystectomy">
      <data key="d0">Partial Cystectomy</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Partial cystectomy is a surgical procedure to remove part of the bladder, but it is rarely used for bladder cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversion">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversion</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary diversion is a surgical procedure required after bladder removal to create a new way for urine to exit the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ileal Conduit">
      <data key="d0">Ileal Conduit</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ileal conduit is a type of urinary diversion where a section of the small intestine is used to create a tube for urine to exit the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neobladder">
      <data key="d0">Neobladder</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Neobladder is a surgical procedure that replaces the bladder with a segment of the small intestine, allowing urine to exit the body through the urethra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch)">
      <data key="d0">Continent Cutaneous Pouch (Indiana Pouch)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Continent cutaneous pouch, or Indiana pouch, is a type of urinary diversion that allows for urine storage in a pouch created from intestine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burning Sensation">
      <data key="d0">Burning Sensation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Burning sensation during urination is a common symptom associated with bladder conditions and can indicate a medical issue.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Diversion Types">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Diversion Types</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">There are several types of urinary diversions that are used post-cystectomy to manage urination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana University">
      <data key="d0">Indiana University</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Indiana University is an academic institution known for developing the Indiana pouch surgical technique for urinary diversion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical BCG">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical BCG</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical BCG therapy is a treatment for bladder cancer that uses a weakened bacterium to stimulate the immune response against cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intravesical Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Intravesical Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intravesical Chemotherapy involves administering chemotherapy drugs directly into the bladder to kill cancer cells and prevent recurrence.&lt;SEP&gt;Intravesical chemotherapy is a localized treatment method that directly affects the bladder, as opposed to systemic chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indiana Pouch">
      <data key="d0">Indiana Pouch</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indiana Pouch is a type of urinary diversion created by surgeons at Indiana University, using a piece of the small intestine to store urine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG)">
      <data key="d0">Bacillus Calmette-Guérin (BCG)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">BCG is a weakened bacterium used in intravesical therapy for bladder cancer that prompts the immune system to attack cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Symptoms of Intravesical BCG">
      <data key="d0">Symptoms of Intravesical BCG</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Possible side effects of intravesical BCG therapy include fever, chills, urination urgency, and pain when urinating.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0a584e54ffa22e1c25f839302bcd727</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dose-Dense Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Dose-Dense Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Dose-dense chemotherapy is a protocol designed to speed up treatment by reducing the interval between doses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Checkpoint Inhibitors">
      <data key="d0">Checkpoint Inhibitors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Checkpoint inhibitors are a type of immunotherapy that helps the immune system detect and attack cancer cells more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 1 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 1 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 1 clinical trials assess the safety, dosage, and early efficacy of a new treatment, aimed at establishing initial data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 2 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 2 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 2 clinical trials evaluate the effectiveness of a treatment specifically against a certain type of cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Phase 2 trials focus on studying how effective a drug or approach is against a specific type of cancer in a controlled environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 3 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 3 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 3 clinical trials test a new treatment against existing standards to determine the new treatment's effectiveness.&lt;SEP&gt;Phase 3 trials test a drug or approach against standard treatments to determine their efficacy and benefits.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase 4 Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase 4 Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase 4 trials assess the long-term safety and benefits of an FDA-approved treatment after it has entered the market.&lt;SEP&gt;Phase 4 trials monitor the long-term effects and benefits of an FDA-approved treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Chemotherapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic chemotherapy is a treatment method that administers drugs intravenously to target cancer cells throughout the body.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vinblastine">
      <data key="d0">Vinblastine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vinblastine is another drug utilized in the combination chemotherapy regimen for treating bladder cancer, specifically within the ddMVAC protocol.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Padcev">
      <data key="d0">Padcev</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Padcev (enfortumab vedotin-ejfv) is a targeted therapy specifically designed to attach to the protein Nectin-4 found on bladder cancer cells.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Balversa">
      <data key="d0">Balversa</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Balversa (erdafitinib) is a targeted therapy effective for bladder cancer cases associated with specific genetic mutations in the FGFR3 gene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trodelvy">
      <data key="d0">Trodelvy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trodelvy (sacituzumab govitecan-hziy) is a targeted therapy that binds to the Trop-2 protein, aiding in the treatment of certain bladder cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trimodal Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Trimodal Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trimodal therapy is a treatment approach combining transurethral resection of bladder tumor (TURBT), chemotherapy, and radiation therapy to preserve the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-48b513355d29308e212a3074ba326b10</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCI’s Cancer Information Service">
      <data key="d0">NCI’s Cancer Information Service</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">NCI’s Cancer Information Service offers assistance in finding clinical trials and cancer treatment information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="placebo">
      <data key="d0">placebo</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A placebo is an inactive version of a medicine often used in clinical trials to compare the effects of the treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="supportive care">
      <data key="d0">supportive care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Supportive care refers to health care that alleviates symptoms and improves quality of life for patients with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="side effects of treatment">
      <data key="d0">side effects of treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Side effects of treatment encompass various negative symptoms experienced by patients undergoing cancer therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-541f1323a24c5990b4a6408e0c5442ab</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor (TURBT)">
      <data key="d0">Transurethral Resection of Bladder Tumor (TURBT)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">TURBT is a medical procedure used to examine and remove tumors from the bladder wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urinary Incontinence">
      <data key="d0">Urinary Incontinence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Urinary incontinence is a condition characterized by the involuntary leakage of urine, which can occur unexpectedly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor Exercises">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor Exercises</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic floor exercises are physical activities designed to strengthen the pelvic floor muscles and help regain control of urinary function.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Surgical Team">
      <data key="d0">Surgical Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The surgical team consists of healthcare professionals who perform and assist in surgical procedures, including those for bladder cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Stage">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Stage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer stage refers to the extent of cancer in the body, determining treatment options and prognosis for the patient.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV Strains">
      <data key="d0">HPV Strains</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV strains refer to the different types of human papillomavirus, some of which are high-risk and linked to various cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Dysplasia">
      <data key="d0">Anal Dysplasia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal dysplasia is a condition characterized by abnormal cell development in the anal area, which can progress to anal cancer if not treated.&lt;SEP&gt;Anal dysplasia refers to abnormal cell growth in the anus, which can progress to anal cancer if not treated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA)">
      <data key="d0">High-Resolution Anoscopy (HRA)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HRA is a diagnostic procedure used to examine the anal canal closely, especially when abnormal cells are suspected.&lt;SEP&gt;HRA is an advanced diagnostic procedure that allows healthcare providers to closely examine the anal canal for abnormalities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE is a physical examination technique used for the diagnosis and staging of anal cancer, involving the insertion of a finger into the rectum to check for abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;DRE is a routine examination technique used for the early diagnosis of anal cancer, involving the insertion of a finger in the rectum to check for lumps or abnormalities.&lt;SEP&gt;The digital rectal exam is a procedure recommended for monitoring anal cancer recurrence, particularly if there was no prior APR.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SISCCA">
      <data key="d0">SISCCA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SISCCA refers to a category of anal cancer that does not require extensive treatment but may still necessitate follow-up testing for potential spread.&lt;SEP&gt;Superficially invasive squamous cell carcinoma (SISCCA) is an early stage of anal cancer, characterized by small lesions that may not require immediate treatment if completely removed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colposcope">
      <data key="d0">Colposcope</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A colposcope is a special magnifying instrument used during HRA to examine tissues of the anal canal for abnormalities more effectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colorectal Surgeons">
      <data key="d0">Colorectal Surgeons</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Colorectal surgeons are specialists trained to treat disorders of the colon, rectum, and anus, including cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Colorectal surgeons are specialized doctors focusing on treating disorders of the rectum and anus, including cancers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HPV (Human Papillomavirus)">
      <data key="d0">HPV (Human Papillomavirus)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HPV is a virus that includes more than 100 strains, some of which are classified as high-risk and can lead to anal cancer, among other types.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SISCCA (Superficially Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma)">
      <data key="d0">SISCCA (Superficially Invasive Squamous Cell Carcinoma)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">SISCCA is the earliest form of anal cancer, represented by small lesions that may not be invasive and can be monitored or treated if necessary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Warts">
      <data key="d0">Anal Warts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal warts are growths that can occur due to HPV infection, which can also be a risk factor for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Autoimmune Disorders">
      <data key="d0">Autoimmune Disorders</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Autoimmune disorders, such as Crohn’s disease, can lead to immunocompromised states that increase the risk of developing anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Certain autoimmune disorders increase the risk of developing anal cancer, as they affect the body's immune response.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs)">
      <data key="d0">Sexually Transmitted Infections (STIs)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Certain STIs may contribute to the risk of developing anal cancer, especially when combined with other risk factors like HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9530f389987c13273449ab22a85170ae</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma">
      <data key="d0">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anal Squamous Cell Carcinoma is a type of invasive anal cancer diagnosed through biopsy and requires additional testing to plan treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Groin Lymph Nodes">
      <data key="d0">Groin Lymph Nodes</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Groin lymph nodes are a specific group of lymph nodes located at the junction of the thigh and abdomen, important in cancer spread assessment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Exam">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The lymph node exam is a medical procedure conducted to check the lymph nodes for signs of cancer spread, particularly in anal cancer patients.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HIV Testing">
      <data key="d0">HIV Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">HIV testing is a required assessment for patients due to the increased risk of developing anal cancer among those with HIV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HRA">
      <data key="d0">HRA</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HRA refers to Health Risk Assessments, which are used to evaluate and monitor the health status of patients, particularly in cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Invasive Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Invasive Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Invasive anal cancer is a more advanced form of anal cancer which requires careful testing and planning for appropriate treatment after diagnosis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pap Test">
      <data key="d0">Pap Test</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Pap test is a medical procedure used to screen for cervical cancer by collecting cells from the cervix.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a18089523f6dcd63cfa5664dc1c01618</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage I Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage I anal cancer indicates that cancer has not yet spread beyond the anus and can often be treated effectively with localized therapies.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage I anal cancer is characterized by a tumor that is 2 cm or smaller, with no spread to nearby lymph nodes or distant areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage II Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage II Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage II anal cancer implies that the cancer may have spread to nearby tissues but not to lymph nodes, requiring more aggressive treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage II anal cancer is divided into two substages based on tumor size, where tumors larger than 2 cm but not larger than 5 cm are classified as stage IIA, and those larger than 5 cm as stage IIB.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage III Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage III Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage III anal cancer includes three substages, where cancer may appear in nearby lymph nodes or invade adjacent organs.&lt;SEP&gt;Stage III anal cancer indicates significant progression, possibly involving local lymph nodes, necessitating a combination of therapies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage IV Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Stage IV Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Stage IV anal cancer is classified as metastatic, indicating that it has spread to distant areas such as the liver or lungs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michael">
      <data key="d0">Michael</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Michael is an anal cancer survivor who shares insights about coping with the illness and encourages mentoring others after recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Semen Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Semen Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Semen cryopreservation is the process of freezing semen for future use, allowing men the option of fathering children post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oocyte Cryopreservation">
      <data key="d0">Oocyte Cryopreservation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oocyte cryopreservation involves freezing unfertilized eggs for later use, providing women options for preserving fertility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Canal">
      <data key="d0">Anal Canal</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The anal canal is the terminal portion of the digestive tract that leads to the anus and is the primary site where anal cancer develops.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Region">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Region</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The perianal region includes the anal opening and the skin surrounding it, significant in discussions related to anal health and diseases.&lt;SEP&gt;The perianal region is the area surrounding the anus and can also be affected by anal cancer, often being the site of precancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Grade Anal Intraepithelial Neoplasia (AIN)">
      <data key="d0">High-Grade Anal Intraepithelial Neoplasia (AIN)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AIN is a precancerous condition characterized by abnormal cells on the anal canal surface that may progress to anal cancer if left untreated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="HSIL (High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesions)">
      <data key="d0">HSIL (High-Grade Squamous Intraepithelial Lesions)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">HSIL refers to severe dysplasia of squamous cells, indicating a high risk of progression to anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Electrocautery">
      <data key="d0">Electrocautery</data>
      <data key="d1">treatment</data>
      <data key="d2">Electrocautery is a procedure that uses electrical current to remove or destroy tissue, often used in treating localized precancerous lesions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship refers to the phase after cancer treatment where individuals focus on recovery, managing health, and psychological well-being.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-06384634b72466c480713ee8d57f25ed</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Immunosuppressant Use">
      <data key="d0">Immunosuppressant Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The use of immunosuppressants is identified as a risk factor for anal cancer, indicating its impact on the immune system's ability to fight diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Smoking History">
      <data key="d0">Smoking History</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A personal history of smoking contributes to increased risks of several cancers, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anoscopy">
      <data key="d0">Anoscopy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anoscopy is a medical procedure recommended for diagnosing anal cancer, allowing examination of the anal canal.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is a medical procedure used to examine the anal canal; its practice may vary based on the patient's condition after treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is a procedure that allows doctors to examine the anal canal and lower rectum for abnormalities during cancer surveillance.&lt;SEP&gt;Anoscopy is a procedure used for examining the anus and lower rectum, recommended for monitoring after treatment of cancer in lymph nodes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRE (Digital Rectal Examination)">
      <data key="d0">DRE (Digital Rectal Examination)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE is a clinical examination procedure utilized for screening and diagnosing various conditions related to the rectal area, including anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cervical Cancer Screen">
      <data key="d0">Cervical Cancer Screen</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Screening for cervical cancer is recommended alongside anal cancer evaluations, particularly for individuals at risk from HPV.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stage I-IV">
      <data key="d0">Stage I-IV</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The stages of anal cancer (I to IV) are critical in determining the extent of cancer spread and appropriate treatment strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High-Energy X-Rays">
      <data key="d0">High-Energy X-Rays</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">High-energy x-rays are utilized in radiation therapy to destroy cancer cells, an essential component in the treatment of anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear Accelerator">
      <data key="d0">Linear Accelerator</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A linear accelerator is a machine used in radiation therapy, delivering precise doses of radiation to treat tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fractionated Radiation">
      <data key="d0">Fractionated Radiation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fractionated radiation refers to the method of delivering radiation in small doses over several treatments, reducing side effects while targeting tumors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Over-the-Counter Ointments">
      <data key="d0">Over-the-Counter Ointments</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Over-the-counter ointments, like Aquaphor, are commonly used to help soothe skin irritation caused by radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Survivorship Guidelines">
      <data key="d0">Survivorship Guidelines</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Survivorship guidelines offer information on managing long-term effects of cancer treatments, including support for fertility and sexual health.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0bad5ee25cb854c10bdbc9d33b54a1a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitomycin">
      <data key="d0">Mitomycin</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug that may be used alongside other agents during treatment for anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Mitomycin is a chemotherapy drug used in regimens for treating anal cancer, administered intravenously.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Capecitabine">
      <data key="d0">Capecitabine</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy drug that can be administered with radiation therapy for anal cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Capecitabine is an oral chemotherapy medication taken twice daily during radiation therapy to treat anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU">
      <data key="d0">5-FU</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">5-FU (5-Fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy medication used in cancer treatment, particularly known for its use with radiation in anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU (5-Fluorouracil) is a chemotherapy medication used in cancer treatment, particularly known for its use with radiation in anal cancer.)&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU is a chemotherapy drug commonly used in various regimens for treating anal cancer and its recurrences.&lt;SEP&gt;5-FU, or fluorouracil, is a chemotherapy drug often administered via continuous infusion for treating cancer, particularly in combination with other agents.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Perianal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Perianal cancer refers to cancer occurring in the area surrounding the anus, which may require various treatment approaches depending on its grade and stage.&lt;SEP&gt;Perianal cancer refers to tumors located around the anus, and staging includes I, II, and III, with treatments including chemoradiation and potentially local excision.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="PICC Line">
      <data key="d0">PICC Line</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A PICC line is a type of catheter used for patients undergoing long-term intravenous therapy, allowing for easy access to the bloodstream.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hand-Foot Syndrome">
      <data key="d0">Hand-Foot Syndrome</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hand-foot syndrome is a side effect of certain chemotherapy drugs characterized by redness, swelling, and pain in the hands and feet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)">
      <data key="d0">Digital Rectal Examination (DRE)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">DRE is a physical exam where a doctor checks the anus and rectum for abnormalities, important for monitoring anal cancer survivors.&lt;SEP&gt;Digital Rectal Examination (DRE) is a physical exam where a doctor checks the anus and rectum for abnormalities, important for monitoring anal cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Progressive Disease">
      <data key="d0">Progressive Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Progressive disease indicates that cancer has advanced despite treatment, requiring further intervention or changes in treatment strategy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)">
      <data key="d0">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">APR is a surgical procedure for rectal cancer that involves removal of the anus, rectum, and surrounding tissue, typically requiring colostomy.&lt;SEP&gt;APR is a surgical procedure used to remove the anus and surrounding tissues, recommended for certain cases of anal cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of the anus and is used for anal cancer treatment, especially when cancer returns.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal resection (APR) is a surgical procedure used to remove the anus and surrounding tissues, typically for advanced anal cancer cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Janet">
      <data key="d0">Janet</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Janet is an anal cancer survivor who shares her insight on the stigma surrounding the disease and the experience of dealing with cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;Janet is an anal cancer survivor who shares her personal insights on the stigma surrounding the disease and the experience of dealing with cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lymph Node Exams">
      <data key="d0">Lymph Node Exams</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Lymph node exams involve checking the inguinal lymph nodes for signs of cancer, crucial in the follow-up care for anal cancer survivors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Complete Remission">
      <data key="d0">Complete Remission</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Complete remission indicates that there are no signs of cancer in the body and follows successful treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persistent Disease">
      <data key="d0">Persistent Disease</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Persistent disease refers to the state where cancer remains in the body but has not grown or spread following treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-29b3a349650a8bb55b318dcc6ecb0519</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d0">Metastatic Anal Cancer</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Metastatic anal cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the anal region to other parts of the body.&lt;SEP&gt;Metastatic anal cancer refers to cancer that has spread beyond the pelvis, often impacting treatment strategies and recommendations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Groin Dissection">
      <data key="d0">Groin Dissection</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Groin dissection is a surgical procedure that involves the removal of lymph nodes in the groin when they are affected by cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muscle Flap Reconstruction">
      <data key="d0">Muscle Flap Reconstruction</data>
      <data key="d1">procedure</data>
      <data key="d2">Muscle flap reconstruction involves taking tissue from another area of the body to close wounds after surgery, improving healing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Colostomy Bag">
      <data key="d0">Colostomy Bag</data>
      <data key="d1">equipment</data>
      <data key="d2">A colostomy bag is an external pouch used after a colostomy to collect stool, resulting from the surgical removal of the anus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="5-FU and Cisplatin">
      <data key="d0">5-FU and Cisplatin</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">A combination of 5-FU and cisplatin is used as part of chemotherapy regimens for treating anal cancer and its metastases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FOLFOX">
      <data key="d0">FOLFOX</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">FOLFOX is a chemotherapy regimen that includes 5-FU, leucovorin, and oxaliplatin, used for treating anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modified DCF">
      <data key="d0">Modified DCF</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Modified DCF is a chemotherapy regimen used in cases of metastatic anal cancer involving docetaxel, cisplatin, and fluorouracil.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cancer Surveillance">
      <data key="d0">Cancer Surveillance</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cancer surveillance involves regular monitoring through imaging and exams to check for recurrence of anal cancer post-treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5349eed06f06d7c9bcb541ced69e4664</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Thom">
      <data key="d0">Thom</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares experiences and encourages others through their recovery process.&lt;SEP&gt;Thom is an anal cancer survivor who shares personal experiences and insights related to cancer treatment and advocacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fluorouracil (5-FU)">
      <data key="d0">Fluorouracil (5-FU)</data>
      <data key="d1">drug</data>
      <data key="d2">Fluorouracil, also known as 5-FU, is a chemotherapy agent utilized to treat cancer by inhibiting cell growth and division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase I Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase I Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase I trials are the first step in testing new treatments, focusing on safety and side effects in humans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase II Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase II Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase II trials assess the efficacy of a drug or treatment in a specific type of cancer after initial safety testing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase III Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase III Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase III trials compare a new treatment against standard treatments to determine effectiveness and safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phase IV Trials">
      <data key="d0">Phase IV Trials</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Phase IV trials study the long-term effects and safety of treatments that have been approved by regulatory agencies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IV Hydration">
      <data key="d0">IV Hydration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">IV hydration is a medical procedure whereby fluids are administered intravenously to patients to prevent dehydration and adverse effects of chemotherapy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kidney Damage">
      <data key="d0">Kidney Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Kidney damage is a potential side effect of certain chemotherapy drugs, notably cisplatin, affecting their safe administration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hearing Problems">
      <data key="d0">Hearing Problems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Hearing problems can arise as a long-term side effect of undergoing chemotherapy treatments with certain drugs such as cisplatin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nerve Damage">
      <data key="d0">Nerve Damage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Nerve damage may occur as a side effect of oxaliplatin treatment, affecting patients' fingers and toes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palliative Radiation Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Palliative Radiation Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Palliative radiation therapy aims to relieve symptoms of cancer rather than cure it, often used in advanced cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Melissa">
      <data key="d0">Melissa</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Melissa is an anal cancer survivor who shares insights and experiences related to clinical trials and cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="IMRT">
      <data key="d0">IMRT</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Intensity-Modulated Radiation Therapy (IMRT) is an advanced radiation technique for treating anal cancer while minimizing damage to surrounding tissues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chemotherapy Regimens">
      <data key="d0">Chemotherapy Regimens</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Various chemotherapy regimens including mitomycin, capecitabine, and 5-FU are recommended for anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Team">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Team</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An Oncology Team comprises healthcare professionals specialized in diagnosing and treating cancer, focusing on patient care and support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Child Care Costs">
      <data key="d0">Child Care Costs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Child Care Costs refer to the expenses incurred for childcare during additional treatment appointments associated with clinical trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Insurance">
      <data key="d0">Insurance</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Insurance provides financial coverage for medical expenses, including some costs associated with cancer treatments and trials.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Copays">
      <data key="d0">Copays</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Copays are the fixed amounts that patients are required to pay out-of-pocket for medical services, which may include treatments related to cancer care.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Follow-Up Exam">
      <data key="d0">Follow-Up Exam</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A Follow-Up Exam is a medical check-up after receiving treatment to assess its effectiveness and see if further care is needed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="DRE">
      <data key="d0">DRE</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Digital Rectal Exam (DRE) is a procedure used to check for abnormalities in the anal area as part of cancer surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="APR Surgery">
      <data key="d0">APR Surgery</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdominoperineal Resection (APR) Surgery is a procedure to remove the anus and part of the rectum, often necessary when cancer does not respond to treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anal Cancer Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Anal Cancer Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Anal Cancer Foundation focuses on raising awareness and funding for anal cancer research and patient support.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation focuses on supporting anal cancer patients and provides resources such as peer-to-peer support programs.&lt;SEP&gt;The Anal Cancer Foundation is focused on increasing awareness and funding research specifically for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0d4cea71149e0e4157126efdaa0ea3e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-419f40f64b13b1b2d0fac3cfb9fde92a</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Pelvic Floor Physical Therapy involves exercises and treatments to strengthen pelvic muscles, which can be beneficial after anal cancer treatment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vaginal Dilator Therapy">
      <data key="d0">Vaginal Dilator Therapy</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vaginal Dilator Therapy helps alleviate discomfort caused by vaginal stenosis, often associated with radiation treatment for anal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oncology Nutrition">
      <data key="d0">Oncology Nutrition</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Oncology Nutrition addresses the dietary needs of cancer patients and survivors to manage symptoms as part of their recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Healthy Habits">
      <data key="d0">Healthy Habits</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Healthy Habits include lifestyle choices that help improve quality of life and prevent recurrence of cancer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Distress During Cancer Care">
      <data key="d0">NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Distress During Cancer Care</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">These guidelines provide resources and advice for handling distress during cancer treatment and recovery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cf41d1c08e8df109ad214d831867a5d9</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fecal Incontinence">
      <data key="d0">Fecal Incontinence</data>
