      <data key="d4">47.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Certain high-risk strains of HPV are known to significantly increase the likelihood of developing anal cancer, linking the virus directly to the disease.&lt;SEP&gt;Certain strains of HPV are linked to an increased risk of developing anal cancer, with high-risk types being particularly concerning.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV infection is a common risk factor for both cervical and anal cancers, indicating a shared causative relationship.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV infection is the most significant risk factor for anal cancer, linking viral presence to cancer development.&lt;SEP&gt;HPV is a major risk factor for developing anal cancer, establishing a clear link between the virus and this specific cancer type.</data>
