      <data key="d5">Chemotherapy is a common treatment approach for rectal cancer, aiming to eliminate remaining cancer cells after surgical removal.&lt;SEP&gt;Chemotherapy is a primary treatment method for rectal cancer, particularly post-surgery or for advanced stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, therapeutic approach</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rectal Cancer" target="Staging">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Staging is critical for rectal cancer management, determining treatment pathways based on the extent of tumor growth.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer management, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="ctDNA">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">ctDNA testing is being studied for its efficacy in detecting residual colorectal cancer cells after treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer monitoring, detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The guidelines include recommended screenings for colorectal cancer, enhancing patient knowledge.".</data>
      <data key="d6">patient education, cancer awareness</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colorectal Cancer" target="Familial Adenomatous Polyposis">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Familial Adenomatous Polyposis leads to numerous colon polyps that significantly raise the risk of colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">hereditary condition, cancer risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="NCCN">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN provides guidelines for colorectal cancer screening that include the recommendation for colonoscopy.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidance, screening recommendations</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients: Colorectal Cancer Screening">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN guidelines recommend colonoscopy as a primary method for screening for polyps and colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">guideline recommendation, screening</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Transabdominal Surgery">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A colonoscopy is a preliminary procedure that helps monitor conditions which may lead to the need for further treatment like transabdominal surgery.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical monitoring, surgical indication</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Local Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A colonoscopy may prompt a local excision if high-risk features are found in the removed tissues.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, treatment decision</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines provide information on the importance and procedure of colonoscopy in preventing colon cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">guidance, prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A polypectomy is often performed during a colonoscopy to remove polyps before they can develop into cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;During a colonoscopy, a polypectomy may be performed to remove polyps that could lead to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical procedure, cancer prevention&lt;SEP&gt;preventive measure, surgical procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Diagnostic Colonoscopy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A diagnostic colonoscopy is a specific procedure that falls under the broader category of colonoscopies, aimed at examining the colon for cancer or polyps.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical examination, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-85e75e869fcfcb7c5431dbcdd532137f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colonoscopy" target="Screening">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colonoscopy is a specific screening method used to detect colorectal cancer and assess polyps in the colon.</data>
      <data key="d6">detection method, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-ee254e84e63bd35682ed56c41cef9247</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polyps" target="Polypectomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is the procedure used to remove polyps, which is essential to preventing the progression to colorectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical removal, preventive measure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polyps" target="Hyperplastic Polyps">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps are a specific type of polyps that are considered benign and usually do not develop into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyp classification, low risk</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polyps" target="Inflammatory Polyps">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Inflammatory polyps, which generally arise from chronic inflammation, are a type of benign polyp that typically doesn't lead to cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">polyp classification, benign growth</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-925a474b4538dda470bfd817f9d9a17d</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyperplastic Polyps" target="Colonic Polyps">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Hyperplastic polyps are classified under colonic polyps and usually do not lead to cancer, but require monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d6">monitoring, benign growths</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Colonic Polyps">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is performed to remove polyps during a colonoscopy, preventing potential cancer.".&lt;SEP&gt;Polypectomy is the procedure used during a colonoscopy to remove polyps, reducing cancer risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, cancer prevention&lt;SEP&gt;surgical intervention, preventive care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-bb68daec67dd8f436d82814b0e61ff21</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Pedunculated Polyps">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is a procedure used to remove pedunculated polyps before they can develop into cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventative procedure, health intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Sessile Polyps">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is also applicable for the removal of sessile polyps to prevent cancer development.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventative surgery, cancer prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Polypectomy" target="Colon Polyp">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Polypectomy is a procedure specifically designed to remove colon polyps to prevent them from becoming cancerous.</data>
      <data key="d6">preventative care, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Sperm Banking">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists discuss sperm banking options with patients prior to starting treatments that may affect fertility.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncologists inform patients about sperm banking as a method of preserving fertility before cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, patient counseling&lt;SEP&gt;fertility preservation, patient guidance</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Egg Freezing">
      <data key="d4">15.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists discuss egg freezing with patients as a fertility preservation option during cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Oncologists inform patients about egg freezing as a method for preserving fertility during cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, patient options&lt;SEP&gt;treatment planning, fertility preservation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Ovarian Transposition">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists may recommend ovarian transposition to protect fertility during pelvic radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, fertility protection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Care Team">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The care team includes oncologists who are responsible for diagnosing and managing cancer treatment plans.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment collaboration, healthcare roles</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="MRI">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">An oncologist orders MRI scans to assess how deep the cancer has spread into the rectal wall.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnosis process, imaging procedure</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Steroid">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists may prescribe steroids to help manage potential allergic reactions during imaging procedures.</data>
      <data key="d6">medication management, treatment preparation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Oophoropexy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists may recommend oophoropexy to protect a patient's ovaries during radiation therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility protection, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Follow-Up Visits">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The oncologist conducts follow-up visits to monitor the patient's health and detect any changes after cancer treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;The oncologist conducts follow-up visits to track the patient's recovery and monitor for any signs of cancer return.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient care, medical monitoring</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Years 1 to 5 Follow-Up Care">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The oncologist determines the appropriate follow-up care schedule for patients in the first five years post-treatment based on individual risk.</data>
      <data key="d6">care management, treatment planning</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b3ba7430f3a31330247f52afe7e9c68a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL)">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists are responsible for overseeing treatment plans and patient care for individuals diagnosed with CLL.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment management, cancer care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-098a64d7118fc8e1eb3e91ba0124258c</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oncologist" target="Pathology Report">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oncologists rely on pathology reports to guide treatment decisions and inform patients about their cancer diagnosis and progression.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment planning, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-717174ace5855cc823d51c46c9a70648</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Transposition" target="Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian transposition can be performed to protect ovaries from radiation damage during cervical cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ovarian Transposition" target="External Beam Radiation Therapy (EBRT)">
      <data key="d4">22.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ovarian Transposition helps protect the ovaries from radiation damage during EBRT treatment.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian Transposition may be performed to protect ovaries from radiation damage during EBRT.&lt;SEP&gt;Ovarian transposition is performed prior to EBRT to protect ovarian function and preserve fertility during radiation treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility preservation, cancer treatment&lt;SEP&gt;surgical protection, treatment strategy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c9bfa651267335dd5e6ae5a24d85a072&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-be6e1cb2fe11f4d9962741c30d446a69</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Staging Protocol" target="Pelvic MRI">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Pelvic MRI is part of the staging protocol used to determine how much rectal cancer has spread.</data>
