      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Staging Protocol" target="Endorectal Ultrasound (EUS)">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Endorectal ultrasound is utilized in the staging protocol to assess rectal cancer in specific cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic procedure, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Staging Protocol" target="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans are incorporated into the staging protocol to check for potential metastasis of rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">imaging technique, staging process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-594536d6aaa61f8f1699ba50bf218a33</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Computed Tomography (CT) Scan" target="Cervical Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">CT scans help determine the extent and spread of cervical cancer, impacting treatment options."&lt;&lt;"diagnostic imaging, cancer assessment</data>
      <data key="d6">8</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-27b3a0f53b4be1d525497f87555d7a06</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fertility Preservation Options" target="Fertility Risks">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Fertility preservation options are critical for patients who face fertility risks from cancer treatments.</data>
      <data key="d6">fertility management, prevention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0bd86bf8fc82f14fa5f7f51d924514e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Low Anterior Resection">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Low Anterior Resection is a specific type of transabdominal surgery for rectal tumors providing a potential for bowel reconnection.&lt;SEP&gt;Low Anterior Resection is a specific type of transabdominal surgery performed for mid to upper rectal tumors.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical categorization, treatment specificity&lt;SEP&gt;surgical method, treatment specificity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Abdominoperineal Resection">
      <data key="d4">16.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Abdominoperineal Resection is another surgical method under the broader category of transabdominal surgery for lower rectal tumors.&lt;SEP&gt;Abdominoperineal Resection is another surgical technique within the scope of transabdominal surgeries for rectal cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical categorization, treatment specificity&lt;SEP&gt;surgical method, treatment specificity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Local Excision">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">If local excision reveals larger tumors or high-risk features, transabdominal surgery may be the next preferred treatment.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment escalation, surgical intervention</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Colorectal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colorectal Anastomosis may be performed during a low anterior resection to restore bowel continuity after tumor removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, restoration of function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Transabdominal Surgery" target="Coloanal Anastomosis">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Coloanal Anastomosis may be an alternative surgical procedure if bowel reconnection is not possible after tumor removal.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, alternative solution</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abdominoperineal Resection" target="Colostomy">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">A permanent colostomy is a necessary outcome following an abdominoperineal resection due to the removal of the anus and rectum.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical outcome, necessity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oxaliplatin" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oxaliplatin is often included in chemotherapy regimens for rectal cancer and has specific side effects linked to its use.&lt;SEP&gt;Oxaliplatin is one of the chemotherapy drugs used to treat rectal cancer and has specific side effects associated with its use.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy agent, treatment option</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oxaliplatin" target="Metastatic Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oxaliplatin is used in cancer treatments and poses risks of nerve damage, warranting careful monitoring.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy, side effects</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Oxaliplatin" target="Nerve Damage">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Oxaliplatin treatment can lead to nerve damage, impacting patients' quality of life and necessitating adjustments in therapy.</data>
      <data key="d6">side effects, treatment management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c35816f05289661283f683145d9f395f</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Irinotecan" target="Chemotherapy">
      <data key="d4">14.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Irinotecan is included in chemotherapy regimens for rectal cancer, linked with notable side effects that patients should be aware of.&lt;SEP&gt;Irinotecan is used in chemotherapy regimens for rectal cancer, with notable side effects impacting patient quality of life.</data>
      <data key="d6">chemotherapy agent, treatment option</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-37bca9f4d2ebaeed359c9b4eb7a0c1de</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="Perianal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Local excision is recommended as a treatment option for low-risk perianal cancers in early stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical treatment, cancer staging</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Local Excision" target="Stage I Anal Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Stage I anal cancer may be effectively treated with local excision, which involves surgically removing the tumor and surrounding tissue.</data>
      <data key="d6">treatment option, early-stage</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-0fdcce851d69aa713ed64ba1895bd5d1</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Staging" target="American Joint Committee">
      <data key="d4">18.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The American Joint Committee provides guidelines for cancer staging, facilitating treatment decisions for colon cancer.&lt;SEP&gt;The American Joint Committee sets the criteria for staging different cancers, including colon cancer, influencing treatment decisions.</data>
      <data key="d6">medical guidelines, cancer treatment</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Colectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy may be performed in conjunction with colectomy when reattachment of the colon is not possible.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical procedure, treatment strategy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-b5cfcc365cd093374564eb27dcbf5b82</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Ostomy Bag">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both ostomy bags and colostomies are related to surgical procedures involving waste diversion after pelvic surgeries.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical equipment, patient care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-9d0e32bdc49dbab9c2c953abf363e317</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Colostomy" target="Pelvic Exenteration">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Colostomy procedures may be performed during pelvic exenteration if the rectum is removed or altered.</data>
