      <data key="d7">chunk-6236b93e627d54f73f1c8fe341127e67</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidney Cancer" target="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Kidney cancer predominantly refers to various forms, with RCC being the most common type diagnosed in patients.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer classification, prevalent type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Urothelial Carcinoma">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Both RCC and Urothelial carcinoma are types of kidney-related cancers, although they originate in different parts of the renal system.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer types, renal origin</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Wilms Tumor">
      <data key="d4">6.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Wilms tumor is a specific form of kidney cancer occurring mainly in children, whereas RCC primarily occurs in adults.</data>
      <data key="d6">pediatric vs adult cancer, cancer variants</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide critical information on managing RCC and its treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient education, treatment guidelines</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Advanced Kidney Cancer">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Advanced kidney cancer often involves Renal Cell Carcinoma, particularly in its more severe stages.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer progression, disease state</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear cell RCC is the most common subtype of renal cell carcinoma that defines the majority of RCC cases.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer subtype, prevalence</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-clear cell RCC includes various subtypes of RCC, indicating the diversity within kidney cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer variant, classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Clear Cell RCC (ccRCC)">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Clear Cell RCC is the most common subtype of RCC, emphasizing its predominant role in the broader category of renal cell cancers.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, cancer type</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Non-Clear Cell RCC (nccRCC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Non-Clear Cell RCC is a significant category within RCC, constituting the remaining subtypes and diversifying the types of kidney cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, cancer diversity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Translocation RCC">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Translocation RCC represents a rare mutation leading to renal cell carcinoma, indicating genetic influences on RCC development.</data>
      <data key="d6">genetic mutation, cancer typology</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Collecting Duct RCC (cdRCC)">
      <data key="d4">12.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Collecting Duct RCC is a rare variant of RCC that arises from specific kidney structures, contributing to the understanding of RCC subtypes.&lt;SEP&gt;Collecting Duct RCC is classified under the broader category of renal cell carcinoma and is noted for its aggressive nature.</data>
      <data key="d6">subtype classification, cancer aggressiveness&lt;SEP&gt;subtype classification, cancer rarity</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Renal Medullary Carcinoma (RMC)">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">RMC is classified under RCC and is notable for its occurrence predominantly in younger patients of African descent.</data>
      <data key="d6">demographic factors, cancer subtypes</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Sarcomatoid RCC (sRCC)">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Sarcomatoid features can develop in any type of RCC, indicating a more aggressive form of the cancer.</data>
      <data key="d6">aggressive cancer features, cancer classification</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Treatment Plan">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The treatment plan for RCC is developed based on histological findings, tumor features, and potential metastasis.</data>
      <data key="d6">cancer treatment, personalized care</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Renal Cell Carcinoma (RCC)" target="Diagnostic Tests">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Diagnostic tests are employed to detect RCC and assist in determining the appropriate treatment plan.</data>
      <data key="d6">diagnostic approach, cancer detection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-a08bd0a819ea613e781d07188a350438</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Urinary System">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The kidneys are the key organs within the urinary system responsible for filtering blood and creating urine.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, bodily system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Artery">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal artery provides the kidneys with blood containing waste substances to be filtered out.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood supply, filtration process</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Tubules">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Renal tubules are part of the kidneys that are specifically involved in filtering and forming urine.</data>
      <data key="d6">filtration process, urine formation</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Renal Vein">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The renal vein is responsible for carrying the clean blood, filtered by the kidneys, back to the heart.</data>
      <data key="d6">blood flow, filtration outcome</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Bladder cancer can impact kidney function, particularly if left untreated, connecting these two organs in the context of urinary health.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ impact, urinary system</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kidneys" target="Ureters">
      <data key="d4">10.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The kidneys filter blood and produce urine, which is transported through the ureters to the bladder.</data>
      <data key="d6">urinary system, organ function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urinary System" target="Bladder">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The bladder is a crucial component of the urinary system that stores urine produced by the kidneys.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ function, waste management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ureter" target="Bladder">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The ureter connects the kidneys to the bladder, transporting urine for storage.</data>
      <data key="d6">urinary transportation, organ connection</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Ureters">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">Ureters carry urine from the kidneys and deposit it into the bladder for storage.</data>
      <data key="d6">urinary system, organ function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-7a890e27eba8c63d10f19b85a72fc14a</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Bladder" target="Urinary Incontinence">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The bladder is the organ affected by urinary incontinence, which involves involuntary urine leakage.</data>
      <data key="d6">organ relationship, condition</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-f2a44e885faf856aa579d824b3cd85e7</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urethra" target="Prostate">
      <data key="d4">7.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urethra is surrounded by the prostate, playing a crucial role in both urine and semen passage.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomical structure, dual function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-c6d28b36fe82792dfb86e98ef76b3b04</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urethra" target="Prostate Gland">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urethra runs through the prostate gland and is surrounded by it, indicating an anatomical and functional relationship.</data>
      <data key="d6">anatomy, function</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-aa251c48fc1fccfe45605a384f743662</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urethra" target="Bladder Cancer">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The urethra is the duct through which urine flows out, and bladder cancer can influence urethral health and function.</data>
      <data key="d6">urinary function, disease interaction</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-8aa2c7020ce6e89888e81972588726e3</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urethra" target="Radical Cystectomy">
      <data key="d4">8.0</data>
      <data key="d5">During a radical cystectomy, surrounding organs like the urethra may also be affected or removed if necessary.</data>
      <data key="d6">surgical intervention, anatomy</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-13e723cb8b0af4bcf2f1b0fd37912cb2</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Advanced Kidney Cancer" target="NCCN Guidelines for Patients">
      <data key="d4">9.0</data>
      <data key="d5">The NCCN Guidelines for Patients provide critical guidance for managing advanced kidney cancer, including treatment options.</data>
      <data key="d6">patient resources, cancer management</data>
      <data key="d7">chunk-48e138ec8da954a5667dc785e919c86b</data>
    </edge>
